var e=[{id:`mj-nbDYwxIeivs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:0,title:`Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:09`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`.NET`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Tuesday deploy that unsubscribed every customer at once`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:20 on a release Tuesday, not some dramatic midnight. The team bounced the API to ship a small feature, and the moment the new instances came up, every webhook integration on the platform went dark. The subscription registry was an in-memory dictionary — the deploy didn't just restart code, it erased the customer list. No errors, no alerts. Subscribers just stopped hearing from us, and support found out from a customer.`,`The fix was obvious: move subscriptions into Postgres with EF Core. But the first cut of the dispatcher made things worse before it made them better — it read subscriptions with fully tracked queries, wrote delivery-attempt rows one at a time inside the request path, and held it all open while firing synchronous HTTP calls to five subscriber endpoints. One slow subscriber and the connection pool started queuing behind outbound HTTP timeouts.`,`The postmortem wrote the roadmap for us: persist subscriptions and every delivery attempt — success or failure — but keep the request path thin. Read with AsNoTracking, let the migration index the foreign keys, wrap each POST in try/catch so failures become rows instead of swallowed exceptions, and be honest that five subscribers inline is fine while five thousand is a design flaw.`],problem:`How do you persist webhook subscriptions and delivery attempts in Postgres without turning the dispatch path into a synchronous, connection-hogging liability?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Npgsql`,`EF Migrations`,`dotnet-ef CLI`,`IHttpClientFactory`,`AsNoTracking`,`.NET`],note:`EF Core over Npgsql makes webhook subscriptions durable in Postgres and records every delivery attempt, while IHttpClientFactory keeps each outbound HTTP client short-lived and safely pooled.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the in-memory repo had to die`,body:[`So, where we left off: subscriptions lived in an in-memory repository. That was fine for a demo, but think about what it actually means — the moment the process stops, every subscription is gone. Your customers' integrations don't fail loudly, they fail silently, and silent is the worst failure mode a webhook system can have.`,`There's a second gap too. We send webhook requests and they either succeed or fail, but we write nothing down. Tracking delivery attempts is the foundation for retries later — you can't retry what you never recorded. So we need two tables, not one: subscriptions, and an append-only log of attempts.`,`Why not just serialize the dictionary to a JSON file on shutdown? Because you want concurrent writers, atomic writes, and the ability to ask 'give me every subscription for this event type' without loading the world into memory. That's a database's job, and Postgres plus EF Core is the boring, correct answer.`],quote:`An in-memory store isn't durable storage — it's a cache with amnesia.`,table:{headers:[`Storage`,`Survives restart`,`Concurrent writes`,`Query by event type`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory dict`,`No`,`Single process only`,`LINQ in memory`,`Demos only`],[`JSON file`,`Maybe`,`No — lock contention`,`Load-all-then-filter`,`Prototypes`],[`Postgres + EF Core`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Indexed WHERE clause`,`Production`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nbDYwxIeivs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Modeling the write: every attempt becomes a row`,body:[`Here's the shape I landed on. WebHookSubscription is the contract; WebHookDeliveryAttempt hangs off it with a foreign key on WebHookSubscriptionId — and the migration creates an index on that column automatically, which is exactly what you want, because 'show me all attempts for this subscription' is the query you'll run forever once retries exist.`,`Every attempt stores its ID, the subscription ID, and the serialized JSON payload. The trick is the try/catch around the HTTP send: a failed delivery isn't an exception to swallow, it's a row to insert. If the subscriber is down, that's data, not noise — and that data is what makes resilience possible in the next installment.`],quote:`A delivery you didn't persist is a retry you can never schedule.`,table:{headers:[`Field on the attempt row`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`Subscription ID (FK + index)`,`Makes per-subscriber history queries cheap`],[`Serialized JSON payload`,`Exact replay bytes for future retries`],[`Success / failure outcome`,`The signal retries and alerting will key off`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nbDYwxIeivs`,at:`6:16`}},{heading:`03 · The read path: AsNoTracking and pushing filters down`,body:[`Fetching subscriptions for an event type is a one-liner with EF Core: query the DbContext, filter where the event type matches, ToListAsync. But the default query does something sneaky — it tracks every entity in the change tracker, ready for a SaveChanges that's never coming. We're not updating subscriptions here; we're reading them to fire HTTP requests.`,`So: AsNoTracking. It skips the change-tracker bookkeeping entirely, which is a real win as the subscriber count grows. And push the event-type filter into the SQL itself — filtering in memory after ToListAsync is the lazy cousin of the N+1: you paid to ship rows you immediately threw away.`],quote:`The change tracker is a tax — only pay it when you plan to write.`,table:{headers:[`Query style`,`What it costs`,`When it's right`],rows:[[`Tracked query`,`Change-tracker overhead per entity`,`You'll update these rows`],[`AsNoTracking + ToListAsync`,`Near-raw read speed`,`Read-only fan-out — our case`],[`Filter in memory`,`Ships rows you discard`,`Almost never`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nbDYwxIeivs`,at:`10:14`}},{heading:`04 · The HTTP side: short-lived clients from the factory`,body:[`Small refactor, big consequences: the dispatcher no longer owns an HttpClient directly. We take IHttpClientFactory, call CreateClient() inside a using, and the client lives exactly as long as the one request it serves. That's the pattern the factory was built for — it manages handler lifetimes and connection pooling so you don't have to.`,`The naive alternative — one static HttpClient forever — works until DNS changes and the stale connections don't, or you scale out and start burning sockets. The other extreme, a new HttpClient per request without the factory, exhausts handlers and sockets under load. The factory is the boring middle ground that's actually correct.`],quote:`One HttpClient forever is a socket leak with a friendly API.`,table:{headers:[`HttpClient strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single shared instance`,`Familiar, one object`,`Stale DNS, no per-call config`,`Legacy code`],[`New per request, no factory`,`Simple mental model`,`Socket and handler exhaustion`,`Never`],[`IHttpClientFactory, per call`,`Pooled handlers, clean lifetimes`,`One more dependency`,`This build`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nbDYwxIeivs`,at:`14:11`}},{heading:`05 · The honest limit: 5 subscribers inline, 5,000 is an outage`,body:[`Run the demo and open the traces: one POST to our endpoint, then five outbound webhook requests, all tied to that single API call. That's the part that should make you uncomfortable. We're doing database reads and synchronous HTTP fan-out inside the request path — the caller's latency is now the sum of our slowest subscriber.`,`Scale the thought experiment: not five subscriptions, five thousand. You cannot reliably fire five thousand HTTP requests inline after a couple of queries — timeouts, thread-pool pressure, connection-pool contention, all of it compounds. Persistence was step one; the delivery attempts we now record are the raw material for a background dispatcher with retries. That's the next video.`],quote:`The database remembers, so the request path doesn't have to do everything.`,table:{headers:[`Dispatch model`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline fan-out`,`Simple, no extra infra`,`Caller latency = slowest subscriber`,`Up to ~10 subscribers`],[`Background worker + attempt log`,`Retries, isolation, backpressure`,`More moving parts`,`Hundreds to thousands`],[`Queue + outbox`,`Durable, at-least-once`,`Most infra to operate`,`Production scale`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Webhook persistence and dispatch topology`,caption:`One API request triggers an AsNoTracking read, N outbound deliveries via IHttpClientFactory, and N delivery-attempt rows in Postgres.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  A["Client: POST /events"] --> B["API Endpoint"]
  B --> D["WebHook Dispatcher"]
  D -- "AsNoTracking query by event type" --> C[("Postgres")]
  D --> E["IHttpClientFactory"]
  E -- "CreateClient()" --> F["HttpClient in using"]
  F -- "POST JSON payload" --> G["Subscriber endpoints x N"]
  F -- "try / catch" --> H["WebHookDeliveryAttempt row"]
  H --> C
  B -. "caller waits for all deliveries" .-> G`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One API request, N deliveries, N attempt rows`,caption:`The dispatcher reads subscriptions once, then per subscription creates a disposable client, posts the payload, and persists the outcome — success or failure.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Caller
  participant A as API Endpoint
  participant D as WebHook Dispatcher
  participant DB as Postgres via EF Core
  participant F as IHttpClientFactory
  participant S as Subscriber

  C->>A: POST /events
  A->>D: Dispatch(eventType, data)
  D->>DB: SELECT subscriptions WHERE event_type = @type (AsNoTracking)
  DB-->>D: matching subscriptions
  loop each subscription
    D->>F: CreateClient()
    F-->>D: disposable HttpClient
    D->>S: POST serialized JSON payload
    alt delivery succeeded
      D->>DB: INSERT delivery attempt (success)
    else HTTP failed or exception
      D->>DB: INSERT delivery attempt (failure)
    end
  end
  A-->>C: response after all deliveries`}],takeaways:[`Replace in-memory subscription stores before your first deploy — a single restart loses 100% of subscribers, and they fail silently.`,`Put AsNoTracking() on every read-only EF Core query — it skips change-tracker overhead and keeps materialization fast as the subscriber count grows.`,`Index every foreign key you filter or join on — the migration's index on WebHookSubscriptionId turns per-subscriber attempt history from a table scan into an index seek.`,`Persist exactly one delivery-attempt row per outbound call, success or failure — wrap the POST in try/catch so failures become data, not swallowed exceptions.`,`Keep inline fan-out under roughly 10 subscribers; at 5,000, synchronous dispatch inside the request path is an outage — move to a background dispatcher fed by the attempt log.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nbDYwxIeivs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-webhooks`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`},{id:`mj-nbDYwxIeivs`,note:`Another take on the same ground, from the same channel.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-mxsF7wOin-U`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:1,title:`Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:48`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The Checkout That Waited for Every Webhook`,paragraphs:[`Launch-week load test, 10:40 on a Tuesday morning. Orders were flowing at a gentle 50 rps and checkout p95 sat at a comfortable 140 ms — right up until our biggest partner flipped on their brand-new subscriber endpoint, which had a 30-second read timeout and the throughput of a sleepy intern. Within minutes our checkout p95 was 12 seconds, and support started asking why 'Place Order' suddenly felt like an international wire transfer.`,`The trace told the whole story in a single span. POST /orders saved the order with EF Core, then awaited webhookDispatcher.DispatchAsync — which queried every subscription from Postgres and foreach'd through them one by one, POSTing the payload with an HttpClient. No parallelism, no timeout budget, no early exit. The endpoint wasn't done until every subscriber had been served, which meant the slowest subscriber on the list set the latency of every single order.`,`The fix wasn't making the POSTs faster. It was refusing to do someone else's work on our clock: rename the doing-method to ProcessAsync, make DispatchAsync nothing but a Channel.Writer.WriteAsync of a tiny WebhookDispatch record, and let a BackgroundService drain the channel — one DI scope per message — while the customer's receipt prints in milliseconds.`],problem:`The endpoint awaited the entire webhook fan-out — the subscription query plus a sequential HttpClient POST loop — so request latency was coupled to the slowest subscriber and collapsed under load.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`EF Core`,`Postgres`,`HttpClient`,`System.Threading.Channels`,`BackgroundService`,`IServiceScopeFactory`],note:`The hot path writes an order via EF Core and used to await the whole webhook fan-out inline; System.Threading.Channels plus a BackgroundService now move that fan-out off the request path.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why is every checkout slow?`,body:[`Let's read the code the way the request experiences it. We create the order, SaveChanges writes it with EF Core, and then — right there in the endpoint — we await webhookDispatcher.DispatchAsync. Inside that method we hit the database for every subscription, then foreach our way through them, firing a POST with the HttpClient one at a time. It works beautifully in a demo with two subscribers. It is a latency bomb with twenty.`,`Do the arithmetic with me. If each POST averages 300 ms and one partner has a bad day at 10 seconds, a single order now costs the sum of everything — and under load those sequential HttpClient calls stack up, threads pile up waiting, and your p95 stops being a checkout number and becomes a webhook number. The first instinct is 'just parallelize it with Task.WhenAll.' Better — but you've still coupled the customer's response time to your slowest subscriber, and you still own delivery reliability inside a request handler that should be doing exactly one thing.`,`So the real question isn't 'how do we make the POSTs faster?' It's 'why is the request waiting for them at all?'`],quote:`The slowest subscriber sets your checkout latency — unless you stop waiting for them.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Await fan-out inline`,`Simple, easy to reason about`,`Latency = sum of all subscriber POSTs`,`Never at scale`],[`Task.WhenAll parallel`,`Wall-clock drops to slowest POST`,`Still couples request to slowest subscriber`,`Small N, low stakes`],[`Fire-and-forget Task.Run`,`Instant response`,`Lost on recycle, no backpressure, silent failures`,`Never for anything that matters`],[`Channel + BackgroundService`,`Sub-ms enqueue, fully decoupled`,`In-memory only, durability owed later`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: dispatch is not processing`,body:[`Here's the boundary I drew, and it's the whole refactor in one sentence: dispatching is recording intent, processing is doing the work. I made a little record — WebhookDispatch — that carries the event type and the payload, renamed the old method to ProcessAsync, and wrote a new DispatchAsync whose entire body is Channel.Writer.WriteAsync of that record. No database, no HTTP, microseconds. The endpoint's job ends the moment the intent is safely in the queue.`,`Why not just Task.Run fire-and-forget? Because it's a trap: the work dies when the app recycles, there's no backpressure when you produce faster than you consume, exceptions vanish into the void, and the backlog is invisible. And why not pull in RabbitMQ or Kafka on day one? Because for in-process fan-out, a channel gives you the same decoupling with zero ops footprint — and if you ever outgrow it, the DispatchAsync seam is exactly where you'd swap in a real broker without touching a single caller.`],quote:`Dispatching is a promise; processing is the work. Never charge the promise to the caller's clock.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,at:`2:14`}},{heading:`03 · The handoff: a channel as the in-memory bus`,body:[`The channel itself is System.Threading.Channels — think of it as an in-memory message bus between my components. The endpoint is the writer, a background service is the reader. Register the Channel<WebhookDispatch> as a singleton, inject it on both sides, and the handoff is one awaited WriteAsync. That's the entire 'message bus' — about five lines of code.`,`The one decision that actually matters here is bounded versus unbounded. Unbounded feels safe — the writer never waits — but if subscribers fall behind during a surge, the queue grows silently until the process OOMs, and your backlog is invisible until it kills you. Bounded with BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait means WriteAsync awaits when the channel is full, which is real backpressure: the system pushes back at the edge instead of dying in the middle. Capacity is a genuine decision — big enough to absorb a burst, small enough that a full channel is a loud signal rather than a slow leak.`],quote:`An unbounded queue doesn't remove the backlog — it just hides it until the OOM.`,table:{headers:[`Queue choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Unbounded channel`,`Writer never blocks`,`OOM under sustained surge, invisible backlog`,`Local dev only`],[`Bounded channel, Wait`,`Real backpressure, predictable memory`,`Writers await when full`,`Production default`],[`Bounded, DropOldest`,`Writer never waits`,`Silently loses events`,`Metrics, not money`],[`External broker`,`Durable, cross-process`,`Ops burden on day one`,`When durability is required`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,at:`2:38`}},{heading:`04 · The processor: BackgroundService, scopes, and the read loop`,body:[`On the consuming side sits WebhookProcessor, a BackgroundService that owns the read loop. It injects the channel and an IServiceScopeFactory, then lives inside an await foreach over Channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync. For every WebhookDispatch that comes off the reader, it creates a fresh scope, resolves the scoped dispatcher from that scope, and calls ProcessAsync. The endpoint never touches processing; the processor never touches the request.`,`Why the scope-per-message dance? Because my dispatcher depends on a scoped DbContext, and a BackgroundService is effectively a singleton — it can't safely capture a scoped service, and DbContext absolutely is not thread-safe. One scope per message gives every webhook its own context with clean disposal, and it costs an allocation that's noise next to an HTTP POST. Wrap the body in a try/catch per message too — one poison payload should log and move on, never take down the loop that everything downstream depends on.`],quote:`One scope per message: the channel carries the data, the scope carries the lifetime.`,table:{headers:[`Lifetime strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Singleton dispatcher`,`No scope cost`,`Captures scoped DbContext, unsafe under concurrency`,`Never`],[`Scope per message`,`Clean lifetime, safe DbContext, tidy disposal`,`Tiny allocation per message`,`Default`],[`Scope per batch`,`Fewer allocations`,`Long-lived context accumulates tracked entities`,`High-throughput tuning`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,at:`4:04`}},{heading:`05 · What we bought, and the bill still owed`,body:[`What did we buy? The endpoint now costs one SaveChanges plus one WriteAsync — checkout p95 is back to database speed and stays there no matter how many subscribers exist or how slow they get. Fan-out throughput is now an independent dial: add parallelism, add timeouts, add retries, all without ever touching the request path again.`,`And what do we still owe? Honesty about the channel: it's memory. Restart the app mid-surge and every queued webhook evaporates — the order committed, the subscriber never hears about it. There are no retries yet, and a flaky subscriber's failure is just a log line. The honest next step is an outbox: persist the WebhookDispatch in the same transaction as the order, and let the processor drain the table instead of the channel. The channel bought us latency; durability is a separate purchase — and the moment a lost webhook costs real money, you make it.`],quote:`In-memory queues buy latency, not reliability — pay for durability before you promise delivery.`,table:{headers:[`Durability step`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Channel only`,`Zero schema, ships in an afternoon`,`Restart loses queued webhooks`,`Non-critical events`],[`Outbox table + channel`,`Atomic with the order write, replayable`,`Extra table plus a poller`,`Money-adjacent events`],[`Managed broker`,`Durable, managed retries`,`Cost, another failure mode to operate`,`Multi-service fan-out`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,at:`4:54`}}],diagrams:[{id:`before-after-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Before and after: where webhook time was spent`,caption:`The left path awaited every subscriber inline; the right path returns after an O(1) enqueue and drains the channel in the background.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph BEFORE["Before - fan-out inside the request"]
        A1["POST /orders"] --> B1["EF Core SaveChanges"]
        B1 --> C1["await DispatchAsync"]
        C1 --> D1["SELECT subscriptions"]
        D1 --> E1["foreach subscriber"]
        E1 --> F1["HttpClient POST - sequential"]
        F1 --> G1["201 Created - seconds later"]
    end
    subgraph AFTER["After - decoupled handoff"]
        A2["POST /orders"] --> B2["EF Core SaveChanges"]
        B2 --> C2["Writer.WriteAsync dispatch"]
        C2 --> D2["201 Created - in milliseconds"]
        C2 --> CH[("Webhooks Channel")]
        CH --> P["WebhookProcessor BackgroundService"]
        P --> S1["Create DI scope"]
        S1 --> S2["ProcessAsync - query + POST fan-out"]
    end`},{id:`dispatch-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The decoupled request lifecycle`,caption:`The endpoint's clock stops at the enqueue; the processor's clock owns everything after the channel.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant E as Orders Endpoint
    participant DB as Postgres via EF Core
    participant CH as Webhooks Channel
    participant P as WebhookProcessor
    participant S as Subscriber

    C->>E: POST /orders
    E->>DB: INSERT order via SaveChanges
    E->>CH: Writer.WriteAsync WebhookDispatch
    E-->>C: 201 Created in milliseconds
    P->>CH: Reader.ReadAllAsync loop
    CH-->>P: WebhookDispatch event
    P->>P: Create scope, resolve dispatcher
    P->>DB: SELECT subscriptions
    loop per subscriber
        P->>S: POST event payload
        S-->>P: 2xx or timeout
    end
    P->>P: Dispose scope, await next message`}],takeaways:[`Never await fan-out in the request path: 20 subscribers at a 2s timeout each is a 40s worst-case checkout — enqueue instead and cap the endpoint at one SaveChanges plus one WriteAsync.`,`Bound the channel on purpose — e.g., capacity 10,000 with BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait — so backpressure surfaces as throttling at the edge instead of an OOM kill in the middle.`,`Enqueue must be sub-millisecond: if Writer.WriteAsync ever shows up on your p99 trace, the channel is full and the real bottleneck is downstream throughput, not the handoff.`,`Create one DI scope per message via IServiceScopeFactory — a singleton BackgroundService can't safely hold a scoped DbContext, and sharing one context across concurrent webhooks is corruption waiting to happen.`,`An in-memory channel loses every queued webhook on restart; the moment a missed delivery costs money, persist the dispatch in the same transaction as the order (outbox) and drain from the table instead of memory.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mxsF7wOin-U`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-webhooks`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-0uV-2latI-4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:2,title:`How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:29`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`LLM`],story:{headline:`The Tuesday Our Primary Melted at Lunchtime`,paragraphs:[`It was 12:40 on a Tuesday — no midnight drama, just a normal lunch-hour deploy. We shipped what the ticket called a "small" feature: a public stats page. By 12:55 our single Postgres primary was at 97% CPU, and the graph didn't spike so much as go vertical, like it was trying to escape the dashboard.`,`The cruel part: our writes — signups, payments, the actual money path — were timing out, yet writes were barely 8% of the query log. Tens of thousands of SELECTs from that one page were hogging the buffer cache, the connection pool, and the IOPS, starving the INSERTs queued behind them. We weren't out of database. We were out of database *for reads*.`,`We survived the afternoon with aggressive caching and rate limits, but the postmortem had one line I've repeated ever since: the primary is for writes; everything else is negotiable. That's the exact first move OpenAI describes in "Scaling Postgres to Power 800 Million ChatGPT Users" — and it's the same play I've run on stages a thousand times smaller.`],problem:`Every scaling story starts in the same place: one relational database, one connection string, and a read workload quietly strangling the write path.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`WAL Streaming`,`Read Replicas`,`Read LB`,`pgbouncer`],note:`One primary writer, its write-ahead log fanning out to a fleet of read replicas, and a health-checked load balancer spreading the selects — the exact ladder OpenAI describes.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the single-box ceiling`,body:[`So where do you start? Same place everyone does: one relational database and one connection string. That's genuinely how ChatGPT's backend started too, and honestly, it's enough for most systems you and I will ever build. Don't skip this step out of ambition — a well-tuned single Postgres is a monster.`,`The first instinct when it strains is vertical scaling: bigger instance, more cores, faster NVMe, more IOPS. And you *should* do it — it's the cheapest scale you'll ever buy, zero code changes. But it has two hard walls. First, a single box is a single point of failure: one bad deploy or hardware fault and you're down, not degraded. Second, every connection and every query — read or write — competes for the same CPU, cache, and disk. Your read traffic will eventually starve your write path, exactly like our Tuesday.`,`So the real question isn't "can one Postgres handle 800 million users?" It's "which workloads can one Postgres handle, and which should we peel off first?"`],quote:`Vertical scaling buys time. Replication buys options.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bigger instance`,`Zero code changes, cheapest first win`,`Still a SPOF, brutal cost curve`,`Always the first move`],[`Read replicas`,`Cheap read scale, read availability`,`Replication lag, primary streams all`,`Read-heavy apps (most apps)`],[`Sharding`,`Scales writes too`,`App complexity, painful resharding`,`Last resort, only when forced`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the WAL is already a changelog`,body:[`Here's the beautiful part about Postgres specifically: it already writes every change to the write-ahead log *before* it touches your tables. The WAL is a complete, ordered changelog of your database. Replication, then, is just subscribing to it.`,`You stand up another Postgres instance, point it at that stream, and it replays changes until it's a near-perfect mirror. OpenAI runs exactly this: one primary write database — still, today — and a fleet of roughly 50 read replicas fanning out from its WAL. One writer, fifty readers.`,`Is this infinitely scalable? No. The primary has to push its WAL stream to every replica, so at some point you're network- and CPU-bound on the writer side. But it's a surprisingly long runway — dozens of replicas off a single stream is normal, and when you outgrow it, you cascade: replicas feeding replicas.`],quote:`The WAL is already a changelog — replication is just hitting subscribe.`,table:{headers:[`Topology`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single-tier streaming`,`Simple, lowest lag`,`Primary pays WAL egress for all`,`Up to ~10–15 replicas`],[`Cascading`,`Offloads primary WAL egress`,`Deeper lag chains to debug`,`Large fleets (50+)`],[`Logical replication`,`Selective tables, cross-version`,`Heavier overhead, no DDL sync`,`Targeted subsets, migrations`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,at:`1:15`}},{heading:`03 · Load balancing the 90%`,body:[`Once you have replicas, the win only materializes if traffic actually goes to them. And the traffic is not 50/50. Most production systems — and ChatGPT is no exception — run something like 80/20 or even 90/10 read-to-write. Reads dominate almost everywhere.`,`So the routing rule is dead simple: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE go to the primary, always. SELECTs go through a load balancer that spreads them across healthy replicas. Add a replica, add read capacity — it's the closest thing Postgres gives you to a scaling dial.`,`And notice the second gift: availability. With one database, that box *is* your single point of failure. With a fleet, if the primary dies, you're degraded — reads keep flowing — while you promote a replica. That's the difference between an incident and an outage.`],quote:`Scale the 90%, protect the 10%.`,table:{headers:[`Routing`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`LB in front of replicas`,`Health checks, even spread`,`One extra network hop`,`Default choice`],[`App-level read/write split`,`No infra, per-query control`,`Logic sprawls across services`,`Small teams, quick win`],[`DNS round-robin`,`Trivial to set up`,`Blind to replica health and lag`,`Avoid in production`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,at:`2:27`}},{heading:`04 · When replicas lie: lag, staleness, and failover`,body:[`Now let's be honest about the catch: a replica is eventually consistent. It is replaying the past. Under load — a big migration, an aggressive vacuum, a burst of writes — lag grows, and a user who just saved a row may not read it back from a replica.`,`So you need a lag budget and a routing rule. Monitor replication lag per replica; if a replica drifts outside the budget, pull it from the load balancer rotation automatically. And for read-your-writes paths — "I just saved my settings, now show me my settings" — route to the primary or pin the session to it.`,`Failover has the same shape: replicas buy you read availability for free, but promoting one to primary is a separate, rehearsed operation. Drill it quarterly, or it's just a theory with a nice diagram.`],quote:`A replica is a promise about the past, not the present.`,table:{headers:[`Read type`,`Route to`,`Why`],rows:[[`Feeds, search, analytics`,`Replicas`,`Stale by seconds is fine`],[`Post-write confirmations`,`Primary`,`Read-your-writes contract`],[`Billing / auth checks`,`Primary or fresh replica only`,`Correctness beats scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,at:`3:36`}},{heading:`05 · Landing it on a .NET runway`,body:[`Let's land this on a .NET runway, because it's very stealable. Two connection strings: a write string to the primary, a read string to the load balancer. In EF Core or Dapper that's a read/write connection factory — an afternoon of work, not a rearchitecture.`,`Put pgbouncer in transaction-pooling mode in front, because Postgres connections are expensive and .NET apps love to open them by the hundreds. And before any of it: log your actual query mix for a week. If you're not read-heavy, none of this is your play yet.`,`That's the whole OpenAI ladder, honestly: one primary, WAL-fed replicas, a load balancer, and discipline about which queries go where. Nothing exotic — just the same moves, executed at a scale where the stakes are 800 million people.`],quote:`Measure your own ratio before you architect for someone else's.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,at:`4:20`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One Primary, a WAL Fan-out, and a Read Fleet`,caption:`Writes land on the single primary; its WAL streams to every replica; a health-checked load balancer spreads the SELECT storm.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    APP["App tier<br/>(.NET services)"] --> W["Write path<br/>INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE"]
    APP --> RD["Read path<br/>SELECT (the 90%)"]
    W --> P[("Primary<br/>single writer")]
    P --> WAL[("WAL<br/>change stream")]
    WAL --> R1[("Replica 1")]
    WAL --> R2[("Replica 2")]
    WAL --> R3[("Replica N<br/>~50 at OpenAI")]
    RD --> LB["Read load balancer<br/>health-checked"]
    LB --> R1
    LB --> R2
    LB --> R3`},{id:`lag-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Read-Your-Writes vs a Lagging Replica`,caption:`The write commits at LSN 100, the replica is still at 92 — the lag budget decides whether the read retries on the primary.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as App (.NET)
    participant LB as Read LB
    participant P as Primary
    participant R as Replica
    App->>P: INSERT order (write)
    P-->>App: commit (LSN 100)
    App->>LB: SELECT order (read)
    LB->>R: route to healthy replica
    R-->>App: rows as of LSN 92 (stale)
    Note over App,R: lag detected — outside budget
    App->>P: retry SELECT on primary
    P-->>App: rows as of LSN 100 (fresh)`}],takeaways:[`Log your query mix for one week before scaling anything — plan around the real ratio, which is typically 80/20 to 90/10 read-heavy.`,`Keep exactly one primary writer and scale reads with replicas: OpenAI serves 800M users with 1 primary and ~50 read replicas.`,`Cap direct WAL streaming at roughly 10–15 replicas; beyond that, cascade replication so the primary isn't paying egress for the whole fleet.`,`Define a replica-lag budget (e.g., p99 under 500 ms) and automatically pull lagging replicas out of load-balancer rotation.`,`Route read-your-writes and billing-critical reads to the primary, and put pgbouncer in transaction mode to cut open connections by ~10x.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How OpenAI Scaled Postgres to 800 Million Users (Step-by-Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uV-2latI-4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`paper-spanner`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-NPduWiPzhpE`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:3,title:`Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 24:14`,minutes:24,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The lunchtime demo where a search box melted Postgres`,paragraphs:[`It's 12:52 on a Tuesday and the biggest prospect of the quarter is watching our product lead demo the new content hub. She types 'analytics' into the search box. First keystroke: 2.1 seconds. Third keystroke: 5. By the sixth, the spinner is doing all the talking — and in the #incidents Slack channel, three completely unrelated endpoints are reporting timeouts.`,`The dashboard tells the story in one glance: the search endpoint is issuing LOWER(title) LIKE '%ana%' OR LOWER(excerpt) LIKE ... OR LOWER(content) LIKE ... against 1.8 million posts. Every keystroke is a fresh sequential scan — roughly 900ms of pure CPU — and demo traffic means eight of them running concurrently. The connection pool starves, CPU pegs at 100%, and every other query in the app is now queued behind a search box.`,`The fix takes an afternoon, and it isn't Elasticsearch, Redis, or a caching layer. It's making Postgres do the job it has shipped for two decades: a tsvector document over title, excerpt, and content, a GIN index on that exact expression, and EF.Functions.Matches in the LINQ query. Same endpoint, same UX — p95 drops from 4.2 seconds to 38 milliseconds, and the deal closes an hour later.`],problem:`Naive case-insensitive LIKE search turns every keystroke into a full sequential scan; one popular search box can exhaust the connection pool and take unrelated endpoints down with it.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`ASP.NET Core`,`tsvector + tsquery`,`GIN Index`,`OpenTelemetry`,`Aspire Dashboard`,`pgAdmin EXPLAIN`],note:`EF Core over Postgres, with the database's native search primitives (tsvector/tsquery plus a GIN index) doing the heavy lifting; OpenTelemetry, Aspire, and pgAdmin exist to expose the SQL that actually ships.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why Contains feels free`,body:[`So let's start where every tutorial starts — and honestly, where you should start too. One GET endpoint, inject the DbContext, and filter: Title.ToLower().Contains(term), same for excerpt, same for content. It's five lines of LINQ, it reads like filtering a list in memory, and on a demo database it feels completely free.`,`But watch what EF Core compiles that into: LOWER(title) LIKE '%xunit%' OR LOWER(excerpt) LIKE ... — and that leading wildcard is the entire story. A B-tree index can only walk a string from its left edge; '%term%' throws the left edge away, so Postgres reads every row on every request. The ToLower trick makes it worse, because now you're also paying a function call per row across three columns.`,`And here's the trap: this cost scales linearly with your table size and with your traffic — the two variables that always rise together. So the real question was never 'does it work', it's 'what does the plan look like at a hundred times this data'. Which means at some point you have to actually look.`],quote:`A leading wildcard is a promise that Postgres reads every row — your index never gets a vote.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Contains → LIKE '%t%'`,`Zero setup, familiar LINQ`,`Seq scan, no ranking`,`Tiny tables, admin screens`],[`ToLower + LIKE`,`Case-insensitive, still simple`,`Defeats indexes, CPU-heavy`,`Prototypes only`],[`pg_trgm GIN`,`Substring + fuzzy match`,`Bigger index, weak ranking`,`Autocomplete, typo tolerance`],[`tsvector + GIN`,`Indexed, ranked, language-aware`,`Whole-word matching only`,`Most product search`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Reading the SQL your ORM actually sent`,body:[`Here's the habit I want you to steal from this talk: never trust the query you think you wrote. I run OpenTelemetry, so in the Aspire dashboard I open the distributed trace for the search endpoint and there it is — the literal SQL statement EF sent to Postgres. I copy it, paste it into pgAdmin's query tool, drop my search term in, add the wildcards around it by hand, and run it with the execution plan on.`,`And the plan says it out loud: Seq Scan on blog_posts, with a Filter computing LOWER() on three columns for every single row. Nothing in the C# hinted at this. The LINQ looked like an in-memory Where; the database experienced it as 'touch all 1.8 million rows and run string functions on each one'.`,`That loop — trace, copy, explain — costs about ninety seconds, and it's the difference between 'works on my dataset' and 'works at production scale'. Make it part of the pull request, not part of the incident review.`],quote:`Your ORM writes the SQL — but only you can read the plan.`,table:{headers:[`Plan node`,`What it means`,`What you do`],rows:[[`Seq Scan on blog_posts`,`Every row is being read`,`Add the right index or rethink the query`],[`Filter: LOWER(col) LIKE ...`,`String function per row`,`Move to an indexed expression (tsvector/trigram)`],[`Bitmap Index Scan (GIN)`,`Index narrowing candidates first`,`Good — check heap fetches next`],[`Sort: ts_rank DESC`,`Ranking cost after the scan`,`Fine at this scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,at:`4:21`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the boundary: tsvector, tsquery, and the GIN index`,body:[`Now the actual move, and it's older than most of the tools we reach for instead. Postgres ships a real search engine: the tsvector type. You take the columns that matter — title, excerpt, content — push them through to_tsvector('english', ...), and you get a normalized, stemmed document: stop-words dropped, 'running' collapsed to 'run', word positions preserved so phrases and ranking work.`,`In EF Core with Npgsql you don't drop to raw SQL for this — you drop to the functions API. EF.Functions.ToTsVector builds the document, and .Matches(...) compiles to the @@ operator. Your search term goes through a tsquery function on the SQL side — my trace showed phraseto_tsquery — and that's the other half of the comparison.`,`But the query alone buys you nothing: an unindexed @@ is still a scan with extra steps. The payoff is a GIN index over that exact same tsvector expression, declared in OnModelCreating and applied when the app starts. And this is where people get burned: the index and the query must use the identical expression and the identical text search configuration. Swap 'english' for 'simple' on one side and the planner quietly stops using the index. Run the plan again and you'll see it flip from Seq Scan to Bitmap Index Scan — and the same query goes from ~900ms to double-digit milliseconds.`],quote:`Index and query must build the identical tsvector — drift one config word and Postgres silently falls back to a scan.`,table:{headers:[`tsquery function`,`Behavior`,`Reach for it when`],rows:[[`plainto_tsquery`,`Sanitizes input, ANDs all words`,`Plain single-box search`],[`phraseto_tsquery`,`Exact phrase adjacency`,`Word order matters to users`],[`websearch_to_tsquery`,`Quotes, OR, minus syntax`,`Google-style power users`],[`to_tsquery`,`Full boolean syntax`,`You control the input format`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,at:`8:21`}},{heading:`04 · Ranking, stemming, and the substring caveat`,body:[`So beyond raw speed, what did we actually buy? Ranking. ts_rank scores every match against the query, so ORDER BY rank DESC puts the post with 'xunit' in the title above the one where it's buried in paragraph nine. That's the line between a search feature and a search experience — and it comes from the database, not from a LINQ OrderBy on CreatedAt.`,`You also get the linguistics for free: stemming means 'tests' finds 'testing', casing is irrelevant by construction, and the 'english' configuration handles the stop-words. But be honest about the boundary — tsvector matches whole words after stemming, not substrings. If your users expect typing 'xun' to surface 'xunit', that's not a full-text search job; that's the pg_trgm extension with a trigram GIN index.`,`The good news is you don't have to pick a religion. Both indexes can live on the same table, and plenty of production search boxes quietly run trigram matching for the prefix-y, typo-ish queries and tsvector ranking for everything else.`],quote:`Relevance is a database feature — stop ordering search results by CreatedAt and calling it search.`,table:{headers:[`Need`,`tsvector + GIN`,`pg_trgm + GIN`],rows:[[`'xunit' finds 'xunit'`,`Yes`,`Yes`],[`'tests' finds 'testing'`,`Yes (stemming)`,`Partial`],[`'xun' finds 'xunit'`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Relevance ranking`,`ts_rank built in`,`Similarity score only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,at:`10:31`}},{heading:`05 · When Postgres stops being enough`,body:[`So the question everyone asks: do I need Elasticsearch? My honest answer is no — until you can name the feature that forces it. Postgres full-text search with a GIN index comfortably serves millions of documents in low tens of milliseconds, inside the database you already back up, monitor, secure, and pay for. One system, one failure domain, one less thing on-call.`,`The forcing functions are real, though. Typo tolerance and synonyms at scale, faceted filters, per-field boosting tuned by a dedicated search team, or a corpus heading past roughly ten million documents where you want independent horizontal scaling — that's when Meilisearch, Typesense, or Elastic earn their operational bill.`,`The discipline is sequencing: add the second system when the requirement shows up in your telemetry, not because a conference slide mentioned it. Start with the database you have, measure the plan, and upgrade on evidence — most apps never leave.`],quote:`Elasticsearch is a great system to add — and an even better one to postpone.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Stay on Postgres`,`Go external`],rows:[[`Corpus size`,`Up to a few million docs`,`10M+ docs, sharding needed`],[`Query features`,`Keywords, phrases, ranking`,`Facets, synonyms, typo tolerance`],[`Ops budget`,`One database to run`,`Team can operate a cluster`],[`Latency target`,`p95 under ~100ms`,`p99 under ~20ms globally`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,at:`14:53`}}],diagrams:[{id:`search-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One search box, two very different plans`,caption:`The same LINQ-shaped intent compiles to a sequential scan or a GIN-backed bitmap scan depending on which EF Core primitive you reach for.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Client: GET /blogs?search=xunit"] --> B["EF Core query pipeline"]
    B --> C["Naive: Title.ToLower().Contains(term)"]
    B --> D["FTS: EF.Functions.Matches on tsvector"]
    C --> E["SQL: LOWER(title) LIKE '%xunit%' OR LOWER(content) LIKE '%xunit%'"]
    D --> F["SQL: to_tsvector('english', title || excerpt || content) @@ phraseto_tsquery('english', 'xunit')"]
    E --> G["Plan: Seq Scan on blog_posts"]
    F --> H["Plan: Bitmap Index Scan via GIN"]
    G --> I["~900 ms per keystroke, CPU pegged"]
    H --> J["~40 ms, ordered by ts_rank"]`},{id:`fts-request-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a full-text search request`,caption:`From LINQ to ranked rows: EF Core lowers to the functions API, Postgres matches the GIN index, and relevance ordering comes back for free.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as User
    participant A as ASP.NET Endpoint
    participant E as EF Core
    participant P as Postgres
    U->>A: GET /blogs/full-text?search=xunit
    A->>E: BlogPosts.Where(SearchDocument.Matches(term))
    E->>P: SELECT ... WHERE to_tsvector('english', ...) @@ phraseto_tsquery('english', 'xunit') ORDER BY ts_rank(...) DESC
    Note over P: Planner matches GIN index on the identical tsvector expression
    P-->>E: Bitmap Index Scan, ranked rows
    E-->>A: List of BlogPost entities
    A-->>U: 200 OK, relevance-ordered results`}],takeaways:[`Any LIKE '%term%' over six-figure row counts is a sequential scan — EXPLAIN it before it ships, not during the demo.`,`Build the GIN index on the exact to_tsvector expression and config ('english') you query with; one word of drift and Postgres silently ignores the index.`,`Swap Contains for EF.Functions.Matches plus ts_rank ordering and expect a 10–100× p95 improvement on million-row tables.`,`Run the 90-second loop on every new query: OpenTelemetry trace → copy the SQL → EXPLAIN in pgAdmin; a Seq Scan means stop and rethink.`,`Stay on Postgres full-text search until roughly 10M documents or until you need facets, synonyms, and typo tolerance — then add a dedicated engine on evidence, not on hype.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Full Text Search in .NET With PostgreSQL and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPduWiPzhpE`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`search-architecture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-V_vdMQ5dpMw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:4,title:`Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 22:08`,minutes:22,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Release Day, Rolling Deploy, and the Cache That Vanished With Every Pod`,paragraphs:[`It's a Tuesday afternoon, not some dark hour — marketing flips the switch on a midday flash sale while we're rolling out the checkout service at the same time. Twelve pods behind a round-robin load balancer, each one proudly holding its own in-memory cache of product and price lookups. The deploy starts draining pods two at a time, and every fresh pod that comes up starts with a cache as empty as the day it was born.`,`Within ten minutes the dashboard tells the whole story: cache hit rate collapses from 92% to single digits, all twelve pods are fetching the same hot products straight from Postgres, and the 100-connection pool is fully leased. p99 latency goes from 40 ms to 4 seconds, health checks start timing out, and the deploy auto-rolls back — which, of course, is another full restart and another cold stampede. We watched the thing we built to protect the database become the reason it fell over.`,`The post-mortem wasn't about hardware. It was about placement: a cache that lives inside your process dies with your process. The fix was to move the cache out-of-process behind ASP.NET Core's IDistributedCache — in-memory for dev, Redis for production — wrapped in our own ICacheService so that not a single call site had to change.`],problem:`In-memory caching is scoped to a single service instance — every deploy, restart, or scale-out event throws it away and sends a thundering herd of cache misses straight into your database.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`IDistributedCache`,`Redis`,`In-Memory Cache`,`Json.NET`],note:`IDistributedCache is the swappable storage abstraction at the heart of the design; ICacheService is the thin typed wrapper the application actually codes against; Redis is the production backing store while the in-memory implementation covers dev and tests.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the cache inside my process lies to me`,body:[`Let's start where the story starts. MemoryCache is genuinely fast — we're talking nanosecond reads, no network hop, no serialization if you store objects directly. For a single-instance app it is the correct answer and I will defend it all day. The trouble begins the moment you run more than one instance, which is to say, the moment your app is successful.`,`Round-robin load balancing means the same user bounces between pods, and each pod holds its own private view of 'the truth.' Your effective hit rate gets divided by your instance count, invalidation now has to fan out to N processes, and a rolling deploy resets all of it at once. That last one is the killer — it converts your cache from a shield into a synchronized stampede trigger.`,`So why not sticky sessions, or just bigger boxes? Sticky sessions fight autoscaling and hand you hot spots; vertical scaling does nothing for cold starts because the cache still dies on every restart. The moment you have two instances or one deploy pipeline, you want the cache to live somewhere that outlives a process.`],quote:`A cache scoped to one process is a memo for one worker — the other eleven never got the memo.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory per instance`,`Nanosecond reads, zero network`,`N divergent caches, cold on every deploy`,`Single instance or L1 tier only`],[`Distributed (Redis)`,`Shared truth, survives deploys`,`0.5-2 ms network hop, ops burden`,`Multi-instance APIs`],[`L1 + L2 hybrid`,`Best hit rate, absorbs stampedes`,`Invalidation complexity`,`High read volume, hot keys`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_vdMQ5dpMw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Designing the surface: what goes on ICacheService`,body:[`Here's the design decision I want you to steal: don't let your controllers and handlers touch IDistributedCache directly. Define your own ICacheService in the application project — GetAsync<T>, SetAsync<T>, RemoveAsync, all async, all taking a CancellationToken — and make it public so the whole application can use it.`,`Why the extra layer? Because the moment you inject IDistributedCache everywhere, every call site has to know about key naming, JSON serialization, and null-means-miss semantics. That's three ways to make a mistake per call site, multiplied by however many call sites you have. The wrapper owns those decisions exactly once.`,`Could you grab a batteries-included caching library instead? Absolutely — stampede protection and cache tagging out of the box are real features. But fifty lines of your own interface keeps every door open, and swapping the backing store becomes a one-line DI change instead of a code review.`],quote:`Wrap the primitive once, and serialization stops being everyone's problem.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inject IDistributedCache directly`,`No extra layer to maintain`,`Serialization and key logic leak everywhere`,`Tiny apps, one call site`],[`Custom ICacheService wrapper`,`Typed API, swappable store`,`~50 lines you now own`,`Most real applications`],[`Full-featured cache library`,`Stampede protection, tagging built in`,`Heavier dependency, strong opinions`,`Large systems with hot keys`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_vdMQ5dpMw`,at:`5:29`}},{heading:`03 · Serialization: the cache only speaks strings`,body:[`Now the implementation, and it's refreshingly boring. GetStringAsync hands you back a nullable string; if it's null, that's a miss and you return null. If it's not, you JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T> it and hand the object back. On the way in, SetAsync serializes with SerializeObject and writes via SetStringAsync. Everything in the cache is a JSON string — that is the entire contract, and the set path and get path must agree on it forever.`,`Why strings instead of the byte[] overloads? Debuggability and portability. You can open redis-cli, type GET product:42, and actually read the payload — on release day that's worth more than the microseconds a binary format saves you. JSON also means any other service, in any language, can consume the same cache without a shared assembly.`,`The trap is schema drift. Deploy a new shape of your Product DTO and yesterday's cached JSON happily deserializes into half-defaulted objects — no exception, just quietly wrong data. That's why I version keys when shapes change and always set a TTL via DistributedCacheEntryOptions on the way in: AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow for most data, SlidingExpiration for hot reference lookups.`],quote:`Every cache read is a trust exercise — assume the bytes were written by a deploy that no longer exists.`,table:{headers:[`Payload format`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`JSON string`,`Human-readable, portable, easy to debug`,`Slower and larger than binary`,`Default choice`],[`Raw byte[] binary`,`Compact, fastest`,`Opaque in debugging, version-fragile`,`Hot paths, huge objects`],[`MessagePack`,`Compact and typed`,`Extra dependency, less inspectable`,`High-throughput services`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_vdMQ5dpMw`,at:`6:28`}},{heading:`04 · The swap: memory in dev, Redis in prod, zero call-site changes`,body:[`Here's the part people get wrong, so let me say it plainly: AddDistributedMemoryCache does not give you a distributed cache. It gives you the same per-process cache wearing an IDistributedCache costume. That's perfect for dev, tests, and single-instance deployments — and a lie everywhere else.`,`In production you register AddStackExchangeRedisCache with your connection string, and — this is the payoff — not one line of the DistributedCacheService implementation or ICacheService consumers changes. The abstraction did its job: the backing store is a configuration decision, not a code decision. Dev gets memory, staging gets Redis, prod gets Redis with a replica, all behind the same fifty lines.`,`But don't let the Redis safety net make you complacent. A flushed cache, a failover, or a brand-new environment still produces a 100% miss rate, and your database feels all of it at once. Distributed caching moves the cold-start problem from 'every deploy' to 'rare events' — it doesn't delete it. Keep your pool sized for the stampede and consider request coalescing for your hottest keys.`],quote:`IDistributedCache doesn't make your cache distributed — the backing store does.`,table:{headers:[`Backing store`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`AddDistributedMemoryCache`,`Zero setup, instant, great for tests`,`Per-process, dies on every deploy`,`Dev, tests, single instance`],[`StackExchange Redis`,`Shared across pods, sub-ms reads`,`Network hop, HA and ops burden`,`Multi-instance production`],[`SQL Server distributed cache`,`Reuses existing DB operations`,`Slowest option, adds DB load`,`Legacy estates, low traffic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_vdMQ5dpMw`,at:`8:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cache topology: one abstraction, N pods, swappable store`,caption:`Every pod runs the identical wiring — ICacheService over IDistributedCache — while the backing store flips between in-memory (dev) and Redis (prod) purely via DI registration. Misses fall through to Postgres.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    LB["Round-Robin Load Balancer"] --> P1["Pod 1: API + ICacheService"]
    LB --> P2["Pod 2: API + ICacheService"]
    LB --> P3["Pod N: API + ICacheService"]
    P1 --> DC["IDistributedCache"]
    P2 --> DC
    P3 --> DC
    DC -->|"prod registration"| R[("Redis")]
    DC -.->|"dev registration"| M["In-Memory per process"]
    P1 -.->|"cache miss"| PG[("Postgres")]
    P2 -.->|"cache miss"| PG
    P3 -.->|"cache miss"| PG`},{id:`cache-aside`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cache-aside read path: hit, miss, and backfill`,caption:`The full GetAsync<T> contract in one sequence: a hit is a string deserialized straight back; a miss queries Postgres, serializes to JSON, and writes it back with a TTL before answering the caller.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant API as API Endpoint
    participant CS as ICacheService
    participant DC as IDistributedCache
    participant R as Redis
    participant DB as Postgres
    API->>CS: GetAsync<Product>("product:42")
    CS->>DC: GetStringAsync("product:42")
    DC->>R: GET product:42
    alt cache hit
        R-->>DC: JSON string
        DC-->>CS: string
        CS->>CS: DeserializeObject<Product>
        CS-->>API: Product
    else cache miss
        R-->>DC: null
        DC-->>CS: null
        CS->>DB: query product 42
        DB-->>CS: row
        CS->>DC: SetStringAsync(key, JSON, TTL 600s)
        DC->>R: SET product:42 EX 600
        CS-->>API: Product
    end`}],takeaways:[`Budget 0.5-2 ms per Redis round-trip and keep cached payloads under ~100 KB — past that, serialization and transfer costs eat the win you came for.`,`Give every key a TTL: 5-15 minutes absolute for reference data, 30-60 s for known-miss negative entries; a cache with no expiry is just a second database you stopped maintaining.`,`Size your database connection pool for a 100% cache-miss deploy — a rolling restart of 12 pods can zero your hit rate in under two minutes.`,`Wrap IDistributedCache behind your own typed ICacheService so swapping in-memory for Redis costs exactly one line of DI registration and zero call-site edits.`,`Version your cache keys (prefix v2:, bump on DTO changes) so a deploy never deserializes yesterday's payload shape into today's objects.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Blazing Fast Caching Service In ASP.NET Core | Redis Compatible`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_vdMQ5dpMw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-pelbKSbL7AU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:5,title:`Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 21:05`,minutes:21,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`AWS`],story:{headline:`Release Day, 11:40 AM: The Catalog Melted the Primary`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:40 on release morning, not some romantic 3 AM. Marketing pulled the trigger on the newsletter, and within six minutes the product catalog — one RDS Postgres instance doing 100% of everything — was pinned at 94% CPU. Browsing is reads, reads were 95% of the traffic, and every one of them was fighting checkout writes for the same connection pool on the same box. p95 on the checkout endpoint went from 180 ms to 4 seconds while the on-call engineer was still finding the dashboard tab.`,`The fix looked obvious: create a read replica in the console, fifteen minutes, done. Someone flipped the app's single connection string over to the replica endpoint, CPU on the primary fell off a cliff, and the room exhaled. Twenty minutes later the first support ticket landed: 'I placed an order and my order history is empty.' Then another. Then eleven more. The replica was replicating — asynchronously — and during the very surge it was created for, it was trailing the primary by tens of seconds.`,`The real fix took an afternoon: two EF Core connection strings, request-scoped read/write routing, and a read-your-own-writes grace window that keeps post-write reads on the primary. The replica now carries the catalog traffic happily. But the lesson from that Tuesday is permanent: a read replica scales reads only if you can honestly answer which reads are allowed to be stale.`],problem:`A single RDS Postgres endpoint serving a 95%-read workload saturates at peak; a naive cutover to the replica endpoint trades CPU saturation for stale-read support tickets.`},tech:{stack:[`RDS Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Read Replicas`,`AsNoTracking`,`Connection Pooling`,`CloudWatch`],note:`RDS Postgres provides the managed writer plus async read replicas; EF Core picks the right endpoint per request, with AsNoTracking and CloudWatch ReplicaLag keeping the split honest.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why a bigger instance is a losing race`,body:[`Let me think out loud about the first instinct everyone has: buy a bigger box. And look, it works — bump the instance class, double the vCPUs, CPU drops from 94% to 60%, and you've bought yourself a week. But the workload shape hasn't changed. You're still paying one endpoint to serve 95% reads with the same hands that need to be doing writes, and the next launch puts you right back on the same curve, one price tier higher.`,`The 'why not X' here is caching, and I get it — catalog pages are cacheable. But before you reach for Redis, notice what you're signing up for: invalidation on every price change, stampede protection, and a partial answer — the cache only covers the keys you thought to warm. A read replica, by contrast, speaks full SQL. It answers every query the primary answers, which is exactly what a catalog API needs.`,`So the first thought I want to leave you with: look at your read/write ratio before you look at your instance class. If reads dominate, the cheapest structural fix is a second endpoint, not a third zero on the monthly bill.`],quote:`You can't out-buy a workload that's 95% reads — you have to split it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bigger instance class`,`Zero code change`,`Cost doubles per step, hard ceiling`,`Short-term relief`],[`Read replica(s)`,`Scales read CPU linearly, full SQL surface`,`Async lag, extra spend`,`Read-heavy APIs`],[`Cache hot keys`,`Microsecond reads, cheap`,`Invalidation, stampedes, partial coverage`,`Small hot key set`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pelbKSbL7AU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what a read replica actually promises`,body:[`Now let's walk the boundary of what a replica actually is, because the console makes it look like magic and it isn't. An RDS read replica is an asynchronous, physical streaming copy of the primary. Every write on the writer endpoint ships its WAL changes to the replica, which replays them. That word 'async' is doing all the work in that sentence — it's why the replica never slows down your writes, and also why it's never guaranteed current.`,`Two distinctions people blur constantly. First: a Multi-AZ standby is not a read replica. It's a synchronous shadow instance for high availability — you can't point queries at it; it exists to survive an AZ failure. Second: a replica is not a backup. It inherits every DELETE the primary accepts, almost instantly. If you need point-in-time recovery, that's automated backups and snapshots, full stop.`,`What you actually get with a replica is a second endpoint and a CloudWatch metric — ReplicaLag — that turns 'eventually consistent' from a vibe into a number. Watch that number during your peaks; it is the honest price tag of this entire pattern.`],quote:`'Eventually consistent' is a number in CloudWatch, not a vibe.`,table:{headers:[`Feature`,`Multi-AZ standby`,`Read replica`],rows:[[`Replication`,`Synchronous`,`Asynchronous`],[`Readable by app`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Purpose`,`HA / AZ failover`,`Read scaling`],[`Promotion`,`Automatic on failure`,`Manual, becomes standalone`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pelbKSbL7AU`,at:`1:56`}},{heading:`03 · Routing reads in EF Core without lying to yourself`,body:[`Here's where EF Core gets interesting, and where teams hurt themselves. A DbContext is bound to one connection string — you cannot cleanly point one context at the writer for the INSERT and at the reader for the next SELECT inside the same unit of work. So the naive move — swapping the single connection string to the replica — is exactly the bug from the story: every read-your-own-writes case becomes a support ticket.`,`The pattern that works is boring and explicit: two contexts over the same model — a WriteDbContext and a ReadDbContext — resolved per request. Writes and post-write reads in the same logical session go to the primary; everything else, especially list and catalog queries, goes to the replica with AsNoTracking. Skipping the change tracker is not a micro-optimization at replica scale — it's the difference between the replica being a relief and becoming a second primary.`,`Why not get fancy with a connection interceptor that checks ReplicaLag per query? You can — and at serious scale, lag-gated routing is the right endgame. But start with the request-scoped rule and a grace window: it's testable, it's explainable in code review, and it fails safe.`],quote:`Splitting reads isn't a connection-string change — it's a promise about which reads you can afford to be stale.`,table:{headers:[`Routing approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single context, swap string`,`One-line change`,`All-or-nothing, stale writes visible`,`Demos only`],[`Two DbContexts`,`Explicit, testable, per-request choice`,`Two registrations, migration care`,`Most read-heavy apps`],[`Connection factory / interceptor`,`Dynamic, can gate on lag`,`More moving parts to test`,`Lag-aware routing at scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pelbKSbL7AU`,at:`7:14`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just cache the catalog instead?`,body:[`Last thought out loud: is the replica even the right tool, or is this a cache problem? Run the query-surface test. If 80% of your read traffic hits twenty well-known keys — product detail by slug, homepage, top sellers — a cache is cheaper and faster and you should take it. If reads are broad and ad hoc — filters, search, pagination, admin reports — a cache turns into a distributed invalidation research project, and the replica wins by default.`,`And don't skip the boring gate: none of this matters if the security group doesn't allow it. The replica endpoint is reachable only through the same VPC rules as the primary — inbound TCP 5432 from your app's security group, never 0.0.0.0/0. Half the 'replica is down' tickets I've seen were a security group rule and a five-minute wait for the endpoint to finish provisioning.`,`So the honest decision matrix: cache the hot keys, replica the long tail, and measure ReplicaLag the whole way. Most real systems at scale end up with both — just not on day one.`],quote:`A cache answers the same question cheaply; a replica answers every question.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Cache (Redis)`,`Fastest hits, lowest cost`,`Invalidation, stampedes, partial surface`,`Hot keys, sessions`],[`Read replica`,`Full SQL surface, offloads primary CPU`,`~2x DB spend, lag`,`Broad read workload`],[`Both`,`Best p99 at scale`,`Most operational complexity`,`Large catalogs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pelbKSbL7AU`,at:`9:41`}}],diagrams:[{id:`rds-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`RDS read/write split topology`,caption:`One writer endpoint, N async replicas, and the two EF Core contexts that keep them honest.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph App["ASP.NET Core API"]
    W["WriteDbContext"]
    R["ReadDbContext · AsNoTracking"]
  end
  subgraph RDS["Amazon RDS · Postgres"]
    P[("Primary · writer endpoint")]
    RR1[("Read replica 1")]
    RR2[("Read replica 2")]
  end
  CW["CloudWatch · ReplicaLag"]
  W -->|"INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE"| P
  R -->|"SELECT"| RR1
  R -->|"SELECT"| RR2
  P -. "async streaming replication" .-> RR1
  P -. "async streaming replication" .-> RR2
  RR1 -. "metrics" .-> CW
  RR2 -. "metrics" .-> CW`},{id:`read-your-writes`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Read-your-own-writes routing`,caption:`Post-write reads stay on the primary for a grace window; everything else rides the replica.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant A as API · EF Core
  participant P as RDS Primary
  participant R as Read Replica
  C->>A: POST /orders
  A->>P: INSERT order (write context)
  P-->>A: 201 Created
  Note over A: mark session wrote-at T
  C->>A: GET /orders/mine
  alt within grace window after write
    A->>P: SELECT (read-your-own-writes)
  else outside grace window
    A->>R: SELECT (AsNoTracking)
  end
  A-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Split reads when primary CPU sustains above ~60-70% during normal peaks — provisioning a replica at 100% during a launch means writes are already queuing.`,`Give read-your-own-writes a grace window of 2-5 seconds on the primary, or gate replica routing on CloudWatch ReplicaLag < 100 ms.`,`Ship writer and reader connection strings on day one; retrofitting read routing into a single DbContext is a refactor, not a config flip.`,`Size pools to the instance: RDS Postgres max_connections = LEAST(DBInstanceClassMemory / 9531392, 5000), and every app instance adds its own default pool of ~100 connections.`,`Budget a replica at roughly the same cost as the primary (~2x DB spend) and use AsNoTracking on every replica query — otherwise cache the hot keys first.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Complete Guide To Amazon RDS, Scaling With Read Replicas and EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pelbKSbL7AU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`paper-spanner`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Tt5zIKVMMbs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:6,title:`Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:20`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`Docker`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Release Day, 2 PM: Every Cached Member Came Back With a Blank Email`,paragraphs:[`It was release day, early afternoon. The new member API had been live behind the load balancer for forty minutes when the first support ticket landed: a customer's profile showed a blank email and an empty name in the admin panel. Then a second ticket. Then five. The database was fine — every row intact, every field populated. The killer detail was the pattern: only members served at least once since the deploy came back hollow.`,`I opened redis-cli, pulled the JSON for one of the broken members, and everything was right there — id, email, first name, last name, all present and correct. The bytes in Redis were perfect. The object our API returned was not: Guid.Empty for the ID, empty value objects across the board. No exception, no warning, no log line. The deserializer was quietly refusing to touch anything behind a private setter, because Newtonsoft's default contract resolver only writes to public ones.`,`That's the moment this lesson lives in: a distributed cache that returns the right bytes and the wrong object. We'll rebuild this cache the proper way — Redis in Docker, IDistributedCache, the cache-aside decorator — and then fix the serialization trap that turned our performance win into silent data corruption.`],problem:`A distributed cache must reconstruct your domain object exactly on a hit; when the serializer can't honor private setters, every cached read silently returns hollow entities — right bytes, wrong object.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`Redis`,`Docker`,`IDistributedCache`,`StackExchange.Redis`,`Newtonsoft.Json`,`Decorator Pattern`,`Cache-Aside`],note:`Redis in a Docker container is the shared hot layer behind every API replica; IDistributedCache (from Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.StackExchangeRedis) plugs it into ASP.NET Core, a decorator wraps the repository, and Newtonsoft serializes entities that use private setters.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why MemoryCache stops being enough`,body:[`So here's where I always start: you've got IMemoryCache working beautifully on your laptop, and someone asks the innocent question — 'what happens when we run two instances behind the load balancer?' The answer is you now have two caches that disagree with each other. Instance A caches a member, instance B updates it, and depending on which server answers, you serve stale data for as long as your TTL — or forever, if you forgot to set one.`,`The fix isn't a smarter in-process cache; it's moving the cache out of process entirely. One Redis instance in a Docker container becomes the single shared hot layer — every replica reads and writes the same keys, restarts don't wipe it, and a deploy doesn't cold-start every server at once. That's what Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.StackExchangeRedis buys you: an IDistributedCache backed by Redis, registered with a single AddStackExchangeRedisCache call.`,`But let's be honest about the trade. An in-process read is nanoseconds; a Redis read is a network round-trip plus serialization — call it a millisecond or two on a good day. That's still ten to a hundred times faster than hitting Postgres for a hot row, which is why cache-aside wins for read-heavy data. But it means every cached type must now survive a serialize-deserialize round-trip intact. Remember that sentence — it's the rest of this talk.`],quote:`An in-process cache scales with your servers; a distributed cache scales with your traffic.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`IMemoryCache`,`Sub-millisecond reads, no network`,`Per-instance copies, invalidation drift`,`Single instance, immutable data`],[`IDistributedCache + Redis`,`Shared across replicas, survives restarts`,`Network RTT plus serialization cost`,`Multi-instance read-heavy APIs`],[`Query DB directly`,`Always fresh, zero invalidation`,`Hot keys melt the database`,`Low traffic or strict consistency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt5zIKVMMbs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the cache-aside decorator`,body:[`Architecturally, I don't want caching smeared through my controllers or domain services. I already have a MemberRepository, so I wrap it: CachedMemberRepository implements the same interface, takes the real repository plus an IDistributedCache, and every caller stays oblivious. That's the decorator pattern doing exactly what it's for — caching becomes a policy you can add, tune, or delete in one registration line.`,`The read flow is textbook cache-aside. Build a key — 'member:' plus the id, versioned so you can invalidate by renaming. GetStringAsync. Hit? Deserialize and return. Miss? Call the decorated repository, and here's the branch people get wrong: if the member comes back null, return null and cache nothing. There's no point caching an absence, and negative entries have a nasty habit of outliving the truth. Only on a real hit do we serialize to JSON and SetStringAsync with a TTL.`,`Why JSON strings instead of some binary format? Debuggability, mostly. When things go wrong at 2 PM on release day — and they will — being able to read the value straight out of redis-cli is worth more than the microseconds a binary format saves you.`],quote:`The decorator keeps caching a policy, not a personality trait smeared across your domain.`,table:{headers:[`Decision`,`Call`,`Why`],rows:[[`Cache null results?`,`No`,`No point; negatives outlive the truth`],[`Where the TTL lives`,`On SetStringAsync`,`Per-entry control beats global defaults`],[`Value format`,`JSON string`,`Human-readable in redis-cli at 2 PM`],[`Structural pattern`,`Decorator`,`Zero changes to callers or the repo`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt5zIKVMMbs`,at:`4:52`}},{heading:`03 · The serialization trap: right bytes, wrong object`,body:[`Now the part that bit us. First request: cache miss, member loads from Postgres, JSON goes into Redis, response is perfect. Second request: cache hit, JSON comes back — and if you look at the raw string, every field is there. But the deserialized Member has Guid.Empty for its ID and empty email, first-name, and last-name value objects. Same JSON, different object. What is going on?`,`Our domain model is encapsulated — private setters everywhere, because a Member shouldn't be mutated from the outside. Perfectly reasonable DDD. But Newtonsoft.Json's DefaultContractResolver only populates public setters by default, so it deserializes the JSON, matches every property, and then silently writes nothing. No exception. The cache returns an object that looks like a Member and behaves like a blank form.`,`The fix is a custom contract resolver: a small PrivateResolver class inheriting DefaultContractResolver, overriding CreateProperty. Call the base implementation, and if the property isn't writable, cast the member info to PropertyInfo, call GetSet(true) — the true says 'consider non-public setters' — and flip Writable on the JsonProperty. Wire it in through the ContractResolver argument on JsonSerializerSettings, and the round-trip is whole again. Ten lines of code standing between you and silent corruption.`],quote:`A cache hit that silently drops half your fields is a data corruption bug wearing a performance costume.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Newtonsoft default resolver`,`Zero configuration`,`Ignores private setters — silent data loss`,`DTOs with public setters`],[`Custom contract resolver`,`Honors encapsulated domain models`,`Extra code you must own and test`,`Rich entities with private setters`],[`System.Text.Json ctor binding`,`Fast, built into the platform`,`Constructor signatures must match JSON`,`New greenfield models`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt5zIKVMMbs`,at:`9:54`}},{heading:`04 · Before you ship: TTLs, hit ratios, and the stampede question`,body:[`Last block — what happens after the demo works. Every SetStringAsync gets a TTL; I start at ten to fifteen minutes for read-heavy entities like members. No expiry means you've delegated invalidation to hope. And when you change your model or your serialization, version the key prefix — 'member:v2:' — so old entries age out instead of colliding with new shapes.`,`Watch your hit ratio from day one. Read-heavy member lookups should sit above eighty percent; if you're below sixty, the problem is almost never Redis — it's key design, TTLs that are too short, or caching data nobody actually re-reads. And keep an eye on payload size: a member profile should be a few kilobytes. Once you're caching hundred-KB blobs, serialization CPU and network time start competing with the very database query you were trying to avoid.`,`One honest caveat: cache-aside has a known failure mode called the stampede. When a hot key expires, every concurrent request misses at once and they all stampede into Postgres. At today's scale, a jittered TTL is enough. If you grow into real herd territory, that's when you reach for per-key locks or request coalescing — but don't build that on day one.`],quote:`An entry without a TTL isn't optimism; it's an incident with a scheduled start time.`,table:{headers:[`Data`,`Suggested TTL`,`Rationale`],rows:[[`Member profile`,`10–15 min`,`Read-heavy, tolerates brief staleness`],[`Session / token data`,`5–30 min`,`Security-sensitive, sliding expiry`],[`Reference / lookup data`,`1–6 hours`,`Rarely changes, high hit ratio`],[`Negative (null) result`,`Skip, or < 60 s`,`Only to blunt a stampede`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt5zIKVMMbs`,at:`13:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cache-aside-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cache-aside decorator topology`,caption:`Hit path returns in ~1 ms from Redis; miss path falls through to Postgres and back-fills the cache with a TTL. Null results are returned but never cached.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Req["GET /members/:id"] --> Dec["CachedMemberRepository<br/>decorator"]
    Dec -->|"GetStringAsync member:id"| Redis[("Redis<br/>Docker container")]
    Redis -->|"hit: JSON string"| Dec
    Dec -->|"deserialize → Member"| Resp["200 OK · full object"]
    Redis -->|"miss: null"| Repo["MemberRepository<br/>decorated target"]
    Repo -->|"SELECT by id"| PG[("Postgres")]
    PG -->|"member row"| Repo
    Repo -->|"Member instance"| Dec
    Dec -->|"null → return, cache nothing"| Resp
    Dec -->|"SetStringAsync key, json, TTL"| Redis`},{id:`request-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request lifecycle: miss, backfill, hit`,caption:`The full read flow, including the null branch that must never be cached and the serialization round-trip that bit us on release day.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant D as CachedMemberRepository
    participant R as Redis in Docker
    participant M as MemberRepository
    participant DB as Postgres
    C->>D: GetMemberById(id)
    D->>R: GetStringAsync("member:" + id)
    alt Cache hit
        R-->>D: cached JSON string
        Note over D: Deserialize via PrivateResolver<br/>non-public setters honored
        D-->>C: Member with all fields intact
    else Cache miss
        R-->>D: null
        D->>M: GetMemberById(id)
        M->>DB: SELECT member by id
        DB-->>M: member row
        M-->>D: Member instance
        alt Member is null
            D-->>C: null — nothing cached
        else Member found
            D->>D: Serialize Member to JSON
            D->>R: SetStringAsync(key, json, ttl)
            D-->>C: Member
        end
    end`}],takeaways:[`Budget under 5 ms p95 for a Redis read on the same network; if you're over it, suspect payload size and connection churn before you blame Redis.`,`Attach a TTL to every SetStringAsync — start at 10–15 minutes for read-heavy entities; an entry without expiry is an incident with a scheduled start time.`,`Keep cached values under ~100 KB; past that, serialization CPU plus network time starts rivaling the database query you were avoiding.`,`Track hit ratio per endpoint and aim above 80% on read-heavy paths; below 60%, fix key design or TTLs before adding more cache surface.`,`Add a serialize→deserialize round-trip test in CI for every cached model — it catches private-setter data loss in minutes instead of on release day.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Caching In ASP.NET Core With Redis | Introduction`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tt5zIKVMMbs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-r_-NVUvyn04`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:7,title:`Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:30`,minutes:9,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Keycloak`,`Docker`,`Azure`,`AWS`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Morning Our Login Page Became the Outage`,paragraphs:[`We had a launch scheduled for 10 a.m. — marketing had been teasing it for two weeks, and the forecast said four to five thousand users in the first hour. Our .NET API was load-tested and ready. What we hadn't tested was Keycloak, which we'd deployed a week earlier with its defaults: the embedded H2 database it ships with for development. It worked perfectly in every demo. It worked perfectly with ten users. It did not work with five hundred people hitting the login button at once.`,`By 10:07 the login page started returning 'unexpected error.' Token requests were timing out — H2's file-based store was choking on concurrent session writes, and every client retry piled more lock contention onto the fire. Then, while someone restarted the container to 'clear it out,' we made it worse: with no persistent volume and no real database, the restart came back with an empty identity store. Every realm, every client, every redirect URI we'd registered — gone. Our support channel filled with people locked out of their own accounts.`,`The fix took an afternoon, and it wasn't exotic: a real Postgres 17 container with a named volume, a dedicated keycloak schema to isolate its tables, and a compose file where Keycloak waits on — and retries against — the database instead of racing it. The lesson stuck: an identity provider is only as available as the database underneath it, and 'it worked in dev' is not a durability guarantee.`],problem:`Keycloak ran on its development-only embedded H2 database with no persistent volume; midday login traffic triggered lock contention and timeouts, and an unplanned restart erased the entire identity configuration — the platform needed a production Postgres backend with a dedicated schema, durable storage, and race-free startup wiring.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres 17`,`Keycloak`,`Docker Compose`,`Named Volumes`,`Keycloak Schema`,`Aspire Dashboard`,`.NET API`],note:`The production identity tier: Keycloak handling OIDC logins, backed by a persistent Postgres 17 instance with a dedicated keycloak schema, all wired together in Docker Compose and observed through the Aspire dashboard.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the embedded database lies to you`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody puts on the slide: Keycloak ships with an embedded H2 database, and it's fantastic — for what it's for. You run the container, you build a realm, you demo OAuth flows, everything's instant. And because it works, the temptation is to think it's a database. It's not, really — it's a scratch pad. Keycloak's own docs are blunt about it: development and testing only.`,`Why does it fall over in production? Think about what an identity provider does all day. Every login, every token refresh, every session check is a small, urgent write. Under a midday surge those writes pile up concurrently, H2's file-based locking serializes them, latency climbs, threads queue — and the login page becomes the outage. That's not a bug; it's a design boundary.`,`Could you just bolt a volume onto H2 and call it durable? You'd solve persistence and still be standing on a configuration the vendor won't support in production. When your auth tier is down, 'unsupported configuration' is not a sentence you want to hear from support. So the first decision is easy: production Keycloak means a production database, and for most .NET shops that's Postgres.`],quote:`A dev database never fails in dev — it waits for the day everyone logs in at once.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Embedded H2`,`Postgres 17`],rows:[[`Concurrency`,`File locks serialize writes`,`MVCC handles parallel sessions`],[`Durability`,`Ephemeral by default`,`Named volume plus backups`],[`Vendor support`,`Dev and test only`,`Fully supported for Keycloak`],[`Ops tooling`,`Almost none`,`pg_dump, monitoring, replicas`],[`Verdict`,`Demos and local dev`,`Production identity tier`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: give Keycloak its own schema`,body:[`Once you've decided on Postgres, the next question is: share or separate? I like one Postgres instance serving both the app and the platform — it's cheaper and it's one thing to monitor — but only if each tenant of that instance gets its own schema. So before Keycloak ever boots, I connect and run CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS keycloak.`,`Why bother? Keycloak brings dozens of its own tables — realms, clients, sessions, event logs. If those land in public next to your application tables, every migration, every backup script, every 'what is this table?' audit gets muddier. With a dedicated schema you can back up the identity store independently, drop and recreate it in a sandbox without touching app data, and see at a glance what belongs to whom.`,`The alternative is a dedicated Postgres instance just for Keycloak, and at serious scale that's the right call — it isolates the failure domain so a runaway identity workload can't starve your app's connections. But for most teams starting out, one instance with clean schema boundaries gets you ninety percent of the benefit for ten percent of the ops cost.`],quote:`One database can serve two masters — if each gets its own schema.`,table:{headers:[`Layout`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Shared instance, separate schema`,`Cheap, one thing to monitor, simple dev`,`Shared failure domain`,`Startups, staging, small prod`],[`Dedicated instance for Keycloak`,`Isolated failure domain, independent scaling`,`More cost and ops surface`,`High-traffic production`],[`Everything in public schema`,`Zero setup`,`Migration and backup tangle`,`Never — avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,at:`0:30`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring the compose file: the boot race you have to rig`,body:[`Now the compose file, and this is where the boot race lives. Postgres first: the postgres 17 image, a container name, the usual environment variables for user and password, a volume mapping so pgdata survives restarts, and port 5432 exposed on the compose network. Nothing exotic — but every one of those lines is doing durability work.`,`Then Keycloak, pointed at Postgres through its database config — the KC_DB style environment variables with the JDBC URL naming our keycloak schema. And here's the trap: depends_on only controls start order, not readiness. Postgres takes a handful of seconds to accept connections, Keycloak boots fast, loses the race, and exits. Without a restart policy you get a crash loop and a mystery.`,`So I rig the race two ways: depends_on for ordering, plus a restart policy so Keycloak keeps retrying until Postgres is genuinely ready. If you want to be stricter, gate it with a Postgres healthcheck and condition service_healthy — then Keycloak doesn't start until the database actually answers. Either way the principle is the same: never let the identity tier assume the database is up; make it wait, and make it recover.`],quote:`depends_on decides who starts first — the restart policy decides who survives the race.`,table:{headers:[`Startup strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`depends_on only`,`One line, correct order`,`No readiness check — boot race`,`Never alone in prod`],[`depends_on + restart policy`,`Self-healing, simple`,`Retry noise in logs`,`Solid default`],[`Healthcheck gate`,`Deterministic, clean start`,`More YAML to maintain`,`Production-grade`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,at:`1:10`}},{heading:`04 · Proving it before the crowd arrives`,body:[`Here's the habit I want you to steal: bring the database up first, alone. Run the compose file with just the Postgres service, open Docker Desktop, confirm the container's green, then connect and create the keycloak schema before Keycloak has ever seen this database. That way Keycloak's migrations land exactly where you planned — no surprises about which schema owns which tables.`,`Then wire the rest of the story: your .NET API's OIDC config pointing at Keycloak, and the Aspire dashboard so every hop — API to Keycloak to Postgres — shows up in one trace. When login latency spikes, you want to know whether the time went to token issuance or to the database, and Aspire answers that without a single extra agent.`,`And don't stop at 'it works.' Restart the whole stack and confirm your realms survived — that's the volume doing its job. Kill the Postgres container for thirty seconds and watch Keycloak recover via the restart policy. A setup isn't finished when the demo passes; it's finished when you've watched it fail and come back.`],quote:`Done isn't when the demo passes — it's when you've watched it fail and recover.`,table:{headers:[`Verification`,`How to test`,`Passes when`],rows:[[`Persistence`,`Restart the whole stack`,`Realms and clients intact`],[`Schema isolation`,`List tables per schema`,`Keycloak tables only in keycloak`],[`Boot race`,`Start Keycloak before Postgres`,`Keycloak retries, then connects`],[`Observability`,`Trace one login in Aspire`,`API, Keycloak, Postgres hops visible`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,at:`1:47`}},{heading:`05 · What production actually demands next`,body:[`Once Postgres is under Keycloak, a few operational realities show up fast. Backups stop being optional — that database is now the single source of truth for who your users are, so a scheduled pg_dump or a managed Postgres tier moves from nice-to-have to day-one requirement. Same for secrets: the plaintext POSTGRES_PASSWORD in your compose file is fine on the laptop, not in the cluster — inject real credentials.`,`And think about upgrade ordering. Postgres minor upgrades and Keycloak upgrades are now independent events, and Keycloak runs its own schema migrations on startup — so test those migrations against a copy of production data before you point the new version at the real thing. The database you choose on launch day is the one you'll be backing up, patching, and migrating for years. Choose like it's permanent, because it is.`],quote:`The database you pick on launch day is the one you'll be backing up for years.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,at:`2:31`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Production identity topology in one compose file`,caption:`Users hit the .NET API, which delegates to Keycloak; Keycloak's state lives in Postgres 17 on a named volume, with Aspire watching every hop.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  users["Users / Browsers"]
  subgraph compose["Docker Compose network"]
    api[".NET API"]
    kc["Keycloak"]
    pg[("Postgres 17")]
    vol[("Named volume - pgdata")]
    aspire["Aspire Dashboard"]
  end
  users --> api
  api -- "OIDC redirect + JWT validation" --> kc
  kc -- "JDBC on port 5432" --> pg
  pg --- vol
  api -. "traces" .-> aspire
  kc -. "traces" .-> aspire`},{id:`boot-race`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The Keycloak boot race, rigged correctly`,caption:`depends_on sets the order, the restart policy absorbs Postgres's boot window, and migrations land in the dedicated keycloak schema.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Docker Compose
  participant P as Postgres 17
  participant K as Keycloak
  C->>P: Start with env vars, volume, port 5432
  C->>K: Start after depends_on
  K->>P: JDBC connect attempt
  P--xK: Connection refused - still booting
  K->>K: Exit nonzero
  C->>K: Restart policy relaunches Keycloak
  K->>P: JDBC connect retry
  P-->>K: Connected
  K->>P: Run migrations inside keycloak schema
  K-->>C: Healthy - serving OIDC tokens`}],takeaways:[`Never ship Keycloak on its embedded dev database — it's development-only by design and buckles under concurrent production logins.`,`Attach a named volume to Postgres on day one; an ephemeral database wipes every realm, client, and user on the first container restart.`,`Isolate Keycloak in its own Postgres schema — one schema per platform service keeps backups, migrations, and drop/recreate operations away from app tables.`,`Pair depends_on with a restart policy or healthcheck gate so Keycloak rides out the 5–30 second Postgres boot window instead of crash-looping.`,`Load-test at 2× your peak login rate before launch — token issuance and session writes are the hottest writes in your identity tier.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Don't Deploy Keycloak Without a Real Database (Postgres Setup)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r_-NVUvyn04`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-enpblqLhzzo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:8,title:`EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 19:57`,minutes:20,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`1,001 UPDATEs: The Routine Raise That Froze Payroll`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:40 on a Tuesday — quarterly compensation review day. HR opens the internal tooling and triggers the bulk raise we shipped last sprint: a .NET 6 minimal API endpoint, PUT /increase-salaries, that bumps every employee of a company by $10. The flagship company has exactly 1,000 employees. The spinner spins.`,`Thirty seconds later the request times out. HR clicks retry — twice. Each attempt loads the company and all 1,000 employees as tracked entities, mutates every salary in memory, sets the company's LastSalaryUpdate to DateTime.UtcNow, and calls SaveChangesAsync — which emits 1,001 individual UPDATE statements inside one transaction. Row locks on Employees stack up across the three concurrent runs, every pooled connection ends up waiting, and suddenly unrelated endpoints start failing health checks too.`,`The postmortem took twenty minutes because the logs made it obvious: over a thousand statements per request for what is conceptually one line of SQL. We swapped the loop for a single set-based UPDATE — ExecuteUpdateAsync on the EF Core 7 services, a parameterized raw UPDATE on the EF Core 6 ones — and the same request dropped from a 30-second timeout to single-digit milliseconds. EF Core wasn't the wrong tool; using it row-by-row for set-based work was.`],problem:`A bulk salary update implemented as a tracked-entity loop drives 1,001 per-row UPDATE statements inside one long transaction, saturating the connection pool and timing out the API under concurrent runs.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`.NET 6`,`SQL Server`,`Minimal APIs`,`Change Tracker`,`ExecuteUpdate`,`Dapper`],note:`EF Core's Change Tracker handles entity loading, diffing, and SaveChanges against SQL Server; ExecuteUpdate (EF Core 7), raw SQL, or Dapper provide the set-based escape hatch when per-row updates stop scaling.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the naive loop costs 1,001 statements`,body:[`So here's the setup from the demo: an empty .NET 6 Web API, two entities — Company and Employee — a DbContext with the one-to-many wired up in OnModelCreating, and seed data: one company, 1,000 employees at a salary of 100. Then the interesting bit: a minimal API endpoint, PUT /increase-salaries, that takes a companyId and raises every employee's salary by 10.`,`The naive version writes itself: fetch the company, fetch the employees — tracked, because that's the default — loop through, salary += 10, set company.LastSalaryUpdate to DateTime.UtcNow, call SaveChangesAsync. Feels clean. But turn on statement logging and count: one UPDATE per employee, plus one for the company. That's 1,001 UPDATE statements for what is, conceptually, a single line of SQL.`,`Yes, EF Core will batch some of those into fewer round trips — but the per-entity diffing, the parameter mapping, and the per-row statements are all still there. You're paying per row, and the database never gets to use its set-based machinery.`],quote:`The Change Tracker doesn't do bulk math — it does 1,001 polite little UPDATEs and calls it a day.`,table:{headers:[`Statement`,`Count`,`Why it's there`],rows:[[`SELECT Company`,`1`,`Load the aggregate root (tracked)`],[`SELECT Employees`,`1`,`Materialize 1,000 rows you never display`],[`UPDATE Employees`,`1,000`,`One per modified entity — snapshot diff`],[`UPDATE Company`,`1`,`LastSalaryUpdate audit column`],[`Total per request`,`1,003`,`1,001 UPDATEs inside one transaction`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what the Change Tracker is actually buying you`,body:[`Before we throw it out, let's be fair to the Change Tracker. What you get for that overhead is real: identity resolution, snapshot-based change detection, navigation fix-up, cascade behavior, optimistic concurrency tokens. For 'load one employee, edit their salary, save' it is exactly the right tool and I'll defend it all day.`,`The trap is scope. People reach for the obvious knobs — AsNoTracking on the read, AutoDetectChanges off, bigger batch sizes — and those genuinely help the read path and the CPU profile. But none of them change the fundamental shape: one UPDATE statement per modified row. You've optimized the walk; you haven't canceled the walk.`,`So the real question isn't 'how do I make the loop faster,' it's 'does this work belong in application memory at all?' For set-based math — raise every salary in a company — the answer is no. Those rows never need to exist in C#.`],quote:`Optimizing the loop still leaves you with a loop — the win is deleting it.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,at:`2:14`}},{heading:`03 · The set-based pivot: ExecuteUpdateAsync`,body:[`Here's the pivot. EF Core 7 gave us ExecuteUpdateAsync, and it reads like the LINQ you already write: filter with Where, then describe the change with SetProperty. The result is one statement — UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary + 10 WHERE CompanyId = @id — executed entirely inside SQL Server. No materialization, no tracking, no SaveChanges. One round trip.`,`On .NET 6 with EF Core 6 you're not stuck, either: Database.ExecuteSqlInterpolatedAsync gives you the same single, parameterized UPDATE, or you reach for Dapper if it's already in the solution. Same shape, same win.`,`But know what you're trading away. ExecuteUpdate bypasses the Change Tracker completely — no entity lifecycle events, no SaveChanges interceptors, no automatic concurrency token checks. If your domain has rules that fire on save, this path skips them. That's not a bug; that's the deal you're signing.`],quote:`If the database can do the math, don't ship 1,000 rows to C# and back.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Tracked loop + SaveChanges`,`Full lifecycle, events, concurrency`,`1 UPDATE per row, dies at volume`,`Single-entity edits`],[`ExecuteUpdateAsync (EF7+)`,`1 statement, no materialization`,`Bypasses tracker and interceptors`,`Bulk set-based math`],[`ExecuteSqlInterpolated`,`Works on EF6, parameterized`,`SQL strings live in code`,`EF6 bulk updates`],[`Dapper`,`Fast, explicit SQL`,`Second data stack to maintain`,`Hot paths, complex reads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,at:`3:54`}},{heading:`04 · Locks, transactions, and that audit column`,body:[`Now the part that actually caused the outage: transactions and locks. SaveChangesAsync wraps all 1,001 statements in a single transaction, which sounds safe — until you realize every UPDATE takes row locks on Employees, and the transaction lives as long as the whole statement chain. Under concurrent payroll runs those locks stack, requests queue behind each other, and all 100 pooled connections end up waiting.`,`The set-based UPDATE is one atomic statement — SQL Server takes the locks, does the math, releases them in milliseconds. And if you still need the audit write to the company — LastSalaryUpdate = UtcNow — chain a second ExecuteUpdateAsync inside one explicit transaction. Two statements, one commit, still a trivially short lock window.`,`The rule of thumb I've landed on after a few of these: it's rarely CPU that takes an API down during bulk writes. It's lock time multiplied by concurrency.`],quote:`Lock time, not CPU, is what takes your API down.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,at:`5:04`}},{heading:`05 · Choosing per workload, not per project`,body:[`So where does this land? I've stopped treating 'EF Core versus raw SQL' as a project-level identity decision. It's a per-statement decision. Single entity with business rules and concurrency? Tracked entities and SaveChanges, every time. Bulk set math? ExecuteUpdateAsync or raw SQL. Complex reporting joins where I don't want to fight LINQ translation? Dapper, or an AsNoTracking projection shaped straight to a DTO.`,`And measure before you choose: turn on statement logging, or call ToQueryString() to see the SQL a query becomes, and count what a request actually emits. In the demo, the difference between 1,001 statements and 1 was the difference between a 30-second timeout and single-digit milliseconds. The framework is perfectly happy to do the slow thing silently — counting is on you.`],quote:`Pick the tool per statement, not per project.`,table:{headers:[`Workload`,`Reach for`,`Why`],rows:[[`Edit one entity, fire domain events`,`Tracked + SaveChanges`,`Lifecycle, concurrency, interceptors`],[`Bulk math over many rows`,`ExecuteUpdateAsync`,`One set-based statement, no reads`],[`Bulk math on EF Core 6`,`Raw SQL / Dapper`,`Same single-statement shape`],[`Read-only reporting`,`AsNoTracking projection`,`No tracker overhead, shape to DTO`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,at:`9:27`}}],diagrams:[{id:`update-strategies`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two paths for the same bulk raise`,caption:`The tracked loop pays per row and holds locks for the whole transaction; the set-based path is one atomic statement.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["PUT /increase-salaries?companyId=1"] --> B{"Update strategy"}
    B -->|"Naive tracked loop"| C["SELECT Company (1 row)"]
    C --> D["SELECT Employees (1,000 rows, tracked)"]
    D --> E["salary += 10 in memory"]
    E --> F["SaveChangesAsync()"]
    F --> G["1,001 UPDATE statements<br/>in one transaction"]
    G --> H["Locks held for seconds<br/>pool saturates"]
    B -->|"Set-based"| I["ExecuteUpdateAsync:<br/>UPDATE Employees<br/>SET Salary = Salary + 10<br/>WHERE CompanyId = @id"]
    I --> J["1 statement, 1 round trip<br/>atomic, ms-level lock window"]`},{id:`naive-request-timeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of the timing-out request`,caption:`What the wire actually sees when SaveChangesAsync runs a tracked loop against SQL Server.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant HR as HR client
    participant API as Minimal API (.NET 6)
    participant EF as EF Core Change Tracker
    participant DB as SQL Server
    HR->>API: PUT /increase-salaries?companyId=1
    API->>EF: Companies.FindAsync(1)
    EF->>DB: SELECT Company
    DB-->>EF: 1 row (tracked)
    API->>EF: Employees.Where(CompanyId == 1).ToListAsync()
    EF->>DB: SELECT 1,000 Employees
    DB-->>EF: 1,000 rows (all tracked)
    API->>API: foreach: salary += 10, LastSalaryUpdate = UtcNow
    API->>EF: SaveChangesAsync()
    EF->>DB: BEGIN TRAN
    loop 1,001 UPDATEs (one transaction)
        EF->>DB: UPDATE Employees SET Salary = @p
    end
    EF->>DB: COMMIT
    DB-->>API: lock waits pile up across concurrent runs
    Note over API,DB: Pool (100 conns) saturates, health checks fail
    API-->>HR: 500 after 30s timeout`}],takeaways:[`A tracked-entity loop emits one UPDATE per modified row: 1,000 employees + 1 company = 1,001 UPDATE statements per request.`,`Use ExecuteUpdateAsync (EF Core 7+) for set-based math — 1,001 statements become 1, with zero rows materialized in memory.`,`On EF Core 6, ExecuteSqlInterpolatedAsync or Dapper gets you the same single, parameterized UPDATE.`,`ExecuteUpdate bypasses the Change Tracker — no interceptors, no concurrency tokens — so keep tracked SaveChanges for entity lifecycle logic.`,`Load test bulk writes at realistic volume (1,000+ rows) and count emitted statements: long transactions holding row locks are what starve a 100-connection pool.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance: How Do You Optimize Database Updates?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=enpblqLhzzo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-jSiGyPHqnpY`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:9,title:`EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:43`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The leaderboard that ate the connection pool`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 12:40, release day. Marketing flips the flag on the new 'Literary Leaders' widget — top two authors by book count, Serbia only, aged 27, with every book they published before 1900. Cute feature, tiny result set. By 12:55 the widget is timing out, homepage p95 has tripled, and two endpoints that have never touched the authors table are failing health checks.`,`The dashboard reads like a heist in progress: Postgres CPU pinned, the connection pool showing 100 of 100 in use, and rows-out per second climbing like it's a backup job. The culprit is one EF Core query doing something spectacularly wrong: it calls ToList() on the authors table, ToList() on the books table — shipping both entire tables into the app — and then does the filtering, the counting, the sorting, and the top-2 in C#. Every widget view is a full-table heist, and the lunchtime traffic surge is the getaway driver.`,`The fix is embarrassingly small: delete two ToList calls, push the filters into the SQL, project straight to the DTO. Same feature, same data, same afternoon — the endpoint goes from a nine-second p95 to double-digit milliseconds, and the challenge benchmark's multiplier lands at 233x. No caching layer, no replicas, no new hardware. We just stopped asking the database for everything and doing the database's job in C#.`],problem:`The database was never the bottleneck — the query was. Every intermediate ToList() turned the query engine back into a dumb file share, leaving C# to run WHERE, ORDER BY and TOP 2 over hundreds of thousands of rows it never needed.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`.NET 6`,`LINQ Projections`,`BenchmarkDotNet`,`Postgres`,`AsNoTracking`],note:`An ORM that translates LINQ intent into SQL — as long as you don't materialize early and take the wheel away from it — plus a benchmark harness honest enough to catch your regressions.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: read the query like a crime scene`,body:[`So here's the challenge my friend set up, and it's still open if you want a go: return the top two authors ordered by book count, where the country is Serbia and they're 27 years old, including every book they published before 1900. Small result, simple spec. And yet the baseline query is a masterclass in how to make a small result expensive.`,`Before I touch a single line of query logic, I do the unglamorous thing: bump the project from .NET Core 3.1 to .NET 6. Why first? Because the runtime and EF internals got serious performance work between those versions, and I want my benchmark numbers to measure my changes — not the framework catching up underneath me. It's the cheapest multiplier you'll ever collect.`,`Then I read the baseline like a crime scene. Where does the data volume explode? Two places, and both are the same suspect wearing different hats: intermediate ToList() calls that materialize entire tables before any Where or Select has had its say.`],quote:`Before you optimize a line of code, ask what that line makes the database carry.`,table:{headers:[`Suspect`,`What it costs`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Intermediate ToList()`,`Entire tables over the wire, every call`,`Guilty`],[`In-memory filtering`,`App CPU and GC pressure per request`,`Guilty`],[`.NET Core 3.1 runtime`,`Older JIT and EF internals`,`Plea bargain: upgrade first`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSiGyPHqnpY`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The ToList() tax — composition dies at the first materialize`,body:[`Here's the mechanic people miss: the moment you call ToList(), your IQueryable becomes a List. EF Core's translator can only see what's still IQueryable — everything after the materialize is just C# running in your process. So that innocent-looking ToList in the middle of the pipeline doesn't just fetch data early; it amputates the query. Every Where and every Select below it is now your CPU's problem.`,`The fix is two deletions. Remove the ToList sitting before the filtering, remove the one before the shaping, and suddenly the whole pipeline — filter, count, sort, top 2 — translates into one SQL statement. I run the benchmark: 54.46x faster than baseline. Not because I did something clever, but because the database finally got to do the job it was built for.`,`Could I stop here? Sure, and most teams do. But the scoreboard is right there, and 'only fetch what the result needs' is a principle, not a finish line.`],quote:`Every early ToList() is a resignation letter from the query optimizer.`,table:{headers:[`Placement`,`Runs where`,`Rows over the wire`,`Fit`],rows:[[`ToList before Where`,`App, in C#`,`Entire table`,`Never`],[`ToList after Select`,`App, but slim`,`Only needed columns`,`Acceptable`],[`No ToList until the end`,`Database`,`Matching rows only`,`Correct`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSiGyPHqnpY`,at:`1:56`}},{heading:`03 · Push the work downhill, then benchmark the shove`,body:[`Next candidate: the book filter — published before 1900 — is still applied in memory after the books come back. Textbook move: push it into the query, filter the books collection right before selecting the BookDto, delete the now-redundant C# check underneath. Intuition says closer to the data is always faster. I run the benchmark anyway, because I've been burned before.`,`And there it is — a regression. We drop from roughly 180x to 156x. The intent of the query didn't change; the generated SQL shape did, and on this dataset the new shape moves more bytes, not fewer. This is the block I'd put on a poster: an optimization is a hypothesis, and the benchmark is the experiment. 'Obviously faster' has an empirical track record of being wrong at exactly the wrong moment.`,`So I keep iterating on the shape — re-arranging where the filter and the projection compose, stripping what the plan doesn't need — and a few honest rounds later the same intent translates into a leaner plan and the number climbs to 233x. The regression wasn't a failure; it was the benchmark telling me the truth before production did.`],quote:`In performance work, 'obviously faster' is a hypothesis. The benchmark is the experiment.`,table:{headers:[`Change`,`Expected`,`Measured`,`Lesson`],rows:[[`Filter books in SQL`,`Faster`,`156x, down from ~180x`,`Plan shape matters`],[`Recompose filter + projection`,`Unsure`,`Recovered, then 233x`,`Compose, then measure`],[`Trust intuition alone`,`Faster`,`Unmeasured`,`Never ship it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSiGyPHqnpY`,at:`4:42`}},{heading:`04 · Dead weight: Includes and tracking on projections`,body:[`Two cleanups that are less about speed and more about mental model. First: the Include() calls. Include exists to populate navigation properties on entities — but this query projects straight into AuthorDto and never returns an entity, so EF ignores them entirely. Dead weight in the code, zero effect on the plan. Delete on sight, if only so the next reader isn't hunting for a purpose that isn't there.`,`Second: AsNoTracking. It's a real, meaningful win — when you're materializing entities, because it skips the change tracker's bookkeeping for every row. But we're projecting to DTOs, and projections were never tracked in the first place. Slapping AsNoTracking on this query changes nothing. Knowing when an optimization doesn't apply is worth exactly as much as knowing when it does — otherwise you're cargo-culting attributes.`],quote:`AsNoTracking pays for entities, not for DTOs — projections were never on the tracker's books.`,table:{headers:[`Query shape`,`Tracked?`,`AsNoTracking helps?`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Materialize entities`,`Yes`,`Yes — skips change tracker`,`Add it`],[`Project to DTO`,`No`,`No — already untracked`,`Skip it`],[`Projection plus Include()`,`No`,`Include is ignored anyway`,`Delete the Include`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSiGyPHqnpY`,at:`7:42`}},{heading:`05 · The scoreboard — how 233x actually compounds`,body:[`Let's read the ladder one more time, because the shape of it is the lesson. Baseline, 1x. Runtime upgrade to .NET 6 — free, before any query edit. Delete the two early ToList calls: 54.46x. Push the book filter into SQL and eat a brief regression to 156x while the plan shape settles. Recompose the filter with the projection and strip the dead weight: 233x.`,`Notice what's absent from that list: no cache, no read replica, no new index, no hardware. The entire 233x is the difference between 'ship me everything and I'll figure it out' and 'here is precisely the data the result needs, in the shape the result needs.' That gap exists in almost every codebase I've audited, and it's usually hiding behind a ToList someone added to fix a compiler error at 4:55 on a Friday.`,`Is there more on the table? Absolutely — indexes that cover the country-and-age filter and the sort are the next conversation. But that conversation starts the same way this one did: with a benchmark, not an opinion.`],quote:`233x wasn't one big idea. It was five small truths, each one verified.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Multiplier after`,`Cost`,`Risk`],rows:[[`.NET 6 upgrade`,`Free gain, unmeasured alone`,`One afternoon`,`Near zero`],[`Delete early ToList`,`54.46x`,`Two deletions`,`None`],[`DB-side book filter`,`156x, brief regression`,`One line`,`Plan shape`],[`Recompose + cleanup`,`233x`,`Rearrangement`,`Low`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`baseline-vs-optimized`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two philosophies of the same query`,caption:`Baseline ships whole tables and filters in C#; optimized lets Postgres filter, sort and project in one round trip.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph B["Baseline - database as a dumb file share"]
        direction LR
        B1["Authors table"] --> B2["ToList: every row"]
        B3["Books table"] --> B4["ToList: every row"]
        B2 --> B5["C# filter: country + age"]
        B4 --> B5
        B5 --> B6["C# sort + top 2"]
    end
    subgraph O["Optimized - database as a query engine"]
        direction LR
        O1["One LINQ query"] --> O2["Postgres: WHERE + ORDER BY + LIMIT"]
        O2 --> O3["Projection: needed columns only"]
        O3 --> O4["2 authors + pre-1900 books"]
    end
    B6 -->|"rewrite"| O1`},{id:`optimized-roundtrip`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of the 233x round trip`,caption:`Nothing materializes until the end, so the entire pipeline translates into a single filtered, projected SQL statement.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as API endpoint
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant PG as Postgres
    App->>EF: LINQ - top 2 authors, Serbia, age 27
    Note over EF: Still IQueryable - nothing materialized
    EF->>PG: Single SELECT - WHERE, ORDER BY count, projection
    Note over PG: Books filtered before 1900 server-side
    PG-->>EF: Matching rows only
    EF-->>App: List of AuthorDto - untracked, no Include`}],takeaways:[`Never call ToList() before Where or Select — deleting just two early materializations took the challenge from baseline to 54.46x.`,`Upgrade the runtime before touching query code — .NET Core 3.1 to .NET 6 is a free multiplier collected before your first edit.`,`Benchmark every change, especially the 'obvious' wins — a textbook DB-side filter regressed the run from ~180x to 156x before the shape settled at 233x.`,`AsNoTracking only pays when you materialize entities — projections to DTOs were never tracked, so there is nothing left for it to save.`,`Delete Include() from projection queries — if you never return an entity, navigation loading is dead weight; the full composite landed at 233x.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Performance Optimization Challenge | 233x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSiGyPHqnpY`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-hXfHWvezBoA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:10,title:`EF Core + Stored Procedures: The Right Way to Call Legacy SQL`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:29`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The presale that sold the same VIP pass three times`,paragraphs:[`It's 12:04 on a Tuesday and the festival presale has just opened — forty thousand fans hammering one checkout endpoint. Three weeks earlier we'd migrated the legacy Postgres ticketing backend from hand-rolled ADO.NET to EF Core, keeping every function and procedure in the ticketing schema exactly as the original team wrote them. Staging was spotless. Then the first real customer searched for 'O'Brien VIP'.`,`Two landmines went off at once. The search path built its SQL by concatenating the term into FromSqlRaw, so a single apostrophe unbalanced the query and the endpoint started 500-ing — and a public search box that accepts a second quote is an injection surface, not just a bug. Meanwhile the reserve path called the legacy procedure with ExecuteSqlRawAsync and branched on the returned row count to decide success. Procedures return -1, 'not applicable', so every successful reservation was read as a failure, clients retried, and the same VIP pass got reserved three times while the dashboard cheerfully reported negative tickets sold.`,`The post-mortem took an afternoon; the fix took a day. We didn't rewrite a line of the legacy SQL — we changed how EF Core calls it: interpolated FormattableStrings so every hole becomes a parameter, a HasDbFunction stub so reads compose inside LINQ, keyless entities for the rowset functions, ExecuteSqlAsync for the CALL, and a hard team rule that nobody branches on -1.`],problem:`How do you call legacy Postgres functions and procedures from EF Core — scalars, rowsets, and transactional CALLs — with structural parameterization, correct result mapping, and no false failures?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 8`,`Postgres`,`Npgsql`,`HasDbFunction`,`FromSqlInterpolated`,`ExecuteSqlAsync`,`Keyless Entities`,`SqlQuery<T>`],note:`Legacy logic stays in Postgres — scalar and rowset functions for reads, one transactional procedure for reservations — while EF Core supplies the parameterization, mapping stubs, and typed call APIs layered on top.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: 'just paste the SQL into FromSqlRaw'`,body:[`So the first instinct with a legacy database is always the same: the SQL already works, don't touch it, just wrap it. You find the function, you write FromSqlRaw with the search term concatenated right into the string, every test passes on clean demo data, and you ship it. That string is a landmine. The day a customer searches for O'Brien — one apostrophe — the query is unbalanced, the endpoint 500s, and worse: a public search box that happily accepts a second quote is an injection surface, not just a bug.`,`The fix isn't discipline and it isn't regex-scrubbing the input. It's making parameterization structural. FromSqlInterpolated, Database.SqlQuery, and ExecuteSqlAsync all take a FormattableString, and every interpolated hole becomes a real DbParameter before it reaches Npgsql. You physically can't forget to parameterize — the syntax is the safety. If you inherit old FromSqlRaw code with hand-built SqlParameter lists, that's safe too, just verbose and easy to misalign; for anything new, interpolated is the default reflex.`],quote:`If your SQL string contains a quote character, you've already lost — let the FormattableString own every hole.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`FromSqlRaw + concatenation`,`Zero new syntax to learn`,`Injection surface, breaks on apostrophes`,`Never`],[`FromSqlRaw + SqlParameters`,`Explicit and safe`,`Verbose, easy to misalign placeholders`,`Inherited code you must keep`],[`FromSqlInterpolated`,`Safe by construction, reads like SQL`,`Only composes over SELECT`,`Default for rowsets`],[`Database.SqlQuery<T>`,`Typed scalar or DTO one-liner`,`Unmapped types only`,`One-off function calls`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core + Stored Procedures: The Right Way to Call Legacy SQL`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hXfHWvezBoA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: function vs procedure`,body:[`Now, people say 'stored procedure' loosely, but in Postgres these are two different animals, and the difference decides which EF Core API you reach for. A function is invoked with SELECT, it must return something — a scalar or a set — and it runs inside your transaction. It cannot issue COMMIT or ROLLBACK, full stop. A procedure is invoked with CALL, can return nothing at all, and that's precisely why it exists: it's allowed to manage its own transactions mid-body.`,`So the mapping onto EF Core is almost embarrassingly clean. Functions are queries — SqlQuery, FromSql, or a mapped DbFunction. Procedures are commands — ExecuteSqlAsync, the same shape as Dapper's ExecuteAsync or ADO's ExecuteNonQuery. If your legacy reserve logic commits in stages, it must stay a procedure, and no amount of LINQ wishful thinking changes that. If it's a pure calculation like tickets_left, keep it a function, because functions are the only side that composes.`],quote:`Functions answer questions; procedures take actions — and only procedures get to hold the COMMIT button.`,table:{headers:[``,`Function`,`Procedure`],rows:[[`Invoked with`,`SELECT fn(x)`,`CALL proc(x)`],[`Returns`,`Scalar or SETOF`,`Nothing via CALL`],[`Transaction control`,`Forbidden`,`COMMIT and ROLLBACK allowed`],[`EF Core API`,`SqlQuery, FromSql, HasDbFunction`,`ExecuteSqlAsync`],[`Composes in LINQ`,`Yes, via mapped stub`,`No`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core + Stored Procedures: The Right Way to Call Legacy SQL`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hXfHWvezBoA`,at:`0:27`}},{heading:`03 · Mapping it in: stubs, keyless entities, and what EF won't track`,body:[`Next question: where does this live in the model? For scalar functions I don't want raw SQL scattered across call sites. You declare a static C# stub that just throws — EF never executes it — then wire it up with HasDbFunction, HasName('tickets_left'), HasSchema('ticketing'). From that moment the stub is usable inside LINQ Where and Select, and EF translates it into the real database call: parameterized, composable with the rest of the query. One declaration, N call sites, zero raw SQL.`,`Rowset functions are the other half. A function returning SETOF can't be tracked as a regular entity — there's no key — and honestly you don't want tracking anyway; these are read shapes. So you map a keyless entity: HasNoKey, plus ToView(null) so EF doesn't go hunting for a table or view that doesn't exist. Then FromSqlInterpolated over 'SELECT * FROM ticketing.search_tickets({term})' hands you an IQueryable you can filter and order on top of — and because it's a SELECT, that composition executes in Postgres, not in your process. On EF 8 you can even skip the model registration and use Database.SqlQuery for unmapped shapes.`,`The trade-off to say out loud: keyless types are read-only and never tracked. If you need to mutate and SaveChanges, project into a real mapped entity instead.`],quote:`A keyless entity is a contract, not a table — it tells EF: shape this result set, track nothing.`,table:{headers:[`Mapping`,`What you get`,`Cost`,`Use when`],rows:[[`HasDbFunction stub`,`Composable in LINQ, translated SQL`,`One throwaway C# method`,`Scalar fn used in queries`],[`Keyless entity + ToView(null)`,`Typed rowsets from SETOF fns`,`Read-only, never tracked`,`Search and report functions`],[`Database.SqlQuery<T> per call`,`Zero model changes`,`SQL scattered across call sites`,`One-off reads on EF 8`],[`Mapped entity + FromSql`,`Trackable, SaveChanges-ready`,`Must select all key columns`,`You must mutate the result`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core + Stored Procedures: The Right Way to Call Legacy SQL`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hXfHWvezBoA`,at:`8:36`}},{heading:`04 · The call sheet: picking the API and surviving the -1`,body:[`Last piece: the call sheet, and the trap that actually hurt us in the story. Procedures go through ExecuteSqlAsync — CALL ticketing.reserve_tickets with the type id and quantity interpolated — FormattableString again, so the arguments arrive as parameters. The await returns an int of rows affected, and for a CALL, Postgres doesn't report a row count, so you get -1. That's 'not applicable', not 'zero'. Our reserve path branched on rowsAffected greater than zero, read every success as a failure, retried, and triple-sold the same VIP pass. Treat that return value as telemetry at best; get your truth from the procedure's own outputs or a follow-up read.`,`Two hygiene rules that sound boring until release day. Schema-qualify every call — ticketing.tickets_left, not bare tickets_left — because search_path differs between your app role and your migration role, and that's a classic deploy surprise. And keep the function and procedure DDL in your migrations with CREATE OR REPLACE, so staging and prod can't drift away from what the code expects. The database owns the logic; your job is to call it deterministically.`],quote:`-1 doesn't mean nothing happened — it means EF refuses to guess. Read your truth back from the database.`,table:{headers:[`Need`,`Reach for`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`Scalar from a function`,`Database.SqlQuery<T>`,`Composable IQueryable, still parameterized`],[`Rowset from a function`,`FromSqlInterpolated on keyless set`,`Composes only over SELECT`],[`Run a procedure`,`ExecuteSqlAsync`,`Returns -1 — never branch on it`],[`Function inside LINQ`,`HasDbFunction stub`,`Name and schema must match exactly`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`call-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One legacy schema, three call paths`,caption:`How EF Core routes scalar functions, rowset functions, and transactional procedures to the right API.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Legacy SQL in ticketing schema"] --> B{"What must come back?"}
    B -->|"A scalar"| C["Function: tickets_left"]
    B -->|"A rowset"| D["Function returning SETOF"]
    B -->|"Nothing, owns the txn"| E["Procedure: reserve_tickets"]
    C --> F["HasDbFunction stub<br/>composes inside LINQ"]
    C --> G["Database.SqlQuery<br/>one-off, parameterized"]
    D --> H["Keyless entity<br/>HasNoKey + ToView null"]
    D --> I["FromSqlInterpolated<br/>SELECT * FROM fn"]
    E --> J["ExecuteSqlAsync<br/>CALL proc with params"]
    F --> K["Translated + parameterized<br/>still composable"]
    H --> L["Read-only, untracked<br/>zero tracker overhead"]
    J --> M["Returns -1 by design<br/>never branch on it"]`},{id:`reserve-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of one reservation`,caption:`The wire-level round trip for a presale checkout: parameterized read, the CALL, and exactly where the -1 comes from.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Checkout API
    participant EF as EF Core DbContext
    participant NP as Npgsql
    participant PG as Postgres ticketing
    API->>EF: Reserve(typeId, qty)
    EF->>NP: BEGIN transaction
    NP->>PG: BEGIN
    EF->>NP: scalar SqlQuery for tickets_left
    NP->>PG: SELECT ticketing.tickets_left($1)
    PG-->>NP: 42
    NP-->>EF: 42
    EF->>NP: ExecuteSqlAsync CALL
    NP->>PG: CALL ticketing.reserve_tickets($1, $2)
    Note over PG: procedure may COMMIT internally<br/>functions never can
    PG-->>NP: rows affected n/a
    NP-->>EF: -1
    Note over EF: -1 means not applicable<br/>not zero rows
    EF->>NP: COMMIT
    NP->>PG: COMMIT
    EF-->>API: Reserved, verified by read-back`}],takeaways:[`Functions are SELECTed, procedures are CALLed — and in Postgres only procedures may run COMMIT/ROLLBACK; pick the kind by who owns the transaction.`,`Route 100% of inputs through FormattableString interpolation (FromSqlInterpolated / SqlQuery / ExecuteSqlAsync); zero concatenated strings ever reach FromSqlRaw.`,`ExecuteSqlAsync on a procedure returns -1 ('not applicable') — 0 branches on that row count, ever; confirm success via out params or a read-back.`,`Declare scalar functions once with HasDbFunction (name + schema must match exactly) so they compose inside LINQ — one stub, N call sites, no raw SQL.`,`Rowset functions: keyless entity + HasNoKey + ToView(null) — typed, read-only, untracked; schema-qualify every call (ticketing.fn) and keep function DDL in migrations.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core + Stored Procedures: The Right Way to Call Legacy SQL`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hXfHWvezBoA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-gg-kGOZqUrw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:11,title:`How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:15`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Database Was Fine. The CPU Wasn't.`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:40 on a Tuesday — not a dramatic hour, just an inconvenient one. Marketing had fired a push notification at four million users, traffic on the customer-profile service jumped eight-fold, and the API pods pinned at 90% CPU. The strangest part: SQL Server was loafing along at 20%. The database wasn't the bottleneck. The application was melting while the database watched.`,`We grabbed a CPU sample from a live pod and the flame graph told the story: a huge share of samples sat inside EF Core's query pipeline — expression tree building, cache-key hashing, plan-cache lookups — for a query shape that never changes: SELECT by customer ID. Every deploy made it worse, because each freshly started pod had to re-compile and re-cache the same handful of queries on its first requests.`,`The fix wasn't a new index or a bigger instance — it was telling EF Core, ahead of time, exactly which queries matter. We pinned the three hottest lookups with EF.CompileQuery, benchmarked before and after with BenchmarkDotNet, and watched the by-ID lookup shed roughly 30 microseconds per call — a little over 10% on the tracking path. Boring numbers, but at forty thousand calls a minute, boring buys back cores.`],problem:`A hot-path lookup was paying a per-request CPU tax to find and validate query plans EF Core had already compiled — we needed to skip the plan-cache round trip entirely without giving up LINQ.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`EF.CompileQuery`,`AsNoTracking`,`BenchmarkDotNet`],note:`A deliberately tiny surface: one DbContext, one Customer entity, and EF.CompileQuery pinned as static delegates — with BenchmarkDotNet proving whether the ceremony pays for itself.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: EF already caches plans — what's left to save?`,body:[`Here's the question I get first every time: doesn't EF Core already cache compiled queries? It does. The first time any query shape runs, EF walks the expression tree, translates it to SQL, and stashes the result in an internal cache keyed by the query's shape. So on request two, we're not recompiling — we're looking up. And that's exactly where the remaining cost lives.`,`Every single request still builds the expression tree from your LINQ, computes a cache key, hashes it, and does a dictionary lookup before it can touch the database. None of that is SQL work — it's pure CPU inside your process. On a cold endpoint you'll never notice. On an endpoint getting hammered forty thousand times a minute, those microseconds per call turn into pods you're paying for. EF.CompileQuery hoists the whole find-the-plan step out of the request path: you declare a static delegate once, and invoking it goes straight to execution.`,`The obvious counter is 'just warm the cache after deploy.' Warming helps the first request, but it does nothing for the per-call lookup overhead — that tax is paid on every invocation, forever. That's the distinction people miss: the plan cache removes compilation; the compiled query removes the trip to the cache.`],quote:`The plan cache saves you the compile; the compiled query saves you the trip to the cache.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Plain LINQ (cached)`,`Readable, composable, zero ceremony`,`Per-call tree build + cache lookup`,`Default for most queries`],[`Warm-up on startup`,`Kills first-request latency spike`,`Per-call lookup cost remains`,`Deploy-sensitive endpoints`],[`EF.CompileQuery`,`Skips cache lookup, ~5–10% faster`,`Static ceremony, frozen shape`,`Measured hot paths`],[`Raw SQL / Dapper`,`Fastest, minimal pipeline`,`No type safety, two languages`,`Trivial hot selects`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gg-kGOZqUrw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what CompileQuery actually pins down`,body:[`The signature is the whole API, and it's worth reading slowly. You declare a private static readonly field whose type is a Func: the DbContext comes first, then any parameters — in our case the customer ID as a long — and the return type last, a Customer. The value is EF.CompileQuery with an expression tree: (context, id) => context.Customers.SingleOrDefault(c => c.Id == id). That delegate is built once, at type load, and reused for the life of the process.`,`That 'built once' is a double-edged sword, and it's where people get burned. The query shape is frozen: you can't add an Include later at the call site, you can't swap the predicate based on a flag, you can't compose on top of the result the way you can with an IQueryable. If the shape varies at runtime, you don't have one query — you have two, and you should declare two compiled queries. Parameters are the only thing that moves, and that's by design: same shape, different values, one delegate.`,`Two practical notes from the trenches. Async is its own API — EF.CompileAsyncQuery — returning a Task-wrapped result you await per call. And because the field initializer runs when the containing type is first touched, the compilation cost lands at startup rather than on some unlucky first customer request. That's a quiet little win nobody puts in the benchmark charts.`],quote:`A compiled query is a contract: the shape never moves, only the parameters do.`,table:{headers:[`Constraint`,`Why it exists`,`Workaround`],rows:[[`Frozen query shape`,`The delegate is compiled once`,`Declare one compiled query per shape`],[`No call-site composition`,`Result materializes per invocation`,`Fold Includes and filters into the expression`],[`Async needs its own API`,`Sync and async pipelines differ`,`Use EF.CompileAsyncQuery`],[`Static readonly only`,`Delegate must be thread-safe, built once`,`Keep them in one static Queries class`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gg-kGOZqUrw`,at:`1:24`}},{heading:`03 · The numbers, and being honest about them`,body:[`We seeded ten thousand random customers into SQL Server and ran BenchmarkDotNet with four contenders: tracking by ID, tracking by ID compiled, no-tracking by ID, no-tracking compiled. The headline result: the compiled tracking lookup came in about 30 microseconds faster — slightly more than 10%. Real, repeatable, and entirely CPU-side; the generated SQL was identical in both runs.`,`Then the honest part. Add AsNoTracking and both versions get faster, but the gap between them narrows — you've already stripped change tracking out of the pipeline, so there's less overhead left for compilation to skip. Go async with a two-predicate query — name and age — and the compiled version wins by maybe 5%. The pattern is consistent: the more of EF's per-call work you eliminate by other means, the smaller the slice compiled queries can take off. The win is a percentage of the pipeline overhead, not of your total latency.`,`So the decision rule writes itself: a 10% cut on your single hottest endpoint is a project; a 5% cut on an endpoint nobody calls is a code-review argument. Benchmark your actual query shapes, at your actual call rates, and let the numbers argue for you.`],quote:`Compiled queries don't make the SQL faster — they make getting to the SQL faster.`,table:{headers:[`Query shape`,`Mode`,`Win vs baseline`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`By ID`,`Tracking`,`~10% (~30µs)`,`Worth it on hot paths`],[`By ID`,`No-tracking`,`Narrower margin`,`Only if already hot`],[`Name + age`,`Async`,`~5%`,`Squeeze-only`],[`Cold endpoints`,`Any`,`Noise`,`Keep plain LINQ`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gg-kGOZqUrw`,at:`5:00`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just write SQL?`,body:[`Every time I show this feature, someone asks the fair question: if you need microsecond-level wins, why not Dapper or a raw command? And honestly — sometimes you should. For a trivial single-row select by key, hand-written SQL is faster still, dead simple, and there's no ceremony. If that's your hot path, measure both; raw SQL might win outright.`,`But compiled queries occupy a sweet spot raw SQL doesn't reach. You keep full LINQ expressiveness — navigations, filters, projections — with compile-time checking against your model. You keep change tracking by simply not writing AsNoTracking. You keep one language in the codebase, refactoring stays safe, and the perf win — while smaller than raw SQL's — arrives without leaving the EF ecosystem. The real comparison isn't compiled-versus-nothing; it's compiled-versus-rewriting the access layer.`,`My rule after a few of these migrations: reach for compiled queries when the query is non-trivial but the path is hot. Reach for raw SQL when the query is trivial and the path is hot. Reach for plain LINQ everywhere else, and sleep well.`],quote:`Complexity is a currency — spend it only where the microseconds compound.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Plain LINQ`,`Composable, refactor-safe`,`Per-call pipeline overhead`,`Everything by default`],[`Compiled query`,`~5–10% off, stays in LINQ`,`Static ceremony, frozen shape`,`Hot, non-trivial queries`],[`Raw SQL / Dapper`,`Fastest path to data`,`No compile-time checks, dual dialect`,`Hot, trivial queries`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gg-kGOZqUrw`,at:`10:22`}},{heading:`05 · Where it lands in production`,body:[`Rollout discipline matters more than the API. We keep every compiled query in one static class — Queries.GetById, Queries.GetByNameAndAge — so there's exactly one place to audit. Each one gets a BenchmarkDotNet sibling before it merges, because 'should be faster' is not a number. And each one gets a comment with the call rate that justified it, so the next person knows why the ceremony exists.`,`The operational surprises are mild but real. First: deploys no longer cause a compilation spike on your hottest endpoints, because the delegates compile at type load — our post-deploy CPU graph flattened noticeably. Second: parameter values flow through as arguments, so you never accidentally fork query shapes by string-interpolating filters — which, as a side effect, kills a whole class of per-value cache entries. Third: resist the urge to convert everything; a codebase where every read is a compiled query is a codebase nobody wants to maintain.`],quote:`Pin the three queries that pay the rent; let the other three hundred stay readable.`}],diagrams:[{id:`exec-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two roads to the same SQL: plan cache vs compiled delegate`,caption:`Normal LINQ pays tree-build plus cache lookup on every call; the compiled delegate jumps straight to execution.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Request: GetCustomerById(id)"] --> B["Build LINQ expression tree"]
    B --> C["Hash query shape to cache key"]
    C --> D{"Plan in query cache?"}
    D -- "Miss" --> E["Translate to SQL and compile"]
    E --> F["Store plan in cache"]
    F --> G["Execute against SQL Server"]
    D -- "Hit" --> G
    H["static readonly EF.CompileQuery delegate"] --> I["Invoke with context and id"]
    I --> G
    G --> J["Materialize Customer entity"]`},{id:`hot-path-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Hot-path lookup with a compiled query`,caption:`The static delegate is compiled at type load, so each request pays only invocation and the SQL round-trip.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant api as API Request
    participant q as Compiled Delegate
    participant ef as EF Core Pipeline
    participant db as SQL Server
    api->>q: GetById(context, customerId)
    q->>ef: Invoke pre-compiled plan
    ef->>db: SELECT Id, Name, Age WHERE Id = @id
    db-->>ef: Single row
    ef-->>api: Customer entity`}],takeaways:[`Expect roughly a 10% latency cut (~30µs in our BenchmarkDotNet run) on tracking by-ID lookups with EF.CompileQuery — re-measure on your own query shapes before believing it.`,`Wins shrink as you strip the pipeline: no-tracking narrows the gap and async multi-predicate queries dropped to ~5% — budget complexity to the measured number, not the hope.`,`Declare compiled queries as private static readonly Func fields: DbContext first, parameters next, return type last; async lookups need the separate EF.CompileAsyncQuery API.`,`Static field initializers compile at type load, so hot endpoints skip the post-deploy first-request compilation spike — a free latency win on every rollout.`,`Adopt only on measured hot paths (tens of thousands of calls per minute); keep plain LINQ everywhere else, and compare trivial hot selects against raw SQL before adding ceremony.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Optimize EF Core Query Performance With Compiled Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gg-kGOZqUrw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-VYitXAc_htI`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:12,title:`EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:23`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`],story:{headline:`Raise Day, 9:41 AM: One Endpoint, 100,000 UPDATEs`,paragraphs:[`It's 9:41 on the first business day of the fiscal year, and every payroll admin on the platform is clicking 'Apply merit increase' at the same time. The first customer through the door is a logistics giant with just over 100,000 employees. The endpoint looks innocent: take a company ID, load the company, loop its employees, bump each salary by 10%, call SaveChanges.`,`By the third customer, the pattern stops being cute. EF Core materializes every employee into the change tracker, snapshots each one, marks it Modified, then fires UPDATE after UPDATE — a hundred thousand single-row statements marching through one long transaction. The request blows past its 30-second timeout, the connection pool fills with queued payroll calls, and every other endpoint on the service starts backing up behind the pileup. Support says 'the raise button is broken.' The database says 'stop asking me the same question a hundred thousand times in a row.'`,`The fix that shipped that week wasn't Dapper, and it wasn't a hand-written SQL string buried in a repository. It was EF Core 7's new ExecuteUpdateAsync: one set-based statement — UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary * 1.1 WHERE CompanyId = @id — doing in roughly 12 milliseconds what the loop did in seconds. Same LINQ, same DbContext, no extra library.`],problem:`Bulk updates were executed as per-row UPDATE statements through the change tracker, when a single set-based UPDATE could do identical work an order of magnitude faster — without loading a single entity into memory.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 7`,`ExecuteUpdate`,`ExecuteDelete`,`LINQ`,`Minimal APIs`],note:`The moving parts this lesson names: EF Core 7's set-based operators replacing the tracked loop, LINQ as the typed surface for expressing bulk filters, and minimal APIs as the thin delivery layer.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the loop-and-save pattern melts down`,body:[`Let's walk the naive path the way the demo walks it. We fetch the company, we loop the employees collection, we add 10% to each salary, and we call SaveChanges. Sounds harmless — until you ask what EF Core actually did. It ran a SELECT that materialized every employee row into memory, took a snapshot of each one for change tracking, and then at SaveChanges emitted one UPDATE per modified row. Batching softens the round trips, but the work still scales linearly with headcount.`,`And here's the part people underestimate: it's not just the UPDATEs. It's the memory for 100k tracked entities, the snapshot copies, and the transaction that stays open long enough for everything else on the pool to feel it. A company with a million employees doesn't make this endpoint a little slower — it makes it a different kind of problem.`,`So the question becomes: why are we shipping every row over the wire just to multiply a number and ship it back? The database can do that math where the data lives.`],quote:`The change tracker is a superb historian and a terrible bulldozer.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VYitXAc_htI`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what ExecuteUpdate actually emits`,body:[`Now the new path. Instead of loading entities, we build the same LINQ query — Where(e => e.CompanyId == id) — and instead of ToListAsync, we call ExecuteUpdateAsync with SetProperty(e => e.Salary, e => e.Salary * 1.1m). EF Core translates that expression tree straight into set-based SQL: UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary * 1.1 WHERE CompanyId = @CompanyId.`,`Look at what's missing: no SELECT, no materialization, no change tracker entries, no SaveChanges. The call goes to the database immediately and returns the number of rows affected. One round trip, one statement, and the company ID travels as a parameter, not a concatenated literal.`,`And because the filter is still LINQ, it composes. Add a salary threshold, a department filter, a condition across a join — EF re-translates the whole thing into one UPDATE with a bigger WHERE clause. The shape of your code doesn't change; the shape of the SQL does.`],quote:`Stop shipping rows over the wire just to multiply them on the other side.`,table:{headers:[`What crosses the wire`,`Loop + SaveChanges`,`ExecuteUpdate`],rows:[[`SELECTs`,`1 (every row)`,`0`],[`UPDATEs`,`1 per row`,`1 total`],[`Entities tracked`,`100,000`,`0`],[`Round trips`,`N / batch size + 1`,`1`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VYitXAc_htI`,at:`4:54`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just Dapper or raw SQL?`,body:[`Fair question, because before EF Core 7 this was exactly the escape hatch — I've got a whole earlier video doing this with Dapper. You write UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary * 1.1 WHERE CompanyId = @CompanyId, execute it, done. Roughly the same speed.`,`The cost is what you give up: the SQL lives in a string, so renaming the Salary column compiles cleanly and detonates at runtime. The filter logic drifts away from the LINQ you use everywhere else, and suddenly 'which employees get a raise?' is answered in two different dialects in the same codebase.`,`ExecuteUpdate keeps both the filter and the mutation as C# expressions. Rename a property and the compiler catches it before Postgres ever sees a bad column. You keep set-based speed without leaving the type system. Where Dapper still wins: exotic SQL — CTEs, upserts, vendor-specific constructs — that EF's translator won't emit for you.`],quote:`Set-based speed used to cost you the type system. EF Core 7 stopped charging that fee.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Loop + SaveChanges`,`Full tracking, interceptors, per-row logic`,`1 UPDATE per row, scales with headcount`,`Small sets, complex rules`],[`Raw SQL string`,`Fast, full SQL power`,`No compile-time checks, drift risk`,`One-off scripts`],[`Dapper`,`Fast, explicit SQL control`,`String SQL, manual mapping`,`Exotic queries, CTEs`],[`ExecuteUpdate / Delete`,`Set-based speed, LINQ-typed filters`,`No tracking, small SET vocabulary`,`Bulk uniform changes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VYitXAc_htI`,at:`6:13`}},{heading:`04 · ExecuteDelete and the change-tracker blind spot`,body:[`Same story for deletes, and this one's even more blunt. ExecuteDeleteAsync takes no arguments at all — you describe the set with your Where clause and EF emits a single DELETE. In the demo: employees belonging to company 1 with a salary above 10,000. One statement, gone.`,`But pause on what's not happening, because this is where teams get burned. No entities are loaded, so nothing is tracked. SaveChanges is never called — the delete already happened. That means your SaveChanges interceptors don't fire, your domain events don't publish, and your optimistic concurrency tokens are ignored. If your audit trail depends on the change tracker, a bulk delete just walked around it.`,`Two habits I'd bake in on day one: capture the affected-rows count the method returns and log it — a purge that reports 0 rows when you expected 40,000 is a bug you want to know about immediately. And remember cascade behavior lives in the database: FK cascades fire server-side, but EF's client-side cascade logic never sees a single row.`],quote:`A bulk delete that skips the change tracker also skips every safety net you built on top of it.`,table:{headers:[`Mechanism`,`Fires with ExecuteDelete?`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`SaveChanges interceptors`,`No`,`Audit hooks silently skipped`],[`Optimistic concurrency tokens`,`Ignored`,`Last-writer wins by definition`],[`DB-level FK cascades`,`Yes`,`Handled server-side by the database`],[`Client-side cascade logic`,`No`,`EF never loads the rows`],[`Affected-rows return value`,`Yes`,`Your one honest signal — log it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VYitXAc_htI`,at:`8:49`}},{heading:`05 · Where I'd still reach for the tracked path`,body:[`Before anyone rewrites their codebase, the counterweight. If the mutation differs per row — a raise matrix by grade and tenure, proration logic, anything where the new value depends on state EF can't express in a SET clause — ExecuteUpdate isn't your tool. Its SetProperty vocabulary is deliberately small: constants, column references, and simple computed expressions.`,`Same for anything that leans on the change tracker: interceptors, domain events, per-row concurrency checks, or a unit of work that mixes this update with other changes and wants one atomic SaveChanges. The tracked path buys you orchestration; the set-based path buys you throughput.`,`My rule after shipping this: if the operation can be described as a sentence about a set — 'raise everyone at company X by 10%' — it belongs to ExecuteUpdate or ExecuteDelete. If it's a story about individual rows, keep the loop.`],quote:`Choose the tracked path when the row's story matters; choose set-based when only the outcome does.`}],diagrams:[{id:`bulk-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two paths to a 10% raise`,caption:`The tracked loop versus the set-based operator — what actually crosses the wire.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  A["POST /increase-salaries?companyId=1"] --> B{"Bulk strategy"}
  B -->|"Loop + SaveChanges"| C["SELECT all employees for company"]
  C --> D["Materialize 100k entities + snapshots"]
  D --> E["Mutate each Salary in memory"]
  E --> F["SaveChanges: one UPDATE per row"]
  F --> G["Long transaction, pool pressure, seconds"]
  B -->|"ExecuteUpdateAsync"| H["UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary * 1.1 WHERE CompanyId = @id"]
  H --> I["Single round trip, ~12 ms, zero tracked entities"]`},{id:`execute-update-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of one ExecuteUpdateAsync call`,caption:`LINQ in, set-based SQL out — no materialization, no SaveChanges, rows-affected back.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant API as Minimal API
  participant Ctx as DbContext
  participant PG as Postgres
  API->>Ctx: Where(e => e.CompanyId == id).ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(e => e.Salary, e => e.Salary * 1.1m))
  Ctx->>Ctx: Translate expression tree to SQL
  Ctx->>PG: UPDATE Employees SET Salary = Salary * 1.1 WHERE CompanyId = @p0
  PG-->>Ctx: rows affected = 100000
  Ctx-->>API: 100000 (no SaveChanges needed)`}],takeaways:[`A loop-and-SaveChanges bulk update emits one UPDATE per modified row: 100,000 employees means 100,000 statements, while ExecuteUpdateAsync collapses the same work into a single set-based UPDATE.`,`Expect roughly a 10x latency win on uniform bulk changes — the demo went from ~140 ms with the tracked loop to ~12 ms with ExecuteUpdate on identical data.`,`ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete fire immediately and bypass the change tracker: no entity materialization, no SaveChanges, and no interceptors, domain events, or concurrency tokens.`,`Express bulk filters and SET values as LINQ expressions, not SQL strings — you keep parameterized queries and compile-time safety when a column gets renamed.`,`Always capture and log the affected-rows count these methods return; a threshold purge (Salary > 10,000) that reports 0 rows is an incident, not a rounding error.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 7 Performance Improvements With The New ExecuteUpdate & ExecuteDelete`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VYitXAc_htI`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-hSSatvEOp3w`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:13,title:`Do You Know The Fastest Way To Delete Data With EF Core?`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 10:10`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Delete Endpoint That Doubled Our Database Load On Release Day`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday afternoon, mid-traffic, not some dramatic 3 AM scene. We had just shipped a catalog purge feature: merchants could bulk-retire discontinued products, and the queue was humming at a few hundred deletes per minute. Nothing exotic — a simple ASP.NET endpoint, one DbContext, one Products table.`,`By 1:15 PM the connection pool was saturated and p99 on every write endpoint had tripled. The dashboards showed something odd: for every DELETE hitting Postgres, there was a matching SELECT right before it. Every single delete was paying for a full row read it never used — load the entity, track it, flag it, then delete it.`,`We rewrote the hot path twice that day. First to a stub-entity attach, then to ExecuteDelete for the batch lanes. Pool pressure collapsed within minutes, and the purge queue that was projected to run four hours finished in eleven minutes. The lesson stuck: the 'obvious' EF delete is really two queries wearing one trench coat.`],problem:`Every delete was executing a SELECT followed by a DELETE — two round trips where one would do — and at a few hundred deletes per minute, that redundant read starved the connection pool and inflated p99 across every write endpoint on the same database.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 7`,`Change Tracker`,`Attach + State`,`ExecuteDelete`,`Postgres`],note:`The moving parts this talk names, in the order the speaker meets them: the DbContext change tracker behind Find and Remove, the attach-and-set-state trick that skips the read, and the bulk ExecuteDelete lane that skips everything.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the textbook delete pays twice`,body:[`Let's start with the version everyone writes first. You await context.Products.FindAsync(id), null-check the result, call Remove, then SaveChangesAsync. FindAsync checks the change tracker first, then queries the database, materializes the full row into memory, and starts tracking it. Remove doesn't touch the database at all — it just flags the tracked entity as Deleted. Only when SaveChanges runs does EF generate DELETE FROM products WHERE id = @id.`,`So count with me: one round trip for the SELECT, one for the DELETE. Two round trips on the happy path, and the SELECT buys you almost nothing — you already have the primary key from the route. The only thing it really gives you is a clean 404 signal and access to the row's data before it dies.`,`Why not just accept it? Because deletes cluster. A purge job, a merchant bulk action, a GDPR erasure — delete traffic arrives in bursts, and doubling the round trips per delete doubles pool pressure exactly when you can least afford it. The SELECT is pure overhead unless you genuinely need the row.`],quote:`If you already hold the key, fetching the row to delete it is paying ransom for data you'll never read.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Round trips`,`What actually happens`],rows:[[`FindAsync(id)`,`1`,`SELECT full row, materialize, start tracking`],[`Remove(entity)`,`0`,`Flags entity Deleted in the change tracker only`],[`SaveChangesAsync`,`1`,`Emits DELETE FROM products WHERE id = @id`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Do You Know The Fastest Way To Delete Data With EF Core?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hSSatvEOp3w`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the stub-entity trick`,body:[`Here's the first upgrade: skip the read entirely. Build a stub — new Product { Id = id } — and call context.Products.Attach(stub). Attach places the entity in the change tracker in the Unchanged state without touching the database. Then grab the returned EntityEntry and set entry.State = EntityState.Deleted.`,`When SaveChanges runs, EF sees a Deleted entity with a known key and emits exactly one DELETE WHERE id = @id. One round trip total. And you get a bonus: if the row doesn't exist, the DELETE affects zero rows and EF throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException — that's your 404 signal, delivered by the same single query.`,`The trade-offs are real, though. You never see the row's data, so any business logic that needed the entity's values is out of luck. And if you use rowversion concurrency tokens, a stub with no original token value means the DELETE's WHERE clause matches on the primary key only — if you want the optimistic check, you must supply the original concurrency value on the stub before saving.`],quote:`Attach a stub, flip the state to Deleted, and the SELECT you never needed simply vanishes.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Do You Know The Fastest Way To Delete Data With EF Core?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hSSatvEOp3w`,at:`0:24`}},{heading:`03 · The fast lane: ExecuteDelete and what it skips`,body:[`EF Core 7 gave us the express lane: await context.Products.Where(p => p.Id == id).ExecuteDeleteAsync(). This translates directly to a single DELETE statement executed against the database — no entity materialization, no change tracker, no SaveChanges call. It returns the number of rows affected, which doubles as your 404 check.`,`The same API scales to bulk work: Where(p => p.Discontinued && p.UpdatedOn < cutoff).ExecuteDeleteAsync() becomes one set-based DELETE for the whole predicate. For a two-million-row purge, that's the difference between millions of tracked entities and one statement per chunk.`,`But read the fine print before you sprint down this lane. ExecuteDelete bypasses the change tracker entirely, so SaveChanges interceptors never fire. It ignores concurrency tokens — no rowversion check, period. Model-configured cascade behaviors aren't applied (though database-level ON DELETE CASCADE still is), and you can't return the deleted entity because you never had it. It's fast precisely because it opts out of everything EF does for you.`],quote:`ExecuteDelete is fast because it trusts the database and fires one statement — everything EF usually does for you, it simply doesn't do.`,table:{headers:[`Capability`,`Fetch + Remove`,`Stub attach`,`ExecuteDelete`],rows:[[`Round trips (by ID)`,`2`,`1`,`1`],[`Row data available`,`Yes`,`No`,`No`],[`Concurrency token check`,`Yes`,`Only if you supply original value`,`No`],[`SaveChanges interceptors`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Bulk by predicate`,`Painful loop`,`Painful loop`,`Native, one statement`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Do You Know The Fastest Way To Delete Data With EF Core?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hSSatvEOp3w`,at:`2:28`}},{heading:`04 · Choosing the lane per call site`,body:[`So which one wins? Wrong question — they're different tools for different call sites. If your endpoint needs the row's data, runs authorization checks against the entity, or must fire interceptors and respect optimistic concurrency, fetch-then-delete is correct and the extra round trip is the price of correctness.`,`If it's a hot-path delete-by-ID where you only need 'gone or 404', the stub attach is the sweet spot: one round trip, still inside the SaveChanges pipeline, still cascade-aware for tracked dependents. If it's bulk or predicate-based cleanup, ExecuteDelete chunked into statements of five to ten thousand rows keeps transactions short and lock footprints small.`,`The anti-pattern to hunt down is the loop: foreach over IDs calling FindAsync and Remove. That's the shape that melted our pool on release day — two million rows becomes four million round trips. Any delete shaped like a loop should be rewritten as a chunked ExecuteDelete before it ever sees production traffic.`],quote:`There is no fastest delete — there is the right delete for the call site, and a loop-shaped one is always wrong.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Best fit`,`Why`],rows:[[`Delete by ID, need entity data or authz checks`,`Fetch + Remove`,`Only approach that gives you the row`],[`Hot-path delete by ID, rows-affected is enough`,`Stub attach`,`1 round trip, stays in SaveChanges pipeline`],[`Bulk purge by predicate`,`Chunked ExecuteDelete`,`Set-based, no tracking, short transactions`],[`Must fire interceptors / rowversion checks`,`Fetch + Remove or stub + token`,`ExecuteDelete silently skips both`]]}},{heading:`05 · Guardrails: measure round trips, not vibes`,body:[`You can't fix what you can't see, so turn on SQL logging in development — LogTo or a simple logger factory — and actually read the statements your delete endpoints emit. The smell is unmistakable once you look: a SELECT immediately followed by a DELETE with the same WHERE clause. That's a round trip you're paying for out of pool capacity.`,`Second guardrail: index the predicate. ExecuteDelete is only as fast as the WHERE clause behind it — an unindexed delete predicate on a large table turns your 'one fast statement' into a sequential scan holding locks. Third: chunk bulk deletes and keep each transaction short, because one giant DELETE on a hot table blocks readers, bloats your WAL, and can stall replicas.`,`And finally, decide explicitly what you're giving up. Every lane that skips the change tracker also skips its safety nets — interceptors, cascade behaviors, concurrency checks. Write that decision down at the call site. 'Fast' that silently drops a rowversion check is not fast, it's a data-loss bug with good latency numbers.`],quote:`Log the SQL once, and every redundant SELECT before a DELETE will confess on the spot.`}],diagrams:[{id:`delete-lanes`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Three lanes from HTTP DELETE to Postgres`,caption:`Round-trip cost of each strategy for a delete-by-ID request — the SELECT in lane one is the tax you can usually avoid.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["DELETE /products/{id}"] --> B{"Strategy"}
    B -->|"Fetch + Remove"| C["Round trip 1<br/>SELECT full row<br/>track entity"]
    C --> D["Round trip 2<br/>SaveChanges emits<br/>DELETE WHERE id = @id"]
    B -->|"Stub attach"| E["new Product with Id<br/>Attach → State = Deleted"]
    E --> F["Round trip 1<br/>SaveChanges emits<br/>DELETE WHERE id = @id"]
    B -->|"ExecuteDelete"| G["Round trip 1<br/>DELETE WHERE id = @id<br/>no tracker, no SaveChanges"]
    D --> H["204 No Content"]
    F --> H
    G --> H`},{id:`old-vs-fast-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Old path vs fast path on the wire`,caption:`The same business outcome, side by side: two round trips through the change tracker, or one statement straight to the database.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant API as Delete endpoint
    participant CT as Change Tracker
    participant PG as Postgres
    rect rgb(255, 235, 235)
        Note over API,PG: Old path — two round trips
        API->>PG: SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = 1
        PG-->>API: full row, materialized
        API->>CT: Remove — flag Deleted
        API->>PG: SaveChanges → DELETE FROM products WHERE id = 1
        PG-->>API: 1 row affected
    end
    rect rgb(235, 245, 255)
        Note over API,PG: Fast path — one round trip
        API->>PG: ExecuteDeleteAsync → DELETE FROM products WHERE id = 1
        PG-->>API: rows affected — 0 means 404
    end`}],takeaways:[`Fetch-then-delete costs 2 round trips per row — a 1M-row loop becomes 2M queries; never delete in a loop.`,`Stub attach (new Entity { Id }, Attach, State = Deleted) cuts a by-ID delete to exactly 1 round trip and still runs inside SaveChanges.`,`ExecuteDeleteAsync is 1 statement with zero change-tracker overhead; chunk bulk deletes at 5–10k rows per statement to keep transactions short and locks contained.`,`ExecuteDelete silently skips SaveChanges interceptors, model cascade behaviors, and concurrency tokens — if you need any of those, it's the wrong lane.`,`Log generated SQL in dev: any delete endpoint emitting a SELECT before its DELETE is a smell — target ≤1 round trip per delete and index the WHERE predicate.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Do You Know The Fastest Way To Delete Data With EF Core?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hSSatvEOp3w`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-GY7QwSFeVBQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:14,title:`One Trick To Improve EF Core Performance Using Query Splitting`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:51`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`Release-Day Load Test: 500 Attendees, 500 Invitations, One JOIN That Multiplied Everything`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:04 on release morning, not some dark-hour pager incident — we were running the standard pre-deploy load profile against our events platform. The target was the flagship gathering detail endpoint: GET /gatherings/{id}, the one that returns the gathering, its creator, its attendees, and its invitations. At 10 concurrent users it was fine. At 200, SQL Server CPU pinned at 100% and p95 latency went from 40 milliseconds to over 4 seconds. Nothing had changed in the query for weeks.`,`I pulled the SQL out of the EF Core logs and stared at it. One statement. Three JOINs — Creator, Attendees, Invitations — stacked on top of each other. The flagship gathering had 500 attendees and 500 invitations, and because both collections were joined in the same statement, the database handed back 500 × 500 = 250,000 rows, every single one repeating the full gathering and member columns. We weren't transferring a gathering; we were transferring the same gathering a quarter of a million times, then paying EF Core to deduplicate it during materialization.`,`The fix took one line: AsSplitQuery() on the query. EF Core sent three lean statements instead — the gathering first, then attendees, then invitations, each select trimmed down to just the columns and join keys it needed. Roughly 1,001 rows came back instead of 250,000, and p95 dropped right back to double-digit milliseconds before the demo audience noticed anything had ever been wrong.`],problem:`One LINQ query with multiple collection Includes compiles into a single JOIN-heavy SQL statement whose row count multiplies across collections, drowning the network, the database, and the materializer in duplicated data.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 5`,`SQL Server`,`MediatR`,`AsSplitQuery`],note:`An API endpoint pulls a gathering graph through MediatR into a repository, and EF Core decides whether to emit one JOIN-heavy statement or a fan-out of lean queries against SQL Server.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the single JOIN lies to you`,body:[`Let me set the scene the way the code does. The Gatherings controller has one endpoint — get gathering by ID. It pushes a query through MediatR, the handler hits the gathering repository, and the response touches the Creator, Attendees, and Invitations navigation properties. So the LINQ query carries three Includes, and EF Core compiles that into one SQL statement with stacked JOINs. On paper that looks efficient: one round trip, one result set, done.`,`But here's the trap nobody warns you about at code review: when you JOIN two independent collections in the same statement, the database doesn't add the rows — it multiplies them. 500 attendees joined against 500 invitations isn't 1,000 rows, it's 250,000. Every row drags the full gathering and member columns along for the ride, so your payload balloons by orders of magnitude before EF Core even starts stitching the object graph back together.`,`My first instinct was 'this is an indexing problem, right?' It wasn't. Every lookup in that plan was an index seek — the shape of the result set was the problem, not the seek strategy. You can't index your way out of a cartesian product.`],quote:`Two collection Includes in one query don't add rows — they multiply them.`,table:{headers:[`Collection Includes`,`Single-query rows`,`Split-query rows`],rows:[[`1 collection`,`~N`,`~N`],[`2 collections`,`N × M`,`N + M`],[`3 collections`,`N × M × K`,`N + M + K`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One Trick To Improve EF Core Performance Using Query Splitting`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GY7QwSFeVBQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the split path, query by query`,body:[`So what does AsSplitQuery() actually change? EF Core stops trying to cram everything into one statement. The first query fetches the root entity plus any reference includes — in our case the gathering and its creator. Then it sends a second query for the attendees, and a third for the invitations. Watch the select lists on those follow-up queries: they're trimmed to the entity's columns plus only the keys needed to correlate back to the gathering. We're not re-fetching gathering and member data over and over — just the attendees, then just the invitations.`,`That's the part I had to see in the logs to believe. Three statements, but the total row count is additive, not multiplicative: 1 + 500 + 500 ≈ 1,001 rows instead of 250,000. EF Core then stitches the graph in memory using identity resolution, so the navigation properties come back fully populated exactly as before. Same response in Postman, byte for byte — nothing downstream breaks.`,`And to be clear, this isn't a hack bolted onto the side. Query splitting shipped in EF Core 5 as a first-class feature, with the AsSplitQuery operator sitting right next to its sibling AsSingleQuery, so you can flip per query, not per codebase.`],quote:`Split queries trade one fat result set for a few lean ones — additive rows instead of multiplied rows.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One Trick To Improve EF Core Performance Using Query Splitting`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GY7QwSFeVBQ`,at:`4:22`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just split everything? The honest trade-offs`,body:[`At this point the obvious question from the chat is: 'if splitting is faster, why isn't it the default everywhere?' Because it isn't free. Every split query is a separate round trip to the database. On a low-latency link with three queries, that's noise. On a chatty path with ten Includes across a WAN, you've traded a payload problem for a latency problem, and the network will eat the win.`,`There's a subtler cost too: those three statements execute as separate auto-commit operations, not one transaction. If an attendee signs up between query two and query three, your materialized graph is a slightly inconsistent snapshot in time. For a read endpoint serving a UI, that's usually acceptable. For anything where cross-entity consistency matters, you need to wrap the queries in an explicit transaction — or stay single-query.`,`And splitting isn't always faster even locally. With a single collection Include and modest duplication, one round trip beats three. The rule of thumb I've landed on: two or more collection Includes in one query is the trigger; one collection Include usually stays single.`],quote:`Splitting buys you rows and pays in round trips — make sure you have rows to spare.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single query`,`One round trip; single consistent statement`,`Cartesian row explosion with 2+ collection Includes; duplicated payload`,`One collection Include, low-latency link, strict consistency`],[`Split query`,`Additive rows; lean selects; 50–90% less data on multi-include reads`,`N round trips; no single snapshot by default`,`2+ collection Includes, read-heavy API endpoints`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One Trick To Improve EF Core Performance Using Query Splitting`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GY7QwSFeVBQ`,at:`8:03`}},{heading:`04 · Dialing it in: per-query versus context-level`,body:[`Here's where the flexibility gets genuinely nice. If you don't want to sprinkle AsSplitQuery across every repository method, drop into Program.cs where the DbContext is configured. Inside the UseSqlServer call there's an options action, and on it sits UseQuerySplittingBehavior. Pass QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery and every query on that context is split by default — which is exactly what you want for a read-heavy API where multi-include queries are the norm.`,`The escape hatch matters just as much. Back in the repository, the AsSplitQuery call becomes redundant because the context handles it — but if one hot path genuinely benefits from a single round trip, you call AsSingleQuery() on that specific query and it reverts, just for that one statement. Per-query override, context-wide default, and both directions work.`,`My recommendation after living with this: default your read-side contexts to split, audit the generated SQL once with a realistic dataset, and flip the outliers back to single. Measure both ways — the numbers, not the vibes, make the call.`],quote:`Set the default at the context, override at the query — and let the execution plans settle the argument.`}],diagrams:[{id:`query-shape-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Single Query vs Split Query Execution Paths`,caption:`The same LINQ query with three Includes, routed through two different SQL shapes — one multiplies rows, the other adds them.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["GET /gatherings/{id}"] --> B["Query Handler\\nInclude Creator, Attendees, Invitations"]
    B --> C{"Query shape"}
    C -->|"Single query"| D["1 SQL statement\\n3 stacked JOINs"]
    D --> E["~250,000 rows\\ngathering + member columns repeated"]
    E --> F["Slow materialization\\nSQL CPU pinned"]
    C -->|"AsSplitQuery"| G["Query 1: gathering + creator"]
    G --> H["Query 2: attendees\\njoin keys only"]
    H --> I["Query 3: invitations\\njoin keys only"]
    I --> J["~1,001 rows total\\nEF stitches graph in memory"]
    F --> K["p95: 4+ seconds"]
    J --> L["p95: ~40 ms"]`},{id:`split-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Split Query Round-Trip Sequence`,caption:`What the wire actually looks like with AsSplitQuery: root first, then one lean collection query per Include, stitched client-side.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant API as Gatherings API
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant DB as SQL Server
    API->>EF: GetGatheringById(id)
    EF->>DB: Query 1 — gathering + creator
    DB-->>EF: 1 row
    EF->>DB: Query 2 — attendees (join keys only)
    DB-->>EF: 500 rows
    EF->>DB: Query 3 — invitations (join keys only)
    DB-->>EF: 500 rows
    EF-->>API: Materialized gathering graph (identity resolution)`}],takeaways:[`Two or more collection Includes in one query is your AsSplitQuery trigger — row count multiplies (500 × 500 = 250,000), it doesn't add.`,`Splitting a 3-include read typically collapses transferred rows from ~250k to ~1k and pulls p95 from seconds back to tens of milliseconds.`,`Every split adds a round trip: keep it to 3–5 queries per logical read or network latency eats the payload win.`,`Set QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery once in UseSqlServer for read-heavy contexts, and use AsSingleQuery() as the per-query escape hatch.`,`Split queries run as separate auto-commit statements — wrap them in an explicit transaction whenever cross-query consistency matters.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — One Trick To Improve EF Core Performance Using Query Splitting`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GY7QwSFeVBQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-mAlO3OuoQvo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:15,title:`How To Track Entity Changes With EF Core | Audit Logging`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:38`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Auditor Asked 'When Did This Change?' — And Our Database Had No Answer`,paragraphs:[`It was a quiet Tuesday, halfway through a SOC 2 walkthrough — the kind of audit where the reviewer picks one record at random and asks you to narrate its entire life story. She picked a member account, pointed at a discount that had quietly dropped from 15% to 5%, and asked the question that ended the meeting: 'Show me when this changed, and by whom.' The admin screen showed current values. That was it. No timestamps, no history, nothing.`,`The engineers spent the afternoon digging. Some handlers dutifully set ModifiedOn by hand right next to the domain call. Others forgot. One background job stamped DateTime.Now from an app server in a different timezone, so a handful of rows appeared to have been modified before they were created. Forty-plus write paths, three competing stamping conventions, and zero confidence in any of them.`,`Nobody needed more discipline — they needed less surface area for forgetting. The fix that came out of the retro was two files: an IAuditableEntity interface in the domain, and a single SaveChangesInterceptor that stamps every auditable entity in the ChangeTracker before SaveChanges ever builds SQL. Every write path, covered by default, forever.`],problem:`Every write path had to remember to stamp its own timestamps — and the paths that forgot left no trace at all.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`ChangeTracker`,`IAuditableEntity`,`EntityState`,`DateTime.UtcNow`],note:`An IAuditableEntity contract in the domain layer plus a SaveChangesInterceptor on the DbContext stamps CreatedOnUtc/ModifiedOnUtc centrally, so auditing happens by default on every SaveChanges call.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why manual stamping always leaks`,body:["So here's the instinct everyone has first: put `member.ModifiedOn = DateTime.UtcNow` right there in the handler, next to `ChangeName`. And for one endpoint, one entity, it works. Ship it. The problem shows up on endpoint number seven, when a teammate copies an older handler that didn't have the stamping line — and now two conventions live in the same codebase.",`The deeper issue is that 'every write path must remember to stamp' is a convention, not a mechanism. Conventions live in people's heads, and people go on vacation. Background jobs, seed methods, admin scripts — they all bypass the handler layer entirely, and they're exactly the writes you most want on record.`,`So my first filter for any auditing design: does it work for code that doesn't know it's being audited? If the answer is no, you've built a reminder system, not an audit system.`],quote:`Any rule that lives in a developer's memory is a bug waiting for a deadline.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Manual stamping in handlers`,`Explicit, easy to read`,`Forgotten paths, inconsistent clocks`,`Never`],[`Domain method sets it`,`Colocated with behavior`,`Misses jobs, seeds, direct saves`,`Rare`],[`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`One hook, all write paths, testable`,`Slightly 'magic' for newcomers`,`Default choice`],[`DB trigger`,`Catches even raw SQL writes`,`No app context, hidden from review`,`Defense in depth`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Track Entity Changes With EF Core | Audit Logging`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mAlO3OuoQvo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the interface lives in the domain`,body:["Next decision: where does the auditable shape live? I put an `IAuditableEntity` interface in the domain's Primitives folder with two nullable DateTime properties — `CreatedOnUtc` and `ModifiedOnUtc`. Nullable matters more than people think: a brand-new entity genuinely hasn't been modified yet, and forcing a value there just teaches your team to lie.","Why an interface and not a base class? Single inheritance in C# is a budget. Your `Member` aggregate might already inherit from something, or you might not want a base entity at all. An interface is a capability declaration — 'I am auditable' — and the entity keeps its inheritance budget for actual domain structure.","And notice the split of responsibilities: the domain declares the shape, but it never sets the values. No aggregate method writes `ModifiedOnUtc`. That's infrastructure's job — which is exactly where the interceptor comes in."],quote:`The domain declares it's auditable; the infrastructure makes it true.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Track Entity Changes With EF Core | Audit Logging`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mAlO3OuoQvo`,at:`2:17`}},{heading:`03 · The interceptor: one hook before the SQL exists`,body:["Here's the interceptor itself. Inherit from `SaveChangesInterceptor` — it hands you virtual hooks for everything — and override `SavingChangesAsync`. First line of business: grab the `DbContext` off `eventData.Context`. If it's null for any reason, just fall through to the base implementation; no heroics.","Then the money line: `dbContext.ChangeTracker.Entries<IAuditableEntity>()`. The generic filter means you only touch entities that opted in — everything else sails through untouched. For each entry, branch on state: `Added` sets `CreatedOnUtc`, `Modified` sets `ModifiedOnUtc`. One subtle but critical detail: this all happens *before* you call `base.SavingChangesAsync`, because that's the moment EF builds the SQL. Stamp after, and your values miss the train.","Watching it run is the best proof. PUT a member's name: breakpoint hits, state is `Modified`, `ModifiedOnUtc` gets stamped, the UPDATE carries the column. POST a new registration: state is `Added`, `CreatedOnUtc` gets stamped, `ModifiedOnUtc` stays null. Same interceptor, both shapes, zero handler code."],quote:`Stamp before the base call — once the SQL is built, it's too late to change what it says.`,table:{headers:[`EntityState`,`Action`,`Why`],rows:[[`Added`,`Set CreatedOnUtc (and ModifiedOnUtc if desired)`,`First insert — the creation moment`],[`Modified`,`Set ModifiedOnUtc only`,`Never overwrite creation time`],[`Deleted`,`Skip (or soft-delete stamp)`,`Row is leaving; timestamps moot`],[`Unchanged`,`Skip`,`Nothing to audit`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Track Entity Changes With EF Core | Audit Logging`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mAlO3OuoQvo`,at:`7:22`}},{heading:`04 · Why not triggers, or a SaveChangesAsync override?`,body:[`Now the 'why not' round, because there are always three alternatives in the room. Database triggers first: they're bulletproof against raw SQL and rogue scripts, which is genuinely attractive. But they can't see your application's user context without contortions, they're invisible during code review, and your unit tests now need a live database to verify a timestamp.`,"Overriding `SaveChangesAsync` in the DbContext is the other common move. It works — but it welds the behavior to one base class. The moment you want auditing *and* domain-event dispatch *and* soft delete, you're stacking concerns into a god-context. Interceptors compose: `optionsBuilder.AddInterceptor(...)`, and multiple interceptors chain cleanly per context.",`My honest take: interceptor as the default, trigger as optional defense-in-depth if scripts hit the DB directly. And the quiet superpower of the interceptor is coverage of the future — the handler someone writes next sprint is audited on day one, with no memo required.`],quote:`A trigger knows the database; an interceptor knows your application — audits usually need both.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`Composable, testable, app context available`,`Misses raw SQL outside EF`,`Default`],[`DbContext override`,`Simple, one file`,`Couples concerns, hard to compose`,`Small apps`],[`DB trigger`,`Catches every write path`,`No app context, hidden from tests/review`,`Defense in depth`],[`Audit table via interceptor`,`Full who/what/old-new history`,`More writes, more storage`,`Compliance-grade`]]}},{heading:`05 · The honest limits: timestamps are not a trail`,body:[`Let's be honest about what we built, because overselling audit features is how teams get burned in the next review. Two timestamp columns answer exactly one question: when was this row created and last touched. That's genuinely ~90% of day-to-day questions, and the cost is one interface and one interceptor.`,"The moment the question becomes 'who changed it' or 'what did it look like before', you need more. 'Who' is a small step: inject an `ICurrentUser`-style accessor into the interceptor and add `CreatedBy`/`ModifiedBy` — same pattern, one more property. 'What changed' is a bigger step: an audit table written from the same ChangeTracker entries, or temporal tables if your database supports them.","And one last hygiene rule that saves real pain: `DateTime.UtcNow`, always. `DateTime.Now` on a server in another timezone doesn't just look wrong — it reorders your history. Clocks disagree; UTC is the only version of 'now' a distributed system agrees on."],quote:`Two columns answer 'when'. The day someone asks 'who and what', you're building an audit table.`,table:{headers:[`Need`,`Solution`,`Cost`,`When`],rows:[[`When created/modified`,`Timestamp columns + interceptor`,`Minutes`,`Always`],[`Who made the change`,`CreatedBy/ModifiedBy via user accessor`,`Hours`,`Multi-user systems`],[`What changed (old/new)`,`Audit table from ChangeTracker`,`Days`,`Compliance / disputes`],[`Full row history`,`Temporal tables / history table`,`Days + storage`,`Regulated domains`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`audit-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Audit stamping pipeline`,caption:`Every SaveChanges call funnels through the interceptor, which branches on EntityState before the base call builds SQL.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["PUT /members/{id}"] --> B["UpdateMember handler"]
    B --> C["member.ChangeName(...)"]
    C --> D["SaveChangesAsync()"]
    D --> E["Audit Interceptor<br/>SavingChangesAsync"]
    E --> F["ChangeTracker<br/>Entries of IAuditableEntity"]
    F --> G{"EntityState?"}
    G -->|Added| H["CreatedOnUtc = UtcNow"]
    G -->|Modified| I["ModifiedOnUtc = UtcNow"]
    H --> J["base.SavingChangesAsync"]
    I --> J
    J --> K["INSERT / UPDATE<br/>with stamped columns"]`},{id:`savechanges-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`SaveChanges sequence with the audit interceptor`,caption:`The interceptor inspects tracked entries and stamps timestamps before the base implementation generates the UPDATE.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as API
    participant H as Handler
    participant C as DbContext
    participant I as Audit Interceptor
    participant DB as Database

    API->>H: UpdateMember(command)
    H->>H: member.ChangeName(...)
    H->>C: SaveChangesAsync()
    C->>I: SavingChangesAsync(eventData)
    I->>C: ChangeTracker.Entries of IAuditableEntity
    C-->>I: entries (Member: Modified)
    I->>I: stamp ModifiedOnUtc = UtcNow
    I->>C: base.SavingChangesAsync()
    C->>DB: UPDATE members SET ..., modified_on_utc
    DB-->>C: rows affected
    C-->>H: success
    H-->>API: result`}],takeaways:[`One interceptor replaces dozens of forgotten lines: a single SavingChangesAsync hook audits every write path — handlers, jobs, seeds — with zero per-endpoint code.`,`Branch on EntityState: set CreatedOnUtc only when Added, ModifiedOnUtc only when Modified — never overwrite creation time on update.`,`Stamp DateTime.UtcNow, never DateTime.Now — one app server in the wrong timezone silently reorders your history.`,`Register once with optionsBuilder.AddInterceptor: any code that can reach SaveChanges is audited, including code that doesn't exist yet.`,`Two timestamp columns answer ~90% of 'when' questions; budget for an audit table or temporal tables the day someone asks 'who' and 'what changed'.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Track Entity Changes With EF Core | Audit Logging`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mAlO3OuoQvo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-89D6iFthM9U`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:16,title:`Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:27`,minutes:9,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Noon Newsletter: A Homepage Widget That Materialized the Entire Database`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday at 12:04, not 3 AM — the marketing team's newsletter had just landed in 400,000 inboxes, and the homepage's 'Serbian literary legends' widget was about to have the worst lunch break of its life. Within minutes, that innocent little endpoint — top two authors, country Serbia, age 27, with all their pre-1900 books — was taking eight to twelve seconds per request. Postgres CPU sat pinned at 100%, the connection pool starved, and checkout requests started timing out behind it.`,`The on-call engineer pulled the slow query from APM and opened the repository, and there it was in plain sight: a chain of Include() calls reaching across Authors, Books, and everything they touched, followed by a ToList() that materialized the entire database before a single Where was applied. Every homepage hit was shipping whole tables over the wire, then filtering and sorting them in C# with LINQ-to-Objects. The database was doing full scans while the application server did the real query work — and paid for it in GC pauses.`,`The fix wasn't one silver bullet. It was a projection, a compiled query, a hard empirical decision about which side of the boundary should filter the books, and a couple of well-placed indexes — stacked together, the benchmark landed at 187x faster on that run. This is the story of how each piece earned its place, one BenchmarkDotNet run at a time.`],problem:`Every homepage hit materialized the entire Authors and Books tables into memory before filtering a single row — full table scans on the database, LINQ-to-Objects filtering in the app, and connection pool starvation under midday load.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`BenchmarkDotNet`,`Compiled Queries`,`LINQ Projections`,`Covering Indexes`,`Postgres`],note:`EF Core executes the queries, BenchmarkDotNet keeps us honest, and compiled queries, DTO projections, and covering indexes carry the speedup.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the baseline melts`,body:[`Let's start with the baseline, because it's beautifully bad on purpose. You've got a straightforward LINQ query, a bunch of Include statements pulling in the related columns, and then — the killer — a ToList() call sitting there before any filtering happens. That ToList() materializes the entire database. Everything after it isn't a query anymore; it's just C# loops over objects already sitting in memory.`,`So think about what actually happens per request: EF Core ships the whole Authors and Books tables over the wire, the collection Includes fan out into cartesian bloat, and then your Where for country and age runs as LINQ-to-Objects. The database did full scans, the app did the real query work, and the garbage collector picked up the tab on every single call.`,`Now, the obvious first instinct is 'slap AsNoTracking on it.' And sure, that helps — but it doesn't fix the shape. The shape is the problem. Until filtering, sorting, and Take(2) happen inside the SQL, you're paying for rows you'll throw away.`],quote:`Every early ToList() is a full table you ship over the wire.`,table:{headers:[`Query shape`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Early ToList + Includes`,`Simplest to write`,`Full-table materialization, cartesian bloat, GC churn`,`Never`],[`Server-side projection`,`Only needed columns cross the wire`,`More upfront query design`,`Default choice`],[`AsSplitQuery`,`Avoids join explosion on collections`,`Extra round trips per include`,`When includes multiply rows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89D6iFthM9U`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: push the work into SQL`,body:[`So the rewrite starts by drawing a hard line at the database boundary. Filter authors where Country equals Serbia and Age equals 27. Sort descending by the author's book count. Take the top two. Project straight into a DTO that carries only the columns we're required to return — author fields, plus each book's title and publication year.`,`Why not drop to raw SQL or Dapper? Two reasons. First, the challenge rules say EF Core only. Second, honestly, once the shape is right, EF's translation is fine — and the team maintains LINQ far more easily than hand-written SQL strings. We add AsNoTracking because this is a pure read; there's no reason for the change tracker to babysit two thousand entities it will never save.`,`The projection is where the win actually lives. EF generates a SELECT of exactly the DTO columns — no unused fields, no tracking overhead, no full-entity graphs. The result set shrinks from 'the database' to 'two authors and their old books.'`],quote:`The fastest row is the one you never fetch.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`DTO projection`,`Minimal columns, no tracking, tight SQL`,`Mapping expression upfront`,`Best default`],[`Entities + AsNoTracking`,`Familiar, reusable graphs`,`Still drags unused columns`,`Reads that may mutate later`],[`Raw SQL / Dapper`,`Total control`,`Breaks EF-only rule, loses compile-time checks`,`Out of scope here`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89D6iFthM9U`,at:`4:22`}},{heading:`03 · Compiled queries: pay the parse cost once`,body:[`Next layer: compiled queries. You declare a private static readonly field holding the result of EF.CompileQuery — the first argument is the AppDbContext type, the return type here is an IEnumerable of AuthorDto. What you get back is a Func. At call time, you new up a context, invoke the function with it, and call ToList on the result.`,`What does that buy you? Every regular LINQ query pays a per-call compilation pipeline — expression tree analysis, plan caching lookups, the works. A compiled query skips all of that on the hot path. In the benchmark, the compiled version wasn't the fastest variant overall, but it showed noticeably less allocated memory, which is exactly what you'd expect when you stop re-processing the expression tree thousands of times.`,`The trade-offs are real, though: the query must live in a static field, it can't be composed dynamically at runtime, there's no change tracking, and it's clunkier to tweak. So this is a tool for hot paths with a fixed shape — not something you reach for on every query in the codebase.`],quote:`Compile once at startup, execute a million times.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Regular LINQ`,`Flexible, composable, loggable`,`Pays compile cost on every call`,`Cold paths`],[`EF.CompileQuery`,`No per-call compilation, lower allocations`,`Static, fixed shape, no tracking`,`Hot paths`],[`Pregenerated queries`,`Compiled at build time`,`Extra tooling overhead`,`Large apps with many hot queries`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89D6iFthM9U`,at:`5:17`}},{heading:`04 · The surprise: filter the books in memory, not in SQL`,body:[`Here's the twist nobody expects. We're left with the choice of where to apply the book filter — published year before 1900. My first instinct, and I suspect yours, was 'do it at the database level, obviously.' So I added the Where on PublishedYear into the compiled query and ran the benchmark.`,`The database-side version was slower. Testing showed that filtering the books in memory — after fetching the top two authors with their books — was the more performant option in this dataset. Why? Think about what the SQL-side filter does to the plan: it turns a clean top-two lookup into correlated per-author row evaluation, and published year has terrible selectivity. Meanwhile, each author only has a handful of books, so scanning those few objects in memory is nearly free.`,`This is the lesson I'd tattoo on every ORM user's forearm: the boundary between database and memory is empirical, not ideological. 'Push everything to SQL' is a heuristic, not a law. Benchmark both sides, and let the query plan — not your intuition — make the call.`],quote:`The query plan doesn't care about your intuition — only the benchmark does.`,table:{headers:[`Book filter location`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In SQL (WHERE year < 1900)`,`Less data over the wire in theory`,`Slower plan here, correlated evaluation cost`,`Large per-parent child sets`],[`In memory after fetch`,`Fastest in this dataset, simpler SQL`,`Slightly more rows transferred`,`Small child sets, measured win`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89D6iFthM9U`,at:`5:53`}},{heading:`05 · Reading the numbers like a grown-up`,body:[`Let's talk about the results honestly, because this is where people fool themselves. On that benchmark run, the optimized query came in 187 times faster than baseline — but the compiled-query-only variant was not the fastest overall, even though it allocated noticeably less memory. Both things are true at once, and both matter.`,`The final solution is a stack, not a trick: DTO projection so only required columns cross the wire, a compiled query so the hot path skips compilation, in-memory book filtering because the benchmark said so, and indexes on the filter and sort columns because the rules explicitly allow touching the database. Remove any one layer and the number moves. None of them alone is the 190x.`,`And watch the variance between runs. One run is an anecdote — read the mean, the allocated column, and the Gen0 collections together before you claim victory. The repo is public; submit your own solution and see how far past 190x you can push it. That leaderboard is where the real learning happens.`],quote:`A single benchmark run is an anecdote; the allocation column is the confession.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Baseline`,`Optimized`,`Read it as`],rows:[[`Mean latency`,`Seconds per op`,`Milliseconds (187x faster on the run)`,`Query shape fixed`],[`Allocated memory`,`Huge — full tables in RAM`,`Fraction of baseline`,`Projection + compiled query`],[`Gen0 collections`,`Many per operation`,`Near zero`,`GC pressure eliminated`],[`Rows from DB`,`Entire Authors and Books tables`,`2 authors + their pre-1900 books`,`Boundary drawn correctly`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`query-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Baseline vs Optimized Query Execution Paths`,caption:`The baseline ships whole tables into C# before filtering; the optimized path filters, sorts, and projects inside the database and returns two rows.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph BASE["Baseline path - every homepage hit"]
        A["HTTP request"] --> B["EF Core LINQ with Include chains"]
        B --> C["ToList materializes entire Authors and Books"]
        C --> D["Filter and sort in C# memory"]
        D --> E["GC pressure, pool starvation, 8s responses"]
    end
    subgraph OPT["Optimized path"]
        F["HTTP request"] --> G["Static compiled query func"]
        G --> H["SQL: WHERE + ORDER BY + DTO projection"]
        H --> I[("Postgres with covering indexes")]
        I --> J["2 authors and their pre-1900 books"]
    end
    BASE -. "up to 190x faster" .-> OPT`},{id:`compiled-query-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Compiled Query Execution Sequence`,caption:`How a benchmark invocation flows through the static compiled query function, the DbContext, and Postgres — with a correctness check against the baseline result set.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant Bench as Benchmark method
    participant Func as Compiled query static field
    participant Ctx as AppDbContext
    participant PG as Postgres
    Bench->>Func: Invoke with new AppDbContext
    Func->>Ctx: Run pre-compiled pipeline
    Ctx->>PG: SELECT top 2 authors, filtered and projected
    PG-->>Ctx: 2 authors with pre-1900 books
    Ctx-->>Func: IEnumerable of AuthorDto
    Func-->>Bench: ToList result
    Bench->>Bench: Assert identical data to baseline`}],takeaways:[`Never call ToList() before your Where, OrderBy, and Take — early materialization cost the baseline roughly 190x in end-to-end latency.`,`Project straight to DTOs with AsNoTracking so only the columns you return cross the wire — allocations dropped to a fraction of the baseline per run.`,`Store hot-path queries as EF.CompileQuery in a static readonly field: it skips per-call compilation and showed noticeably lower allocated memory, at the cost of a fixed, non-composable shape.`,`Benchmark both sides of the app-database boundary — in this dataset, filtering books in memory beat SQL-side filtering, so trust the numbers, not the 'push it all to SQL' heuristic.`,`Add covering indexes for your filter and sort columns (country, age, book count) — schema changes were explicitly allowed by the rules and are often the cheapest order-of-magnitude win available.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Awesome EF Core Performance Optimization To Make It 190x FASTER`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89D6iFthM9U`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:17,title:`This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:27`,minutes:9,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Ten Round Trips Hiding in One Endpoint`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday afternoon, not some heroic midnight outage. Finance had scheduled their monthly invoice-reminder email blast for 12:30, and within minutes the customer dashboard — the endpoint that had sailed through staging — started stacking up requests. SQL Server CPU climbed into the nineties, the connection pool queue grew, and support tickets arrived faster than we could close them.`,`The Aspire dashboard made it embarrassingly obvious. One endpoint, the invoice summary, was firing ten identical SELECTs against the line-items table on every single request. The code read like a tutorial: fetch the invoices from the invoice service — a stand-in for another team's external system — then loop over them, querying line items for each invoice one at a time. Ten round trips per request, 0.6 to 1.5 milliseconds each locally, and every request held its pool connection hostage for the entire loop.`,`We didn't rewrite anything. We collected the line item IDs, issued one Contains query, and grouped the rows in memory. Same response shape, four times faster, and the CPU graph flattened within minutes. The mistake is more common than any of us want to admit — there's a decent chance it's sitting in your codebase right now.`],problem:`An innocent-looking foreach loop was turning one logical read into ten database round trips per request, quietly exhausting the connection pool under midday traffic.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`Aspire`,`Connection Pool`],note:`EF Core talking to SQL Server, wired together through Aspire's app host; the connection pool is the shared, finite resource the N+1 loop quietly exhausts.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the innocent loop lies`,body:[`Let me walk you through the endpoint exactly as I found it. You resolve the invoice service and the application DbContext from dependency injection, the service hands you ten invoices — and remember, that service is a stand-in for an external system or another team's microservice, so the parent rows never touch EF. Then the foreach starts: for each invoice, build a DTO, query the line items, attach them, move on.`,`On a whiteboard this looks harmless. But trace what EF actually does: one SELECT per iteration. Ten invoices, ten round trips. Locally, each one costs 600 microseconds to 1.5 milliseconds — invisible on your machine, fine in staging. Put it behind real traffic and a real network, and those round trips stop being microseconds. The latency math is brutal because it multiplies: rows per request, times requests per second, times round-trip cost.`],quote:`The loop wasn't the bug — the round trip inside the loop was.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary you can't join across`,body:[`Here's the constraint that traps people: the invoices come from across a service boundary, so you can't write one join and you can't use Include — EF doesn't own the parent rows. When you can't join across the boundary, the instinct is to query across it, one item at a time. That's how the N+1 gets born, and it feels defensible because each individual query is fast.`,`The fix is almost embarrassingly simple. Before the loop, collect every line item ID from all ten invoices into a list. Then issue one query using Contains — EF Core translates that to WHERE Id IN (...). One round trip, one result set. Group the line items in memory with a lookup, then assemble your DTOs. Same output, same contracts, a quarter of the latency — four times faster in the demo, and the gap widens the moment the database moves behind a network hop.`],quote:`When you can't join across the boundary, batch across it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-item query (N+1)`,`Simple, obvious code`,`N round trips; latency scales with row count`,`Never`],[`Single Contains batch`,`1 round trip; tiny diff`,`Mind the parameter limit on huge ID lists`,`Default choice`],[`Lazy loading`,`Zero code change`,`Doesn't apply to non-EF parents; hides the cost`,`Avoid`],[`Cached dictionary`,`Fastest warm reads`,`Cold request still pays N+1; you own invalidation`,`Hot read-only data`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,at:`0:48`}},{heading:`03 · 'Why not just cache it?' and other tempting detours`,body:[`Every time I present this, someone asks why we don't just cache the line items. And sure — cache the output if it's read-heavy and you can tolerate staleness. But the first cold request still pays ten round trips, and now you own invalidation on top. Caching is a layer you put on top of the fix, not the fix itself.`,`The other tempting wrong answer: fire the ten queries in parallel with Task.WhenAll. Sounds clever, but each concurrent query pins its own connection from the pool. Ten per request, times a hundred concurrent requests — that's pool starvation, and now your timeout alerts are the least of your problems. One batched query holds one connection for exactly one round trip. That's the whole game.`],quote:`Parallel N+1 is still N+1 — it just starves the connection pool faster.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,at:`1:16`}},{heading:`04 · Putting numbers on the fix`,body:[`Let's put numbers on it. Seed the database, warm everything up, and the unoptimized endpoint answers in about 23 to 24 milliseconds — with everything already cached inside the API. Each line-item query costs 600 microseconds to 1.5 milliseconds locally; talk to a remote database and multiply every single round trip by ten to fifty. That's the difference between a quirk and an incident.`,`After batching, the same endpoint is four times faster. But the number I actually watch day to day is queries per request — the Aspire dashboard shows every EF call, and 'ten identical SELECTs' jumps off the screen once you know to look. Make query count a first-class metric in code review and dashboards, not a postmortem discovery.`],quote:`You can't fix what the dashboard never shows you.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Before (N+1)`,`After (batched)`],rows:[[`DB round trips per request`,`10`,`1`],[`Warm latency (local DB)`,`~23-24 ms`,`~6 ms (4x faster)`],[`Per-query cost`,`0.6-1.5 ms each`,`n/a — single batch`],[`Remote DB latency`,`50-200+ ms`,`5-20 ms`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,at:`1:53`}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-path-compare`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: N+1 loop vs one batched query`,caption:`Same endpoint, same DTOs — the only change is collapsing ten round trips into one.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph BEFORE["Before: N+1 loop"]
        A1["GET /invoices/unoptimized"] --> B1["Invoice Service returns 10 invoices"]
        B1 --> C1{"foreach invoice"}
        C1 --> D1["DbContext query for line items"]
        D1 --> DB1[("SQL Server")]
        DB1 --> C1
        C1 -->|"10 iterations"| E1["10 round trips per request"]
    end
    subgraph AFTER["After: one batched query"]
        A2["GET /invoices"] --> B2["Invoice Service returns 10 invoices"]
        B2 --> C2["Collect all line item IDs"]
        C2 --> D2["One query: WHERE Id IN (...)"]
        D2 --> DB2[("SQL Server")]
        DB2 --> E2["Group line items in memory"]
        E2 --> F2["Assemble DTOs: 1 round trip"]
    end`},{id:`wire-level-n-plus-one`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Wire-level view of the unoptimized endpoint`,caption:`Ten sequential round trips per request — each one cheap alone, expensive multiplied by traffic.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as API Endpoint
    participant S as Invoice Service
    participant D as SQL Server
    C->>A: GET /invoices/unoptimized
    A->>S: GetInvoices(companyId)
    S-->>A: 10 invoices
    loop 10 sequential round trips
        A->>D: SELECT LineItems WHERE Id = @id
        D-->>A: one rowset
    end
    A-->>C: 10 DTOs, ~23-24 ms cached
    Note over A,D: 0.6 to 1.5 ms per query locally, far worse over a remote link`}],takeaways:[`Budget round trips, not queries: 10 invoices x 0.6-1.5 ms per query is 6-15 ms of pure database wait per request, before serialization and network hops.`,`Batch parent-child assembly with one Contains query — EF Core translates it to WHERE Id IN (...); the demo endpoint dropped from ~23-24 ms to ~6 ms, a 4x win.`,`Chunk IN lists to ~1,000 IDs — SQL Server caps a single RPC at 2,100 parameters, so an unbounded Contains eventually throws.`,`Never 'fix' N+1 with parallel per-item queries — 10 concurrent queries per request pins 10 pool connections and starves every other endpoint under load.`,`Watch queries-per-request in the Aspire or OpenTelemetry dashboard; treat any endpoint averaging more than ~5 queries per request as a bug to triage, not a style preference.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — This EF Core Mistake Is Wrecking Performance (Fix Inside)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlGLK0Y3Ghw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-lLDBVMj5He8`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:18,title:`Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 6:23`,minutes:6,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Line Item That Refused to Die`,paragraphs:[`It's release day for a B2B ordering portal and the midday flash sale is in full swing. A warehouse coordinator opens order #4821 and deletes a mispriced line item. Seconds later — same order, nearly the same instant — a CS rep bumps the quantity on a different line and hits save. Both requests load the order, both mutate the rich domain model, both call SaveChanges. Under Read Committed, the second write simply overwrites the first: the 'deleted' line item is back on the order, and nobody sees an error, a log line, or anything at all until the customer's invoice lands two hours later.`,`The on-call dev ships the obvious fix within the hour: wrap the command handler in a transaction opened through the DbContext's Database facade, isolation level Serializable. Correctness holds — the second writer genuinely waits now. But every handler pins the entire order aggregate, every line item row it read, until commit. During the next traffic surge, handlers queue behind each other, p95 latency climbs from 80 ms to 4 seconds, and the connection pool starts timing out requests that used to fly through.`,`The fix that stuck took about ten lines: a uint shadow property mapped with IsRowVersion(), which on Postgres rides the xmin system column for free. No locks, no waiting — just a WHERE clause that stops matching when somebody got there first, and one exception the application finally knows how to handle.`],problem:`Concurrent edits to the same order silently overwrite each other under Read Committed, and the Serializable 'fix' trades silent data loss for a latency collapse at peak traffic.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Read Committed`,`Serializable`,`xmin Row Version`,`IsRowVersion()`,`DbUpdateConcurrencyException`],note:`Postgres already maintains a per-row version in the xmin system column; EF Core turns it into a concurrency token so SaveChanges detects conflicts instead of holding locks. BeginTransaction with Serializable is the pessimistic alternative — same goal, opposite economics.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just wrap it in a transaction' fails`,body:[`Here's the trap almost everyone falls into first. You see two users hitting the same order and you think, 'easy — BeginTransaction.' And sure, the Database facade on your IApplicationDbContext gives you exactly that, and it even accepts an isolation level. But the default is Read Committed, and Read Committed only holds a lock while you're reading. The instant the SELECT returns, that lock is gone.`,`So both handlers load the same order, both call into the rich domain model, both persist through the unit of work — and the second SaveChanges wins. No exception, no conflict, just a quiet lost update. The transaction bought you atomicity, not isolation between two business operations. That's the distinction that matters: a transaction protects one writer from half-finished state, not two writers from each other.`],quote:`A transaction at the default isolation level is a comfort blanket, not a concurrency guarantee.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation Level`,`What It Actually Locks`,`Lost Update Possible?`,`Fit for This Case`],rows:[[`Read Committed`,`Rows locked only during each statement`,`Yes — last SaveChanges wins`,`Default, insufficient alone`],[`Repeatable Read`,`Read rows pinned to your snapshot`,`No, but conflicts abort late`,`Possible, still coarse`],[`Serializable`,`All rows read, locked until commit`,`No — conflicts fail fast`,`Correct but heavy`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLDBVMj5He8`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the pessimistic path`,body:[`Let's give pessimistic locking an honest hearing. Serializable isolation through BeginTransaction(isolationLevel) does exactly what you'd hope: every row your DbContext reads inside that transaction stays locked until you call Commit. Two users can't remove line items from the same order at once — the second one waits. Simple to reason about, easy to demo, correct by construction.`,`The problem is the blast radius. To remove one line item, you loaded the whole order aggregate — so you're pinning the order row plus every line item row until commit. Under a midday surge those locks stack: handlers serialize behind each other, throughput falls off a cliff, and you still need retry logic for serialization failures. Pessimistic locking is a great tool when the critical section is short and the hot row is narrow. 'A user is editing an order for thirty seconds' is neither.`],quote:`Pessimistic locking buys certainty with throughput — and the invoice arrives at peak traffic.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Pessimistic (Serializable)`,`Optimistic (Row Version)`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Lock every row read until commit`,`Compare version in the WHERE clause`],[`Best when`,`Short, hot, narrow critical section`,`Conflicts rare, think-time long`],[`Failure mode`,`Latency collapse, serialization errors`,`DbUpdateConcurrencyException`],[`Cost`,`Locks scale with rows read`,`One extra predicate per write`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLDBVMj5He8`,at:`1:15`}},{heading:`03 · Flipping to optimistic: let the row carry its own version`,body:[`Here's the flip: instead of locking the future, detect the past. In the line item configuration I add a shadow property — call it Version, type uint — and chain IsRowVersion(). That one call does two things: it marks the property as a concurrency token, and it tells the database to maintain it automatically.`,`On Postgres this is almost cheating, in a good way. The column maps to xmin — a system column every row already carries, holding the id of the transaction that last wrote it. I generate a migration, and on Postgres there's barely anything physical to create; EF just starts selecting xmin alongside my regular columns. SQL Server folks get the same deal with rowversion. The database was already keeping score — we just asked EF Core to check it.`],quote:`Postgres was already tracking every row's version — xmin — we just asked EF Core to care.`,table:{headers:[`Database`,`Native Row Version`,`What You Configure`],rows:[[`Postgres`,`xmin system column`,`uint shadow property + IsRowVersion()`],[`SQL Server`,`rowversion / timestamp`,`byte[] property + IsRowVersion()`],[`Others`,`App-managed counter or GUID`,`ConcurrencyToken() + manual increment`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLDBVMj5He8`,at:`2:36`}},{heading:`04 · What the conflict actually looks like on the wire`,body:[`Let me show you the moment of truth. I open DBeaver, update the price behind the scenes, and commit — xmin on that row just moved to my transaction's id. Back in the app, I remove the line item and hit SaveChanges. Look at the SQL EF Core emits: DELETE FROM line_items WHERE id = @p0 AND xmin = @p1. The version I read at the start is now part of the predicate.`,`That DELETE matches zero rows, because the row changed under us. EF Core sees 'zero rows affected' for a row it tracked as modified, and that's the trigger: DbUpdateConcurrencyException. No locks were held, nobody waited, and the conflict was caught at exactly the right moment — the write, not the read. And note it's per-row: every row in the SaveChanges batch gets its version checked, so partial conflicts are detectable too.`],quote:`The conflict isn't thrown by the database — it's a WHERE clause that quietly stops matching.`,table:{headers:[`Statement`,`Naive Shape`,`Optimistic Shape`],rows:[[`DELETE`,`WHERE id = @id`,`WHERE id = @id AND xmin = @version`],[`UPDATE`,`SET ... WHERE id = @id`,`SET ... WHERE id = @id AND xmin = @version`],[`SELECT`,`id, price, ...`,`id, price, ..., xmin`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLDBVMj5He8`,at:`4:26`}},{heading:`05 · Handling the exception like an adult`,body:[`So you've caught the conflict — now what? You've got three honest options. Surface it: tell the user 'this order changed while you were editing — refresh and try again.' That's usually right for human-facing flows, because the human has context that no retry loop does.`,`Or retry automatically: reload the aggregate fresh, re-run the domain operation, bounded attempts with backoff. With a rich domain model this is often safe, because RemoveLineItem re-derives its decisions from the new state — but only retry if the operation is genuinely re-runnable against fresh data. And if your conflict rate is high, don't paper over it with retries; that's the system telling you the aggregate is too hot, and that specific path might genuinely want pessimistic locking or a queue.`],quote:`An optimistic conflict isn't an error — it's the database telling you the truth, on time.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Surface to user`,`Human resolves with full context`,`Extra friction per conflict`,`Human-facing edits`],[`Auto-retry (bounded)`,`Invisible when conflicts are rare`,`Unsafe if not re-runnable`,`Background jobs, low conflict rate`],[`Custom merge`,`Best UX for field-level edits`,`Most code to maintain`,`Collaborative editing surfaces`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`lost-update-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Optimistic locking: the conflict, frame by frame`,caption:`User B's write bumps xmin between User A's read and write — the versioned DELETE matches zero rows and EF Core raises DbUpdateConcurrencyException instead of silently overwriting.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as User A - remove line item
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant PG as Postgres
    participant B as User B - edits price

    A->>EF: Load order + line items
    EF->>PG: SELECT id, price, xmin FROM line_items
    PG-->>EF: rows with xmin = 42
    B->>PG: UPDATE price - xmin becomes 43
    A->>EF: RemoveLineItem() then SaveChanges()
    EF->>PG: DELETE WHERE id = @id AND xmin = 42
    PG-->>EF: 0 rows affected
    EF-->>A: DbUpdateConcurrencyException`},{id:`strategy-decision-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Choosing your concurrency strategy`,caption:`The decision path from the incident: contention profile decides between locking the future (pessimistic) and detecting the past (optimistic).`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Two concurrent writers on one order"] --> B{"Contention profile?"}
    B -->|"Hot row, short critical section"| P["Pessimistic path"]
    B -->|"Long think-time, rare conflicts"| O["Optimistic path"]
    P --> P1["BeginTransaction Serializable"]
    P1 --> P2["Every row read is locked until commit"]
    P2 --> P3["Commit - or serialization failure + retries"]
    O --> O1["Map uint version via IsRowVersion"]
    O1 --> O2["EF selects xmin with every read"]
    O2 --> O3{"xmin still matches at SaveChanges?"}
    O3 -->|"Yes"| O4["Write succeeds"]
    O3 -->|"No"| O5["DbUpdateConcurrencyException"]
    O5 --> O6["Bounded retry - or surface conflict to user"]`}],takeaways:[`Read Committed releases its read lock the moment the statement ends — two SaveChanges on the same aggregate is a silent lost update with zero exceptions.`,`Serializable pessimistic locking pins every row your DbContext read: one aggregate load can hold dozens of rows until commit, and p95 latency pays the bill at peak.`,`Rule of thumb: below ~5% write conflict rate, optimistic wins on throughput; sustained conflicts above ~30% mean redesign the aggregate or go pessimistic for that path.`,`On Postgres, xmin is a free row version: one uint shadow property plus IsRowVersion() costs one extra predicate per UPDATE/DELETE and nothing else.`,`Retry DbUpdateConcurrencyException at most 3 times with jittered backoff, and keep any pessimistic critical section under ~100 ms — longer than that, move it to optimistic.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Optimistic Locking vs Pessimistic Locking With EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLDBVMj5He8`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`consistency-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-x4XJe6aGUlw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:19,title:`How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:38`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Redis`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The customer who wouldn't leave: a stale cache, a hot table, and the log that told the truth`,paragraphs:[`It's 12:40 on a Tuesday and marketing has just sent the quarterly 'manage your profile' blast to 40,000 customers. Within minutes, support is forwarding tickets: accounts deleted last week are still showing up in the app, and one customer's corrected billing address keeps reverting on refresh. The API isn't lying — the cache is. Every customer record sits in Redis behind a five-minute TTL, and invalidation is a poller running every sixty seconds.`,`That poller — SELECT id FROM customers WHERE updated_at > @cursor — has three failure modes we never priced in. It can't see deletes, because deleted rows stop having an updated_at. It skips anything committed inside the same second as the cursor. And under the blast's write load it's hammering the hottest table in the database every minute, competing with the very traffic it exists to protect. Cache and database drift apart in front of thousands of logged-in users.`,`We didn't need a better poll. We needed to stop interrogating the table and start listening to the write-ahead log — the one place where every insert, update, and delete appears exactly once, in commit order, the moment it's durable. Postgres will stream that to you through a logical replication slot, and Npgsql will hand it to your .NET code as an IAsyncEnumerable.`],problem:`Polling-based cache invalidation misses deletes, skips same-second commits, and taxes the database exactly when it's busiest — we need a lossless, ordered, low-latency change feed straight from the WAL.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`Npgsql`,`pgoutput`,`Logical Replication`,`Replication Slot`,`Redis`,`HybridCache`,`.NET Aspire`],note:`Postgres's WAL is the change feed: a publication and replication slot decode committed row changes via pgoutput, Npgsql streams them over a dedicated replication connection into a .NET BackgroundService that invalidates a Redis-backed HybridCache.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why polling keeps lying to you`,body:[`So the instinct when your cache goes stale is to make the poller smarter. Poll every ten seconds instead of sixty. Add an index on updated_at. Add a deleted_at column so deletes become visible. And each of those 'fixes' costs you: a faster poll multiplies load on the exact table that's already melting, every write now maintains another index, and soft-deletes mean every read path filters ghosts forever. You're paying the database a tax to approximate information it already has.`,`The next instinct is triggers — write an outbox row on every change, ship it to a queue. Better, but now your OLTP transaction is coupled to a side effect, you're writing every change twice, and you still own delivery, retries, and ordering. Here's the thing though: Postgres already maintains a perfect, ordered, crash-safe outbox. It's called the write-ahead log. Every insert, update, and delete lands there in commit order, the moment it's durable. We just need to subscribe.`],quote:`The table tells you what is true; the WAL tells you what happened, in order.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Polling updated_at`,`Trivial to build`,`Misses deletes, skips same-second writes, adds load`,`Small, low-churn tables`],[`Triggers + outbox`,`Rich app-level events`,`Couples write path, double writes, you own delivery`,`Domain event pipelines`],[`Logical replication CDC`,`Lossless, ordered, zero write-path cost`,`Slot ops: LSNs, retention, monitoring`,`Cache invalidation, projections`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: physical vs logical replication`,body:[`Postgres ships two flavors of replication, and they're nothing alike. Physical replication streams the WAL byte-for-byte — perfect for standbys, useless to application code, because a changed 8 KB page means nothing at the row level. Logical decoding replays that same WAL through an output plugin and reassembles it into row-level semantics: this row was inserted, this one updated, that one deleted, inside this transaction.`,`To unlock it, you flip wal_level to logical — a server-level setting that needs a restart, so bake it into your Postgres container args on day one. Then you create two objects: a publication naming the tables you care about, and a replication slot, which is Postgres promising to retain every byte of WAL you haven't consumed yet. We're using pgoutput, the plugin Postgres itself uses for built-in replication — it's always available and Npgsql decodes it natively, so there's no wal2json extension to install or version-match. One gotcha before anyone copies this: UPDATE and DELETE events need a replica identity, and a primary key is enough by default. Publish a table without one and those events fail to decode.`],quote:`Flip one setting, create two objects, and every commit becomes an event you can subscribe to.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,at:`3:18`}},{heading:`03 · The .NET side: this is not your pooled connection`,body:[`Now the .NET side, and the first surprise is the connection type. This is not your pooled NpgsqlConnection — it's NpgsqlLogicalReplicationConnection, and it doesn't speak the query protocol at all. It speaks the replication protocol: one long-lived, single-consumer stream. Don't pool it, don't share it — if a second consumer tries to start the same slot, it gets an error, not a queue.`,`Inside a BackgroundService's ExecuteAsync, the shape is small. await using the connection, open it, new up a PgOutputReplicationSlot named customers_slot, then StartReplication with PgOutputReplicationOptions pointing at the publication and protocol version 4. What comes back is an IAsyncEnumerable of XLogDataMessage — raw pgoutput frames. The rhythm is Begin, Relation, then Insert/Update/Delete, then Commit. Relation messages map a relation OID to a table shape — cache those, they repeat constantly. And here's the discipline that matters: apply changes on Commit, not as they stream in, or you'll invalidate a cache with a transaction that might still roll back.`],quote:`It's not a query you await; it's a subscription that never ends.`,table:{headers:[`Property`,`NpgsqlConnection`,`Logical replication connection`],rows:[[`Protocol`,`Query (SQL)`,`Replication (XLogData frames)`],[`Lifetime`,`Pooled, short-lived`,`One long-lived stream`],[`Concurrency`,`Many concurrent users`,`Exactly one consumer per slot`],[`Payload`,`Result sets`,`Begin / Relation / DML / Commit`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,at:`4:42`}},{heading:`04 · The part that bites: a slot never forgets`,body:[`Now the part that bites people a week later in production. That slot is a promise Postgres keeps on your disk. If your consumer is down — crashed, redeploying, wedged on a bad message — Postgres retains every WAL byte you haven't acknowledged. Indefinitely. A long weekend with a dead worker can fill a volume, and when WAL can't be recycled, Postgres stops accepting writes. Yes: a background service in your app can take the database down.`,`Two guardrails make this safe. First, acknowledge: after each Commit, call SetReplicationStatus with that message's LSN so Postgres can advance confirmed_flush_lsn. Second, cap and watch: set max_slot_wal_keep_size so a dead slot can only cost bounded disk, and alert on per-slot lag with pg_wal_lsn_diff between the current WAL position and confirmed_flush_lsn. And in throwaway environments, drop the slot when you tear the app down — orphaned slots from local dev are a classic way to fill a colleague's disk.`],quote:`A replication slot never forgets — it just keeps writing your debt to disk.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Check`,`Action`],rows:[[`WAL lag per slot`,`pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), confirmed_flush_lsn)`,`Alert past ~1 GB, wake the consumer`],[`Slot status`,`wal_status in pg_replication_slots`,`'unreserved' means loss risk — act now`],[`Disk guardrail`,`max_slot_wal_keep_size`,`Cap at e.g. 10 GB so a dead slot can't fill the volume`],[`Dead environments`,`pg_drop_replication_slot('name')`,`Drop slots when tearing down apps or branches`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,at:`5:17`}},{heading:`05 · Closing the loop: commit to cache key in one hop`,body:[`The mapping from WAL event to cache action is almost embarrassingly simple. Insert: warm the key with the new row. Update: remove the key and let the next read repopulate — or set the new row directly if reads are hot. Delete: remove the key. All idempotent, and that's not a nice-to-have: reconnects replay from your last acknowledged LSN, so you will invalidate the same key twice. That must be a no-op. Never hang non-idempotent side effects — emails, charges — directly off this stream without a dedupe store.`,`With HybridCache on top, a Remove evicts Redis and the local in-memory layer on the node that saw the event. Other nodes' L1s don't hear about it, so keep a short in-memory TTL — tens of seconds — as a backstop, and you get millisecond-scale invalidation on the hot path with bounded staleness everywhere else. And that's the real payoff: the cache stops being a copy that decays on a timer and becomes a projection of the log, updated the moment Postgres commits.`],quote:`Invalidate on commit, not on TTL — then the cache is a projection of the log, not a guess about it.`,table:{headers:[`WAL event`,`Cache action`,`Why`],rows:[[`Insert`,`Set / warm key with new row`,`Reads hit fresh data immediately`],[`Update`,`Remove key (or Set new row)`,`Next read repopulates with truth`],[`Delete`,`Remove key`,`Ghosts vanish in ms, not TTLs`],[`Commit`,`SetReplicationStatus(lsn)`,`Advances the slot, bounds replay`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,at:`9:54`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cdc-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`WAL to cache: the CDC pipeline`,caption:`Every committed row change flows from the WAL through a replication slot into a .NET background service that invalidates HybridCache — with LSN feedback closing the loop.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph api["Customers API"]
        W["Write path<br/>INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE"]
        R["Read path<br/>cache-aside"]
    end

    subgraph pg["Postgres"]
        WAL["WAL<br/>wal_level = logical"]
        PUB["Publication<br/>customers_pub"]
        SLOT["Replication slot<br/>customers_slot"]
    end

    subgraph worker[".NET background service"]
        BG["WAL streaming service<br/>BackgroundService"]
        DEC["pgoutput decoder<br/>Begin / Relation / DML / Commit"]
    end

    subgraph cache["Cache tier"]
        HC["HybridCache L1<br/>in-process"]
        REDIS["Redis L2<br/>distributed"]
    end

    W --> WAL
    PUB -. "publication option" .-> SLOT
    WAL -- "logical decoding" --> SLOT
    SLOT -- "replication protocol<br/>one long-lived connection" --> BG
    BG --> DEC
    DEC -- "Remove / Set key" --> HC
    HC --- REDIS
    R --> HC
    DEC -- "SetReplicationStatus lsn" --> SLOT`},{id:`transaction-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One transaction, end to end`,caption:`The pgoutput rhythm: Begin, Relation, DML, Commit — apply on Commit, then acknowledge the LSN so the slot can advance.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as Customers API
    participant PG as Postgres WAL
    participant Svc as WAL Streaming Service
    participant Cache as HybridCache + Redis

    App->>PG: BEGIN / UPDATE customers / COMMIT
    PG->>Svc: Begin (xid)
    PG->>Svc: Relation customers
    PG->>Svc: Update old row + new row
    PG->>Svc: Commit lsn 0/1A2B3C
    Svc->>Cache: Remove customers:42
    Svc->>PG: SetReplicationStatus lsn 0/1A2B3C
    Note over Svc,Cache: next read repopulates from Postgres
    Note over PG,Svc: consumer restart replays from last acked LSN`}],takeaways:[`Bake -c wal_level=logical into your Postgres container args on day one — it's a restart-level setting, and retrofitting it during an incident is a self-inflicted outage.`,`Alert when pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), confirmed_flush_lsn) passes ~1 GB on any slot, and set max_slot_wal_keep_size (e.g. 10 GB) so a dead consumer can't fill the disk.`,`Call SetReplicationStatus with each Commit's LSN — unacknowledged work replays on reconnect, so make every cache action idempotent (Remove beats compute-then-Set).`,`Treat the replication connection as a singleton: one NpgsqlLogicalReplicationConnection per slot per process, never pooled — a second consumer on the same slot errors out immediately.`,`Expect invalidation latency to drop from TTL-scale (30–300 s) to single-digit milliseconds after commit; keep a short L1 TTL (~30 s) as a backstop for multi-node in-memory caches.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Stream Postgres WAL Changes Into .NET with Npgsql (Logical Replication & CDC)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4XJe6aGUlw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`change-data-capture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-KWaShWxJzxQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:20,title:`I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:25`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Redis`,`Dapper`,`Docker`,`Cache`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`Release day, 11:40 a.m.: our cache started evicting the exact traffic it existed to absorb`,paragraphs:[`The marketing email fired at 11:30, traffic hit 6× normal by 11:40, and Redis memory climbed to 97%. Allkeys-LRU evictions spiked — and every evicted key became a synchronous read against Postgres, the origin database we were caching in the first place. Hit ratio fell from 92% to 61%, Postgres CPU pinned at 100%, and checkout p99 went from 180 ms to 2.4 s. We were paying for two stateful systems, and the expensive one was amplifying load on the cheap one.`,`In the retro, someone asked the uncomfortable question: the cache is disposable, the data already lives in Postgres — so why do we run a second database, its dashboards, its failover runbook, and its pager? That afternoon I spiked a prototype: one UNLOGGED table with the key as primary key, the value as JSONB, an expires_at column, and a pg_cron job sweeping expired entries every 30 seconds. Two endpoints, one migration, no new infrastructure.`,`Benchmarks on the same hardware told the honest story: cached reads at ~1 ms p99 versus Redis's ~0.2 ms — slower, but comfortably inside our 50 ms budget, and the hit-ratio math said we'd shed ~85% of origin reads either way. We didn't rip Redis out that week. But we stopped treating it as load-bearing infrastructure.`],problem:`Under load, LRU evictions turned the cache from a Postgres shield into a Postgres amplifier — every evicted key triggered a synchronous origin read at the exact moment Postgres could least afford it.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`JSONB`,`pg_cron`,`UNLOGGED tables`,`Npgsql`,`Dapper`,`.NET Aspire`],note:`One Postgres instance plays both roles: an UNLOGGED key/JSONB table as the cache sitting beside the source-of-truth tables, with pg_cron running the eviction function — wired up through Npgsql and Dapper, observed in the Aspire dashboard.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why are we running two databases?`,body:[`The first thing I had to admit on the whiteboard: a cache is the one datastore you're allowed to lose. Redis gives us sub-millisecond reads, but it also gives us a second stateful system — persistence config, a replica, memory ceilings, fragmentation, its own upgrade cadence. For a team of five, that's a real tax, and Tuesday proved the tax gets collected at the worst possible time.`,`So why not just add RAM to Redis? Because the bill scales with cache size, not with value delivered — and every ops hour babysitting a cache is an hour not spent on the product. Then there was a sneaky second benefit I only saw later: if the cache lives in Postgres, I can query it. Hit ratios, hot keys, value-size histograms — plain SQL against JSONB, no new observability stack.`,`I took the counterargument seriously: Redis wins on raw latency and ops/sec, and if we ever need pub/sub or distributed locks, Postgres-as-cache doesn't cover it. So the honest framing wasn't 'Redis is bad' — it was 'our workload is read-through, rebuildable, and already co-located with Postgres.' The cheapest cache is the one that shares a heartbeat with the source of truth.`],quote:`A cache is the only database you're allowed to lose — stop paying production prices to keep it alive.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Redis cluster`,`~0.2 ms reads, native TTL`,`Second stateful system, ops tax`,`Very high QPS, shared infra`],[`Postgres UNLOGGED table`,`One system, SQL analytics, transactional`,`~1 ms reads, DIY TTL`,`Read-through, co-located`],[`In-process MemoryCache`,`Zero network hops`,`No shared eviction, stale per pod`,`Single-instance apps only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KWaShWxJzxQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Modeling the cache as just another table`,body:[`The schema was almost embarrassingly simple: key TEXT as the primary key, value JSONB, expires_at timestamptz, hits integer. The write path is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE — invalidation-by-write falls out for free. Explicit invalidation is a DELETE on the key. Two statements, done.`,`The decision worth arguing about is JSONB versus raw bytea. bytea is smaller and faster if you never look inside the value — but then the cache is a black box. JSONB costs a few percent on serialization and gives me GIN indexes and SQL over cache contents, which is exactly how I discovered that 3% of keys held 60% of our bytes. That's a debugging superpower, not a nicety.`,`And the UNLOGGED keyword: it skips WAL for this table, roughly doubling write throughput, and the trade is that the table gets truncated after a crash. For a cache, that's not a bug — it's a feature. A cold cache rebuilds itself in minutes from the source tables sitting right there in the same database. I would never do this to the source tables, obviously; that's the whole point of keeping them separate.`],quote:`If the cache lives next to your data, cache observability is just... SQL.`,table:{headers:[`Value format`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`JSONB`,`Queryable, GIN-indexable, analytics`,`Slightly larger, parse cost`,`Default choice`],[`bytea`,`Smallest, fastest`,`Opaque blob, zero introspection`,`Pure speed, huge values`],[`text (serialized JSON)`,`Human-readable in psql`,`No indexing inside the doc`,`Debug environments`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KWaShWxJzxQ`,at:`0:14`}},{heading:`03 · Eviction and expiry: building the TTL Postgres never gave you`,body:[`Here's the part Postgres genuinely doesn't hand you: per-key TTL. Redis gives you EXPIRE; Postgres gives you a column and expects you to mean it. So expiry is two layers — lazy on read, where every GET filters on expires_at > now() and treats a stale row as a miss, and active, where a pg_cron job sweeps expired rows on a schedule.`,`Eviction is the same story. When the table crosses a row budget, a scheduled function deletes the lowest-hits entries first — a poor man's LFU — and every delete is batched through a ctid subquery capped at a few thousand rows, because Postgres has no DELETE ... LIMIT. Why not a trigger? Because triggers run on the hot write path, and I refuse to tax every SET for cleanup that can run thirty seconds later. Why not sweep every second? Because each sweep is a scan-plus-delete and vacuum has to keep up — 30 to 60 seconds is the sweet spot where bloat stays invisible.`,`The honest caveat: this is more moving SQL than redis-cli. But it's SQL I can read, test, and EXPLAIN — and it runs inside the same backup, monitoring, and failover story as everything else we already own.`],quote:`Postgres won't give you a TTL — it gives you a scheduler, and the scheduler was the hard part anyway.`,table:{headers:[`Eviction strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`TTL sweep (pg_cron)`,`Simple, predictable`,`Doesn't react to size spikes`,`Default`],[`LFU top-off`,`Protects hot keys under pressure`,`Needs hits counter, biased to history`,`Size-capped caches`],[`LRU by last_access`,`Classic semantics`,`Extra write on every read`,`Mostly-read workloads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KWaShWxJzxQ`,at:`3:27`}},{heading:`04 · The benchmark reality check`,body:[`Numbers time, because vibes don't survive production. On identical hardware, Redis GET landed around 0.2 ms p99; the Postgres cache path — pooled connection, primary-key lookup on the UNLOGGED table — sat at roughly 1 ms. Throughput per connection is bounded by round-trip: at ~1 ms per op, one pooled connection tops out near a thousand ops per second, so the pool size, not Postgres, became my ceiling.`,`What surprised me: the Postgres version let me write the source row and its cache entry in one transaction — cache coherence for free, something Redis can't do without extra machinery. The JSONB side paid off immediately too: one query for hit ratio, one for the top-20 heaviest keys. I wired the migration into the startup path via NpgsqlDataSource, got the GET/POST endpoints right — SingleOrDefaultAsync semantics, NotFound on a miss or unserializable value — and watched every statement execute in the Aspire dashboard before hammering it from Postman.`,`So when does Redis still win? Sustained six-figure ops/sec, hard sub-millisecond p99, pub/sub, or multi-region TTL semantics — take Redis, it's excellent at being Redis. But for a read-through cache serving a co-located Postgres at a few thousand reads per second, the second database was mostly serving our anxiety, not our latency budget.`],quote:`Benchmarks don't pick your cache — your failure modes do.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`Postgres cache`,`Redis`,`Why`],rows:[[`p99 budget ≥ 5 ms`,`Fine`,`Fine`,`~1 ms fits with headroom`],[`> 50k ops/s sustained`,`No`,`Yes`,`~1k ops/s per pooled connection`],[`Cache + source atomicity`,`Yes`,`Hard`,`Same transaction, same DB`],[`Cache analytics needed`,`Yes`,`Extra tooling`,`SQL over JSONB`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KWaShWxJzxQ`,at:`7:46`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One Postgres, two roles: cache and source of truth`,caption:`The API reads and writes the UNLOGGED cache table in the same instance as the source tables, while pg_cron sweeps expiry and tops off the size budget.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    CLI["Client"] --> API[".NET minimal API"]
    subgraph PG["Postgres — single instance"]
        CACHE[("cache_entries<br/>UNLOGGED · key PK · JSONB")]
        SRC[("source tables<br/>source of truth")]
        CRON["pg_cron scheduler"]
        EVICT["evict_expired()<br/>batched DELETE"]
    end
    API -->|"1 · GET key"| CACHE
    API -->|"2 · miss → read"| SRC
    API -->|"3 · upsert<br/>ON CONFLICT (key)"| CACHE
    CRON -->|"every 30–60 s"| EVICT
    EVICT -->|"DELETE ≤ 5k rows"| CACHE
    CACHE -.->|"JSONB analytics"| OPS["Ad-hoc SQL dashboards"]`},{id:`readpath`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Read-through path with lazy expiry and cron eviction`,caption:`A GET is one primary-key lookup; a miss falls through to the source tables and backfills via upsert, while the scheduled job quietly reaps expired and coldest entries.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as API
    participant C as cache_entries
    participant S as source tables
    participant J as pg_cron job
    A->>C: SELECT value, expires_at WHERE key = $1
    alt hit and expires_at > now()
        C-->>A: JSONB value (HIT)
    else miss or expired
        C-->>A: 0 rows (MISS)
        A->>S: SELECT fresh row WHERE id = $1
        S-->>A: row
        A->>C: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE value, expires_at
    end
    J->>C: every 30–60 s: DELETE expired rows (ctid batch ≤ 5k)
    J->>C: over row budget? DELETE lowest hits (LFU top-off)`}],takeaways:[`Keep cache values under ~2 KB — larger JSONB payloads get TOASTed out of line and every hit pays an extra read.`,`Declare the cache table UNLOGGED: skipping WAL roughly doubles write throughput, and losing warm entries on a crash is a feature, not a bug.`,`Budget ~1 ms p99 per Postgres cache op versus ~0.2 ms for Redis — the trade only pays off above an ~85% hit ratio.`,`One pooled connection tops out near ~1,000 ops/s at 1 ms per op — size Max Pool Size to your cache QPS or you'll starve at burst.`,`Sweep with pg_cron every 30–60 s and batch deletes to ≤5,000 rows via a ctid subquery, so vacuum and locks never stall the read path.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I replaced my Redis cache with Postgres... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KWaShWxJzxQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-b-a7CQ_I7JI`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:21,title:`Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:13`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Marten`],story:{headline:`The SKUs That Refused to Die`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:40 on a Tuesday, two hours into our catalog release, when the merchandising lead walked over with her laptop and a look I've learned to fear. 'We deleted these forty products this morning,' she said. 'They're back on the storefront.' No crash, no slow query, no error log. Something worse: data that was supposed to be gone, calmly reappearing with yesterday's prices.`,`The trail led to our pricing service. It replayed a queue of price adjustments on a timer, and its PUT handler called session.Store(product) — Marten's upsert. Store doesn't care that the document was deleted at 9 AM; if it's not there, it inserts it. So every replayed message for a discontinued SKU resurrected it, JSON and all, and the storefront cache happily picked it up. Our 'harmless' convenience verb had turned the queue into a necromancer.`,`The fix was one word: Store became Update, plus a null check on the Product? that LoadAsync hands back. Update refuses to touch a document that doesn't exist — it throws, we catch, we skip and alert. Forty lines of handler code, one semantic difference, and the difference between 'upsert' and 'update' turned out to be the difference between a quiet Tuesday and an incident review.`],problem:`When your write API offers two verbs that look interchangeable — Store (upsert) and Update (explicit) — which one does your business rule actually mean, and what does the null from LoadAsync owe you?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`Marten`,`IDocumentSession`,`MediatR`,`Minimal APIs`,`Clean Architecture`],note:`Marten turns Postgres into a document database over JSONB; IDocumentSession is the per-request unit of work, and MediatR carries commands from Minimal API endpoints into feature-folder handlers.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the session is a unit of work, not a repo`,body:[`The first thing I want you to internalize is that IDocumentSession is not a repository per entity — it's one unit of work for the whole request. You inject it once into the handler, call LoadAsync to pull the document into the session's identity map, mutate the plain C# object, and SaveChangesAsync flushes everything in a single commit. There's no AddOrUpdate gymnastics, no change-tracker ceremony like EF.`,`That shape is why clean architecture and Marten get along so well. A feature folder like UpdateProduct holds the command, the handler, and the validator, and the handler owns the entire flow — load, guard, mutate, save, return. You don't need a ProductRepository abstraction over Marten; the session already is that abstraction, and wrapping it just adds a layer that translates Marten into Marten.`],quote:`One session, one request, one commit — everything else is decoration.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: Store is an upsert, Update is a promise`,body:[`Now the decision that burned us. session.Store(product) is an upsert: if the document isn't in the database, Marten inserts it; if it is, it updates. Fantastic for imports and idempotent sync jobs. Catastrophic for a command endpoint, because 'not in the database' can mean 'someone deleted it on purpose this morning' — and Store will happily put it back.`,`session.Update(product) is the opposite contract: it tells Marten this document must already exist, and if it doesn't, SaveChangesAsync throws instead of silently inserting. Why not Store plus a manual existence check? Because between your check and your write there's a race window — another request can delete the row in between. Update pushes the existence assertion into the same round trip as the write, which is exactly where it belongs.`,`And since we already called LoadAsync at the top of the handler, the null check catches the common case up front; Update is the belt-and-braces that closes the tiny race between load and save. Two verbs, two intents — pick the one that matches your business rule, not the one that throws fewer exceptions.`],quote:`Upserts are optimistic about history. Commands shouldn't be.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`session.Store(doc)`,`One call, insert-or-update, great for bulk imports`,`Silently resurrects deleted docs; hides intent`,`ETL / idempotent sync`],[`session.Update(doc)`,`Fails loudly if doc is gone; intent is explicit`,`Throws on SaveChangesAsync when missing — must handle it`,`Command endpoints (PUT/PATCH)`],[`Load → null-check → Update`,`Full guard: read, verify, mutate, commit`,`Two round trips; tiny race without concurrency tokens`,`When you need the document's current state`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,at:`1:17`}},{heading:`03 · The null that tells the truth`,body:[`Here's the detail I make everyone write explicitly: Product product = await session.LoadAsync<Product>(id, cancellationToken). I refuse to use var here, because the type is Product? — nullable — and I want every reviewer's eye to catch it. LoadAsync returns null when there's no document with that ID, and the compiler nagging you about it is the database asking a question.`,`Our answer is a failure result, not an exception: return Result.Failure with a ProductNotFound error code and the ID from the route baked into the message. Why not throw? Because 'this product doesn't exist' is a domain outcome, not a system fault — the endpoint maps it to a 404-shaped response, and the caller gets something actionable instead of a 500 and a stack trace.`,`And definitely don't 'fix' the null by inserting a default product. That's Store-thinking leaking into a command. If the caller asked to update a product that isn't there, the honest answer is 'not found,' not 'here, I made one up.'`],quote:`A nullable return is the database asking you a question. Answer it in the handler.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,at:`2:08`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it thin: command, handler, endpoint`,body:[`The plumbing is deliberately boring. UpdateProductCommand is a positional record — Id, Name, Price, Tags — implementing ICommand so MediatR can route it. UpdateProductCommandHandler implements ICommandHandler<UpdateProductCommand, Result>, takes IDocumentSession through the constructor, and does the five-step dance: load, guard, mutate, SaveChangesAsync, return.`,`The Minimal API endpoint stays a translator: app.MapPut("/products/{id}") binds the route ID and the [FromBody] UpdateProductRequest, sends the command through ISender, and maps the result to a status code. Notice the request record is separate from the command — your HTTP contract can drift from your application contract without dragging each other along.`,`Why feature folders instead of controller-service-repository lasagna? Because everything about updating a product lives in one place you can read top to bottom. When the incident review happens at 11:40, you open one folder, not five layers.`],quote:`The endpoint is a translator, not a decision-maker.`,table:{headers:[`Layer`,`Owns`,`Must not own`],rows:[[`Minimal API endpoint`,`Routing, binding, status codes`,`Session logic, business rules`],[`Command handler`,`Load, guard, mutate, SaveChangesAsync`,`HTTP concepts, JSON shapes`],[`Domain (Product)`,`Invariants on Name, Price, Tags`,`Persistence concerns`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,at:`5:09`}},{heading:`05 · What we deliberately didn't build`,body:[`Full disclosure on the shortcuts. There's no input validation in this handler — Name, Price, and Tags are assigned directly. In a real system those invariants belong on the Product itself or in a validator before the handler runs; I left it out so the session semantics stay the star of the show.`,`There's also no optimistic concurrency yet. Two simultaneous PUTs to the same product are last-write-wins unless you opt into Marten's version-guided concurrency — worth adding the moment two users can edit the same SKU. And one small habit that pays: SaveChangesAsync accepts a CancellationToken, and so does LoadAsync. Pass them both, or an abandoned request still burns a Postgres round trip after the client is gone.`,`Every one of these gaps is a conscious trade-off, not an oversight. The point of the exercise is that the session API made each gap visible — you can see exactly what you're choosing not to handle.`],quote:`Every feature you don't write is a feature you don't debug at 11:40 on a Tuesday.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,at:`8:11`}}],diagrams:[{id:`update-product-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`PUT /products/{id} — request path through the layers`,caption:`From Minimal API binding to a single SaveChangesAsync commit, with the null guard as the fork in the road.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["PUT /products/{id}"] --> B["Minimal API binds route id + body"]
    B --> C["ISender.Send(UpdateProductCommand)"]
    C --> D["UpdateProductCommandHandler"]
    D --> E["session.LoadAsync<Product>(id, ct)"]
    E --> F{"Product is null?"}
    F -->|yes| G["Result.Failure(ProductNotFound)"]
    F -->|no| H["Mutate Name, Price, Tags"]
    H --> I["session.Update(product)"]
    I --> J["session.SaveChangesAsync(ct)"]
    J --> K["Result.Success"]
    G --> L["HTTP 404-shaped response"]
    K --> M["HTTP 200"]`},{id:`upsert-resurrection-race`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Store vs Update — the resurrection race`,caption:`The same replayed message, two verbs: Store upserts a deleted SKU back into existence, Update refuses and fails loudly.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Q as Price Queue
    participant H as Pricing Handler
    participant S as IDocumentSession
    participant P as Postgres JSONB
    Q->>H: Replay price event for SKU-4171
    H->>S: LoadAsync(id)
    S->>P: SELECT data FROM mt_doc_product
    P-->>S: null — deleted at 09:00
    Note over H,S: Old code: session.Store() = upsert
    H->>S: Store(product)
    S->>P: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE
    P-->>S: OK — deleted SKU resurrected
    Note over H,P: New code: session.Update() throws on SaveChangesAsync → skip + alert`}],takeaways:[`Pick session.Update() over session.Store() for any command endpoint — Store is an upsert and will resurrect deleted documents; ours came back from the dead 40 minutes into a release.`,`Treat every LoadAsync<T> result as T?: one explicit null check per handler converts a potential 500 into a clean failure result with a ProductNotFound code.`,`One IDocumentSession per request and one SaveChangesAsync per handler — two commits in a single request means two round trips and a half-finished state.`,`Keep handlers to the five-step shape — load, guard, mutate, save, return — and push anything that pushes past ~30 lines down into the domain.`,`Thread the CancellationToken into both LoadAsync and SaveChangesAsync every time; an abandoned request should cost exactly zero Postgres work.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Marten - Powerful Document Database With PostgreSQL And Clean Architecture`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b-a7CQ_I7JI`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-6FnsTZOeyng`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:22,title:`I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:31`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Release-Day Traffic, a Melting Catalog Endpoint, and the Cache We Already Owned`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday at noon, not some heroic midnight page. Marketing fired the launch email early, the catalog endpoint started doing the same dozen lookups per page render, and the Postgres dashboard went from green to amber in about eleven minutes. CPU climbed, p95 stretched, and the fix everyone reached for was the reflex: 'put a cache in front of it.'`,`The reflex has a name, and the name is Redis. But 'just add Redis' meant a new stateful service, a failover runbook, a security review, a network hop, and an invoice line finance would circle in red — all bolted on during a live launch. While the thread argued about Redis node sizing, someone dropped a link into the channel: Microsoft had shipped Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Postgres, a distributed cache implementation that runs on the Postgres instance we already operate.`,`Half the team laughed — 'Postgres as a cache, really?' We spiked it on staging that same afternoon. First boot created a schema and an unlogged cache table; every read turned out to be a SELECT plus an expiration-touching UPDATE; a DELETE sweep ran quietly in the background. It worked. And the details of how it worked turned out to be the actual lesson.`],problem:`Can the database we already run double as a distributed cache — and what does Microsoft's official implementation actually trade away (WAL, durability, built-in TTL) to make that work?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`Unlogged Tables`,`IDistributedCache`,`k6`],note:`Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Postgres implements IDistributedCache on top of a Postgres unlogged table, trading crash durability for WAL-free write speed; k6 scripts measure the insert and query paths.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just add Redis' isn't free`,body:[`So my first instinct — probably yours too — is: a cache means Redis. It's the default answer, and for raw speed it's the right one. But look at what 'just add Redis' actually costs a team like ours: a new stateful service to deploy, a failover story to write, a security review, a network hop, and a line on the invoice that finance will circle in red.`,`And here's the thing — we already run Postgres. We have its dashboards, its backup jobs, its failover runbook memorized. Postgres is my favorite database precisely because it keeps picking up new jobs: relational data, JSON, vectors through pgvector, and now caching. The real question was never 'which cache is fastest?' It was 'which cache can this team operate well on a Tuesday at noon during a launch?'`,`Why not just IMemoryCache? Because we run more than one instance, and per-process memory means every instance warms its own cold cache and they quietly disagree with each other. The IDistributedCache abstraction exists exactly for this gap — and Microsoft has now filled it with a Postgres implementation.`],quote:`The fastest cache is the one your team already knows how to operate at 2 p.m. on release day.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Redis`,`Sub-ms reads, native TTL`,`New infra, new failure mode, new bill`,`Very high traffic, dedicated platform team`],[`IMemoryCache`,`Zero network hop`,`Per-instance, caches disagree across pods`,`Single-instance apps`],[`Postgres unlogged`,`No new infra, ops playbook known`,`Crash wipes table, no built-in TTL`,`Teams already running Postgres`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The unlogged table trick: skipping the WAL`,body:[`Here's the trick that makes Postgres interesting for caching at all: unlogged tables. Normally every write to Postgres goes through the write-ahead log first — that's what makes it crash-safe and replicable. An unlogged table skips the WAL entirely, so inserts are dramatically faster.`,`The trade is durability. If Postgres crashes, the unlogged table comes back empty — it's truncated on recovery. For your orders table that would be a catastrophe; for a cache it's just a cold start, a few minutes of slower reads while it warms up. One more caveat worth knowing: unlogged tables aren't shipped to streaming replicas, so never point a read replica at one and expect the data to be there.`,`The library exposes all of this as a single flag — useWriteAheadLog. Set it false and the table is created as UNLOGGED; set it true and you've bought durability a cache doesn't need, paying a WAL fsync on every single cache write for nothing.`],quote:`A cache that survives the crash is a cache you paid for twice.`,table:{headers:[`Table type`,`Write path`,`After crash`,`Replicated`],rows:[[`Logged`,`WAL + table`,`Data intact`,`Yes`],[`Unlogged`,`Table only, no WAL`,`Truncated to empty`,`No`],[`Temp`,`Per-session`,`Gone at session end`,`No`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,at:`1:37`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring it up: what first boot actually creates`,body:[`Wiring it up is pleasantly boring, which is a compliment. Install Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Postgres from NuGet — 1.2.0 was the latest stable when I recorded this — drop the connection settings into appsettings.json, and register it as your IDistributedCache.`,`The configuration surface is four decisions: the schema name, the table name, whether the library is allowed to create the table for you, and whether to use the write-ahead log. On first boot you can watch it work in the logs: it connects, runs CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS — public is already there on any default instance — then creates the cache table as UNLOGGED because we set the WAL flag to false.`,`One habit I'd recommend: give the cache its own schema, not public. When you want to resize it, vacuum it, or drop and recreate it after a bad deploy, you get a clean blast radius instead of poking at shared objects.`],quote:`Configuration is just deciding, up front, what the library is allowed to create on your behalf.`,table:{headers:[`Setting`,`Decision it makes`],rows:[[`Schema name`,`Where the cache lives`],[`Table name`,`What the cache is called`],[`Create table`,`Does the library run DDL on boot?`],[`Use WAL`,`UNLOGGED speed vs crash durability`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,at:`5:55`}},{heading:`04 · Expiration: the tax a relational cache pays`,body:[`Now the part nobody puts on the slide: expiration. Redis gives you a per-key TTL for free, at the server. Postgres has no built-in per-row expiry — a relational database will happily keep an 'expired' row forever until something deletes it.`,`So the library compensates with two mechanisms. Every read is a SELECT of the value plus an UPDATE that pushes the expiration time forward — effectively a sliding-expiration touch. And a DELETE statement runs in the background to purge expired rows; from watching the queries, it looks like roughly every 30 minutes, and there's a setting controlling that interval.`,`Is that update-on-read some unnecessary work? Honestly, yes — it exists to compensate for the missing TTL primitive. But it's the price of cache semantics on a relational engine, and it changes your capacity math: every cache hit is two statements, so your write IOPS scale with your read QPS. Do that math before you flip it on in production, not after.`],quote:`Every cache hit in Postgres is a read and a write — budget the IOPS before launch.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`How it works`,`Cost per hit`],rows:[[`Redis TTL`,`Server-side per-key expiry`,`Near zero`],[`Touch-on-read + sweep`,`UPDATE expiry on hit; periodic DELETE`,`2 statements`],[`App-side filtering`,`Expiry column filtered in WHERE`,`1 statement, but stale rows linger on disk`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,at:`7:20`}},{heading:`05 · The numbers: k6 on the insert and query paths`,body:[`So does it perform? I wrote two k6 scripts — one hammering inserts, one hammering queries — and ran the insert test twice for good measure. The read path is exactly what block four predicted: the SELECT comes back with the value, and the expiration UPDATE rides along in the same interaction.`,`I won't pretend this beats Redis on raw p99 — it doesn't, and it was never trying to. What it wins on is topology: the cache lives in the instance you already run, so you deleted a network hop, a deployment, and a failure mode from your diagram. Run the k6 scripts against your own workload and your own instance size — that number is the only one that matters.`],quote:`Benchmarks are other people's arguments; your workload is the only verdict that counts.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,at:`8:31`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Postgres as a distributed cache: topology`,caption:`One Postgres instance plays both roles — the unlogged cache table skips the WAL, a background sweep purges expired rows, and a crash resets the cache to empty by design.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph APP["ASP.NET Core App"]
    SVC["Catalog Service"] --> DC["IDistributedCache"]
  end
  subgraph PG["Postgres Instance"]
    TBL["cache table - UNLOGGED"]
    SWEEP["DELETE sweep ~every 30 min"]
    REC["Crash recovery"]
  end
  WAL["WAL"]
  DC -->|"cache miss: INSERT"| TBL
  DC -->|"cache hit: SELECT + UPDATE expiry"| TBL
  SWEEP -->|"purge expired rows"| TBL
  TBL -.->|"cache writes skip"| WAL
  REC -.->|"truncates unlogged table"| TBL`},{id:`readpath`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a cache hit and miss`,caption:`Every hit is a SELECT plus an expiry-touching UPDATE — the sliding-expiration tax that compensates for Postgres having no built-in per-row TTL.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant App as Application
  participant Lib as Caching.Postgres
  participant PG as Postgres
  App->>Lib: GetAsync(key)
  Lib->>PG: SELECT value, expires_at WHERE key = @key
  alt cache hit
    PG-->>Lib: row with value
    Lib->>PG: UPDATE expires_at - sliding touch
    PG-->>Lib: ack
    Lib-->>App: deserialized value
  else cache miss
    PG-->>Lib: no rows
    Lib-->>App: null
    App->>Lib: SetAsync(key, value, ttl)
    Lib->>PG: INSERT key, value, expires_at
  end
  Note over Lib,PG: background DELETE sweep purges expired rows every ~30 min`}],takeaways:[`Set useWriteAheadLog = false so the cache table is created UNLOGGED — you skip the WAL on every write and accept a 100% cache wipe on crash, which is the correct trade for volatile data.`,`Budget every cache hit as 2 statements — a SELECT plus the expiry-touch UPDATE — so 5,000 reads/sec means roughly 10,000 statements/sec against your Postgres.`,`The expired-row DELETE sweep runs roughly every 30 minutes (the interval is configurable) — size the cache table for up to one full sweep interval of dead rows.`,`Unlogged tables are not replicated to streaming replicas and are truncated on crash recovery — keep the cache in its own schema so drop/recreate never touches real data.`,`Pin the NuGet version (1.2.0 stable at recording time) and validate insert + query paths with k6 scripts on your own instance before trusting any benchmark — including this one.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I'm Not Giving Up On Postgres as Cache (and neither is Microsoft)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FnsTZOeyng`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-U_MxJ0IcJXU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:23,title:`Advanced Caching Strategies: L1/L2 Synchronization with .NET + Redis`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:59`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`.NET`,`Cache`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`Load-balancer roulette: the $89 price that wouldn't die`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:42 on a Tuesday, forty minutes before the lunch rush, when merch pushes a price correction: the flagship bundle should be $49, not $89. The admin panel says saved. The database says $49. Redis says $49. Within ten minutes, support has six tickets — some customers see $49, some see $89, and the same customer can flip between the two by refreshing the page.`,`The dashboards look clean: five API replicas behind a load balancer, each running HybridCache with an in-process L1 over a shared Redis L2. The write endpoint removed the key from Redis and from its own local memory — on the one replica that happened to handle the request. The other four never got the memo. Their L1 caches, warm and confident, kept serving the old price until their five-minute TTL expired.`,`The fix took an afternoon: a Redis pub/sub channel as an invalidation backplane, so every replica hears about every changed key and drops its local copy. And then a second, quieter bug surfaced — the broadcast made every node, including the origin, remove the key from L2 twice. Two cache tiers, one channel, and a surprising number of ways to be subtly wrong.`],problem:`In any multi-replica deployment, the hard part isn't caching — it's invalidating the level-one in-memory cache on every node, not just the one that happened to handle the write.`},tech:{stack:[`HybridCache`,`IMemoryCache`,`Redis L2`,`StackExchange.Redis`,`Pub/Sub backplane`,`.NET Aspire`],note:`HybridCache layers a per-process IMemoryCache (L1) over a shared Redis L2 via StackExchange.Redis; since HybridCache doesn't ship cross-node invalidation yet, a Redis pub/sub channel acts as the backplane, and .NET Aspire spins up Redis plus five API replicas to prove it works.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why two levels when Redis already works?`,body:[`Let's start where the talk starts: ninety percent of what we build is CRUD. An app server, a database, and reads that get expensive because of joins, row width, or raw traffic. So we put a cache in front. The question I want to poke at is: why two layers? Redis alone is already fast, right?`,`Here's the honest math. A Redis GET is a network hop — call it half a millisecond to a millisecond round trip, plus serialization on both ends. At ten thousand requests a second, that's real CPU, real bandwidth, real money. An in-process IMemoryCache hit is nanoseconds. Three orders of magnitude. That gap is the entire reason L1 exists.`,`So HybridCache's model is: L1 is a small, per-process memory cache; L2 is shared Redis. The read path walks L1, then L2, then the database, filling as it goes. The price you pay is that you now hold N copies of the truth — one per replica. That's the debt we spend the rest of this session paying down.`],quote:`Every cache tier you add is another copy of the truth you now have to keep honest.`,table:{headers:[`Property`,`L1 · IMemoryCache`,`L2 · Redis`],rows:[[`Hit latency`,`~100ns–1µs, in-process`,`~0.5–1ms, network hop`],[`Scope`,`Per replica — N copies`,`One copy, whole cluster`],[`Capacity`,`Bounded by pod RAM`,`Gigabytes, shared`],[`Survives deploy?`,`No — restart wipes it`,`Yes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Advanced Caching Strategies: L1/L2 Synchronization with .NET + Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U_MxJ0IcJXU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: invalidation is the actual problem`,body:[`Now the write path. A delete comes in, we remove the key from Redis, we remove it from local memory. On a single node, we're done. But we run five replicas behind a load balancer — and the other four still hold that key in L1, confidently serving it until their TTL expires. That's the support ticket from the story, in one sentence.`,`Why not just crank TTLs down to five seconds? You can, and for low-stakes data you should. But hot keys then get hammered with misses every five seconds, and you still serve stale inside the window. Why not version-stamped keys — product:42:v7? Elegant, no stale reads ever, but every reader needs the current version number, which is either another lookup or... another invalidation problem. We've just moved the target.`,`The pragmatic middle ground: publish the key on a Redis channel when it changes, and have every replica subscribe and drop its local copy. Near-instant, a few dozen lines. But be honest about the semantics: pub/sub is fire-and-forget. A replica that's mid-deploy or reconnecting misses the message, and nobody resends it. The backplane is the fast path; the TTL is the floor.`],quote:`The backplane makes invalidation fast; the TTL makes it inevitable.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`TTL only`,`Zero extra infra`,`Stale for full TTL; miss storms on hot keys`,`Low-stakes, tolerant data`],[`Pub/sub backplane`,`Near-instant, ~50 LOC`,`At-most-once; missed on restart`,`Most CRUD read paths`],[`Version-stamped keys`,`No stale reads`,`Version lookup per read`,`Strict freshness needs`],[`Skip L1 entirely`,`Trivially consistent`,`Pays the network hop every read`,`Almost never worth it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Advanced Caching Strategies: L1/L2 Synchronization with .NET + Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U_MxJ0IcJXU`,at:`4:01`}},{heading:`03 · Rolling the backplane: one channel, one key, one subscription`,body:[`So how do we build it? HybridCache, as of this recording, doesn't ship cross-node invalidation — so we roll a minimal backplane ourselves. Register the ConnectionMultiplexer as a singleton, spin up a background service, and in ExecuteAsync subscribe to a channel — call it cache-invalidation.`,`The callback hands you the channel and the key. That key is the entire payload — no data, just 'this thing changed, drop it.' The publisher side is equally boring: the delete endpoint does its normal L2 removal, then PUBLISHes the key. Five lines on each side, and every replica in the fleet hears the event.`,`Why not Redis keyspace notifications instead? They fire on changes inside Redis, which sounds perfect — but they couple your invalidation to whatever happens to live in L2, and they don't cover local-only decisions. A channel you own is easier to reason about, and you control the contract. Keep the message a hint, not a payload.`],quote:`A backplane is one channel, one key, and the humility to know some nodes won't hear it.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Advanced Caching Strategies: L1/L2 Synchronization with .NET + Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U_MxJ0IcJXU`,at:`6:25`}},{heading:`04 · The double-remove: when your own echo costs you`,body:[`Here's the subtle one I only caught in review. The delete endpoint removes from L2 and publishes. Every subscriber — including the node that published — receives the message and calls HybridCache.RemoveAsync. Which removes from L2. Again. Same key, redundant round trip, multiplied by every replica in the fleet.`,`The fix is to split the responsibilities. The origin's explicit call handles L2 exactly once. The broadcast handler should only care about local state — so inject IMemoryCache into the background service and call memoryCache.Remove(key). One network DEL, N local evictions. That's the shape you want.`,`And yes, the origin node processes its own echo. That's fine — removing an already-removed key is a no-op — as long as the handler is cheap. Design the subscriber so the echo costs you nothing, because you will always hear it.`],quote:`The origin always hears its own echo — make sure the echo is cheap.`},{heading:`05 · Proving it: five replicas and the refresh test`,body:[`Last piece: proof. In the Aspire app host I add a Redis resource and configure the API with five replicas — WithReplicas(5) — which reproduces the load-balancer roulette from the incident, locally, in minutes.`,`The test reads like the incident report: GET the product on replica B so its L1 is warm, DELETE through replica A, then GET on B again. Before the backplane, B serves the stale value until its TTL burns down. After, it's gone in milliseconds. Then run the ugly case: restart a replica mid-broadcast, confirm it misses the message, and confirm the TTL catches what pub/sub dropped.`,`That second test is the one teams skip, and it's the one that bites. Single-node tests can't catch stale-L1 bugs by definition — the bug lives in the space between nodes.`],quote:`If your invalidation test runs on one node, you haven't tested invalidation.`,table:{headers:[`Test`,`Without backplane`,`With backplane`],rows:[[`GET after DELETE, same replica`,`Fresh immediately`,`Fresh immediately`],[`GET after DELETE, other replica`,`Stale until TTL (up to 5 min)`,`Fresh within ~10ms`],[`Replica restarted mid-broadcast`,`Stale until TTL`,`Stale until TTL — the TTL is the net`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two-tier cache topology with pub/sub backplane`,caption:`Five replicas each hold a private L1; Redis plays double duty as the shared L2 and the invalidation bus.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    C["Client traffic"] --> LB["Load balancer"]
    LB --> R1["Replica 1<br/>L1: IMemoryCache"]
    LB --> R2["Replica 2<br/>L1: IMemoryCache"]
    LB --> R3["Replica N<br/>L1: IMemoryCache"]
    R1 --> L2[("Redis<br/>L2 + pub/sub")]
    R2 --> L2
    R3 --> L2
    L2 --> DB[("Database")]
    L2 -. invalidation channel .-> R1
    L2 -.-> R2
    L2 -.-> R3`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`A delete propagating across the fleet`,caption:`The write path: one L2 removal, one publish, N local evictions — with the TTL catching anything pub/sub drops.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as Replica A (writer)
    participant B as Replica B (stale L1)
    participant R as Redis (L2 + pub/sub)
    participant D as Database
    C->>B: GET /products/42
    B->>R: GET product:42
    R-->>B: miss
    B->>D: SELECT
    D-->>B: row v1
    B->>R: SET product:42
    B->>B: fill L1 (v1)
    B-->>C: 200 - old value
    C->>A: DELETE /products/42
    A->>D: DELETE row
    A->>R: DEL product:42
    A->>R: PUBLISH cache-invalidate product:42
    R-->>B: deliver key
    R-->>A: deliver key (self-echo)
    B->>B: IMemoryCache.Remove(product:42)
    A->>A: IMemoryCache.Remove(product:42)
    C->>B: GET /products/42
    B->>R: GET product:42
    R-->>B: miss
    B->>D: SELECT
    D-->>B: not found
    B-->>C: 404 - correct`}],takeaways:[`An in-process L1 hit costs ~100ns–1µs; a Redis L2 hit costs ~0.5–1ms plus serialization — that three-orders-of-magnitude gap is why L1 exists even when L2 is warm.`,`Cap L1 TTLs at 5–30 seconds: the TTL is your guaranteed staleness bound for any invalidation message a replica misses.`,`Treat Redis pub/sub as at-most-once — a deploying or disconnected replica silently drops the message; the backplane accelerates invalidation, the TTL guarantees it.`,`Publish only the key on the channel, and in the subscriber clear local state with IMemoryCache.Remove — otherwise every replica (including the origin) issues a redundant L2 RemoveAsync per event.`,`Rehearse invalidation on 3–5 replicas (Aspire's WithReplicas makes it one line) and include a restart mid-broadcast — single-node tests cannot catch stale-L1 bugs.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Advanced Caching Strategies: L1/L2 Synchronization with .NET + Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U_MxJ0IcJXU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-MQ96krIOjs4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:24,title:`HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:25`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The midday trailer drop that turned one IMDb ID into 4,000 upstream calls`,paragraphs:[`It's 12:40 on a Tuesday, not some dark 3 AM night — a flagship show's trailer goes viral and the catalog service's movie-detail endpoint becomes the hottest route we own. Every request carries the same IMDb ID for Avatar, and with no caching in place, each one walks the full network path: our API, then a typed HttpClient call to the Open Movie Database, roughly two seconds per request. The Aspire dashboard makes it brutally visible: two spans per request, one for our API and one for the external call — the same external call, thousands of times over.`,`Then the upstream starts answering 429s. We had IMemoryCache on some pods, but a rolling deploy fifteen minutes earlier had wiped every pod's warm cache simultaneously — a synchronized cold start. And because IMemoryCache has zero coalescing, 500 concurrent misses meant 500 identical upstream fetches racing each other. We weren't under attack; we were DDoS-ing a third-party API with our own traffic.`,`The fix wasn't more pods or a bigger rate-limit quota. It was making the second, third, and four-hundredth request for the same key wait on the first one's answer — and storing that answer somewhere a deploy can't erase.`],problem:`The pre-.NET 9 abstractions force a bad trade: IMemoryCache is fast but per-pod and dies on every restart; IDistributedCache survives restarts but is a raw byte[] API with hand-rolled serialization — and neither protects you from a stampede, where concurrent misses for the same key each trigger their own expensive upstream call.`},tech:{stack:[`HybridCache`,`.NET 9`,`IMemoryCache`,`Redis`,`IDistributedCache`,`Typed HttpClient`,`Aspire`,`OpenTelemetry`],note:`HybridCache fronts an in-process IMemoryCache (L1) and a distributed store (L2) — Redis registered as IDistributedCache via Aspire — sitting in front of a typed HttpClient calling the OMDB metadata API, with every hop visible in the Aspire OpenTelemetry dashboard.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the uncached path is a tax on every request`,body:[`Let's start with the baseline, because you need to feel the pain before the API makes sense. I've got a typed HttpClient pointed at OMDB — the Open Movie Database — and an endpoint that fetches a movie by its IMDb ID. The response is fat: title, plot, ratings, the full cast. With an artificial delay standing in for a genuinely expensive call, a single request takes about two seconds. No caching, so every request pays that toll, and the trace shows two spans every single time: our API, then the external call.`,`So why not just sprinkle IMemoryCache on it? Because IMemoryCache is per-pod and per-process. It evaporates on every deploy, every restart, every scale-out event — and it has no idea that five hundred other requests are missing the same key at the same moment. And why not IDistributedCache alone? Better — it survives restarts — but it's a raw byte[] API where you hand-roll JSON serialization, and it still has no stampede protection. The herd just moves from OMDB to Redis.`,`That's the exact gap HybridCache was built to close: one API that gives you both tiers and treats concurrent misses as one logical operation.`],quote:`A cache miss is never one request's problem — it's every concurrent request's problem arriving at once.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Survives restart`,`Stampede-safe`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No cache`,`—`,`No`,`Never`],[`IMemoryCache`,`No`,`No`,`Single pod, dev only`],[`IDistributedCache`,`Yes`,`No`,`Shared state, DIY serialization`],[`HybridCache`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Default choice in .NET 9+`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MQ96krIOjs4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: two TTLs, one key`,body:[`Registration is one line — builder.Services.AddHybridCache() — and the interesting part is the options. HybridCacheEntryOptions exposes two different expirations, and that's the whole design in miniature: Expiration governs the distributed L2 entry, while LocalCacheExpiration governs the in-process L1, which is IMemoryCache under the hood. In the demo I set local to five minutes and the distributed TTL to an hour.`,`Why two knobs? Because your pods don't share L1. When pod A invalidates a key, pods B through F still hold their local copy until it expires — so the local TTL is literally your cross-pod staleness budget. Keep it short relative to L2 and you bound staleness without paying a Redis round trip on every hit.`,`The other thing you get for free is serialization. L2 needs bytes on the wire; HybridCache handles that with System.Text.Json by default, so there's no byte[] ceremony in your code — you GetOrCreate a strongly typed Movie and move on with your day.`],quote:`Your local TTL is the staleness budget you spend so the hot path never leaves the box.`,table:{headers:[`Knob`,`Controls`,`Rule of thumb`],rows:[[`Expiration`,`L2 (Redis) TTL`,`1 h for slow-changing reference data`],[`LocalCacheExpiration`,`L1 (in-process) TTL`,`10–20% of L2 — e.g. 5 min vs 1 h`],[`Cache key`,`Identity`,`movie:{id}, version it on schema change`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MQ96krIOjs4`,at:`4:58`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring Redis as L2 without touching HybridCache`,body:[`Now let's give it a real L2. In the Aspire apphost it's three lines: AddRedis("redis"), WithReference(redis), and WaitFor(redis) so the API doesn't start before the cache is actually accepting connections — a .NET 9-era capability, with community packages backporting it if you're on earlier versions.`,`On the client side, install Aspire.StackExchange.Redis.DistributedCaching and call builder.AddRedisDistributedCache("redis"). That registers an IDistributedCache backed by Redis — and here's the part I love: HybridCache doesn't need to be told anything. It discovers the registered IDistributedCache at startup and starts using it as L2 automatically.`,`And the failure mode is graceful: if you never register an IDistributedCache, HybridCache quietly runs as an L1-only cache. Same API, same stampede protection — you just lose the survives-a-restart property until you add Redis.`],quote:`HybridCache doesn't care where L2 lives — it asks the container for an IDistributedCache and gets out of the way.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MQ96krIOjs4`,at:`10:42`}},{heading:`04 · Stampede protection: the part that pays the bill`,body:[`Here's the demo that sells it. Same endpoint, same movie ID, fired concurrently. The first request takes about two seconds — that's my artificial delay. The rest come back at 200 milliseconds, 900, and 4. Nothing was cached when they arrived — they all missed — so what happened?`,`Stampede protection. HybridCache detects concurrent requests for the same cache key and coalesces them onto a single in-flight factory call. Look at the distributed trace: one span for our API, one Redis touch, and exactly one call to the external movie API. That four-millisecond response is a late waiter that joined an already-completed operation and read the answer from L1.`,`Could you build this yourself? In-process, it's honestly just a concurrent dictionary of in-flight tasks — but cross-pod it becomes a distributed-lock project: SET NX with a TTL, fencing tokens, what happens when the lock holder dies mid-fetch. HybridCache hands you the in-process coalescing for free, and L2 absorbs the residual — worst case, one upstream miss per pod per TTL window.`],quote:`Stampede protection is a concurrent dictionary of in-flight tasks — and it's worth more than any hit-ratio dashboard.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Upstream calls in a herd of N`,`Complexity`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No cache`,`N`,`None`,`Never`],[`IMemoryCache`,`Up to N — all miss`,`Low`,`Dev only`],[`DIY distributed lock`,`~1 + retries`,`High`,`Legacy systems`],[`HybridCache`,`1 per key`,`One line`,`Default in .NET 9+`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MQ96krIOjs4`,at:`16:18`}},{heading:`05 · Where I'd draw the line on Monday`,body:[`So my recommendation: in .NET 9 and later, HybridCache is the default caching surface. One API — GetOrCreateAsync, GetAsync, SetAsync, RemoveAsync — covers the 90% case, plus tag-based invalidation for dropping groups of related keys. One honest caveat from the recording: the package was still pre-release at the time, so pin your version and read the release notes before a major bump.`,`Where wouldn't I use it? Keys with unbounded cardinality — if every request generates a unique key, your cache is just a garbage collector with extra steps. Strongly consistent reads, where a five-minute-old answer is a correctness bug rather than a trade-off. And anything where the 'expensive operation' is a write rather than a read — caches are for read paths, full stop.`],quote:`The best cache API is the one your team never has to think about — it coalesces, serializes, and expires while you ship features.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`HybridCache?`,`Why`],rows:[[`Movie metadata by ID`,`Yes`,`Hot keys, slow upstream, stable identity`],[`Per-user recommendations`,`Yes, short local TTL`,`Staleness bounded by LocalCacheExpiration`],[`Live stock ticker`,`No`,`Unbounded churn, consistency required`],[`Unique key per request`,`No`,`Cache becomes a garbage collector`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`hybridcache-lookup-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`HybridCache L1/L2 lookup path`,caption:`Every read walks L1 first, then L2, and only the true cold miss pays the ~2 s upstream toll — after which both tiers are warmed in one pass.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Client request<br/>GET /movies/{id}"] --> B["API endpoint"]
    B --> C["HybridCache<br/>GetOrCreateAsync"]
    C --> D{"L1 hit?<br/>IMemoryCache"}
    D -->|"yes: ~microseconds"| H["Return payload"]
    D -->|"no"| E{"L2 hit?<br/>Redis"}
    E -->|"yes: ~1 ms"| F["Deserialize<br/>populate L1"]
    F --> H
    E -->|"no"| G["Factory fires once<br/>typed HttpClient -> OMDB ~2 s"]
    G --> S["Write L2 TTL 1 h<br/>+ L1 TTL 5 min"]
    S --> H`},{id:`stampede-coalescing`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Stampede protection: three concurrent misses, one upstream call`,caption:`Requests B and C arrive to a cold cache but coalesce onto A's in-flight factory task — the trace shows exactly one OMDB span, and the next request hits L1 in ~4 ms.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Request A
    participant B as Request B
    participant C as Request C
    participant HC as HybridCache
    participant UP as OMDB API
    A->>HC: GetOrCreateAsync("movie:tt0499549")
    B->>HC: GetOrCreateAsync("movie:tt0499549")
    C->>HC: GetOrCreateAsync("movie:tt0499549")
    HC->>HC: L1 miss + L2 miss
    HC->>UP: 1 factory call - only A executes
    Note over B,C: B and C await the same in-flight task
    UP-->>HC: payload ~2 s
    HC-->>A: 2.1 s
    HC-->>B: 2.1 s coalesced
    HC-->>C: 2.1 s coalesced
    Note over HC,UP: next request hits L1 in ~4 ms - zero upstream calls`}],takeaways:[`Set LocalCacheExpiration to 10–20% of Expiration — e.g., 5 min local vs 1 h distributed — to cap cross-pod staleness without a Redis round trip on every hit.`,`Stampede protection collapses N concurrent misses into exactly 1 upstream call: measured first request ~2.1 s, last coalesced waiter ~4 ms, and exactly one OMDB span in the trace.`,`In any multi-pod deployment, register an IDistributedCache (Redis) — an L1-only cache is wiped by every rolling deploy, and a synchronized cold start is a self-inflicted thundering herd.`,`Budget L2 egress: a 50 KB payload served from Redis at 10k RPS is ~500 MB/s of network — keep the local TTL generous so 90%+ of hits never leave the box.`,`Version keys on schema change (movie:v2:{id}) instead of flushing everything; use RemoveAsync for surgical drops and tags for grouped invalidation.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache - The New Caching Library in .NET 9`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MQ96krIOjs4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-uUWhU8H5uzg`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:25,title:`5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:21`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Flash Sale at Noon: The Day Our Read-Only Dashboard Ate the Connection Pool`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:40 on a Tuesday, not some dramatic midnight hour. Marketing fired the flash-sale email, traffic tripled in four minutes, and the orders dashboard — a page that only ever reads data — started timing out. The weird part? SQL Server looked bored. CPU at 20%, disk idle. Whatever was killing us wasn't happening in the database; it was happening in our application process.`,`A quick dotnet-counters session told the story: Gen2 garbage collections firing every few seconds, allocation graphs dominated by EF materialization, and connection pool wait timeouts stacking up — 'Max pool size was reached.' Every tracked query was holding connections longer while the change tracker dutifully snapshotting thousands of entities nobody was ever going to update. The pool starved because each read was doing write-preparation work.`,`The post-incident audit turned into a greatest-hits list: tracked queries on pure read endpoints, a SELECT * against a 40-column table to render two fields, a dashboard filter with no index behind it, and one Include-heavy query fanning out into cartesian row soup. Every fix on that list is measurable, and that's what this session is — five tips, with BenchmarkDotNet numbers behind each one.`],problem:`Read-only endpoints running tracked, wide, unindexed queries — the change tracker and the network wire did the damage, not the database server.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`BenchmarkDotNet`,`SQL Server`,`AsNoTracking`,`DTO Projections`,`Covering Index`,`Split Query`,`Compiled Queries`],note:`The EF Core query pipeline pieces this session benchmarks: tracking behavior, projection shape, index design, and query splitting — every claim backed by a measurement, not vibes.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does tracking cost anything on a read?`,body:[`Let's start with the one that surprises people most. When EF materializes an entity from a normal query, it doesn't just hand you an object — it registers that entity in the change tracker, stores an identity entry, and snapshots the original values of every property so SaveChanges can diff against them later. That's brilliant when you're about to write. When you're building a read-only dashboard, it's pure overhead: CPU to snapshot, memory to hold, and GC pressure to clean it all up.`,`So I wrote the same query twice — filter on SalesPersonId, ToListAsync — once tracked, once with AsNoTracking. The benchmark result is not subtle: the no-tracking version is significantly faster and allocates a fraction of the memory. Now the obvious pushback: why not slap AsNoTracking on everything? Two reasons. First, tracked queries deduplicate — if one query loads the same entity twice through joins, you get one instance; if you need that behavior without write support, there's AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution as the middle ground. Second, you can't update an entity you never tracked without an Attach dance.`,`The rule I've settled on: tracking is a feature you opt into, not a default you pay for blindly. Read endpoints opt out.`],quote:`The change tracker is a write-buffer in disguise — if you're not writing, you're paying rent on an empty room.`,table:{headers:[`Mode`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Tracked`,`SaveChanges-ready, identity dedupe`,`Slowest, highest allocation`,`Edit and update flows`],[`AsNoTracking`,`Fastest, least memory`,`No dedupe, read-only`,`Read APIs, exports, dashboards`],[`NoTrackingWithIdentityResolution`,`Dedupe without write overhead`,`Extra resolution pass`,`Joins that repeat parent rows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: projections and the SELECT * tax`,body:[`Tip two is projection — instead of loading Sale entities, I Select into a SalesDto. And here's where I want to be honest with you, because the benchmark result is uncomfortable: on this tiny three-column model, the timing between 'project everything' and 'project only ProductId and SalesPersonId' is almost identical. The only real difference is allocation — the leaner query allocates less. If I stopped here, you'd walk away thinking projections are a micro-optimization.`,`But extrapolate with me. This model has three columns. Real tables have forty, with nvarchar(max) descriptions and blob-ish payloads. The bytes you select are the bytes that cross the network, get buffered, and get materialized. On a wide schema, that gap between 'select everything' and 'select what you render' stops being an allocation footnote and becomes query-time you can see on a graph. Projections also unlock the optimizer — the engine can serve your query from a narrow index instead of the full row.`,`Why not just fetch entities and map in memory? Because you pay for every column whether you render it or not, and you forfeit the index synergy we're about to talk about. Select what you use. Nothing more.`],quote:`Every column you don't select is a byte you never ship.`,table:{headers:[`Fetch shape`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Full entity`,`Simplest code, update-ready`,`Wide rows, maximum allocation`,`Admin detail screens`],[`DTO projection`,`Minimal wire payload and allocation`,`One more type to maintain`,`Read APIs, list views`],[`Keyed projection (IDs only)`,`Tiny payloads, index-friendly`,`May need follow-up fetches`,`Grids, autocomplete, counts`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,at:`0:58`}},{heading:`03 · Covering the hot path: an index that ends the lookup`,body:[`Tip three: the query from tip two filters on SalesPersonId — which is also the foreign key to Salesperson. With no index on it, SQL Server has to walk the clustered index and check every row. So we declare an index on SalesPersonId in the DbContext, and here's the trick — we use INCLUDE to attach ProductId to the index's leaf level.`,`Why does that matter? Because now the filter column and the payload column both live in the index itself. The execution plan flips from a scan-plus-key-lookup pattern to a pure seek: the engine finds the matching range and never touches the table at all. Look for 'Seek' versus 'Scan' in your plan — that single word is the difference between a shortcut and a stroll through every row in the neighborhood.`,`The obvious 'why not index everything?' — because every index is a tax on every INSERT and UPDATE that follows. Included columns are the cheap version: they're stored only at the leaf, they don't reorder anything, and they turn your hottest read path into a one-stop lookup. Index the filter, include the payload, and leave the rest alone.`],quote:`A seek is a shortcut; a scan is a stroll through every row in the neighborhood.`,table:{headers:[`Index choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No index`,`Zero write amplification`,`Scans grow linearly with data`,`Tiny or write-heavy tables`],[`Index on filter column`,`Seek on the filter`,`Key lookup per row afterwards`,`General point queries`],[`Index with INCLUDE payload`,`Seek with no table touch`,`Slightly larger index footprint`,`Hot, well-shaped read paths`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,at:`1:54`}},{heading:`04 · The join explosion: when one query is one too many`,body:[`Tip four is the one that bites everyone eventually. You Include two collections — say Order.Lines and Order.Payments — in a single query. SQL Server flattens that into one giant JOIN, and the rows multiply: 100 orders times 10 lines times 5 payments is 5,000 rows shipped over the wire, most of them duplicated parent data that EF then deduplicates during assembly. Add a third collection and it gets quadratic-fast worse. That's the cartesian explosion.`,`SplitQuery is the escape hatch: EF sends one query per include — orders first, then lines, then payments — and assembles the graph in memory. Rows grow additively instead of multiplicatively. The trade-off is honest: more round trips, and by default the split queries don't share a single transaction unless you open one explicitly, so data can shift between them. For a read model, that's usually fine; for a financial consistency requirement, wrap it.`,`And why not lazy loading instead? Because that's the same N+1 pattern, hidden behind a property accessor where you can't see it or benchmark it. Explicit split queries at least put the round trips on the counter where they belong.`],quote:`One query with 50,000 multiplied rows or five queries with 100 each — round trips are usually cheaper than row multiplication.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single query`,`One round trip, one consistent snapshot`,`Cartesian row explosion`,`Scalars plus one collection`],[`Split query`,`Linear row counts, simple per-query SQL`,`N+1 round trips, weaker default atomicity`,`Multiple collection includes`],[`Lazy loading`,`Zero upfront query code`,`Hidden N+1, worst-case performance`,`Prototypes only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,at:`3:58`}},{heading:`05 · The last mile: compiled queries and honest benchmarking`,body:[`Fifth tip, and it's the one for your hottest paths. Every time EF runs a LINQ query it has to translate the expression tree to SQL — the plan is cached by query shape, but the cache lookup, the closure allocations, and the translation checks still cost microseconds per call. For the handful of queries that run thousands of times per second, EF.CompileAsyncQuery lets you precompile once and invoke with parameters, skipping the pipeline entirely.`,`But the bigger lesson from this whole session is the benchmarking discipline itself. Remember tip two: the projection benchmark showed nearly identical times at small scale. If I'd cherry-picked, I'd have told you projections don't matter — and on a wide table that's flatly wrong. Use BenchmarkDotNet with MemoryDiagnoser so you see allocation alongside time, benchmark your actual data shape, and let the numbers — not the vibes — decide which of these five tips is your next pull request.`,`Performance work in EF isn't one big lever. It's five small, compounding ones, and each one is measurable before and after.`],quote:`Measure before you optimize — but only after you've removed the tracking, the extra columns, and the scans.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,at:`5:14`}}],diagrams:[{id:`tracking-decision-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two roads for one LINQ query`,caption:`The branch every read endpoint should hit: tracked materialization feeds the change tracker and the GC, AsNoTracking goes straight to the response.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  A["LINQ query"] --> B["EF Core pipeline"]
  B --> C{"SaveChanges later?"}
  C -->|Yes| D["Tracked materialization"]
  D --> E["Change tracker snapshots"]
  E --> F["Gen2 GC pressure"]
  F --> I["Slower p95"]
  C -->|No| G["AsNoTracking"]
  G --> H["Plain objects"]
  H --> J["Fast response"]`},{id:`split-query-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Single query vs SplitQuery over the wire`,caption:`One JOIN-heavy query ships multiplied rows; SplitQuery ships three small result sets and assembles the graph in memory.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant App
  participant EF as EF Core
  participant DB as SQL Server
  App->>EF: Orders + Lines + Payments, single query
  EF->>DB: SELECT with JOIN Lines JOIN Payments
  DB-->>EF: 50000 multiplied rows
  Note over EF: Dedupe and assemble graph
  App->>EF: Same shape, SplitQuery
  EF->>DB: Query 1 - orders
  DB-->>EF: 100 rows
  EF->>DB: Query 2 - lines
  DB-->>EF: 1000 rows
  EF->>DB: Query 3 - payments
  DB-->>EF: 500 rows
  Note over EF: Assemble graph, no multiplication`}],takeaways:[`Any query you never call SaveChanges on gets AsNoTracking — expect roughly 2x faster execution and a fraction of the allocation on entity loads.`,`Project to DTOs with Select; even on a 3-column demo the leaner query cut allocation measurably — on wide tables expect 2-5x less wire traffic and query time that finally moves.`,`Index the filter column and INCLUDE the payload columns — flipping a clustered scan into a covering seek takes reads from O(table) to O(matched rows).`,`Two or more collection Includes means SplitQuery: rows grow multiplicatively in one query versus additively across N round trips — and wrap in a transaction if you need cross-query consistency.`,`Precompile your top 5 hottest queries with EF.CompileAsyncQuery and always benchmark with MemoryDiagnoser — microseconds and kilobytes per call compound hard at thousands of requests per second.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — 5 EF Performance Tips You NEED to Know + BENCHMARKS`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uUWhU8H5uzg`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-G7VR14Gt2O0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:26,title:`Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:19`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`AWS`],story:{headline:`Release-Day Smoke Test: Green Dashboards, a 30-Second Hang, and One Guilty Include`,paragraphs:[`It's release day, 2 PM, and the smoke test is supposed to be a formality — until it isn't. Our HR service has three new endpoints on the department workspace: one loads a department with its teams, one adds employees, one goes all the way down to tasks. The first two come back in under 100 and 200 milliseconds. The third one spins. We watch it hang for exactly thirty seconds — EF Core's default command timeout — and die.`,`The war room assembles with the usual suspects. RDS instance too small? CloudWatch says CPU never left the teens during the hang. Missing index? EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the logged SQL returns in tens of milliseconds. Connection pool exhausted? The pool is half empty. Every infrastructure theory dies against the same awkward fact: Postgres answers instantly, and the request still times out.`,`The breakthrough is almost embarrassing. We paste the raw SQL — a chain of LEFT JOINs across departments, teams, employees, and tasks — into a reader and start counting. One employee joined to their payment history alone flattens to 119 rows for a single person. The full tree multiplies out to thousands of duplicated rows for a few hundred real entities. The database was never slow. We had just asked it a Cartesian question.`],problem:`A query Postgres executes in tens of milliseconds still burns through EF Core's 30-second default command timeout — the cost lives in the flat JOIN's row multiplication, the wire transfer, and identity resolution, none of which an index or a bigger instance can fix.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`AWS RDS`,`Minimal APIs`,`AsSplitQuery`,`Include/ThenInclude`],note:`An ASP.NET Core HR service reading a hierarchical department → team → employee → task schema from Postgres on RDS. Every moving part is healthy — the failure lives entirely in how chained Includes translate into one flat JOIN.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the database must be slow`,body:[`So the timeout fires at exactly thirty seconds, and thirty seconds is not a random number — that's EF Core's default command timeout. Your brain does the normal thing: it's the database. We were on a modest RDS instance, so the room's first instinct was 'scale it up and move on.' But pull up the CloudWatch panel during the hang and the database is basically asleep — CPU in the teens, IOPS flat, no lock waits, pool half empty.`,`Then we take the SQL straight from the log, run EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and it returns in tens of milliseconds. That's the uncomfortable moment: Postgres executed our query fine. Everything that's slow happens after the answer — shipping the result set over the wire, and EF Core turning thousands of flat rows back into an object graph. We spent an hour interrogating the one suspect with a perfect alibi.`],quote:`The database answered in milliseconds; the timeout was spent after the answer.`,table:{headers:[`Suspect`,`Evidence checked`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`RDS instance size`,`CloudWatch CPU/IOPS during hang`,`Idle — CPU under 15%`],[`Missing index`,`EXPLAIN ANALYZE of logged SQL`,`Tens of milliseconds`],[`Connection pool`,`Pool counters at hang time`,`Half empty, no starvation`],[`Network to RDS`,`Same-region latency, other endpoints`,`Sub-100 ms elsewhere`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where the rows multiply`,body:[`Here's the mechanics, because once you see it you can't unsee it. Every collection Include adds another one-to-many JOIN, and SQL returns flat rows — so the row counts multiply, they don't add. Five teams, forty employees, eight tasks each: that's 5 × 40 × 8 = 1,600 rows coming back for exactly 366 real entities. Add a sibling collection like salary payments and it multiplies again. Even the raw SQL demo makes the point at small scale — one employee joined to their payment history flattens to 119 rows for one person.`,`And here's the twist: the database isn't doing anything wrong. The plan is clean, there's no cross-product error inside Postgres. The cost lands on EF Core, which has to materialize every one of those 1,600 rows, deduplicate them back into 366 entities via identity resolution, and — because this is a tracking query — feed each row through the change tracker. Every duplicated row also drags the parent's columns across the wire again. That's your thirty seconds.`],quote:`Every collection Include multiplies the result set — it never adds.`,table:{headers:[`Endpoint`,`Joined rows`,`Distinct entities`,`Latency`],rows:[[`Dept + Teams`,`5`,`6`,`< 100 ms`],[`Dept + Teams + Employees`,`40`,`46`,`< 200 ms`],[`Dept + Teams + Employees + Tasks`,`1,600`,`366`,`30 s timeout`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,at:`3:50`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just throw hardware or lazy loading at it?`,body:[`So why not the obvious fixes? A bigger instance speeds up materialization a little, but the 1,600-to-366 ratio doesn't move — you've just bought a faster machine to do pointless work. Lazy loading looks tempting until you realize it trades one explosion for N+1 round trips: fine for a tiny graph, brutal in a loop over forty employees.`,`The real decision is about shape, not power. Split queries break the one flat JOIN into one query per collection — rows ship as a sum instead of a product. Projections with Select go further: you fetch only the columns the endpoint needs, skip identity resolution entirely, and return DTOs. The trade you're making is round trips and mapping code versus rows and CPU — and for read-only APIs, that trade is almost always worth it.`],quote:`You can't index or instance-size your way out of a result-shape problem.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bigger instance`,`Zero code change`,`Cost scales, ratio unchanged`,`Poor`],[`Lazy loading`,`Small queries per navigation`,`N+1 round trips in loops`,`Poor`],[`AsSplitQuery`,`Sum not product, one line`,`Multiple round trips, torn reads`,`Great`],[`Projection (Select)`,`Only needed columns, no tracking`,`DTO mapping, loses entity graph`,`Best for read-only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,at:`7:44`}},{heading:`04 · Split queries: paying round trips instead of rows`,body:[`AsSplitQuery is the one-line version of the fix. EF Core compiles the same graph into three targeted queries — departments with teams, teams with employees, employees with tasks — stitches them together in memory by identity, and hands you the same 366 entities. Our thirty-second timeout dropped to roughly 150 milliseconds. The row count went from a product to a sum: 5 + 40 + 320 instead of 5 × 40 × 8.`,`But be honest about the cost. You've traded one round trip for three, which is nothing in-region and painful cross-region. And those three queries are not one snapshot — unless you wrap them in a transaction, a concurrent write between hops can hand you a slightly torn graph. If you need strict read-your-writes consistency and the graph is genuinely small, the single query is still the right call. Know which regime you're in.`],quote:`Round trips are cheap; row explosions compound.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Single query`,`Split query`],rows:[[`Round trips`,`1`,`1 per collection (~3–4)`],[`Rows shipped`,`Product of collection sizes`,`Sum of collection sizes`],[`Consistency`,`One snapshot`,`Needs a transaction`],[`30 s timeout risk`,`High with 2+ collection Includes`,`Low`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,at:`9:21`}},{heading:`05 · The rule I carry into every review now`,body:[`So here's the heuristic I run in every review: count the collection Includes. One is almost always fine. Two sibling collections at the same level, or a third level chained on — that's the measurement trigger. If joined rows exceed roughly five times the distinct entity count, or you're materializing more than about ten thousand rows, split it or project it.`,`And log the SQL. EF Core will show you exactly the JOIN chain it built, and the bug is visible in the shape before it's visible in the latency. The fix that day was one line — AsSplitQuery — but the habit of reading generated SQL is what keeps it from coming back on the next endpoint.`],quote:`Shape the query before you size the database.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,at:`10:20`}}],diagrams:[{id:`join-fanout`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One flat JOIN, multiplied`,caption:`How three chained Includes turn 366 entities into 1,600 shipped rows — and where the thirty seconds actually go.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  A["GET /departments/1?tree=full"] --> B["EF Core query<br/>Include Teams → ThenInclude Employees → ThenInclude Tasks"]
  B --> C["Single LEFT JOIN chain<br/>on Postgres RDS"]
  C --> D["Flat result set<br/>5 teams × 40 employees × 8 tasks"]
  D --> E["1,600 duplicated rows<br/>for 366 distinct entities"]
  E --> F["Wire transfer from RDS<br/>parent columns repeated per row"]
  F --> G["EF Core identity resolution<br/>dedupe rows into entities + change tracker"]
  G --> H["30s command timeout hit<br/>while Postgres CPU idles"]`},{id:`split-query-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`AsSplitQuery: pay round trips, not rows`,caption:`The same graph fetched as three targeted queries — the row count becomes a sum, not a product.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant API as Minimal API
  participant EF as EF Core
  participant PG as Postgres RDS
  API->>EF: GET department graph with AsSplitQuery
  EF->>PG: Q1: departments JOIN teams
  PG-->>EF: 5 rows
  EF->>PG: Q2: teams JOIN employees
  PG-->>EF: 40 rows
  EF->>PG: Q3: employees JOIN tasks
  PG-->>EF: 320 rows
  EF-->>API: 366 distinct entities from 365 shipped rows
  Note over EF,PG: Sum instead of product — 3 round trips, ~150 ms total`}],takeaways:[`Rows multiply, entities don't: a 5 × 40 × 8 Include chain ships 1,600 rows for 366 entities — compute the product before you merge the query.`,`Treat the third chained collection Include (or any two sibling collections) as a measurement trigger: once joined rows exceed ~5× distinct entities, reach for AsSplitQuery or a projection.`,`If a query hangs while Postgres CPU stays under ~20% and EXPLAIN ANALYZE returns in tens of milliseconds, it's row explosion — stop resizing the instance.`,`AsSplitQuery turns a product into a sum: budget one extra round trip per collection (~1–5 ms in-region) and wrap it in a transaction when the graph must be a consistent snapshot.`,`For read-only endpoints, project to DTOs with Select: you skip identity resolution and the change tracker, often shipping 10× fewer bytes than full entities.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Are You Accidentally Crippling Your EF Core Queries?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7VR14Gt2O0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-90_6J5tnwkw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:27,title:`EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:42`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Release Day, 11 A.M.: The Startup Migration That Froze Checkout`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday release, midday, traffic already climbing toward the lunch peak. The team's new ordering service called Database.Migrate() on startup — the pattern every EF Core tutorial shows you. Three instances spun up behind the load balancer at the same time, and all three raced to apply the same pending migration. One won. The other two sat blocked on the schema lock while EF re-checked __EFMigrationsHistory for the fourth time.`,`Then the real problem surfaced. The pending migration looked innocent in C# — a new column with a default value, configured through the fluent API. But EF translated it into an ALTER that forced a full table rewrite on a 40-million-row orders table, inside an exclusive lock. Checkout latency climbed from 80 ms to 9 seconds, the connection pool starved, and the on-call engineer killed the deploy mid-flight — leaving the history table claiming one state and the actual schema claiming another.`,`Recovery ate the whole afternoon: a hand-written idempotent script, a manual reconciliation of the history rows, and a very quiet retro. The new rule that came out of it: migrations leave the laptop as reviewed SQL artifacts, never as an unobserved side effect of application startup.`],problem:`Applying migrations as an unreviewed startup side effect turns a routine schema change into an outage: racing instances, surprise table rewrites, and a history table that no longer tells the truth about your database.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`dotnet ef CLI`,`Migration Bundles`,`Idempotent Scripts`,`__EFMigrationsHistory`,`Database.Migrate()`,`Fluent API`,`DbUp`,`Grate`,`FluentMigrator`],note:`The moving parts of schema versioning: C#-first migrations and the history table that tracks state, the four apply paths (CLI, startup code, bundles, generated SQL), and the external runners that can take over when EF's workflow stops fitting.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just migrate on startup' feels right — and isn't`,body:[`Let's start where everyone starts: context.Database.Migrate() in Program.cs. It's honest, it's one line, and in development it's genuinely great — spin up the app, schema is current, zero ceremony. I use it every single day in dev and I will defend it there.`,`But think about what that line means in production. You've made schema evolution a side effect of booting a web request handler. Now multiply by N instances behind a load balancer — they all race the same migration, and EF has no coordination between them. Worse, there's no review gate: the SQL that runs is whatever the migration emitted, executed by whatever process boots first. And if the migration fails halfway, your app doesn't boot — you've coupled deployability to schema state.`,`So the question isn't 'can we migrate at startup?' — it's 'who is allowed to change the schema, and when?' That question decides everything that follows.`],quote:`Convenience at startup is how outages get scheduled.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`CLI: database update`,`Fast, interactive, great feedback loop`,`Needs EF tooling + connection access from your machine`,`Local dev`],[`Startup: Database.Migrate()`,`Zero ops, works on boot`,`Races with N instances, no review gate, failed migration = app won't start`,`Dev, single-instance only`],[`Migration Bundle (EF Core 6+)`,`Self-contained exe, no SDK needed in CI`,`Opaque binary — nobody reviews what it runs`,`CI/CD without .NET tooling`],[`Idempotent SQL script`,`Auditable, re-runnable, DBA-friendly, gated`,`Extra step: generate, review, store`,`Production, any team size`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90_6J5tnwkw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the history table is the contract`,body:[`Here's the part nobody teaches you first: EF doesn't actually know your schema by looking at it. It knows it by reading __EFMigrationsHistory — a tiny table with a MigrationId (a timestamp plus the migration name) and a ProductVersion column. When you run an update, EF diffs the history table against the migrations in your assembly and applies the difference, in timestamp order, one by one.`,`That has consequences people learn the hard way. You can't reorder migrations after the fact — the timestamp in the ID is the order. You can't safely hand-edit the table, but you will eventually have to reconcile it after a botched partial apply, because drift between environments is the number one cause of a failed database update. The schema didn't break; the contract did.`,`So before any production apply, I run dotnet ef migrations list and compare it against what the target environment claims. Two minutes of diffing beats an afternoon of forensic archaeology. And if you need to pin state, database update with a specific migration name lets you apply up to an exact point — not 'whatever is pending'.`],quote:`Your schema's source of truth isn't the database — it's a two-column table everyone forgets exists.`,table:{headers:[`What a history row tells you`,`What it doesn't`],rows:[[`Which migrations were applied, in order`,`Whether the SQL actually succeeded cleanly`],[`Which EF version produced it`,`Whether someone manually altered the schema after`],[`A unique, sortable identity per migration`,`Whether the model and the schema still agree`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90_6J5tnwkw`,at:`6:45`}},{heading:`03 · Generating SQL scripts: the gate every production change should pass`,body:[`My preferred production path is dotnet ef migrations script. You get the exact SQL for a migration range — you can script from a specific migration to another, or the whole history. Add --idempotent and EF wraps every block in a check against the history table, so the script applies only what's missing and safely no-ops on a database that's already current. That property is gold in pipelines that might re-run.`,`Why do I like scripts over bundles? Bundles are brilliant for CI runners with no .NET SDK — a self-contained executable that applies pending migrations. But it's still an opaque binary pressing 'go' against production. A script is text. It goes into the pull request, the DBA reads it, the approver sees the ALTER statements before they exist in the world. In regulated or DBA-gated shops, that's not a nice-to-have, it's the whole game.`,`The trade-off is honest: you now maintain a generation step and store the artifacts. I think that cost is trivial next to the alternative — an unread migration executing against your hottest table at 11 a.m.`],quote:`A migration you haven't read as SQL is a migration you don't actually understand.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Migration Bundle`,`Idempotent SQL Script`],rows:[[`Reviewability`,`Opaque executable`,`Plain SQL in the PR`],[`Re-runnability`,`Applies pending only`,`No-ops when current — safe retries`],[`Tooling needs`,`None (self-contained exe)`,`EF tooling to generate, nothing to run`],[`Approval workflows`,`Hard to gate`,`Natural fit for DBA / change gates`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90_6J5tnwkw`,at:`7:53`}},{heading:`04 · The escape hatch: migrationBuilder.Sql() and what the fluent API really emits`,body:[`Sooner or later EF's generator can't express what you need — a data backfill, an online index build, a view, a permission grant. That's the escape hatch: add an empty migration and call migrationBuilder.Sql() with your own command. But understand the deal you're signing: EF no longer knows if that SQL is correct, and it definitely can't reverse it. You own the Down() side too, or you deliberately write a one-way migration and say so.`,`The same 'know what it emits' rule applies to the fluent API even when EF does generate the SQL. HasPrecision on a decimal becomes a column type in the DDL; HasIndex().IsUnique() becomes a unique constraint that can fail on existing dirty data. These are exactly the changes that look trivial in C# and do heavy lifting in the database — a default value on a big table can mean a full rewrite under lock, which is precisely how our story started.`,`So my habit: after every add migration, open the generated file and read the Up() method as SQL, not as C#. Thirty seconds of reading catches the table rewrites, the implicit index drops, and the backfills hiding behind a friendly property name.`],quote:`EF writes the easy 90% — the escape hatch is where you sign your name.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90_6J5tnwkw`,at:`12:23`}},{heading:`05 · When to graduate: FluentMigrator, DbUp, and Grate`,body:[`EF Core migrations are excellent, but they assume one codebase owns the schema. The moment two applications share a database, or your DBA team demands folder-based SQL with their own approval flow, it's time to look around. FluentMigrator gives you C#-defined migrations with a more bare-metal feel — same mental model, less EF coupling.`,`Then there are the pure script runners. DbUp is a small .NET library that executes SQL scripts from your project and journals what ran in its own table — dead simple to wire into any pipeline. Grate — short for 'migrate', and the successor to the long-popular RoundhouseE — takes the folder-of-plain-SQL approach: up scripts, down scripts, run-once and every-time folders. Your DBAs can read every artifact without knowing a line of C#.`,`You can even run these alongside EF: EF owns the schema DDL, DbUp or Grate owns the data scripts and operational SQL. The tool you pick should answer one question — who is allowed to touch this database, and what format can they review?`],quote:`Pick the tool that matches who's allowed to touch the database — not the one with the best demo.`,table:{headers:[`Tool`,`Model`,`Best fit`],rows:[[`EF Core Migrations`,`C# model-first, auto-generated DDL`,`One app owns the schema`],[`FluentMigrator`,`Hand-written C# migrations`,`Bare-metal control, no EF coupling`],[`DbUp`,`SQL scripts + journal table`,`Script runner inside any .NET pipeline`],[`Grate (ex-RoundhouseE)`,`Folders of plain SQL`,`DBA-gated, multi-team, SQL-first shops`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`apply-paths`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From C# model to applied schema — the four apply paths`,caption:`Every path starts at the same migration files; only the production paths differ in review and gating.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  A["C# Model + Fluent API"] --> B["dotnet ef migrations add"]
  B --> C["Migration Files<br/>Up() / Down()"]
  C --> P1["CLI<br/>database update"]
  C --> P2["Startup<br/>Database.Migrate()"]
  C --> P3["Migration Bundle<br/>self-contained exe"]
  C --> P4["SQL Script<br/>--idempotent"]
  P1 --> DB[("SQL Database")]
  P2 --> DB
  P3 --> DB
  P4 --> R{"PR Review +<br/>Approval Gate"}
  R --> DB
  DB --> H[("__EFMigrationsHistory")]`},{id:`release-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Release pipeline: the idempotent script as a gated step`,caption:`The script checks the history table itself, so re-runs and retries are always safe.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant Dev as Developer
  participant PR as Pull Request
  participant CI as Release Pipeline
  participant DB as Prod Database

  Dev->>PR: dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent
  Note over PR: SQL reviewed like code<br/>ALTERs visible before they exist
  PR->>CI: merge to main
  CI->>DB: execute script
  DB->>DB: read __EFMigrationsHistory
  alt pending migrations found
      DB->>DB: apply in timestamp order
      DB->>DB: insert history rows
  else already applied
      DB-->>CI: no-op, skip safely
  end
  CI->>DB: verify schema state
  CI->>CI: deploy application bits`}],takeaways:[`Never ship Database.Migrate() to more than one instance — two pods racing one migration is a deadlock lottery; keep startup migrations for dev and single-instance services only.`,`Generate --idempotent scripts for every release and read them as SQL: any migration touching more than ~1M rows gets its own change window, not a deploy step.`,`One migration, one intent — if a reviewer needs more than 5 minutes to read the generated Up() method, split it into smaller migrations.`,`Diff dotnet ef migrations list against __EFMigrationsHistory in every environment before an update; roughly 9 out of 10 failed applies are environment drift, not bad SQL.`,`Separate schema from data: keep ALTERs to a few seconds of lock time and push backfills into follow-up migrations or a DbUp/Grate script batch.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Migrations Deep Dive, Applying Migration, SQL Scripts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90_6J5tnwkw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Gf1sCvikpgI`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:28,title:`EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:42`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The onboarding demo where company 7 belonged to someone else`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 11:40 in the morning, onboarding call with a prospect's ops lead, screen share on. Our rep walks the companies endpoint to show off the data model: company 6 renders perfectly. She clicks company 7 — and the tenant ID in the payload changes. Company 8 — it changes again. The prospect asks the one question you never want to hear on a demo: "Why am I looking at another customer's data?"`,`There was no breach and no auth bypass. Every query returned exactly the row that was asked for — the isolation lived entirely in a TenantId column that nothing actually enforced. Filtering was a convention each endpoint had to remember, and an endpoint shipped the previous week simply didn't. Worst kind of failure: silent. 200 OK, wrong customer's data.`,`The fix wasn't patching that one endpoint. It was resolving the tenant once per request from a validated header, registering that resolver as a scoped service, and teaching the EF Core model to inject the tenant predicate into every query — so the API surface never changes, and forgetting the filter becomes impossible.`],problem:`How do you enforce tenant isolation at the data layer — so no query, written by anyone, ever crosses the tenant boundary — without changing your API surface?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`Global Query Filters`,`IHttpContextAccessor`,`Scoped DI`,`Tenant-Id Header`,`Per-Tenant Connections`],note:`A scoped tenant resolver feeds the request's tenant ID into EF Core's model, where global query filters append the tenant predicate to every query — with a connection resolver taking over when each tenant gets its own database.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the WHERE clause nobody wrote`,body:[`Let's replay the repro, because it's deceptively boring. GET /companies/6 returns company 6 — correct. GET /companies/7 returns company 7, and the tenant ID in the payload is different. Same story on the sales endpoint: change the company ID, watch the tenant ID change. By SQL standards every query is flawless; by SaaS standards every query is a lawsuit.`,`Why not just add Where(c => c.TenantId == tenantId) to each endpoint? Because that's a social contract, not a control. Endpoints multiply, people rotate, one Include() or a new projection skips the check — and nothing throws. You get a 200 with the wrong customer's data, and silent failures are the expensive kind.`,`So my first instinct is: stop trusting application code. The filter should live where queries are born — in the model itself.`],quote:`A filter you have to remember is a filter you will eventually forget.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-query Where()`,`Explicit, greppable`,`One miss = cross-tenant leak`,`Never — too fragile`],[`Repository base class`,`Somewhat centralized`,`Bypassable; leaks via Include()`,`Small internal apps`],[`Global query filters`,`Model-enforced, automatic`,`Must combine with soft-delete filters`,`Default for shared schema`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: who's actually asking?`,body:[`Before EF can filter anything, it needs to know who's asking. I build a tiny TenantProvider: inject IHttpContextAccessor, read the Tenant-Id request header, int.TryParse it, and — critically — check it against a known-tenant list. Missing header, unparseable value, or unknown tenant? Throw. Fail closed, always.`,`Lifetime matters more than people expect: register it scoped. HttpContext only exists for the duration of a request; make the provider a singleton and you're either capturing a dead context or, worse, serving one request's tenant to another. Scoped also lets the DbContext take the provider in its constructor and stay honest per request.`,`A header is the demo-friendly choice, but the seam is what matters — swap in a subdomain or a JWT claim later and nothing downstream changes, because only the provider knows where the tenant comes from.`],quote:`Resolve the tenant once, at the edge, and validate it before it ever reaches SQL.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Header`,`Explicit, trivial to test`,`Client must send it; spoofable without auth`,`Internal APIs, quick wins`],[`Subdomain`,`Zero client changes`,`DNS/TLS plumbing; awkward in local dev`,`Customer-facing portals`],[`JWT claim`,`Binds tenant to identity`,`Token refresh coupling`,`Authenticated SaaS APIs`],[`Path segment`,`Visible, cache-friendly`,`Pollutes routes and links`,`Docs-heavy public APIs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,at:`1:21`}},{heading:`03 · Teaching the model, not the developers`,body:[`Here's the move that changes the game: give every tenant-owned entity a TenantId (an ITenantable interface makes them easy to find), then in OnModelCreating loop over all entity types and call HasQueryFilter with a predicate comparing TenantId to the provider's value. From that moment, every LINQ query EF generates — including navigations and Includes — carries AND tenant_id = @tenant. Last week's unfiltered endpoint is fixed without touching it.`,`One gotcha worth saying out loud: EF Core allows a single query filter per entity. If you also do soft deletes via HasQueryFilter, the second call overwrites the first — combine both conditions with && in one predicate. And raw SQL roots via FromSqlRaw bypass the filter, so compose LINQ over them or filter inside the SQL itself.`,`The API surface doesn't change at all — same routes, same response shapes. Cross-tenant IDs just return 404 now, which is exactly the answer you want to give an attacker: nothing to learn from.`],quote:`One HasQueryFilter in model building beats a hundred WHERE clauses in code review.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,at:`2:44`}},{heading:`04 · Shared database or database-per-tenant?`,body:[`Everything so far assumes one database, one schema, a TenantId column — the shared-everything model. It's the cheapest to run: one migration, one connection pool, one backup story. But the isolation is logical, which means the query filter is load-bearing infrastructure, not a nicety.`,`Database-per-tenant flips the trade. Isolation becomes physical — restore one customer's data, move a noisy neighbor to a bigger instance, satisfy the auditor who asks "where exactly is customer X's data?" The bill arrives as operations: N connection strings, N migration runs, and connection pools that fragment per connection string — a hundred tenants each holding pool minimums quietly eats sockets.`,`There's a middle path — schema-per-tenant in Postgres — but I only reach for it when a single tenant genuinely outgrows the shared tables. Decide by compliance pressure and tenant size variance, not by fashion.`],quote:`Database-per-tenant buys you isolation and invoices you in migrations.`,table:{headers:[`Model`,`Isolation`,`Ops cost`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Shared schema`,`Logical (query filters)`,`Lowest — one migration, one pool`,`Most SaaS, many small tenants`],[`Schema per tenant`,`Hybrid`,`Medium — namespace sprawl`,`Few large tenants on Postgres`],[`Database per tenant`,`Physical`,`High — N migrations, N pools`,`Compliance-heavy, big tenants`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,at:`4:21`}},{heading:`05 · The parts that bite later: indexes, migrations, tests`,body:[`Three things bite months later, so let's front-run them. First, indexes: in a shared schema every query now leads with TenantId, so composite indexes must lead with it too — (TenantId, CompanyId), not (CompanyId). Otherwise the filter doesn't narrow the scan; it just makes every scan walk across everyone's pages.`,`Second, migrations. Shared schema is one migration. Database-per-tenant is a fan-out — track applied migrations per database, make scripts idempotent, and budget the time linearly with tenant count. Third, tests: the filter is code, so test it like code. Seed a row for tenant 1, run a query under tenant 2's context, assert empty — in CI, on every commit.`,`And once, on purpose, log the SQL EF generates for a normal endpoint. Seeing AND tenant_id = @tenant appended to everything is the moment the whole design clicks.`],quote:`If a cross-tenant query can't fail your CI, assume it will fail your customers.`,table:{headers:[`Failure mode`,`Symptom`,`Guardrail`],rows:[[`Missing composite index`,`p99 climbs as data grows`,`Lead every index with TenantId`],[`Migration drift`,`One tenant DB on an old schema`,`Idempotent fan-out + per-DB tracking`],[`Untested filter`,`Leak discovered by a customer`,`CI test: tenant B queries tenant A's ID, assert empty`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,at:`6:43`}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Tenant-scoped request lifecycle`,caption:`The header becomes a validated tenant, and the filter rides along on every query EF generates.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as Companies API
    participant T as TenantProvider
    participant D as App DbContext
    participant P as Postgres

    C->>A: GET /companies/7 with Tenant-Id header
    A->>T: GetTenantId()
    T->>T: TryParse header, check known tenants
    T-->>A: tenantId
    A->>D: Companies.SingleOrDefault(c => c.Id == 7)
    D->>P: SELECT * FROM companies WHERE id = 7 AND tenant_id = @tenant
    alt row belongs to this tenant
        P-->>D: company row
        D-->>A: Company entity
        A-->>C: 200 OK
    else row belongs to another tenant
        P-->>D: empty result
        D-->>A: null
        A-->>C: 404 Not Found
    end`},{id:`tenancy-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One database or many: where the tenant boundary lives`,caption:`Shared schema leans on query filters; database-per-tenant moves the boundary into the connection string.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    REQ["Request + Tenant-Id header"] --> VAL{"Tenant valid?"}
    VAL -- "no" --> REJ["Throw: missing or unknown tenant"]
    VAL -- "yes" --> TP["TenantProvider, scoped"]
    TP --> CTX["DbContext, scoped"]
    CTX -->|"shared schema"| GF["Global query filter<br/>AND tenant_id = tenant"]
    CTX -->|"database per tenant"| CR["Connection resolver<br/>per-tenant connection string"]
    GF --> SDB[("Shared database<br/>companies + sales rows")]
    CR --> TDB1[("Tenant DB 1")]
    CR --> TDB2[("Tenant DB 2")]`}],takeaways:[`One missing Where() is a breach, not a bug — move tenant filtering into global query filters so no endpoint can ever forget it.`,`Register the tenant provider as scoped: HttpContext lives exactly one request, and a singleton will serve tenant A's context to tenant B.`,`Validate the Tenant-Id header against a known-tenant list before it reaches SQL — parse-and-pray fails open.`,`Lead composite indexes with TenantId — (TenantId, CompanyId), not (CompanyId) — or shared-schema queries scan across every tenant's pages.`,`Database-per-tenant multiplies migration runs by tenant count: 200 tenants means 200 migration executions — automate the fan-out or stay on shared schema.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Multitenancy For Your SaaS Applications`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf1sCvikpgI`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Egd-BMPCHNc`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:29,title:`Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:11`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`],story:{headline:`Release Day, 11:42 AM: The Order Endpoint That Starved Our Connection Pool`,paragraphs:[`The checkout rewrite shipped on a Tuesday, and the marketing email went out at noon sharp. By 12:20 the order-status endpoint — a 'simple' GetOrder query — was timing out in waves. The handler loaded the entire Order aggregate with Include(o => o.LineItems), dragging every mapped column including a fat JSON metadata blob nobody had read since 2022, then tracked every entity in the change tracker. Under flash-sale concurrency each request held its connection longer, the 100-connection pool starved, and the RDS volume blew through its burst IOPS balance: SELECTs that ran in 3ms in staging were taking 900ms in prod.`,`The hotfix that week was the classic one: bolt Dapper onto the hot path. Hand-written SQL, straight into OrderResponse records, no tracking, no fixup — p99 fell from 1.4 seconds to 60ms and the pool stopped bleeding. Everyone celebrated. Then, three weeks later, a developer renamed CustomerId to BuyerId in the EF model. The LINQ paths updated at compile time; the Dapper SQL string didn't, and its name-based mapping quietly handed back NULLs. No exception, no log line — finance found it in a settlement report.`,`That's the real question this lesson answers: not 'which ORM is faster' but 'how do you keep one source of truth for your schema and still get raw-SQL speed on hot reads.' EF Core 8's SqlQuery for unmapped types is the first credible answer to that — raw SQL mapped straight to plain records, nothing tracked, LINQ still composable on top. We rebuild the GetOrder query three ways and see exactly where Dapper still wins.`],problem:`How do you get Dapper-grade raw SQL materialization inside an EF Core codebase — without maintaining a second mapping layer that silently drifts from your model?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 8`,`Dapper`,`SqlQuery Unmapped`,`MediatR`,`Postgres`,`Change Tracker`],note:`The read-path cast: EF Core 8's SqlQuery over unmapped types against Postgres, Dapper as the incumbent hot-path micro-ORM, and MediatR wrapping the query pipeline.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: what actually makes Dapper fast`,body:[`Let's start where the benchmark numbers come from, because 'Dapper is fast' is doing a lot of unexamined work in that sentence. When you run a Dapper query, it hands your SQL to ADO.NET and materializes rows straight into your type by column name. That's it. No change tracker, no identity resolution, no relationship fixup, no snapshot of every property for later comparison.`,`Now look at what the naive EF version of GetOrder does: Include(o => o.LineItems) pulls every mapped column on both tables, then the change tracker snapshots each entity, runs identity resolution per row, and wires up the navigation fixup. None of that is wasted for a write flow. For a read-only API response, it's pure overhead.`,`So my first thought reframes the whole debate: the gap isn't the ORM, it's the job we asked it to do. Which means before reaching for a second library, the first thing to try is asking EF for less.`],quote:`Dapper isn't fast because it's minimal — it's fast because it refuses to do work you never asked for.`,table:{headers:[`Per-query cost`,`Dapper`,`EF tracked + Include`],rows:[[`SQL authoring`,`You write it`,`Generated from LINQ`],[`Materialization`,`Direct to POCO`,`Entity + state manager`],[`Change tracking`,`None`,`Every entity snapshotted`],[`Identity resolution`,`None`,`Per-row key lookup`],[`Relationship fixup`,`Manual (splitOn)`,`Automatic via Include`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: three ways to return OrderResponse`,body:[`Here's approach one, the one from the incident: load the tracked aggregate, Include the line items, map to the response record. It works, it's readable, and for a write flow it's exactly right. For this query it's the most expensive correct answer in the room.`,`Approach two is the one I wish more people reached for first: a LINQ projection. Select straight into OrderResponse inside the query — EF translates it to SQL that fetches only OrderId, CustomerId, and the two line-item columns, and because the result isn't an entity, nothing gets tracked. You don't even need AsNoTracking; projections are never tracked anyway. This is the quiet 90% answer.`,`So why not stop here? Because not every query is expressible as clean LINQ. DBA-owned reports with CTEs, index hints, recursive queries, a window function doing running totals — you can fight the translator or you can write the SQL. That's the boundary, and it's exactly where the old answer was 'grab Dapper.'`],quote:`Projection queries were already the 90% answer — EF8 just covers the last 10% where the SQL is non-negotiable.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,at:`1:38`}},{heading:`03 · The EF8 feature: SqlQuery meets unmapped types`,body:[`Here's the EF8 feature itself. Before version 8, Database.SqlQuery could hand you scalars (that arrived in EF7) or mapped entity types — but not arbitrary types. EF8 lifts that: SqlQuery of any plain record, columns matched to properties by name, so you alias in the SQL — o.id AS OrderId — and the materializer does the rest. Nothing is tracked, because the type isn't in the model. Expose the Database facade on your context interface and the handler reads almost like Dapper.`,`And it's not a dumb escape hatch: the raw SQL is composable. Wrap it with Where or OrderBy in LINQ and EF wraps your SQL in a subquery and translates on top. So you get hand-written SQL with part of LINQ's safety net still intact.`,`Now the catch, and it's a real one: unmapped means unmapped. There's no model, so no Include, no split queries, no relationship fixup. Order plus line items means a second SqlQuery round trip, or one join you map by hand. Dapper's Query with splitOn still hydrates parent and children in a single round trip — that remains its genuine party trick.`],quote:`Unmapped means unmapped: you trade the model's memory for SQL's freedom — and you pay for it in round trips.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Tracked entity + Include`,`Full aggregate, unit of work ready`,`All mapped columns, tracking overhead`,`Write flows`],[`LINQ projection`,`Only needed columns, never tracked, refactor-safe`,`Limited by what the translator supports`,`Default read path`],[`EF8 SqlQuery (unmapped)`,`Hand-written SQL, direct record mapping, LINQ-composable`,`No Include; children cost a 2nd round trip`,`Hot reads, reports, DBA-owned SQL`],[`Dapper Query`,`Fastest materialization, one-round-trip multi-mapping`,`Second mapping layer, no built-in execution strategy`,`Extreme hot paths, bulk reads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,at:`2:21`}},{heading:`04 · The hidden cost nobody benchmarks: two ORMs, one schema`,body:[`Back to the drift bug from the story, because it generalizes. The moment Dapper enters, your schema has two sources of truth: the EF model and a pile of SQL strings. Renames land in the model first, because that's where the compiler lives. Name-based mapping doesn't throw on a missing column — it hands back defaults. The failure mode is silence, discovered in a finance report.`,`There's a second, quieter cost: execution strategies. EF's retrying execution strategy wraps EF operations; a raw Dapper call isn't automatically inside that retry policy unless you deliberately share the connection and wire it up. Same for logging and interceptors — your observability story forks the moment you add the second stack.`,`Why not just accept it and keep Dapper for hot paths? Because 'hot path' is a claim, not a fact. If you can't produce the benchmark that proves the split pays for its drift risk, you're paying complexity rent on vibes.`],quote:`Every extra mapping layer is a second schema truth — and second truths drift.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Single stack (EF only)`,`EF + Dapper split`],rows:[[`Schema truth`,`One model`,`Model + string SQL`],[`Rename safety`,`Compile-time via LINQ`,`Silent NULLs at runtime`],[`Retries & transactions`,`Execution strategy built in`,`Manual wiring required`],[`Hot-path perf`,`Within ~10-20% of Dapper`,`Fastest raw materialization`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,at:`6:49`}},{heading:`05 · Verdict: does EF8 end Dapper?`,body:[`So — the end of Dapper? For a greenfield .NET 8 codebase, mostly yes for read paths. Projections cover the default case, SqlQuery covers the raw-SQL escape hatch, and micro-benchmarks put EF8's materialization within striking distance of Dapper. Dapper still wins the raw loop, but we're talking single-digit to low-double-digit percentages now, not the 5x gaps of the EF6 era.`,`Where Dapper keeps a legitimate seat: one-round-trip multi-mapping for parent/children hydration, extreme hot paths with a per-request microsecond budget, bulk operations, and teams whose center of gravity is SQL first. My default posture: start EF-only, and let Dapper earn its way in with a named benchmark and a named owner for every SQL string it brings.`],quote:`EF8 didn't kill Dapper — it killed the reason most of us reached for it.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,at:`7:39`}}],diagrams:[{id:`read-path-decision`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Choosing the read path in an EF Core 8 codebase`,caption:`The decision tree I now walk before any second ORM gets invited to the party.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Q["Incoming read: GetOrderQuery"] --> W{"Mutates the aggregate?"}
    W -- "Yes" --> T["Tracked entity + Include LineItems"]
    W -- "No" --> S{"SQL beyond LINQ? CTEs, hints, DBA-owned"}
    S -- "No" --> P["LINQ projection into OrderResponse"]
    S -- "Yes" --> SQ["EF8 SqlQuery to unmapped record"]
    SQ --> M{"Parent + children in one round trip?"}
    M -- "Yes" --> D["Dapper multi-mapping via splitOn"]
    M -- "No" --> SQ2["Second SqlQuery for line items"]
    T --> X["Change tracker + all mapped columns"]
    P --> Y["Untracked, minimal columns"]
    SQ2 --> Y
    D --> Z["One round trip, fastest hydration"]`},{id:`sqlquery-round-trips`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`EF8 SqlQuery: two round trips for order + line items`,caption:`Raw SQL straight into records, nothing tracked — and the round-trip tax you pay for skipping the model.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant H as GetOrderQuery Handler
    participant EF as EF Core 8 Pipeline
    participant PG as Postgres
    Note over H,PG: Hot read via EF8 SqlQuery - unmapped types, nothing tracked
    H->>EF: Database.SqlQuery for OrderResponse
    EF->>PG: SELECT o.id AS OrderId, o.customer_id AS CustomerId FROM orders o WHERE o.id = @id
    PG-->>EF: 1 row
    EF-->>H: OrderResponse record, untracked
    H->>EF: Database.SqlQuery for LineItemResponse
    EF->>PG: SELECT li.id AS LineItemId, li.price FROM order_line_items li WHERE li.order_id = @id
    PG-->>EF: N rows
    EF-->>H: List of LineItemResponse, untracked
    Note over H,PG: Dapper equivalent: Query multi-mapping with splitOn hydrates both in one round trip`}],takeaways:[`Default every read to a LINQ projection — it typically cuts transferred columns by 50-80% versus Include and never touches the change tracker.`,`Reserve EF8 SqlQuery for SQL you can't express in LINQ (CTEs, hints, DBA-owned reports) — expect materialization within roughly 10-20% of Dapper.`,`Remember unmapped means unmapped: no Include, so parent + children costs two round trips unless you hand-map a join or use Dapper's splitOn.`,`Cap tracked-entity reads around 100 entities per request; past that, change-tracker snapshots and identity resolution start showing up in p99.`,`Adopt a second ORM only with a named benchmark in hand — every extra mapping layer is a second schema truth that drifts silently.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Will This New EF Core Feature Be The End Of Dapper?`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Egd-BMPCHNc`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-25H84BXcr9M`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:30,title:`Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:11`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Release Day Half-Commits: The Invitations That Accepted Themselves`,paragraphs:[`It was release day for Gatherly's invitation revamp, and the launch dashboard was green by 10 a.m. By noon, support had a folder of tickets that all read the same: 'I accepted the invite, but I'm not on the attendee list.' The product manager forwarded the first one with a single word: 'Urgent?'`,`The database audit made it worse. Dozens of invitations sat at status Accepted with no matching attendee row. The accept-invitation handler called SaveChanges twice — once right after accepting the invitation, once after adding the attendee. Each call opened its own implicit transaction. The first commit landed the invitation flip; the second hit a unique-constraint violation on a re-invited member and rolled back alone.`,`Nothing crashed. No error reached the user on the first write. The system just quietly told every future reader a lie: this invitation was accepted, even though the attendee never existed. That's the day the team stopped trusting SaveChanges' default boundary and learned to draw their own.`],problem:`Two unrelated implicit transactions inside one request: the first commits the invitation status flip, the second fails and rolls back by itself — leaving Accepted invitations with no attendee rows, silently.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SaveChanges`,`BeginTransaction`,`IDbContextTransaction`,`Isolation Levels`,`Unit of Work`,`Read Committed`],note:`EF Core's implicit per-SaveChanges transactions, the explicit BeginTransaction escape hatch with isolation levels, and a Unit of Work that keeps EF's storage types out of the application layer.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: isn't SaveChanges already transactional?`,body:[`Here's the first thing I want you to internalize: EF Core already gives you transactions for free. Every single call to SaveChanges — or SaveChangesAsync — is wrapped in an implicit transaction. If that one call is tracking five inserts, three updates and a delete, they all hit the database together or not at all. You don't configure it, you don't see it, it just works. That's genuinely great — don't build ceremony around use cases that already fit inside one call.`,`But — and this is the trap — the transaction boundary is the SaveChanges call, not your use case. The moment your handler calls SaveChanges twice, you have two independent transactions in one request. EF Core isn't smart enough to know that 'accept invitation' and 'add attendee' belong together; it only sees two separate flushes. Nobody warns you at compile time. The failure mode is silent partial commits, and those are the worst kind of bug because the first write looks perfectly healthy.`],quote:`EF Core makes each SaveChanges atomic — it has no idea your use case is bigger than one call.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Implicit per SaveChanges`,`Zero setup, automatic rollback on failure`,`Boundary is the call, not the use case`,`Single-flush use cases`],[`Explicit BeginTransaction`,`Covers multiple calls and reads`,`Manual commit, rollback, dispose`,`Multi-step handlers`],[`Ambient TransactionScope`,`Declarative, spans resources`,`Async pitfalls, heavier runtime`,`Legacy or multi-resource flows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where Gatherly half-commits`,body:[`Let's walk the actual handler, because this is where Gatherly bled. We fetch the gathering from the repository, find the invitation matching the request, validate it — not null, status still Pending — and then accept it, which hands us back an attendee. First SaveChanges right there. Then we add the attendee to the attendee repository and call SaveChanges again. Two flushes, two transactions, one use case.`,`Now ask: what can happen between commit one and commit two? A unique constraint on a re-invited member. A deadlock picked as the victim. The pod getting rescheduled mid-request. Any of those and transaction two rolls back — alone. The invitation is already Accepted in the database, the attendee row never lands, and your read model now disagrees with reality. That is exactly what production showed us on release day.`,`The obvious question: why not just merge into one SaveChanges? Honestly — if you can, do it. One flush is the cheapest correct transaction you will ever get. But real codebases push back: repositories own their aggregates, domain events fire between steps, sometimes the transaction needs to cover reads or writes outside EF Core. When the split is structural, you don't shrink the use case — you widen the transaction.`],quote:`Two commits in one request is one commit too many.`,table:{headers:[`Fix`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Collapse to one SaveChanges`,`Free atomicity, no new code`,`Not always possible with repo boundaries`,`Simple handlers`],[`Explicit transaction`,`Covers the whole use case`,`Manual lifecycle to get right`,`Multi-call handlers`],[`Outbox / eventual consistency`,`Decouples side effects`,`More moving parts, read-your-writes gaps`,`Cross-service flows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,at:`1:08`}},{heading:`03 · Reaching for BeginTransaction — and the isolation knob`,body:[`So we reach for the escape hatch: context.Database.BeginTransaction(). The Database facade is EF Core's window into the lower-level database world, and BeginTransaction hands you an IDbContextTransaction. Optionally you pass an isolation level as an argument — that's the knob deciding how much locking applies to the records in your transaction.`,`The menu, straight from the docs: Read Committed — the default — plus Read Uncommitted, Repeatable Read, Serializable, and Snapshot. Read Committed means you never read uncommitted data, but a row can still change between two reads inside your transaction. Serializable locks like it's 1999 and gives you full isolation; Snapshot trades versioned reads for version-store pressure. For the invitation flow we're guarding against partial writes, not phantom reads — Read Committed is plenty, and I deliberately left the isolation argument off the method signature for simplicity.`,`My rule: default first, escalate on evidence. If a concrete anomaly shows up — a lost update, a non-repeatable read that actually corrupts something — then reach for the stronger level and pay the locking bill knowingly. Cargo-culting Serializable across every transaction is how you invent your next deadlock incident.`],quote:`Isolation is a dial you pay for with locks — turn it only when an anomaly proves you must.`,table:{headers:[`Level`,`Prevents`,`Cost`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Read Uncommitted`,`Nothing — dirty reads allowed`,`Cheapest`,`Approximate counts, dashboards`],[`Read Committed`,`Dirty reads`,`Low — the default`,`Most OLTP workloads`],[`Repeatable Read`,`Non-repeatable reads`,`Moderate locking`,`Read-then-write checks`],[`Serializable`,`Phantom reads, all anomalies`,`Highest, deadlock-prone`,`Rare, provable invariants`],[`Snapshot`,`Readers never block writers`,`Version-store overhead`,`Report-heavy reads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,at:`3:29`}},{heading:`04 · Keeping EF's types out of the application layer`,body:[`Here's a subtlety that bites clean architectures. BeginTransaction returns IDbContextTransaction, and that type lives in Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Storage. If my Unit of Work interface — which sits in the application layer — returns that type, I've just smuggled EF Core into every handler that accepts an invitation. The dependency arrow now points the wrong way.`,`The fix is one line: call GetDbTransaction() on the context transaction. That returns the provider's underlying DbTransaction, which satisfies the plain System.Data.IDbTransaction interface. So the Unit of Work exposes BeginTransaction returning IDbTransaction, the implementation wraps EF's transaction internally, and the application layer never hears the words Entity Framework. Swap EF Core out tomorrow and your handlers don't notice.`],quote:`The storage namespace stops at the infrastructure door.`,table:{headers:[`Choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Expose IDbContextTransaction`,`Zero wrapping code`,`EF Core leaks into app layer`,`Throwaway prototypes`],[`Expose IDbTransaction via GetDbTransaction`,`Framework-agnostic interface`,`One adapter method to maintain`,`Clean architecture codebases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,at:`4:41`}},{heading:`05 · The ceremony: try, commit, catch, rollback`,body:[`Last piece is the ceremony, and it's not optional. Begin the transaction, then a try block containing everything that applies your changes plus the Commit call. The catch block calls Rollback and rethrows. And wrap the whole thing in a using, because Dispose is your safety net — if you forget to roll back, disposing the transaction rolls it back anyway before the connection returns to the pool.`,`Why write the explicit rollback if dispose covers you? Intent. The next engineer reading the catch block should see that failure means 'nothing happened', not have to infer it from dispose semantics. And one production-grade caveat: if you've enabled EnableRetryOnFailure for transient fault handling, a manual BeginTransaction will throw unless you run it inside the execution strategy from CreateExecutionStrategy().Execute — retries and manual transactions have to be coordinated, not stacked.`],quote:`Commit is a decision, rollback is the safety net — write both down where the next engineer can see them.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,at:`6:01`}}],diagrams:[{id:`half-commit-failure`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The half-commit: two SaveChanges, two transactions`,caption:`Release-day failure path — commit point 1 persists the invitation flip, commit point 2 fails and rolls back alone.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant H as AcceptInvitation Handler
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant DB as SQL Database
    H->>EF: SaveChanges 1 — invitation accepted
    EF->>DB: BEGIN TRAN
    EF->>DB: UPDATE invitation SET status = Accepted
    EF->>DB: COMMIT
    Note over DB: Commit point 1 — invitation persisted
    H->>EF: SaveChanges 2 — add attendee
    EF->>DB: BEGIN TRAN
    EF->>DB: INSERT attendee
    DB-->>EF: Error — constraint violation
    EF->>DB: ROLLBACK
    Note over H,DB: Invitation Accepted, attendee missing — silent inconsistency`},{id:`explicit-transaction-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Explicit transaction around the whole use case`,caption:`One BeginTransaction spans both writes: commit only after everything succeeds, rollback on any failure.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["BeginTransaction on Database facade"] --> B["TRY"]
    B --> C["Accept invitation in domain"]
    C --> D["Add attendee to repository"]
    D --> E["SaveChangesAsync"]
    E --> F["Commit"]
    F --> G["Invitation Accepted + attendee row together"]
    C --> H["CATCH any exception"]
    D --> H
    E --> H
    H --> I["Rollback"]
    I --> J["Invitation stays Pending — nothing committed"]
    A -.-> K["using disposes transaction as safety net"]`}],takeaways:[`One SaveChanges call equals one implicit transaction — a handler with two calls has two commit points and one inconsistency window between them.`,`When a use case spans multiple SaveChanges calls, wrap it: BeginTransaction, try with the work plus Commit, catch with Rollback, all inside a using.`,`Stay on Read Committed — the default — and escalate to Repeatable Read, Serializable, or Snapshot only when a proven anomaly, not a hunch, demands it.`,`Never leak IDbContextTransaction past the infrastructure layer — return GetDbTransaction() as IDbTransaction from the Unit of Work so handlers stay EF-agnostic.`,`Running with EnableRetryOnFailure? Manual transactions must execute inside CreateExecutionStrategy().Execute, or EF Core throws InvalidOperationException at runtime.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Working With Database Transactions In EF Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=25H84BXcr9M`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`consistency-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-OwCgDPa0DnA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:31,title:`Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:33`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Commented-Out Filter That Merged Two Tenants`,paragraphs:[`It's release day, mid-afternoon, and QA is running the smoke suite with two browser profiles side by side — user A on the left, user B on the right. Both hit GET /notes. B's list looks right until the scroll: three notes down, there's A's grocery list, A's meeting notes, A's everything. Same endpoint, same 200 OK, same JSON shape — wrong rows.`,`The root cause is embarrassingly small. During a debugging session three days earlier, someone commented out the global query filter in the DbContext to 'see all the data for a second,' and the comment survived the merge. Every authorization check in the app lived in application code — the JWT was parsed, the user id extracted, the Where clause applied by hand on every query. One double-slash and the whole isolation model evaporated, silently: no exception, no warning, no log line.`,`The team restored the filter, then did the more interesting thing: they stopped trusting C# to be the last line of defense. They enabled Row-Level Security in Postgres, set the user id into a session variable from an EF Core interceptor, and let the database itself refuse to return rows that don't belong to the current session — no matter what the application code happens to say that day.`],problem:`How do you turn tenant isolation from a coding convention that any refactor can delete into a guarantee the database enforces on every single query?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Npgsql`,`RLS Policies`,`EF Interceptors`,`JWT Claims`,`Aspire`],note:`A .NET API reaches Postgres through EF Core and Npgsql; JWT claims become a session variable set by an interceptor, and RLS policies enforce row scoping inside the database engine itself.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why app-level filters are a convention, not a guarantee`,body:[`Here's the setup everyone ships on day one: the endpoint pulls the user id off the ClaimsPrincipal, the DbContext declares a global query filter like HasQueryFilter(n => n.UserId == userId), and every read comes back scoped. It works. It demos beautifully. And it's a convention — nothing in the database knows or cares that it exists.`,`Watch what happens when I comment that filter out and restart: the same GET returns 200 OK with the same JSON shape, except now it's everybody's notes. No exception, no log, nothing to alert on. That's the scary part — the failure mode of a missing WHERE clause isn't an error, it's an honest answer to a question nobody should have been allowed to ask.`,`So why not just lean on code review? Because the filter has to be re-asserted by every new endpoint, every new query, every hotfix branch. The probability of a leak isn't 'will someone make a mistake' — it's 'when'. I want the guarantee to live somewhere a refactor can't reach.`],quote:`A missing WHERE clause doesn't throw — it just answers honestly.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF global query filter`,`One line in DbContext, typed, testable`,`Silently removable by any refactor`,`Convenience layer`],[`Postgres RLS`,`Enforced by the engine, refactor-proof`,`Session plumbing plus owner-bypass gotchas`,`Guarantee layer`],[`Both together`,`Fast path plus backstop, defense in depth`,`Two places to keep in sync`,`Multi-tenant APIs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwCgDPa0DnA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: how Postgres RLS decides row by row`,body:[`Row-Level Security is a Postgres feature you switch on per table: ALTER TABLE notes ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, then CREATE POLICY with a USING clause — in our case user_id = current_setting('app.app_user')::int. The application sets that session variable, and from then on the engine itself appends the filter to every query touching the table.`,`Don't take my word for it — run EXPLAIN ANALYZE. You'll see an extra filter node in the plan checking user_id against current_setting. That's the policy riding along with your query. And because current_setting is STABLE, Postgres can fold it into index conditions, so with an index on user_id this stays cheap.`,`Why not views with a WHERE clause instead? Views execute with the owner's privileges by default and are just SQL — anything referencing the base table skips them. Why not a separate connection string per tenant? Pool fragmentation: fifty tenants means fifty pools and cold connections everywhere. A session variable keeps one pool and one policy.`],quote:`The policy doesn't know your tenants — it knows the session, and the session can't lie.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation mechanism`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Session variable`,`One pool, one policy, pooling-friendly`,`Must be set on every command`,`Default choice`],[`Per-tenant DB role`,`Policy can key off the role itself`,`Role explosion, fragmented pools`,`Few large tenants`],[`Per-tenant connection string`,`Zero session plumbing`,`One pool per tenant, cold starts`,`Tiny tenant counts`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwCgDPa0DnA`,at:`2:18`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring identity into the wire: EF Core interceptors`,body:[`So how does the session variable get set from a .NET app? Interceptors. EF Core lets you hook its lifecycle events, and on CommandCreating I resolve the user id — from the JWT claims via a scoped service — and prepend SET app.app_user = '...' before the command ships. Set a breakpoint and you can watch it: request lands, query builds, interceptor fires, SET executes, then the SELECT, and the policy does the rest.`,`Why an interceptor instead of doing it in the DbContext constructor? Because SaveChanges batches, raw SQL executes, and migrations all travel through the command pipeline — the interceptor sees every command, not just the ones you remembered. One place, one handshake between HTTP identity and database identity.`,`One more thing people forget: USING governs which rows you can see — SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE. For INSERT and UPDATE you also want WITH CHECK in the policy, so a tenant can't write a row stamped with someone else's user id. Reading safely and writing safely are two halves of the same policy.`],quote:`The interceptor is the handshake: HTTP identity becomes database identity on every single command.`,table:{headers:[`Where to set the variable`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Interceptor CommandCreating`,`Covers every EF command, one prepended statement`,`Raw SQL string needs care`,`Recommended`],[`Transaction-scoped set_config`,`Atomic, auto-reverts on commit or rollback`,`Requires an explicit transaction`,`Strict consistency`],[`On connection open`,`Set once per connection`,`Pooling resets can surprise you`,`Long-lived connections`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwCgDPa0DnA`,at:`6:54`}},{heading:`04 · The three gotchas that bite everyone exactly once`,body:[`Gotcha one: table owners and superusers bypass RLS by default. Our demo connected as postgres — the policy looked enabled and did nothing until we ran FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, or better, gave the app its own non-owner role. If your EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows no policy filter, this is usually why.`,`Gotcha two: connection pooling. A session-level SET outlives the query — it sticks to the connection. Npgsql resets session state when a connection returns to the pool, but that's a provider behavior, not a law of nature; if you're unsure, use set_config('app.app_user', ..., true) so the setting dies with the transaction.`,`Gotcha three: your own tooling. Migrations, seeding, and admin scripts typically run as the owner and suddenly see either everything or nothing once RLS is on. Give operations a dedicated role and decide explicitly what it may bypass — don't let the policy surprise your own deploy pipeline.`],quote:`RLS fails silently in both directions — all rows or no rows — and both look like an application bug.`,table:{headers:[`Gotcha`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Owner or superuser bypass`,`Policy never appears in the query plan`,`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY or a non-owner app role`],[`Pooled session state`,`Tenant A's identity survives pool reuse`,`DISCARD ALL on return or transaction-scoped SET`],[`Migrations and seeds`,`Admin tooling sees everything or nothing`,`Dedicated ops role with explicit bypass`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwCgDPa0DnA`,at:`11:38`}},{heading:`05 · Scaling the pattern to real multi-tenancy`,body:[`Everything we did with user_id generalizes straight to tenant_id: the JWT carries the tenant claim, the interceptor sets app.tenant_id, and each table gets a policy keyed on its tenant column. The pattern scales by repetition — one policy per table — not by cleverness. Keep the EF global query filter too; it keeps LINQ results tenant-shaped and saves the database from scanning rows the policy would discard anyway.`,`Performance-wise, make the tenant column indexed and check the plan at realistic row counts: you want the policy filter folded into the index scan, not bolted on after joins. At millions of rows per table, a policy that evaluates post-join is the difference between milliseconds and seconds.`,`And know when to graduate: shared tables with RLS is the right default for most SaaS, but if you need per-tenant backups, per-tenant performance isolation, or a hard compliance boundary, schema-per-tenant or database-per-tenant is the next rung — at an operational price you pay forever.`],quote:`Start with one table, one policy, one session variable — multi-tenancy scales by repetition, not by cleverness.`,table:{headers:[`Isolation model`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Shared tables + RLS`,`Cheapest to operate, single schema`,`A policy on every tenant table`,`SaaS default`],[`Schema per tenant`,`Strong isolation, per-tenant restores`,`Migration cost scales with tenants`,`Hundreds of tenants`],[`Database per tenant`,`Max isolation, per-tenant tuning`,`Fleet operations and cost`,`Enterprise and compliance`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One request through the RLS handshake`,caption:`Identity flows from the JWT, through the interceptor, into a session variable the policy trusts.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as .NET API
    participant E as EF Core + Interceptor
    participant P as Postgres with RLS
    C->>A: GET /notes (Bearer JWT)
    A->>A: Read user id from ClaimsPrincipal
    A->>E: LINQ query on Notes
    E->>P: SET app.app_user = '42'
    E->>P: SELECT ... FROM notes
    P->>P: Policy filter: user_id = current_setting('app.app_user')
    P-->>E: Rows for user 42 only
    E-->>A: Entities
    A-->>C: 200 OK - tenant-scoped data`},{id:`defense-in-depth`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two layers, one contract`,caption:`The EF filter is the fast path; the RLS policy is the backstop that survives a commented-out line.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Req["GET /notes with JWT"] --> Claim["Resolve user id from ClaimsPrincipal"]
    Claim --> Int["EF Interceptor: prepend SET app.app_user = '42'"]
    Int --> Q["SELECT * FROM notes"]
    Q -->|"filter present"| L1["Layer 1: EF global query filter"]
    Q -->|"filter commented out"| L2["Layer 2: Postgres RLS policy"]
    L1 --> L2
    L2 --> Rows["Only rows where user_id = 42 leave the table"]
    Rows --> Resp["200 OK - tenant-scoped data"]`}],takeaways:[`Run ALTER TABLE ... FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on every tenant table: owners and superusers bypass RLS by default, which silently disables the policy in the ~100% of demos that connect as postgres.`,`Prove engagement with EXPLAIN ANALYZE: the plan must show the user_id = current_setting('app.app_user') filter — if that line is missing, you have a policy, not a protection.`,`Budget one extra statement per scoped command for the SET (or fold it into the transaction with set_config(..., true)) — microseconds per query against an unbounded breach bill.`,`Layer exactly two defenses: the EF global query filter for ergonomics and plan shape, RLS as the refactor-proof backstop — and write a test that deletes the filter and still expects scoped rows.`,`Trust but verify pooling: Npgsql resets session state (DISCARD ALL) when a connection returns to the pool, but confirm your provider's reset behavior or scope the SET to the transaction so a reused connection can never carry tenant A's identity into tenant B's query.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Multi-Tenant APIs Using EF Core and Row-Level Security`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwCgDPa0DnA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-intiD25ixVA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:32,title:`EF Core Bulk Updates: The Big Problem You Must Know About`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:40`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The flash sale that quietly un-discounted itself`,paragraphs:[`Release day, 12:04 PM. Marketing flips the switch on the ten-percent electronics sale, and the pricing service does the modern thing: one ExecuteUpdate over Products where Category = Electronics, setting Price = Price * 1.10. The call returns in 40 milliseconds — 18,204 rows affected. Dashboards go green, the team moves on to the next ticket.`,`What nobody clocked is that the same request handler had earlier loaded a handful of merchandiser-edited SKUs into the DbContext, tweaked a price override in memory, and called SaveChanges at the end of the request. Those tracked entities still carried their pre-sale prices — ExecuteUpdate never says a word to the change tracker — so SaveChanges 'helpfully' wrote the old values right back over the fresh discount.`,`The next morning's audit found 214 SKUs at full price under a banner promising ten percent off. Two writes, one request, two separate transactions — and the second one silently clobbered the first. The sale engine had done exactly what the docs said it would; the rest of the room, like the change tracker, simply never got the memo.`],problem:`ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete fire immediately against the database and completely bypass the change tracker — tracked entities go stale, and any later SaveChanges can silently overwrite the bulk update with old in-memory values.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 7/8`,`ExecuteUpdate`,`ExecuteDelete`,`Change Tracker`,`SaveChanges`,`Set-based SQL`],note:`Two worlds collide: ExecuteUpdate/ExecuteDelete translate LINQ into a single set-based UPDATE or DELETE that runs at once, while the change tracker and SaveChanges remain the deferred, per-entity write path. Every gotcha in this talk lives on the seam between them.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why were we shipping rows we never needed?`,body:[`Let's start where most of us lived before EF Core 7. You need to bump a large set of rows, so you write the polite version: query the entities, loop, mutate, SaveChanges. And EF dutifully materializes every row, tracks every instance, then emits one UPDATE per entity. That's two round trips per row, a change tracker swollen with objects you never actually wanted, and a save phase that feels like watching paint dry.`,`The obvious fix — 'just loop faster, batch the SaveChanges' — doesn't touch the real cost, which is the wire and the tracking, not your C# loop. If you already know the new value at write time, shipping the old value to the client just to compute the new one is pure waste. That's the gap ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete were built to close: LINQ goes in, one set-based statement comes out, and you get the affected row count back.`],quote:`If you already know the new value, paying to ship the old one across the wire is pure waste.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Load + SaveChanges`,`Change tracker, domain events, interceptors, unit-of-work`,`2N round trips, tracks N entities, memory heavy`,`Small sets, per-row domain logic`],[`ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete`,`1 statement, no materialization, rows-affected returned`,`Bypasses tracker, fires immediately`,`Large set-based updates and deletes`],[`Raw SQL / Dapper`,`Full control, provider-specific hints`,`No compile-time LINQ safety, manual mapping`,`Very complex set operations`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Bulk Updates: The Big Problem You Must Know About`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=intiD25ixVA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: LINQ in, one UPDATE out`,body:[`Here's the shape of the API. You start from the DbSet like any LINQ query — context.Products.Where(p => p.Category == "Electronics") — but instead of ToList, you finish with ExecuteUpdate, telling it which property to set and how: SetProperty(p => p.Price, p => p.Price * 1.10m). EF translates that whole expression into a single UPDATE statement and executes it right there. ExecuteDelete is the same story with a DELETE, and both have async overloads.`,`Notice the constraint hiding in plain sight: the new value has to be known at compose time. It's either a constant, a parameter, or an expression over columns — price times 1.10, description plus a suffix — because there is no later phase where you can fill it in. These APIs landed in EF Core 7 and got smoother in EF Core 8, but the model hasn't changed: the query is the write. There is no pending state between composing the query and hitting the database.`],quote:`The query is the write — there's no pending state between compose and commit.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Bulk Updates: The Big Problem You Must Know About`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=intiD25ixVA`,at:`2:33`}},{heading:`03 · The trap: the change tracker never gets the memo`,body:[`Now the part the release notes whisper and production screams. Run the classic demo: load product number one, set its Price to 99.99 in memory, add a brand-new product, then fire an ExecuteUpdate that bumps every electronics price. Look at the tracked product afterwards — its price hasn't moved. Not because EF is buggy, but because ExecuteUpdate never enters the change tracker's universe. The console shows the UPDATE went straight to the database, immediately.`,`And here's where it turns from quirk into incident: if that tracked entity has pending changes, SaveChanges will happily write the stale in-memory values right back over your fresh bulk update. Memory and disk diverged the moment you called ExecuteUpdate, and SaveChanges has no idea. This is exactly how a sale un-discounts itself overnight.`],quote:`Bulk updates write to the database, not to your objects — memory and disk diverge the instant you call them.`,table:{headers:[`Behavior`,`Tracked save (SaveChanges)`,`Bulk (ExecuteUpdate/Delete)`],rows:[[`When it executes`,`Deferred until SaveChanges`,`Immediately at call time`],[`Change tracker`,`Full awareness, diffing, events`,`Completely bypassed`],[`In-memory entities`,`Updated to match the database`,`Left stale and untouched`],[`Transaction scope`,`One unit-of-work transaction`,`Its own implicit transaction`],[`Return value`,`Accepted entity count`,`Rows affected by the SQL statement`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Bulk Updates: The Big Problem You Must Know About`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=intiD25ixVA`,at:`5:29`}},{heading:`04 · Two writes, two transactions: who owns the boundary?`,body:[`Second consequence of 'fires immediately': transactional boundaries. A tracked save batches everything into SaveChanges' single unit of work. The moment you mix in an ExecuteUpdate, you've got two separate transactions — the bulk statement commits even if your later SaveChanges throws, and if the process dies in between, the business operation is half-applied.`,`The fix is boring and non-negotiable: when the bulk write must be atomic with other changes, wrap both in an explicit transaction — Database.BeginTransaction or TransactionScope — and suddenly they commit or roll back as one. Also mind the ordering: the bulk statement reaches the database before any pending tracked changes, which matters to triggers, audit columns, and anyone assuming optimistic concurrency is watching.`],quote:`One method call, one transaction — the moment you mix paradigms, you own the boundary.`},{heading:`05 · So when do I actually reach for it?`,body:[`When the operation is genuinely set-based: you don't need the rows in memory, no domain events have to fire, no interceptors need to see it, and the new value is expressible in SQL. Mass price reindexing, flagging expired sessions, purging old audit rows, soft-delete sweeps — that's ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete territory, and it's dramatically cheaper there.`,`Keep load-then-save when the business rules live inside the entities or SaveChanges' events and interceptors are part of the contract — checkout flows, anything with domain events. And one hygiene rule to tape to your monitor: after a bulk operation touches rows you still hold tracked, reload them or detach them, so the next SaveChanges physically cannot clobber fresh data with stale memory.`],quote:`Use the bulk API for the many, the tracker for the few — and never let the few overwrite the many.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Reach for`,`Why`],rows:[[`Reprice 100k rows by a formula`,`ExecuteUpdate`,`1 statement, no materialization`],[`Purge expired sessions`,`ExecuteDelete`,`Set-based delete, rows-affected logged`],[`Checkout touching cart, order, events`,`Tracked entities + SaveChanges`,`Atomic unit-of-work, domain events fire`],[`Bulk update, then reuse loaded entities`,`ExecuteUpdate + Reload()`,`Resync the tracker, avoid stale writes`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`seq-overwrite`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of the silent overwrite`,caption:`Replay of the flash-sale incident: the bulk update commits in its own transaction, the change tracker never learns, and a later SaveChanges writes stale memory back over fresh data.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant H as Pricing Handler
    participant CT as Change Tracker
    participant DB as Database
    H->>DB: ExecuteUpdate SET price = price * 1.10 WHERE category = Electronics
    DB-->>H: 18204 rows affected - own transaction, already committed
    Note over CT: Never notified - tracked entities keep pre-sale prices
    H->>CT: Merch override applied to tracked SKU
    H->>DB: SaveChanges - UPDATE price = 99.99 from stale memory
    DB-->>H: 1 row affected
    Note over DB: Fresh bulk update silently overwritten by stale memory`},{id:`flow-write-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Choosing the write path`,caption:`Decision flow for picking between tracked saves and the bulk APIs, including the two hygiene checks: atomicity with other writes, and stale tracked entities.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Many rows to update or delete"] --> B{"Need the entities in memory?"}
    B -->|"Yes - events, interceptors, per-row logic"| C["Load tracked entities, mutate"]
    C --> E["SaveChanges - one transaction, tracker aware"]
    B -->|"No - pure set-based change"| D["ExecuteUpdate or ExecuteDelete"]
    D --> F{"Must be atomic with other SaveChanges work?"}
    F -->|"Yes"| G["Wrap both in one explicit transaction"]
    F -->|"No"| H["Single immediate statement, rows affected returned"]
    G --> I["Reload or detach tracked entities the bulk op touched"]
    H --> I
    E --> J["Done - memory and database agree"]
    I --> J`}],takeaways:[`ExecuteUpdate/ExecuteDelete touch 0 tracked entities and issue exactly 1 immediate SQL statement — after calling them, assume every in-memory entity is stale.`,`Mixing bulk APIs with SaveChanges means 2 separate transactions by default; if the operation must be atomic, wrap both in 1 explicit transaction.`,`Load-then-save costs roughly 2N round trips (N SELECTs + N UPDATEs) and N tracked instances; ExecuteUpdate is 1 round trip and 0 tracked instances — switch to it past a few hundred rows.`,`The new value must exist at compose time: express it in SQL (price * 1.10) or as a parameter — you cannot defer the calculation to SaveChanges.`,`After a bulk update touches rows you still hold tracked, reload them (Entry(e).Reload()) or detach them — otherwise the next SaveChanges writes old values over fresh data.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core Bulk Updates: The Big Problem You Must Know About`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=intiD25ixVA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-q09fw5OCa_w`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:33,title:`How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:35`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Cancelled Gathering That Refused to Die`,paragraphs:[`Thursday afternoon, final demo of a long sales cycle. The account team is walking a prospect through the booking flow, and everything is crisp until the 'related gatherings' panel renders an event their own team cancelled three weeks ago — refund issued, venue released, invite list archived. The prospect clicks it. It opens. It's bookable. The room goes very quiet.`,`The postmortem was boring in the most embarrassing way. The rule existed: 'cancelled gatherings are unreadable.' It lived as a hand-written WHERE NOT is_cancelled pasted into fourteen repository methods, each one individually correct. The related-gatherings endpoint, added the previous sprint, was the fifteenth read path — and the first whose author had never been told the ritual. No malice, no clever bug: just arithmetic. Fifteen places to remember one rule means one miss was always scheduled.`,`The fix wasn't a fifteenth WHERE clause. They deleted fourteen of them and declared the predicate once, on the entity itself, in the EF Core configuration: builder.HasQueryFilter(g => !g.IsCancelled). From that release on, every query EF generated carried the condition in its SQL — whether or not the author remembered it.`],problem:`How do you enforce a read-time business rule — 'cancelled gatherings are invisible' — so it holds for every query written next sprint, without depending on the memory of whoever writes it?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`HasQueryFilter`,`LINQ`,`PostgreSQL`,`Soft Delete`,`IgnoreQueryFilters`],note:`EF Core's model configuration declares the predicate once via HasQueryFilter; the LINQ-to-SQL pipeline then injects it into every generated query, with IgnoreQueryFilters as the documented escape hatch.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the WHERE you must remember is the WHERE you'll forget`,body:[`Here's where I start every time someone shows me a repository with the same predicate pasted fifteen times. The SQL is fine. Every one of those WHERE clauses works. The failure mode isn't technical — it's arithmetic. You've taken one business rule, 'cancelled gatherings are unreadable,' and given it fourteen enforcement points. Every new read path — an export endpoint, a related-items sidebar, a reporting projection — is a fresh chance to write the fifteenth one and forget it.`,`And before you say 'just put it in a base repository method' — I've tried that, and it holds until the first query that doesn't go through the repository. Projections, Includes, a new handler that hits the DbSet directly. The rule escapes. The bug class you're fighting is 'invariant with N enforcement points,' and the only durable fix is getting N down to 1.`],quote:`An invariant with fourteen enforcement points is a bug with thirteen known locations.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`WHERE in every method`,`Explicit at each call site`,`Drifts; every new query re-rolls the dice`,`Small, frozen codebases`],[`Base repository helper`,`One place to write it`,`Doesn't cover queries that skip the repo`,`Strict repo-only access`],[`Global query filter`,`Declared once, enforced in SQL`,`Invisible; needs documented escape hatches`,`Cross-cutting read rules`],[`Service-layer check`,`Flexible, language-level`,`Misses any query that bypasses it`,`Never — it's the leaky layer`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q09fw5OCa_w`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the rule lives in the model, not the query`,body:[`Here's the mental shift that makes query filters click: the predicate isn't something you add to queries — it's something you declare about the entity. In the persistence project, in the IEntityTypeConfiguration for Gathering, one line: builder.HasQueryFilter(g => !g.IsCancelled). That's model metadata. EF Core carries it in the model and injects it into the SQL of every LINQ query that touches Gathering.`,`And this is the part I always show on screen: run the same GetById, dump the generated SQL, and the WHERE clause looks exactly like the hand-written one — AND is_cancelled = false — except nobody wrote it in the repository. Same SQL, one declaration. Two mechanics to memorize: you get one filter per entity type — a second HasQueryFilter call replaces the first, so chain conditions with && — and filters defined on a base type are inherited by derived types in a TPH hierarchy.`],quote:`Declare the rule where the entity is born, and every query inherits it for free.`,table:{headers:[`Mechanic`,`What it means`],rows:[[`One filter per entity`,`A second HasQueryFilter call overwrites the first — chain with &&`],[`Injected at SQL generation`,`Shows up in the WHERE clause of every LINQ query against the entity`],[`Inherited by derived types`,`A TPH base filter covers the whole hierarchy`],[`Per-query opt-out`,`IgnoreQueryFilters() restores unfiltered semantics for that query`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q09fw5OCa_w`,at:`2:22`}},{heading:`03 · The sharp edges: every default has an escape door`,body:[`Now the part the demos skip: a global filter is a default, not a law. IgnoreQueryFilters() turns it off for a single query — that's your admin screen, your restore flow, your 'show me everything' backoffice report. Raw SQL bypasses it too: FromSqlRaw never injects your predicate, so any hand-written SQL needs the condition added by hand and a second pair of eyes in review.`,`Then there's identity resolution, which bites someone about once a quarter: if the entity is already tracked by the context, queries and Find hand you the tracked instance — filter or no filter. Watch required navigations as well: a required navigation pointing at a filtered entity can make rows vanish from joins in surprising ways, which is why the EF docs recommend optional navigations when filters are in play. And if you're doing soft delete, a plain UNIQUE constraint is now a landmine — the cancelled row still owns the key, so you need a partial unique index: CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... WHERE is_cancelled = false.`],quote:`A global filter is a default, not a law — enumerate the doors it doesn't lock.`,table:{headers:[`Edge case`,`What happens`,`Countermeasure`],rows:[[`IgnoreQueryFilters()`,`Filter skipped for that one query`,`Reserve for admin/restore paths, document them`],[`FromSqlRaw / raw SQL`,`Predicate never injected`,`Re-add the condition by hand; review raw SQL`],[`Entity already tracked`,`Identity resolution returns the tracked instance`,`Fresh context per request; don't long-track`],[`Required navigation to filtered entity`,`Rows can vanish from joins unexpectedly`,`Prefer optional navigations (per EF docs)`],[`Plain UNIQUE + soft delete`,`Cancelled row still owns the key`,`Partial unique index: WHERE is_cancelled = false`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q09fw5OCa_w`,at:`3:42`}},{heading:`04 · Making it stick: tenant filters, partial indexes, and three tests`,body:[`Two patterns make this feature earn its keep at scale. First, parameterized filters: inject a scoped tenant service into the DbContext constructor, hold it in a field, and close over it — builder.HasQueryFilter(g => g.TenantId == _tenantId). Every query in the request is now tenant-scoped with zero per-query code, and a background job just constructs the context with the right tenant value.`,`Second, testing — and here's the ratio I give teams: don't write fifteen tests for fifteen read paths. Write one integration test that asserts a cancelled id 404s through the public API, plus one test per bypass door you deliberately left open — the IgnoreQueryFilters admin path, the raw-SQL path. Three tests replace the entire copy-paste audit, and what's under test is the model, not the call sites.`],quote:`Test the rule once at the model, then test only the doors you chose to leave open.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`Filter expression`,`Watch for`],rows:[[`Soft delete`,`e => !e.IsDeleted`,`Partial unique indexes; restore needs IgnoreQueryFilters`],[`Multi-tenancy`,`e => e.TenantId == _tenantId`,`Inject tenant via scoped service; background jobs`],[`Cancelled / archived`,`e => !g.IsCancelled`,`Admin views, exports, and any raw SQL`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q09fw5OCa_w`,at:`4:06`}}],diagrams:[{id:`filter-injection-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One declaration, every query`,caption:`The predicate is declared once in model metadata and compiled into the SQL of every LINQ query EF Core generates.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph APP["Application code"]
        A["GET /gatherings/42"] --> B["Repository.GetById<br/>no WHERE on IsCancelled"]
        B --> C["LINQ query on DbSet"]
    end
    subgraph EF["EF Core pipeline"]
        C --> D["Model metadata<br/>HasQueryFilter(g => !g.IsCancelled)"]
        D --> E["Query compiler injects predicate<br/>into every query plan"]
        E --> F["Generated SQL<br/>SELECT ... WHERE Id = @id<br/>AND is_cancelled = false"]
    end
    F --> DB[("PostgreSQL")]
    DB --> H{"Row matches filter?"}
    H -->|"yes"| I["200 OK - gathering returned"]
    H -->|"no"| J["404 - cancelled is invisible"]`},{id:`bypass-decision-map`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Where the filter does not save you`,caption:`A global filter is a default, not a law — map every door that bypasses it and decide, deliberately, which ones stay open.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Q["Every read of a Gathering"] --> K{"How is the query written?"}
    K -->|"LINQ over DbSet"| OK["Filter compiled into SQL - rule holds"]
    K -->|"IgnoreQueryFilters()"| B1["Bypass - restrict to admin/restore"]
    K -->|"FromSqlRaw / raw SQL"| B2["Bypass - re-add predicate by hand"]
    K -->|"Entity already tracked"| B3["Bypass - identity resolution returns it"]
    K -->|"Required nav to filtered entity"| B4["Surprise - rows vanish from joins"]
    OK --> R["Invariant holds by default"]
    B1 --> D["Documented, audited bypass"]
    B2 --> D
    B3 --> D
    B4 --> D`}],takeaways:[`Promote any read rule pasted into 2+ query methods into HasQueryFilter — one declaration replaced our 14 hand-written WHERE clauses and closed the leak on the 15th read path.`,`One filter per entity type: a second HasQueryFilter call silently overwrites the first, so chain conditions with && (g => !g.IsCancelled && g.TenantId == _tenantId).`,`Index what you filter — the predicate lands in every query's WHERE clause; on PostgreSQL use a partial index (WHERE is_cancelled = false) to keep it small and hot.`,`Soft delete breaks plain UNIQUE constraints — a cancelled row still owns the key; pair the filter with a partial unique index or you'll trade read leaks for insert failures.`,`Test the rule once, then test the doors: one integration test that a cancelled id 404s, plus one test per bypass (IgnoreQueryFilters, FromSqlRaw, tracked-entity identity resolution) — 3 tests replace a 15-method audit.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Apply Global Filters With EF Core Query Filters`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q09fw5OCa_w`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-wpbFS6OC5bA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:34,title:`Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:21`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The midday demo where Task.WhenAll met its match`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 11:40 AM, release-review call. The support dashboard endpoint — users, recent orders, audit logs, three totally unrelated tables — was clocking 550 ms in staging. The distributed trace told the whole story: three EF Core queries executing back-to-back, 180 ms plus 210 ms plus 160 ms, strictly one after the other. Someone on the call said the obvious thing: 'They're independent. Just run them in parallel.'`,`So the developer did exactly that, live on the call. Wrapped the three queries in Task.WhenAll, hit refresh, and got greeted by InvalidOperationException: 'A second operation was started on this context instance before a previous operation completed.' Which is deeply confusing the first time, because the code looks fine — three awaits, one shared DbContext injected into the controller. The problem is that WhenAll doesn't await anything before starting the next task; it kicks off all three queries simultaneously against one context that permits exactly one operation at a time.`,`The fix took twenty minutes: register AddDbContextFactory, have each parallel branch create its own context with CreateDbContextAsync, run its query, dispose, and join with Task.WhenAll. The endpoint dropped from ~550 ms to ~210 ms — the slowest query, not the sum. The part nobody put on the slide: every dashboard request now rents three connections from the pool instead of one, and that bill shows up later under load.`],problem:`EF Core allows only one concurrent operation per DbContext instance, so Task.WhenAll over queries sharing a context throws 'a second operation was started on this context.' To parallelize, each task needs its own context — via IDbContextFactory — and you must budget for the multiplied connection demand.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`IDbContextFactory`,`Task.WhenAll`,`Connection Pooling`,`SQL Server`,`Dapper`],note:`A dashboard endpoint, one shared DbContext that forbids concurrency, and the per-task factory plus connection pool that make parallel reads safe.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why Task.WhenAll detonates`,body:[`Let's start with the exception, because it's the moment everyone hits. A DbContext is not thread-safe, and more specifically it's designed around a single active operation: one connection, one open result set, one change tracker being mutated as rows materialize. When you write three queries and hand them to Task.WhenAll, all three start immediately on the same context instance. The second one to touch the context wins a lovely InvalidOperationException, and your endpoint returns a 500 in front of your whole team.`,`The instinctive fix is to wrap the context access in a lock or a SemaphoreSlim(1,1). And sure, that stops the exception — but think about what you've built: a queue. Your 'parallel' queries now execute one at a time anyway, you've just added ceremony and a lock to the sequential version. You paid complexity to keep the sum-of-latencies behavior. That's a bad trade.`,`So the real question isn't 'how do I make this context thread-safe' — it's 'why am I forcing three independent read-only queries through one single-lane pipe at all?' They don't share data, they don't share a transaction, they have no reason to share a context.`],quote:`A DbContext is a single-lane road: Task.WhenAll doesn't add lanes, it just causes crashes.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wpbFS6OC5bA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what 'one operation at a time' really means`,body:[`Here's the boundary as I've come to draw it: the concurrency restriction is per context instance, not per process, not per endpoint, not per database. Nothing stops you from running ten EF Core queries at once — as long as each one owns its own context. Contexts are cheap to create, especially with context pooling, which is exactly why IDbContextFactory exists.`,`Now, the 'why not X' pass. Why not MARS, multiple active result sets on one connection? That's a SQL Server connection-string feature, it applies to raw ADO.NET/Dapper scenarios, and EF Core doesn't give you concurrent queries on one context even with MARS enabled — the context itself is the bottleneck, not just the reader. Why not split into three endpoints and let the frontend fire parallel HTTP calls? It works, but you pay N−1 extra round trips, extra serialization, extra auth checks, and your UI now orchestrates your backend's fan-out. Why not a stored proc that unions everything? Fine until the three datasets diverge in shape or ownership.`,`The clean answer is context-per-task: register the factory with AddDbContextFactory, and each parallel branch does await factory.CreateDbContextAsync(), runs its query, and disposes in a finally. Three contexts, three pooled connections, zero shared mutable state.`],quote:`You don't parallelize the context — you parallelize across contexts.`,table:{headers:[`Quick fix`,`Verdict`,`Why`],rows:[[`Lock around the context`,`Serializes anyway`,`You keep sum-of-latencies and add ceremony`],[`MARS on one connection`,`Doesn't apply`,`SQL Server-only, and the context still forbids concurrency`],[`Split into N endpoints`,`Works, costly`,`N−1 extra HTTP round trips, UI does the orchestration`],[`New context per task`,`This is the one`,`Each task owns a connection; the pool feeds them`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wpbFS6OC5bA`,at:`0:24`}},{heading:`03 · The math, and the catch nobody mentions`,body:[`The performance model is simple enough to put on a napkin. Sequential: total time is the sum of the query latencies — 180 + 210 + 160 is 550 ms. Parallel with separate contexts: total time is roughly the max — about 210 ms, plus a little scheduling overhead. And the win scales with distance: on localhost those queries are so fast the difference is noise, but put the database behind a network with 20–30 ms RTT per round trip and sequential stacking gets brutal, because every query pays its own latency tax on top of execution.`,`Now the catch, and I mean this one genuinely bites people in production. Parallel fan-out multiplies your connection demand. Before: one dashboard request, one connection, for the duration of the request. After: one request, three connections, concurrently. Multiply by your peak requests-per-second and compare against max pool size — the SqlClient default is 100 — and you can starve the pool exactly when traffic peaks. Symptoms look like random timeouts on endpoints that have nothing to do with your dashboard.`,`So the honest decision rule: parallelize when the queries are latency-bound and unrelated — dashboards, aggregation pages, fan-out reads. Stay sequential when each query is a few milliseconds, when the database is already CPU-saturated (parallel just queues at the server), or when you can't afford the connection multiplier. And if you must fan out under heavy load, cap it with a SemaphoreSlim sized to your pool budget.`],quote:`Parallel reads buy you max(latency) and sell you N connections per request.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sequential, one context`,`Simple, 1 connection per request`,`Latency = sum of all queries`,`Fast queries, low traffic`],[`Task.WhenAll + factory`,`Latency ≈ slowest query`,`N connections per request`,`Dashboards, unrelated reads`],[`Split into N endpoints`,`Isolates failures per call`,`Extra HTTP round trips, client complexity`,`Separately-owned services`],[`Dapper + MARS`,`Concurrent queries, one connection`,`SQL Server only, raw SQL`,`Stored-proc-heavy apps`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wpbFS6OC5bA`,at:`2:45`}},{heading:`04 · Shipping the pattern without melting the pool`,body:[`The shape I keep reaching for is a tiny helper: a generic method that takes a Func<AppDbContext, Task<T>>, creates a context from the factory, executes the function, and disposes the context in a finally. Then the endpoint builds three of those, hands them to Task.WhenAll, and awaits the array. Roughly fifteen lines, and suddenly the parallel version is shorter than the sequential one was.`,`Two details that matter when you ship it. First, dispose every context — CreateDbContextAsync hands you an IDisposable/AwaitableDisposable object, and leaking contexts leaks connections straight out of the pool. Second, measure with a distributed trace before and after: you should see the three query spans overlapping in time instead of stacking, which is your proof the parallelism is real and not an illusion behind a lock.`,`And keep the sequential endpoint around during the rollout. Run both under load, watch pool counters and DB CPU, and let the numbers pick the winner per endpoint. Parallelism is a tool for latency-bound fan-out, not a default style — the moment your database becomes the bottleneck, concurrency just moves the queue somewhere less visible.`],quote:`Create, query, dispose — per task. The factory is the whole trick.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wpbFS6OC5bA`,at:`7:07`}}],diagrams:[{id:`seq-vs-par`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Sequential stack vs parallel fan-out`,caption:`Same three queries, two shapes: one shared context stacks latencies; per-task contexts from the factory overlap them.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  REQ["GET /dashboard"] --> SEQ["Sequential\\none shared DbContext"]
  SEQ --> S1["Users · 180 ms"]
  S1 --> S2["Orders · 210 ms"]
  S2 --> S3["Logs · 160 ms"]
  S3 --> ST["Total ≈ 550 ms (sum)"]
  REQ --> PAR["Parallel\\nIDbContextFactory"]
  PAR --> P1["ctx1 · Users"]
  PAR --> P2["ctx2 · Orders"]
  PAR --> P3["ctx3 · Logs"]
  P1 --> W["Task.WhenAll"]
  P2 --> W
  P3 --> W
  W --> PT["Total ≈ 210 ms (max)"]`},{id:`fanout-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request lifecycle with IDbContextFactory`,caption:`Each branch rents its own context and connection from the pool, queries concurrently, and disposes before the join.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant API as Dashboard endpoint
  participant F as IDbContextFactory
  participant P as Connection pool
  participant DB as SQL Server
  API->>F: CreateDbContextAsync() ×3
  F->>P: Rent 3 connections
  P-->>F: conn1, conn2, conn3
  F-->>API: ctx1, ctx2, ctx3
  par users
    API->>DB: SELECT Users (ctx1)
  and orders
    API->>DB: SELECT Orders (ctx2)
  and logs
    API->>DB: SELECT Logs (ctx3)
  end
  DB-->>API: 3 result sets
  API->>API: await Task.WhenAll
  API->>P: Return 3 connections
  API-->>API: 200 OK in ~max(query) ms`}],takeaways:[`One DbContext instance = one concurrent operation, ever. Fan out with IDbContextFactory and give every task its own context — never share one across Task.WhenAll branches.`,`Sequential latency is the sum, parallel is roughly the max: three 150 ms queries drop from ~450 ms to ~150 ms, and the win grows with network RTT to a remote database.`,`Fan-out multiplies connection demand: a 3-query WhenAll rents 3 pooled connections per request — cap concurrency with a SemaphoreSlim or size the pool for peak RPS × fan-out.`,`One endpoint with Task.WhenAll beats splitting into N endpoints: you save N−1 HTTP round trips (tens of ms each) and keep a single response shape for the UI.`,`If each query runs under ~10 ms or the database is already CPU-bound, stay sequential — parallelizing adds pool pressure to save milliseconds nobody will notice.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Running EF Core Queries One-By-One (Do This Instead)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wpbFS6OC5bA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-cUszBUYSFzw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:35,title:`Stop Making This EF Core Transaction Mistake`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:10`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Raise That Half-Happened`,paragraphs:[`It's 9:04 on raise-cycle morning — not some 3 AM apocalypse, just a normal Tuesday. HR pastes a company ID into the internal tool, hits 'apply 4% raise', and walks off to get coffee. The endpoint does the sensible-looking thing: one raw UPDATE to bump 1,200 employee salaries in a single round trip, then SaveChanges to stamp the company's raise-cycle row. Fast, clean, shipped last sprint.`,`Except the company update hits a check constraint — someone made the budget column non-nullable in last night's migration — and SaveChanges fails. EF rolls back its implicit transaction, which contains exactly one row: the company stamp. The salary UPDATE? That was ExecuteSql, running in auto-commit outside the umbrella. It committed the instant the call returned. The API returns 500; the database says 1,200 people just got a raise and no raise ever happened.`,`Finance finds it in reconciliation, not in the logs. The code fix took ten minutes — one explicit transaction around both writes — but the real fix was the model in our heads: know exactly where your implicit transaction ends, because everything past that boundary commits on its own.`],problem:`ExecuteSql runs in auto-commit outside SaveChanges' implicit transaction, so a mid-endpoint failure persists the salary bump while rolling back the company update — two independent commit points in one unit of work, leaving the database half-mutated.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core 7`,`ExecuteSqlRaw`,`ExecuteUpdate`,`BeginTransactionAsync`],note:`The write paths this talk collides: EF Core's tracked, batched SaveChanges, Postgres auto-commit for raw SQL, and the explicit transaction (plus ExecuteUpdate) that has to fuse them into one unit of work.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: isn't SaveChanges already a transaction?`,body:[`Here's the question I get most: 'EF wraps SaveChanges in a transaction anyway, so why would I ever open one myself?' And that's half right. When you call SaveChanges once, EF Core batches every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE it has tracked and ships them inside one implicit transaction — all of it commits, or all of it rolls back. Watch the distributed trace and you'll see multiple statements, one BEGIN, one COMMIT.`,`The trap is the word 'SaveChanges'. That umbrella only covers the change tracker's pending writes. The moment your endpoint also calls ExecuteSqlRaw or ExecuteSql — 'just this one fast UPDATE' — that statement runs on the same connection but outside the umbrella. If no transaction is open, Postgres runs it in auto-commit: it's durable the instant the call returns.`,`So the mental model I want you to leave with: atomicity is per write path, not per request. Mixing write paths in one endpoint is exactly where this mistake lives.`],quote:`SaveChanges is a transaction; your endpoint is not.`,table:{headers:[`Write path`,`Transaction it runs in`,`When it commits`],rows:[[`SaveChanges (tracked)`,`Implicit transaction`,`When SaveChanges succeeds`],[`ExecuteSqlRaw / ExecuteSql`,`Auto-commit (none)`,`The moment the call returns`],[`ExecuteUpdate`,`Current tx if one is open`,`On CommitAsync (or auto-commit if none)`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Making This EF Core Transaction Mistake`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUszBUYSFzw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where the implicit transaction ends`,body:[`Let's replay the incident with that model. The endpoint bumps 1,200 salaries with one raw UPDATE — fast, one round trip, done. Then it stamps the company's raise cycle with SaveChanges. The raw UPDATE already auto-committed; the implicit transaction only ever contained the company row. The check constraint fails, EF rolls back... the company row. The salaries stay raised.`,`Nothing exotic had to happen — no crash, no deadlock, no timeout storm. One failed constraint was enough, because there were two independent commit points and only one of them was protected. And notice the failure mode is quiet: the API returns 500, the salaries are live, and the audit row says no raise ever ran. You find out in reconciliation, not in the logs.`,`Could we just do it all through SaveChanges? Yes — load the company with its employees, bump each salary in memory, save once. That's genuinely atomic. But it's roughly 1,201 statements for 1,200 employees and it materializes every row. Correct, just the wrong tool at bulk scale. That tension — atomic versus set-based — is exactly what the explicit transaction resolves.`],quote:`Auto-commit is still a commit — just not the one you planned.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Tracked SaveChanges only`,`All-or-nothing, change tracker validates`,`Loads every row, ~N statements for N rows`,`Small batches, entity logic needed`],[`Raw SQL + SaveChanges, no tx`,`Fast, familiar`,`Two commit points — half-applied writes`,`Never, in one unit of work`],[`Explicit tx + raw SQL`,`Atomic, fast`,`No type safety, failures surface at runtime`,`Legacy escape hatch`],[`Explicit tx + ExecuteUpdate`,`Atomic, type-safe, 1 statement`,`No entity events or interceptors`,`Bulk sets + tracked writes together`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Making This EF Core Transaction Mistake`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUszBUYSFzw`,at:`3:22`}},{heading:`03 · The fix: one transaction, one commit point`,body:[`The fix is almost boring: open the umbrella yourself. BeginTransactionAsync before the first write, run the bulk update, run SaveChanges, then CommitAsync. Both write paths now enlist in the same transaction on the same connection, and there is exactly one commit point. Either the raise fully happened, or it didn't happen at all.`,`Two details people miss. First, wrap it in await using — disposing a transaction without CommitAsync rolls it back, so every exception path is covered without writing a single catch block. Second, if you've enabled a retrying execution strategy like EnableRetryOnFailure or Npgsql's, a bare BeginTransactionAsync throws InvalidOperationException — you have to start the transaction inside executionStrategy.ExecuteAsync so retries and transactions actually compose.`,`And if you're on EF Core 7 or later, you can often skip the raw string entirely: ExecuteUpdate gives you the same single-statement bulk write, translated from LINQ, and it happily joins the transaction you already opened. Which brings us to type safety.`],quote:`One unit of work, one commit point — anything else is a coin flip.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Making This EF Core Transaction Mistake`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUszBUYSFzw`,at:`6:33`}},{heading:`04 · Type safety: why ExecuteUpdate beats a SQL string`,body:[`The raw SQL version works — until someone renames Salary to BaseSalary and the string keeps 'compiling'. ExecuteSqlRaw fails at runtime, in production, on the one code path your tests didn't cover. ExecuteUpdate is the same shape of operation — one UPDATE, one round trip, no change tracker — but the column names are expression trees. Rename the property and the compiler finds every call site.`,`You write it as a LINQ chain: filter employees by companyId, then ExecuteUpdate with SetProperty(e => e.Salary, e => e.Salary * 1.04m). EF translates that to a single set-based UPDATE. It returns the affected row count, it never materializes entities, and it participates in whatever transaction is already open on the context.`,`The honest trade-off: you give up entity lifecycle events, interceptors, and concurrency tokens — this is a server-side bulk write, not tracked change. And you can't fold it into SaveChanges' implicit batch; the moment you combine it with tracked writes, that's precisely when you need the explicit transaction from the last block. Bulk write plus tracked write plus one transaction — that's the pattern.`],quote:`If LINQ can express it, don't ship it as a string.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`ExecuteSqlRaw`,`ExecuteUpdate`],rows:[[`Failure mode`,`Runtime (string never checked)`,`Compile time (expression trees)`],[`Statements for 1,200 rows`,`1`,`1`],[`Joins an open transaction`,`Yes`,`Yes`],[`Refactor-safe`,`No`,`Yes`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`broken-commit-points`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two commit points, one failure`,caption:`The raw salary UPDATE auto-commits before SaveChanges ever runs — the implicit transaction can only roll back its own half.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant API as Raise endpoint
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant PG as Postgres
    API->>EF: ExecuteSql raw salary UPDATE
    EF->>PG: UPDATE employees SET salary = salary * 1.04
    PG-->>EF: 1200 rows affected (auto-committed)
    Note over PG: Salaries are now permanent
    API->>EF: SaveChanges company row
    EF->>PG: BEGIN (implicit tx)
    EF->>PG: UPDATE companies SET raise_cycle ...
    PG-->>EF: check constraint violation
    EF->>PG: ROLLBACK
    Note over PG: Company update undone,<br/>salaries still raised`},{id:`one-commit-point`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One transaction, one commit point`,caption:`BeginTransactionAsync opens the umbrella; ExecuteUpdate and SaveChanges both enlist; dispose rolls back if anything throws before CommitAsync.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant API as Raise endpoint
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant PG as Postgres
    API->>EF: BeginTransactionAsync()
    EF->>PG: BEGIN
    API->>EF: ExecuteUpdate salary * 1.04
    EF->>PG: UPDATE employees ... (1 statement)
    API->>EF: SaveChanges company row
    EF->>PG: UPDATE companies ... (joins open tx)
    alt every statement succeeds
        API->>EF: CommitAsync()
        EF->>PG: COMMIT
    else anything throws
        Note over EF,PG: await using disposes uncommitted tx
        EF->>PG: ROLLBACK
    end`}],takeaways:[`One SaveChanges call is one implicit transaction over every batched command — if all writes are tracked, you're already atomic; add nothing.`,`The moment ExecuteSqlRaw or ExecuteSql sits beside SaveChanges you have two commit points — wrap the whole unit of work in BeginTransactionAsync/CommitAsync or it can half-apply.`,`1,200-employee raise: tracked row-by-row updates batch ~1,201 statements; one ExecuteUpdate sends 1 — three orders of magnitude fewer round trips.`,`Open transactions with await using: dispose without CommitAsync always rolls back, so every exception path is covered without a single catch block.`,`With a retrying execution strategy (EnableRetryOnFailure, Npgsql), start transactions inside executionStrategy.ExecuteAsync — a bare BeginTransactionAsync throws InvalidOperationException.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Stop Making This EF Core Transaction Mistake`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUszBUYSFzw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`consistency-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-YYgRdMW9Omo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:36,title:`One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:08`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Release Day, 11:52 AM: The Search Box That Lied`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 11:52 AM. Marketing fires the midday campaign and the brand-new "advanced customer search" had shipped an hour earlier with three optional filters: name, email, minimum age. Twenty minutes later the first support ticket lands, and it's a weird one — a search for "ana" returns a customer named Jonas Brecht. A screenshot confirms it: no "ana" anywhere in his name, but he is older than 30. Nobody panics yet. They should have.`,`The query behind the endpoint was one giant WHERE clause with all three optional filters guarded by string.IsNullOrEmpty(x) || ... OR arms — the pattern everyone writes because EF Core dutifully translates it. The age filter had been bolted on last, without its own parentheses. In both C# and SQL, && binds tighter than ||, so the logic silently re-grouped: the age condition started OR-ing into the name check instead of AND-ing against it. Every customer over the threshold matched regardless of name. Silently wrong rows, no exception, no log line. Meanwhile the hedge-everything SQL — @p IS NULL OR ... LIKE @p — forced the planner into scan-shaped plans, and with campaign traffic the connection pool wait time climbed into seconds.`,`Rollback at 12:31. The permanent fix was about forty lines: a WhereIf extension method so each optional filter only enters the query when it's actually set. The generated SQL now matches the request shape exactly, the query reads like the feature spec, and the whole bug class — precedence accidents buried inside a hedge-everything WHERE — is structurally impossible.`],problem:`How do you compose optional filters so the generated SQL contains exactly the filters that were applied — and the C# reads like the intent instead of a precedence trap?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`LINQ`,`IQueryable`,`WhereIf`,`Expression Trees`,`Postgres`],note:`The whole pattern lives in EF Core's IQueryable pipeline: extension methods over expression trees that EF translates into parameterized SQL, with Postgres executing whatever shape the composed query produces.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the innocent SELECT that invites trouble`,body:[`Let's start where everyone starts: context.Customers.ToList(). EF Core sends a beautifully boring SELECT of Id, Name, Email, Age. You run it, you peek at the SQL in the output window or via ToQueryString(), and it's exactly what you expected. Clean slate.`,`Then feature request number one arrives: filter customers by name. So we append Where(c => c.Name.Contains(nameFilter)) and EF translates it into a parameterized LIKE. Still clean, still one predicate, still obviously correct. Here's the thing I want you to notice though — the first filter is never the problem. The trap doesn't spring on filter one. It springs on filter two, and detonates on filter three, when the filters become *optional*.`,`Because "optional" is where the query stops being a statement of intent and starts being a logic puzzle. And logic puzzles in a WHERE clause are exactly the kind of thing that ships fine on Friday and embarrasses you on release day.`],quote:`The first filter is never the problem. The third one is where queries go to die.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where the OR guard starts to rot`,body:[`The reflex most of us have — and honestly, it's a reasonable reflex — is to guard the filter inline: Where(c => string.IsNullOrEmpty(nameFilter) || c.Name.Contains(nameFilter)). Credit where it's due: EF Core is smart here. It parameterizes nameFilter and emits SQL like WHERE @p IS NULL OR @p = '' OR Name LIKE @p, so an empty filter short-circuits in the database and you get all rows back. It works. It even demos well.`,`Now add the email filter the same way. Then the age filter. You've got a hedge-everything WHERE with three OR arms, and two failure modes creep in. First, readability collapse — nobody can review that clause and say with a straight face what it does. Second, and far worse: precedence. In both C# and SQL, AND binds tighter than OR, so a || b && c groups as a || (b && c). Bolt on a third condition without wrapping it in its own brackets and you haven't added a filter — you've *rewired* the query. And it fails silently: wrong rows come back, no exception, nothing in the logs.`,`There's a quieter cost too: the planner now hedges on parameters it can't evaluate at plan time, so you're nudging it toward scan-shaped plans for every request, filtered or not. One shape-fits-all SQL string sounds tidy. It rarely plans tidy.`],quote:`An OR guard in your WHERE is a branch the database re-litigates on every single row.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,at:`0:46`}},{heading:`03 · Why not just if-statements? (and other detours)`,body:[`So why not branch in C#? var query = context.Customers; if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(nameFilter)) query = query.Where(...). This is legitimately fine — the SQL comes out clean per shape. But watch what happens at three, four, five optional filters: either a staircase of nested ifs or a combinatorial copy-paste mess, and every developer after you re-solves the same puzzle slightly differently. The pattern is begging to be an extension method.`,`What about pulling all customers and filtering in memory? For a demo entity with ten rows, sure. For a real customers table under campaign traffic, you've just traded a WHERE clause for a full-table materialize on every keystroke. Never for entity lists. What about a full dynamic-query library where the client sends arbitrary predicates? Right tool when users genuinely build their own queries; heavy overkill for a fixed set of optional filters on one endpoint.`,`The sweet spot is embarrassingly small: encode the branch-once pattern into a tiny extension, and every optional filter in your codebase gets the same, reviewable treatment. Let me put the options side by side.`],quote:`Every hand-rolled branch around a query is a pattern begging to become an extension method.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline OR guards`,`One SQL string, no C# branches`,`Precedence traps, unreadable at 3+ filters, hedge-shaped plans`,`1-2 filters, max`],[`C# if-branches`,`Clean SQL per shape`,`Copy-paste explosion as filters grow`,`Small, fixed forms`],[`WhereIf extension`,`Composable, SQL matches intent, one idiom`,`Must take Expression, needs team discipline`,`Default for optional filters`],[`Filter in memory`,`Simplest possible code`,`Full table loads, latency and memory blowups`,`Never for entity lists`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,at:`1:50`}},{heading:`04 · Building WhereIf live, in ten lines`,body:[`Here's the whole method: public static IQueryable<T> WhereIf<T>(this IQueryable<T> source, bool condition, Expression<Func<T, bool>> predicate) => condition ? source.Where(predicate) : source; That's it. Two details carry all the weight, and both are where people trip.`,`Detail one: the predicate must be Expression<Func<T, bool>>, not Func<T, bool>. The expression tree is the thing EF Core translates into SQL — hand it a compiled Func and translation either throws or silently falls apart. Detail two: the condition is evaluated at *composition time*, in C#, before EF ever sees the tree. If the filter isn't set, the Where call never happens, so the clause physically cannot appear in the SQL. The branch lives in the query builder, not in the database.`,`Usage then reads like the feature spec: .WhereIf(!string.IsNullOrEmpty(nameFilter), c => c.Name.Contains(nameFilter)).WhereIf(!string.IsNullOrEmpty(emailFilter), c => c.Email.Contains(emailFilter)).WhereIf(minAge.HasValue, c => c.Age >= minAge.Value). Each request gets a SQL shape that contains only its own filters — and the missing-parentheses bug from our release-day story is impossible by construction, because there are no OR arms to mis-group.`],quote:`The branch belongs in the query builder, not in the SQL.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,at:`2:18`}},{heading:`05 · The payoff — and what WhereIf will not fix for you`,body:[`The payoff is threefold: SQL per request contains only the filters actually applied, precedence bugs are structurally gone, and the query reads like the ticket it implements. The honest trade-off: you now emit a handful of SQL shapes instead of one, which means a few more distinct plans to manage — a real consideration on SQL Server's plan cache, much less so on Postgres. In practice that's almost always a favorable trade versus one hedge-everything plan that's mediocre for every request.`,`Now the part I insist on saying out loud: WhereIf fixes *composition*, not *sargability*. Contains still compiles to a leading-wildcard LIKE '%term%', and that's a scan no matter how beautiful your query reads. If search is hot-path, pair it with a pg_trgm GIN index on Postgres or full-text search — that's a different lever, and no extension method pulls it for you. Same for missing indexes on filtered columns and N+1s from lazy navigation. Different muscles.`,`So make ToQueryString() a dev-time habit: run each filter combination, eyeball the SQL. If you ever see IS NULL OR arms you didn't intend, the composition has rotted and you'll catch it at your desk instead of at 11:52 on release day.`],quote:`Composition hygiene and index design are different muscles — WhereIf only trains the first.`,table:{headers:[`WhereIf fixes`,`WhereIf does not fix`],rows:[[`SQL contains only applied filters`,`Non-sargable LIKE '%term%' scans`],[`AND/OR precedence bugs, by construction`,`Missing indexes on filtered columns`],[`Query reads like the feature spec`,`N+1s from lazy navigation misuse`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,at:`3:46`}}],diagrams:[{id:`whereif-composition`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`WhereIf composition pipeline: filters gate the query before EF ever sees them`,caption:`Each optional filter is evaluated in C# at build time — unset filters emit no SQL clause at all.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Search request<br/>name + email + minAge"] --> B{"nameFilter set?"}
    B -->|yes| C["Append Where:<br/>Name.Contains(name)"]
    B -->|no| D["Skip — no clause emitted"]
    C --> E{"emailFilter set?"}
    D --> E
    E -->|yes| F["Append Where:<br/>Email.Contains(email)"]
    E -->|no| G["Skip — no clause emitted"]
    F --> H{"minAge set?"}
    G --> H
    H -->|yes| I["Append Where:<br/>Age >= minAge"]
    H -->|no| J["Skip — no clause emitted"]
    I --> K["Composed IQueryable"]
    J --> K
    K --> L["EF Core translates<br/>expression tree to SQL"]
    L --> M["SQL contains only<br/>the filters actually applied"]`},{id:`request-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request lifecycle: from filters to parameterized SQL`,caption:`The hedge arms (IS NULL OR ...) never reach the database — only the predicates the request actually needs.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Search Endpoint
    participant QB as Query Builder
    participant EF as EF Core
    participant PG as Postgres

    API->>QB: nameFilter, emailFilter, minAge
    QB->>QB: WhereIf evaluates each condition in C#
    Note over QB: Only set filters append a Where clause
    QB->>EF: Final IQueryable expression tree
    EF->>EF: Translate tree to parameterized SQL
    EF->>PG: SELECT ... WHERE Name LIKE @p AND Age >= @q
    Note over EF,PG: No IS NULL OR hedge arms in the SQL
    PG-->>EF: Matching rows
    EF-->>API: List<Customer>`}],takeaways:[`Three or more optional filters: switch to WhereIf before the WHERE clause passes ~15 lines of OR guards.`,`The extension is ~10 lines of code; a 5-filter hedge-WHERE is ~5x longer and one missing parenthesis away from a precedence bug.`,`Always accept Expression<Func<T, bool>> in the extension — a Func overload silently kills SQL translation and composition.`,`Contains compiles to LIKE '%term%' — non-sargable no matter how clean the composition; add pg_trgm/GIN indexes (Postgres) or full-text search for hot search columns.`,`Call ToQueryString() on every filtered query in dev — if you spot IS NULL OR arms you didn't intend, the composition has rotted.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — One LINQ Extension Method You NEED To Know For Cleaner EF Core Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYgRdMW9Omo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-vhshkKRHE9o`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:37,title:`EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 10:04`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`Release Day: The Left Join We Accidentally Deleted`,paragraphs:["Tuesday, 11:40 AM. A vendor's flagship product goes live on our marketplace, and the catalog endpoint — 'products with their reviews' — starts the day like any other. Except that the previous sprint, a well-meaning cleanup had replaced the gnarly `join ... into` + `DefaultIfEmpty` query with a tidy `Join`, because the diff read better. Nobody noticed the semantics had quietly changed from a left join to an inner join. The compiler said nothing. The tests passed, because the seed data had no orphans.",`The launch product had zero reviews — it was brand new. So it silently vanished from the storefront listing. By noon there were merchant escalations, support tickets blaming the search team, and a lunchtime war-room. The hotfix was literally one keyword. But the postmortem question stuck: why did our most important join have no name in the language?`,"The quarter before, the same endpoint had collected a different scar: paging over joined rows meant a single product with 50 reviews ate the entire page — `Take(20)` rendered one espresso machine. Two bugs, one root cause: LINQ made the easy join verbose and the dangerous join invisible."],problem:`The endpoint must return every product — reviewed or not — with its reviews attached, as an async Task, without silently dropping zero-review rows or fanning the page out of existence.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 10`,`LINQ LeftJoin`,`RightJoin`,`DefaultIfEmpty`,`SQL Server`],note:`EF Core 10's first-class LeftJoin/RightJoin operators, the GroupJoin + DefaultIfEmpty ceremony they replace, and the SQL LEFT/RIGHT JOIN that EF translates them to.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the old pattern hurt`,body:["So before EF Core 10, a left join meant query syntax: `from p in Products join r in Reviews on p.Id equals r.ProductId into g`, then `from r in g.DefaultIfEmpty()`, then your projection. It works, it translates fine — but that's four lines of ceremony to say one word SQL has had forever. And the method-syntax version with GroupJoin + SelectMany is where most people's eyes glaze over.",`Here's the part that bit us: the ceremony isn't just ugly, it's fragile in the worst way. Swap GroupJoin for Join, or drop the DefaultIfEmpty line, and you've silently turned it into an inner join. No compiler error, no failing test if your seed data happens to have no orphans. That is exactly the refactor that killed our launch product at midday.`,"Now, why not just use navigation properties? Honestly, that's the right answer most of the time — `Select(p => new { p, p.Reviews })` is cleaner and EF writes the join for you. But it hands you nested collections, and sometimes you want flat rows for a DTO, or there's no navigation because the relationship is keyless or lives in another context. LeftJoin gives you the flat shape with one operator."],quote:`Every line of join ceremony is a place for the next refactor to change your semantics.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`GroupJoin + DefaultIfEmpty`,`Works on EF Core 3–9`,`4+ lines, fragile semantics`,`Legacy codebases`],[`EF Core 10 LeftJoin`,`One operator, real LEFT JOIN`,`Needs .NET 10 / EF 10`,`New default`],[`Navigation projection`,`No explicit join, EF plans it`,`Nested shape, fan-out risk`,`Object graphs`],[`Raw SQL`,`Total control`,`No compile-time checks`,`Window fns, edge cases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhshkKRHE9o`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what actually ships to the database`,body:["Let's walk what actually goes over the wire. `Products.LeftJoin(Reviews, p => p.Id, r => r.ProductId, (p, r) => ...)` translates to a plain LEFT JOIN — every product row comes back, and where there's no matching review, the review columns are NULL. Order by product id, then review id, and your flat rows stay grouped per product, which keeps the result readable.",`RightJoin is the mirror image: every review comes back even if the product row is gone. That sounds exotic until you run an orphan sweep after a hard delete or an ETL load — reviews pointing at products that no longer exist. One RightJoin query per release in CI catches that for free. And a nice bonus: .NET 10 added the same operators to Enumerable, so the mental model transfers to in-memory joins too.`,`Why not just fire two queries and stitch in memory? You can — products in one round trip, reviews in another, merge on the client. But now the join lives in application code, you lose server-side ordering and paging, and you've signed up for the coordination yourself. The database has done this well for decades; let it keep the job.`],quote:`The join you can read is the join you can review.`,table:{headers:[`Operator`,`Preserves`,`SQL emitted`,`Typical use`],rows:[[`LeftJoin`,`All left rows, NULL-filled`,`LEFT JOIN`,`Catalogs, dashboards`],[`RightJoin`,`All right rows`,`RIGHT JOIN`,`Orphan sweeps, coverage`],[`Join`,`Matches only`,`INNER JOIN`,`Strict relational reads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhshkKRHE9o`,at:`1:00`}},{heading:`03 · The trap nobody warns you about: fan-out`,body:["Now the trap. A join across a one-to-many multiplies rows: one product with 50 reviews becomes 50 result rows. That's correct SQL semantics, and it will wreck your paging — `Take(20)` is 20 rows, not 20 products. We shipped exactly that bug: a page that claimed 20 items and showed one espresso machine.","If the UI only needs a count, don't join at all — project `p.Reviews.Count()` and EF translates it to a correlated subquery: one row per product, zero fan-out. If you need 'latest review per product', that's a top-1-per-group problem — a window function in raw SQL, or a subquery on max review id. Reach for the flattened LeftJoin only when row-per-match is genuinely the shape the consumer wants.",`And if you do take the fan-out, page by the left key, not by row offset — otherwise the NULL-filled and duplicated rows make Skip/Take lie to you.`],quote:`Row count is a contract: if the page says 20 products, the query must return 20 product rows.`,table:{headers:[`Shape`,`Rows returned`,`Paging safe?`,`Use when`],rows:[[`Flattened LeftJoin`,`1 per match`,`Only by left key`,`Flat DTO lists`],[`Count subquery`,`1 per product`,`Yes`,`Badges, counts`],[`Navigation projection`,`Nested sets`,`Yes (split query)`,`Full graphs`],[`Window function (raw)`,`1 per group`,`Yes`,`Latest-per-group`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhshkKRHE9o`,at:`1:54`}},{heading:`04 · Choosing the shape in code review`,body:[`So here's how I call it in review now. If the requirement reads 'show all X with optional Y', that's LeftJoin, full stop — the nulls are the feature, not a bug to defend against. If it reads 'find Y with no X', that's RightJoin or a reversed left join, and it's usually an audit, not a product feature. If it reads 'count of Y per X', it's a subquery, not a join.`,`One discipline rule I've added to my checklist: every consumer of a LeftJoin result needs a null branch on the right side — the mapping, the DTO, the renderer. One missed check and your first zero-review product in production is a NullReferenceException at midday. And yes, materialize with ToListAsync — the endpoint returns a Task, and the await is the whole point.`],quote:`A left join without a null check is an inner join waiting to throw.`,table:{headers:[`Review signal`,`Reach for`,`Why`],rows:[[`'All X with optional Y'`,`LeftJoin`,`Nulls are the feature`],[`'Y with missing X'`,`RightJoin`,`Coverage / audit`],[`'Count of Y per X'`,`Count subquery`,`No fan-out`],[`'Page of X with Y'`,`LeftJoin, page by X key`,`Rows must equal items`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhshkKRHE9o`,at:`2:30`}}],diagrams:[{id:`leftjoin-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From LINQ LeftJoin to SQL LEFT JOIN`,caption:`EF Core 10 translates the operator directly — no GroupJoin ceremony in between.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["db.Products.LeftJoin(db.Reviews, p => p.Id, r => r.ProductId, selector)"] --> B["EF Core 10 query pipeline"]
    B --> C["SQL: FROM Products p<br/>LEFT JOIN Reviews r<br/>ON r.ProductId = p.Id"]
    C --> D{"Match found?"}
    D -- "yes" --> E["product + review row"]
    D -- "no" --> F["product + NULL review row"]
    E --> G["ToListAsync()"]
    F --> G`},{id:`left-join-semantics`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Four products, three reviews, five result rows`,caption:`Products without reviews survive as NULL-filled rows — that's the whole point.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph Products["Products"]
        P1["P1 · Espresso Machine"]
        P2["P2 · Grinder — no reviews"]
        P3["P3 · Kettle"]
        P4["P4 · Scale — no reviews"]
    end
    subgraph Reviews["Reviews"]
        R1["R1 → P1"]
        R2["R2 → P1"]
        R3["R3 → P3"]
    end
    subgraph Result["LEFT JOIN result — 5 rows"]
        X1["P1 + R1"]
        X2["P1 + R2"]
        X3["P2 + NULL"]
        X4["P3 + R3"]
        X5["P4 + NULL"]
    end
    P1 --> X1
    P1 --> X2
    P2 --> X3
    P3 --> X4
    P4 --> X5`}],takeaways:[`EF Core 10's LeftJoin collapses the 4-line GroupJoin + SelectMany + DefaultIfEmpty ceremony into 1 operator that still emits a real SQL LEFT JOIN.`,`Fan-out math: a product with 50 reviews becomes 50 joined rows — page by the left key, or Take(20) can render as few as 1 product.`,`Counts are not joins: p.Reviews.Count() translates to a correlated subquery (1 row per product, zero fan-out) — use it whenever the UI only needs a number.`,`Null discipline: every consumer of a LeftJoin result needs a null branch on the right side; the first zero-review row in production is where the missing check throws.`,`Run one RightJoin orphan sweep per release (children pointing at deleted parents) — catching orphans in CI costs minutes; in production it costs a merchant escalation.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — EF Core 10 Finally Adds LeftJoin + RightJoin (Too Little, Too Late?)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhshkKRHE9o`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-ReQXiZpfxBI`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:38,title:`DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 10:02`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`Onsale Day, and the Model Was Skimming Cents`,paragraphs:[`It was 10:04 on a festival onsale — peak traffic, not some quiet night — and the ticketing API wasn't down. That was the unsettling part. Orders flowed, seats assigned, confirmations sent. Then finance flagged it: refunds were coming out one and two cents off, across thousands of transactions, all drifting in the same direction.`,`The event-management Postgres database had arrived with an acquisition — wiki-documented, DBA-owned, older than the team's tenure. They had hand-written their EF Core entities from that wiki page, last edited fourteen months earlier. The price column was numeric(10,2); the entity said double. Binary floating point can't represent 0.10 exactly, and every refund calculation quietly drifted. Meanwhile a DBA had widened the seat column in prod months ago, and no model anywhere knew.`,`The fix took one command, not a war room: dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold pointed at the live database, letting the schema write its own biography. The rule that stuck afterward: the database is the source of truth, and the model is regenerated — never transcribed.`],problem:`How do you keep an EF Core model honest about a database you didn't design, don't own, and can't reshape on a whim?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core 9`,`.NET 9`,`Aspire`,`Docker`,`DBeaver`,`dotnet-ef`],note:`Aspire stands up a persistent Postgres container with a data volume and injects the connection string; dotnet-ef scaffolding turns the live schema into a DbContext and entities; DBeaver is where the seed SQL meets the database.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why hand-written models lie`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody tells you when you start with EF Core: the tutorials all assume you own the database. Code-first, migrations, the whole pipeline assumes your C# is the source of truth. But the moment you inherit a schema — an acquisition, an internal platform, a DBA-governed estate — that assumption flips. The database was here first, and it will be here after your pull request.`,`So the first instinct is to hand-write entities from the docs. Don't. Documentation is a snapshot of someone's good intentions; the schema is what actually runs. Every hand-transcribed column is a bet that nobody has touched that table since the wiki page was written. In my story, that bet lost money — literally, two cents at a time.`,`The alternative is to make the database write its own model. That's what scaffolding is: EF Core reads the live schema through the provider and generates the DbContext and entity classes from what's actually there. You trade away migration authority — you're no longer the one evolving the schema — and in exchange, your model can't drift. For brownfield databases, that's the best trade on the board.`],quote:`A hand-written model is a rumor about the schema; a scaffolded model is a photograph.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Code-first migrations`,`Versioned schema history, reviewable diffs`,`You must own the database`,`Greenfield, single team`],[`DB-first scaffold`,`Model always matches the live schema`,`No migration authority; regen discipline needed`,`Brownfield, DBA-owned DBs`],[`Hand-written entities`,`Total control, zero tooling`,`Drifts the moment the DB changes`,`Tiny, frozen schemas only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Standing up Postgres before the code exists`,body:[`Before we can scaffold anything, we need a database that behaves like a real one. I'm using Aspire because it solves the boring parts: one line in the AppHost — AddPostgreSQL — and I get a container, a port, and a password without writing a compose file. Then AddDatabase('eventmanagement') gives me a named logical database I can reference from the API project, and the connection string arrives as injected configuration, not a hardcoded constant.`,`Two knobs matter more than people expect. WithLifetime(ContainerLifetime.Persistent) keeps the Docker container alive across restarts — otherwise every F5 throws away your database. And WithDataVolume puts the data on a volume, so even a container replacement keeps your seeded rows. Skip either one and you'll re-run your seed script twice a day, then once a week, then never — and quietly test against an empty schema.`,`One more detail: Aspire assigns the Postgres port randomly. Don't hardcode it. Read host, port, and password from the dashboard or configuration, and hand them to DBeaver when you want to poke at the schema. The connection string is a runtime fact, not a build-time constant.`],quote:`If your dev database dies with your debugger session, you'll stop trusting your own tests.`,table:{headers:[`Aspire knob`,`What it buys you`,`Skip it and...`],rows:[[`ContainerLifetime.Persistent`,`Container survives app restarts`,`Re-seed after every F5`],[`WithDataVolume`,`Data survives container replacement`,`Lose seeded rows on rebuild`],[`AddDatabase + WithReference`,`Connection string injected as config`,`Hardcode localhost and a magic port`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,at:`0:50`}},{heading:`03 · Seeding the schema: SQL is the migration`,body:[`Now the mental flip. In code-first, the migration folder is the history book and the database is just the latest page. In DB-first, it's inverted: the SQL script is the history book. I open DBeaver, connect with the details from the Aspire dashboard, and run the seed script that creates the event-management tables and data. That script lives in source control, versioned and reviewed, exactly where migrations would live.`,`Notice what's missing: no __EFMigrationsHistory table driving anything, no dotnet ef database update in the loop. The schema exists because SQL said so. EF Core's job is now to describe reality, not dictate it — which is why scaffolding comes after seeding. You can't photograph a room you haven't built.`,`This is also why the seed SQL belongs in the repo rather than in clicks. Clicks aren't reproducible; scripts are. When a teammate spins up the AppHost, they run the same script, get the same schema, and scaffold the same model. Reproducibility is the whole game.`],quote:`In DB-first, the migration folder is empty on purpose — the schema's history lives in SQL, not in C#.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,at:`1:17`}},{heading:`04 · The scaffold command, flag by flag`,body:[`Here's the command that does the real work. First, tooling: the dotnet-ef global tool, Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design in the API project, and the provider package — for Postgres that's Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL. Then: dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold with the connection string, the provider name, and a handful of flags that decide whether this is a pleasant workflow or a one-shot miracle.`,`The flags I never skip: -o Models/Generated quarantines everything the generator writes so it can't stomp my own files. --no-onconfiguring keeps the connection string out of the generated context — I register it with AddDbContext in Program.cs and let configuration own the connection. --force makes the command re-runnable, which is the entire point: schema changed? Run it again, thirty seconds, model's honest again. And against a shared database, --schemas and --table keep the context from ballooning into a 200-class museum of tables I'll never touch.`,`One habit worth building: after scaffolding, actually read the generated OnModelCreating. It's a complete fluent description of your schema — keys, indexes, string lengths, defaults. It's the most accurate documentation your database will ever have, because it was generated from the database, not from someone's memory of it.`],quote:`Scaffold with --force and --no-onconfiguring, and regenerating becomes a pet you can rerun instead of a one-shot miracle.`,table:{headers:[`Flag`,`Why it matters`,`If you skip it`],rows:[[`-o Models/Generated`,`Quarantines generator output`,`Regen overwrites hand-written code`],[`--no-onconfiguring`,`Connection stays in configuration`,`Secrets baked into generated files`],[`--force`,`Makes the command re-runnable`,`Stale model after every schema change`],[`--schemas / --table`,`Filters to tables you own`,`A 200-class context for a shared DB`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,at:`1:56`}},{heading:`05 · When DB-first fits — and when it bites`,body:[`So when does DB-first actually fit? Brownfield, obviously — the schema exists and it's authoritative. Shared databases where a DBA or another team owns the DDL — you couldn't run migrations against it even if you wanted to. Polyglot estates where three apps and a reporting tool hit the same Postgres — one schema, many consumers, nobody gets to be the source of truth.`,`When does it bite? Greenfield products, where migrations give you a versioned, reviewable history that evolves with your code. And watch the edges of scaffolding: views and stored procedures need manual mapping, and any tweak you make inside the generated files dies on the next --force. The defense is discipline: never edit generated files — put customization in partial classes or separate IEntityTypeConfiguration classes outside the generated folder.`,`Finally, make regeneration boring. The strongest version of this workflow is a CI job that scaffolds against the real schema and diffs the output — drift gets caught in minutes, in a pull request, instead of on onsale day in a refund report. DB-first isn't the opposite of EF Core; it's EF Core with the arrow of truth reversed. Accept which direction the truth flows, and everything else is mechanics.`],quote:`DB-first isn't the opposite of EF Core — it's EF Core with the arrow of truth reversed.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`DB-first fit`,`Why`],rows:[[`Brownfield / acquired DB`,`Strong`,`Schema exists and is already authoritative`],[`DBA-governed shared DB`,`Strong`,`You don't own the DDL anyway`],[`Greenfield product`,`Weak`,`Migrations give versioned, reviewable history`],[`Stored-proc-heavy app`,`Mixed`,`Scaffold misses procs; map them by hand`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,at:`2:31`}}],diagrams:[{id:`db-first-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The DB-first loop: the database writes the model`,caption:`Aspire stands up a persistent Postgres, the seed script defines the schema, and the scaffold step pulls the model out of the live database — the arrow of truth points from DB to code.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Host["Aspire AppHost"]
    A["AddPostgreSQL('postgres')"] --> B["Persistent container + data volume"]
    B --> C["eventmanagement database"]
  end
  subgraph API["Ticketing API (.NET 9)"]
    S["dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold"] --> G["Generated DbContext + entities"]
    G --> P["Program.cs: AddDbContext"]
  end
  D["DBeaver"] -- "seed SQL script" --> C
  C -- "host / port / password" --> D
  C -- "connection string via env" --> S
  P -- "queries" --> C`},{id:`scaffold-run`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One scaffold run, end to end`,caption:`The CLI introspects the live schema, writes the model into a quarantined folder, and the app registers it through DI — the connection string never lands in generated code.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant Dev as Developer
  participant CLI as dotnet-ef CLI
  participant PG as Postgres DB
  participant Gen as Generated folder
  participant App as Program.cs
  Dev->>CLI: dbcontext scaffold, conn string, Npgsql provider
  CLI->>PG: introspect tables, columns, keys, indexes
  PG-->>CLI: schema metadata
  CLI-->>Gen: EventManagementContext.cs + entity classes
  Dev->>App: register context via AddDbContext
  App->>PG: queries against live schema
  Note over Gen,App: --no-onconfiguring keeps the connection string in config`}],takeaways:[`Never hand-transcribe a schema beyond ~5 tables — scaffold it instead; hand-written models drift from prod within the first sprint.`,`Run scaffold with --force --no-onconfiguring -o Models/Generated so a full regen takes ~30 seconds and never stomps your custom code.`,`Set ContainerLifetime.Persistent plus WithDataVolume in Aspire, or you'll re-seed Postgres on every F5 — two wasted seed runs a day, minimum.`,`Install the provider package (Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL) and pass it explicitly to scaffold — an unspecified provider is a guaranteed NuGet scavenger hunt.`,`Add a CI drift check that scaffolds against the real schema and diffs the output — catch drift in minutes in a PR, not on onsale day in a refund report.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — DB-First with EF Core: Scaffold Your DbContext from an Existing Database (Step by Step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ReQXiZpfxBI`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-SSV7yaH4n9g`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:39,title:`Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:55`,minutes:9,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`],story:{headline:`The Tuesday Demo Where Tenant B Saw Tenant A's 'Deleted' Orders`,paragraphs:[`It was a quiet Tuesday afternoon demo — our account exec sharing her screen with our biggest customer's ops team, walking them through the new order-history page. Halfway through, she hit refresh and an order appeared that this customer had deleted weeks ago. Worse, it wasn't even theirs: the tenant ID belonged to a completely different customer. She kept narrating the feature; I stopped breathing.`,`The post-mortem took twenty minutes because the offending code was innocent-looking. Two weeks earlier, a teammate had added soft delete the way every tutorial shows: HasQueryFilter(o => !o.IsDeleted) in OnModelCreating. Under the old EF Core rules — one query filter per entity — that single line silently replaced our tenant-isolation filter. No exception, no warning, the model built clean, and every orders query started returning other tenants' rows, deleted or not.`],problem:`EF Core only allowed a single query filter per entity, so a second HasQueryFilter call silently overwrote the first — no error, no log, just a cross-tenant leak discovered live on a customer call.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 10`,`Named Query Filters`,`SQL Server`,`.NET Aspire`,`LINQ`],note:`EF Core 10's named query filters compile into the WHERE clause of every query against an entity; .NET Aspire hosting a SQL Server resource gives us the distributed trace where the generated SQL is visible.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: what a query filter actually buys you`,body:[`Let's start from the basics, because the magic is easy to take for granted. A query filter is a predicate you attach to an entity in OnModelCreating via HasQueryFilter, and EF Core turns it into a SQL WHERE clause on every query against that entity — direct queries, navigations, joins, all of it. You write the predicate once, the database enforces it everywhere.`,`The classic use cases are exactly the ones that bite you when forgotten: soft delete (IsDeleted equals false), multi-tenancy (TenantId equals the current user's tenant), maybe an active/inactive flag. Anything you'd otherwise paste into forty different LINQ queries becomes one line of model configuration. The trade-off? It's invisible. You have to read the generated SQL to remember it's even there — which is exactly why we always verify through the trace span.`],quote:`A query filter is the WHERE clause you never wrote — and never get to forget.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the one-filter ceiling`,body:[`Here's where the old model hurt. For years, the API allowed exactly one filter per entity. Call HasQueryFilter a second time and the second predicate silently replaces the first — no exception, no log line, nothing. That's the failure mode from our story: soft delete landed, tenant isolation vanished, and the app kept green tests because nobody asserted on the model itself.`,`So teams worked around it. You combined predicates with && into one mega-filter, usually fed by a tenant provider injected through a DbContext constructor. It works, but it's brittle: you can't toggle pieces independently, IgnoreQueryFilters() with no arguments disabled everything including tenant isolation, and every reporting or admin path had to re-add predicates by hand. EF Core 10's named filters exist precisely because that workaround plateaued.`],quote:`One filter per entity meant one shot per entity — and silence when you missed.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single combined filter (pre-EF10)`,`One WHERE clause; works on older EF versions`,`Brittle && chains; IgnoreQueryFilters nukes everything at once`,`Legacy apps pinned to EF 8/9`],[`Explicit WHERE per query`,`Fully visible and easy to test`,`Duplicated across dozens of queries; one miss is a leak`,`One-off reporting and admin paths`],[`Named filters (EF Core 10)`,`Multiple per entity; disable selectively by name`,`Same-name overwrite; no cascade to related entities`,`Multi-tenant apps with soft delete`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,at:`0:38`}},{heading:`03 · Named filters: how EF Core 10 changes the model`,body:[`Now the new shape of the API. In EF Core 10 you give each filter a name: HasQueryFilter("SoftDelete", o => !o.IsDeleted) and HasQueryFilter("Tenant", o => o.TenantId == tenantId) can live on the same Order entity. At query time both compile into the same WHERE clause, ANDed together — one predicate per named filter, exactly what you'd have written by hand.`,`The killer feature is selective disabling. IgnoreQueryFilters now accepts filter names, so a history-report endpoint can drop just the soft-delete filter while keeping tenant isolation intact. That single capability removes the biggest reason teams avoided global filters for admin surfaces.`,`But let's not get ahead of ourselves — the gotchas are real. Redefining a filter with the same name still overwrites silently. Filters don't cascade: your Order filter does nothing for OrderItem unless you declare it there too. And a filter on a required navigation can make parent rows vanish through inner-join semantics. Named filters give you room to be correct; they don't make you correct.`],quote:`Name your filters after the job they do — because now you'll be calling them by name.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,at:`1:23`}},{heading:`04 · Reading the SQL: trust but verify`,body:[`In the demo I curl the endpoint and open the distributed trace. Inside the database span sits the exact statement: SELECT ... FROM Orders WHERE IsDeleted = 0 AND TenantId = @t. That span is your proof that both named filters made it into the plan. If only one predicate shows up, a filter got overwritten — and you want to learn that from a trace on your machine, not from a customer on a screen share.`,`So make it mechanical. Every new filter earns an integration test that asserts the generated SQL or the row-level behavior: a soft-deleted row from the wrong tenant must never surface, period. And audit every call site of IgnoreQueryFilters — in EF 10 it should always list names, never run bare. The filter pipeline is only as trustworthy as your verification loop around it.`],quote:`If a filter isn't visible in your trace or your tests, it isn't real.`,table:{headers:[`Gotcha`,`Symptom`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`Same filter name redefined`,`Old predicate silently replaced`,`Assert filter names and count per entity in a model test`],[`Filter doesn't cascade to related entities`,`OrderItems leak while Orders are filtered`,`Declare the filter on every queryable entity`],[`Required navigation plus filter`,`Parent rows vanish via inner-join semantics`,`Make navigations optional or align filters across the pair`],[`IgnoreQueryFilters() with no args`,`Tenant isolation dropped in an admin path`,`Pass filter names explicitly in EF 10`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,at:`1:52`}},{heading:`05 · Where I'd draw the line`,body:[`So where do named filters belong? They shine for cross-cutting row-level invariants: tenancy and soft delete on every hot entity. Those are rules that must be true in every query, forever — exactly the contract a global filter encodes. If a predicate must always hold, filter it.`,`But don't filter everything. A status flag that only matters in two dashboard queries is better left explicit, where the reader can see it. And remember the boundaries of the pipeline: hand-written SQL and bulk operations live outside it, so they need their own audit. Filters are for invariants, not conveniences — the moment a filter is 'sometimes optional', it's a query, not a rule.`],quote:`Filters are for invariants, not preferences — if it must always be true, filter it; if it's sometimes true, write it.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,at:`3:04`}}],diagrams:[{id:`filter-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From LINQ to filtered SQL with named query filters`,caption:`Two named filters on Order compose into a single WHERE clause — visible in the trace's database span.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Req["GET /orders"] --> Linq["LINQ query<br/>context.Orders"]
    Linq --> Pipe["EF Core 10 query pipeline"]
    Pipe --> F1["Named filter 'soft-delete'<br/>o.IsDeleted == false"]
    Pipe --> F2["Named filter 'tenant'<br/>o.TenantId == currentTenant"]
    F1 --> Sql["Generated SQL"]
    F2 --> Sql
    Sql --> Db[("SQL Server<br/>Orders table")]
    Sql --> Trace["Trace span shows<br/>WHERE IsDeleted = 0 AND TenantId = @t"]`},{id:`selective-disable`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Selective filter disabling in EF Core 10`,caption:`IgnoreQueryFilters by name drops only the soft-delete filter — tenant isolation survives the reporting path.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant Job as Reporting job
    participant Ctx as DbContext
    participant EF as EF Core 10
    participant DB as SQL Server
    Job->>Ctx: Orders.IgnoreQueryFilters("soft-delete")
    Ctx->>EF: Compile query
    EF->>EF: Drop 'soft-delete' only<br/>keep 'tenant' filter
    EF->>DB: SELECT ... WHERE TenantId = @t
    DB-->>EF: Rows incl. soft-deleted, same tenant only
    EF-->>Ctx: Materialize entities
    Ctx-->>Job: Full history, still tenant-safe`}],takeaways:[`EF Core 10 lifts the one-filter-per-entity ceiling: stack soft-delete, tenant, and status as named filters, and name each after its job ('SoftDelete', 'Tenant').`,`Two named filters compile to one WHERE clause with two ANDed predicates — confirm both appear in the trace's SQL span before you merge.`,`Redefining a filter with the same name overwrites silently — add a model-config test asserting the exact filter names and count per entity.`,`IgnoreQueryFilters() with no arguments disables tenant isolation too — in EF 10 always pass filter names to disable one while keeping the rest.`,`Filters don't cascade: an Order filter does not protect OrderItem — budget one filter declaration per queryable entity and audit raw-SQL and bulk paths separately.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Named Query Filters in EF Core 10 Are a Game Changer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SSV7yaH4n9g`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-tBSWhHaFB-c`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:40,title:`Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:44`,minutes:9,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Dapper`],story:{headline:`The Pen Test That Killed Our 'Fast Path' SQL`,paragraphs:[`It's 11 AM on the Tuesday before a Friday launch, and the external pen testers drop their interim report into the team channel. Finding #3, high severity: the order-summary endpoint on our new .NET 8 API is concatenating an orderId straight into a SQL string. We wrote that query by hand — 'EF was too heavy for this one,' we said — and in doing so, we hand-rolled the exact class of bug parameterized queries exist to prevent.`,`The fix had to land before launch. Rewriting it in Dapper meant adding a second connection pipeline, a second mapping layer, and a second set of tests for exactly one query. That's when the EF Core 8 preview started looking less like a toy and more like an escape hatch: Database.SqlQuery<T> takes raw SQL, returns unmapped DTOs, and turns every interpolated value into a parameter whether you remember to ask or not.`,`We shipped the rewrite that afternoon — one query, one stack, zero string concatenation. The pen testers re-ran the test Wednesday morning and closed the finding. The performance tax we were worried about never showed up on any dashboard anyone was watching.`],problem:`How do you run raw SQL against unmapped types without opening an injection hole — and without dragging a second micro-ORM into the codebase for one query?`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core 8`,`SqlQuery<T>`,`FormattableString`,`LINQ Composition`,`SQL Views`,`Dapper`],note:`The moving parts: EF Core 8's Database facade runs raw SQL into unmapped DTOs, FormattableString supplies automatic parameterization, LINQ composes on top of the result, and Dapper stands as the performance baseline this feature is chasing.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why reach for raw SQL at all?`,body:[`Let's start where I started: why would you ever drop out of LINQ? Because some queries are just naturally SQL — a reporting view with window functions, a recursive CTE, a hint the optimizer needs. Before EF Core 8, your options were awkward: map a keyless entity and use FromSqlInterpolated, or pull in Dapper and accept a second data stack. Keyless entities worked, but they dragged entity-style configuration along for what is really just a read-only DTO.`,`EF Core 8's SqlQuery<T> on the Database facade changes the shape of that decision. T can be any CLR type — a record, a DTO, a projection straight off a view — no key, no entity mapping, no DbSet required. So the question stops being 'do I import Dapper for this one query?' and becomes 'is this query hot enough to justify maintaining a second pipeline?' For the long tail of raw SQL, the answer is usually no.`],quote:`Raw SQL isn't a failure of the ORM — it's a boundary you should be able to cross without switching vehicles.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`LINQ only`,`Type-safe, composable, testable`,`Awkward for views, window functions, hints`,`Default for CRUD`],[`FromSql + keyless entity (EF 7)`,`Raw SQL with composition`,`Entity config for a read-only DTO`,`Existing EF 7 code`],[`SqlQuery<T> (EF 8)`,`Unmapped DTOs, views, LINQ on top`,`Preview perf, no change tracking`,`Long-tail raw SQL`],[`Dapper`,`Fastest simple reads`,`Second stack, manual mapping`,`Top hot paths`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tBSWhHaFB-c`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the FormattableString: why interpolation is safe here`,body:[`Now the part that made me raise an eyebrow the first time I saw it: the argument to SqlQuery is a FormattableString, so you write an interpolated verbatim string — $@"SELECT * FROM OrderSummaries WHERE Id = {orderId}" — and it looks like injection bait. It isn't. The compiler doesn't hand EF a finished string; it hands it the template plus the holes. EF turns each hole into a parameter — your orderId becomes @p0, and the query goes over the wire as sp_executesql carrying that parameter.`,`The distinction that matters: interpolation is only dangerous when the string is built before the framework sees it. The moment you concatenate — string.Concat, +=, an interpolated string assigned to a plain string variable — the hole is gone and the SQL is already baked. That's exactly why SqlQueryRaw exists for the rare case you truly need raw text, and why I flag every SqlQueryRaw call in code review.`],quote:`The framework can only parameterize what it can still see.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tBSWhHaFB-c`,at:`0:36`}},{heading:`03 · The IQueryable payoff: composing LINQ on top of raw SQL`,body:[`Here's the part I didn't expect to care about: SqlQuery returns IQueryable<T>, so the raw SQL is just the base of the query. EF wraps it as a subquery and translates whatever LINQ you stack on top — Where, OrderBy, Select — server-side. Fixed predicates go in the FormattableString where they read best; dynamic filters, sorting, and paging ride on top in LINQ, and it all becomes one round trip.`,`Know the edges, though. Unmapped types get no change tracking and no keys — this is read-only by design, so don't try to SaveChanges your way out of it. Include doesn't apply to unmapped results, and a few constructs will either fail translation or force client evaluation, so watch the query log the first time you compose something exotic.`],quote:`Write the SQL you'd write by hand — then let LINQ finish the sentence.`,table:{headers:[`Composed operation`,`Composes?`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Where / OrderBy`,`Yes`,`Wrapped as subquery, runs server-side`],[`Select projection`,`Yes`,`Shape the DTO in SQL or LINQ`],[`Include`,`No`,`Unmapped types have no navigation graph`],[`Change tracking`,`N/A`,`Unmapped means read-only by design`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tBSWhHaFB-c`,at:`1:12`}},{heading:`04 · The Dapper question: does this replace anything?`,body:[`So — Dapper killer? No, and the benchmarks say so plainly. Nick Chapsas ran the numbers and Dapper is still clearly faster on trivial reads — there's a translation and composition layer EF is paying for that Dapper simply doesn't have. But EF Core 8 is in preview, and that gap is exactly the kind of thing that moves between now and GA, so re-benchmark at release before rewriting anything hot.`,`The argument I actually make to teams isn't nanoseconds, it's pipeline count. One DbContext means one connection pool, one logging and diagnostics story, one retry strategy, and LINQ composition for free. My rule of thumb: keep Dapper for the top sliver of genuinely hot queries, and let SqlQuery<T> absorb the long tail of raw SQL that used to justify importing a whole micro-ORM.`],quote:`Dapper wins the sprint; EF wins the marathon of not maintaining two data stacks.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF SqlQuery<T>`,`Parameters by default, LINQ on top`,`Preview perf, read-only DTOs`,`Long-tail raw SQL, views`],[`Dapper`,`Fastest reads, tiny API`,`Second stack, manual parameters`,`Hottest paths`],[`Hand-rolled ADO.NET`,`Total control`,`You own injection, mapping, pooling`,`Almost never`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tBSWhHaFB-c`,at:`1:47`}}],diagrams:[{id:`sqlquery-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From HTTP request to parameterized raw SQL`,caption:`How a FormattableString becomes a parameterized query and an IQueryable you can keep composing.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  A["GET /order-summaries/42"] --> B["Query Handler"]
  B --> C["context.Database.SqlQuery(OrderSummary)"]
  C --> D["FormattableString: template + holes"]
  D --> E["orderId captured as parameter @p0"]
  E --> F["SELECT * FROM OrderSummaries WHERE Id = @p0"]
  F --> G["IQueryable of OrderSummary"]
  G --> H["LINQ: Where / OrderBy / Select"]
  H --> I["FirstOrDefaultAsync()"]
  I --> J["SQL Server executes against OrderSummaries view"]
  J --> K["Unmapped OrderSummary DTO returned"]`},{id:`composition-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Composition and execution sequence`,caption:`The handler's view of the round trip: template in, subquery out, one round trip, zero concatenated strings.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant A as .NET 8 API
  participant H as Query Handler
  participant E as EF Core 8
  participant D as SQL Server
  C->>A: GET /order-summaries/42
  A->>H: GetOrderSummary(orderId)
  H->>E: SqlQuery with interpolated orderId
  Note over E: orderId extracted as parameter @p0
  E->>D: sp_executesql SELECT ... WHERE Id = @p0
  D-->>E: row from OrderSummaries view
  E-->>H: OrderSummary DTO (unmapped)
  H-->>A: result
  A-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Never concatenate into SqlQuery — every {hole} in a FormattableString becomes a SQL parameter, so the safe pattern has zero injection surface by construction.`,`Treat SqlQueryRaw as a code-review flag: it skips parameter capture, so any dynamic text must be parameterized by hand or rejected outright.`,`Use SqlQuery<T> for views and read models — unmapped types need no key, no entity config, and get no change tracking, so they are read-only by design.`,`Push fixed predicates into the SQL string and leave dynamic filters to LINQ — EF wraps your SQL as a subquery, keeping everything server-side in one round trip.`,`Re-benchmark at GA before migrating hot paths: early community benchmarks show Dapper still clearly faster on trivial reads, but EF 8 is in preview and that gap is moving.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Everything You Need To Know About EF Core 8 Raw SQL Queries`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tBSWhHaFB-c`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-hHiNiby7lzc`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:41,title:`Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:02`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`],story:{headline:`A double-click at noon turned our signup form into a raw Postgres error page`,paragraphs:[`It was a Tuesday lunchtime launch — the 'invite your teammates' feature went live and the marketing tweet fired at 12:01. Within minutes support started forwarding screenshots: customers double-clicking Create account, and one of the two racing requests dying with a raw 500. Not a friendly 'email already in use' — a full Postgres error, constraint names and all, dumped into the response body because nothing on that path knew how to translate it.`,`The on-call dev did the natural thing: wrapped SaveChanges in a try/catch and checked whether the message contained 'duplicate key value violates unique constraint'. It worked in staging. Then someone noticed the same catch was also swallowing foreign key failures — the FK message contains 'violates' too — so a genuine referential-integrity bug started masquerading as 'email taken'. Meanwhile the eager validation SELECT we'd added to 'prevent' all this had been running on every single signup, and it had prevented nothing: both racing requests had seen a free email.`,`The unique constraint was doing its job perfectly. Our handling of its exception was the disaster. The fix wasn't smarter string parsing — it was stopping the parsing of prose altogether and letting typed exceptions carry the meaning.`],problem:`How do you turn EF Core's wrapped, provider-specific DbUpdateException into a typed, field-level failure you can map to a clean 409 — without an extra pre-check query on every write and without string-matching error message text?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Npgsql`,`EntityFramework.Exceptions`,`Unique Index`],note:`EF Core persists through Npgsql into Postgres, where a unique index on the customer email is the real guarantee; EntityFramework.Exceptions registers at the DbContext boundary and translates provider errors into typed exceptions like UniqueConstraintException.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the eager check buys comfort, not safety`,body:[`Let's start where the original code started: a validator that runs a SELECT against the customers table before the handler does anything, just to ask 'is this email free?'. Feels safe. It costs you a round trip on every single create — happy path included — and here's the part that stings: it doesn't actually make anything safe. Between your SELECT and your INSERT, another request can commit the same email. Check-then-act is a race, always.`,`So do you keep the check as belt-and-braces? I'd argue no. The unique index is the only component that sees both transactions at commit time — it's the actual referee. Once it exists, the pre-check is redundant in exactly the case you added it for, and pure overhead everywhere else. Keep lightweight, non-database validation for UX if you like, but the truth lives at the index.`],quote:`The database is the only referee that sees both racers.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Eager SELECT before insert`,`Friendly early failure, no exception plumbing`,`Extra round trip every write; still racy (TOCTOU)`,`Drop it`],[`Unique index only (optimistic)`,`One query per create; race-free guarantee`,`You must handle a DB exception on conflict`,`Use it`],[`App-level lock/mutex`,`No DB exception handling`,`Doesn't scale across instances; false safety`,`Avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what DbUpdateException actually is`,body:[`Now the optimistic path: remove the pre-check, wrap the handler in a try/catch, and catch DbUpdateException. That's EF Core's exception for 'SaveChanges failed'. But look at what it actually is: a wrapper. Inside sits the provider exception — for us, Npgsql's PostgresException — and that inner object carries the real signal: SqlState 23505, the unique_violation code.`,`The catch: DbUpdateException is a grab-bag. Unique violations, foreign key violations, not-null violations, string-too-long, numeric overflow — they all arrive in the same envelope. Catching it tells you the write failed, not why. And the 'why' is provider-specific: Postgres uses SqlState codes, SQL Server uses its own error numbers. Any handling written against the inner exception is now coupled to one database engine.`],quote:`DbUpdateException tells you the write failed; only the inner exception knows why.`,table:{headers:[`Violation`,`Postgres SqlState`,`Typed exception`],rows:[[`Unique constraint`,`23505`,`UniqueConstraintException`],[`Foreign key`,`23503`,`ReferenceConstraintException`],[`Not null`,`23502`,`RequiredFieldException`],[`Value too long`,`22001`,`MaxLengthExceededException`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,at:`1:25`}},{heading:`03 · Why string matching rots`,body:[`So the first instinct most of us have: log the exception, spot the message — 'duplicate key value violates unique constraint ux_customers_email' — and write e.Message.Contains(...). I've done it. It works on Friday. Here's how it rots: the wording is provider-specific (SQL Server says something completely different), it can shift between Postgres versions, and if error messages ever get localized, it's gone.`,`Worse, it's imprecise. 'Violates' shows up in foreign key messages too, so your duplicate-email catch starts eating real referential-integrity bugs. And if a table has two unique indexes, message matching can't cleanly tell you which one fired without parsing constraint names out of prose. You've coupled production control flow to the exact phrasing of an error log.`],quote:`Parsing an error message is coupling your code to someone else's documentation.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`String Contains on message`,`Zero setup`,`Brittle to wording, locale, provider; ambiguous`,`Never`],[`Switch on SqlState codes`,`Stable per provider`,`Provider-specific; manual unwrap of inner exception`,`Acceptable`],[`EntityFramework.Exceptions typed`,`Catch by intent; constraint metadata included`,`One package + registration per provider`,`Use it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,at:`2:40`}},{heading:`04 · The library's move: typed exceptions at the SaveChanges boundary`,body:[`Here's the library's move. EntityFramework.Exceptions ships per-provider packages — PostgreSQL, SqlServer, MySQL, SQLite — and you wire it with one line on your DbContext options: optionsBuilder.UseExceptionProcessor(). From then on, when SaveChanges fails, it unwraps the provider exception, reads the code, and throws a typed exception instead: UniqueConstraintException, ReferenceConstraintException, MaxLengthExceededException, and friends.`,`The typed exceptions carry metadata you actually want: ConstraintName and ConstraintProperties — the model properties behind the violated index. So the handler becomes honest code: catch UniqueConstraintException, check whether ConstraintProperties contains Email, and if so return a 409 with a field-level error pointing at email. No prose parsing, no provider coupling, and the catch block reads like the business rule it implements.`],quote:`Catch the failure you planned for; bubble the ones you didn't.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,at:`4:09`}},{heading:`05 · Drawing the line: what maps to 409, what maps to 500`,body:[`Last thing: draw the policy line, because not every database exception deserves the same treatment. Violations you expect from user input — duplicate email, missing required field — map to 4xx with field detail: 409 for the unique conflict, 400 for required. Everything else — FK violations you didn't anticipate, truncation, overflow — should bubble to your global exception handler as a 500 with logging and alerts, because those mean a bug or a data contract breach, not a user mistake.`,`And keep the shape of the system honest: the unique index stays as the source of truth, the eager SELECT goes away, and the write path is one round trip instead of two. On a signup endpoint doing thousands of creates an hour, that's thousands of queries an hour you simply stop paying for.`],quote:`Constraints are the contract; typed exceptions are the contract's error channel.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,at:`4:56`}}],diagrams:[{id:`translation-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Exception translation pipeline`,caption:`How a Postgres unique violation becomes a typed, catchable exception before it reaches your handler.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["POST /customers"] --> B["CreateCustomer handler"]
    B --> C["EF Core SaveChanges"]
    C --> D["Postgres INSERT"]
    D --> E{"unique index on email"}
    E -->|"23505 unique_violation"| F["PostgresException"]
    F --> G["DbUpdateException wrapper"]
    G --> H["UseExceptionProcessor"]
    H --> I["UniqueConstraintException<br/>ConstraintProperties: Email"]
    I --> J["catch in handler"]
    J --> K["409 Conflict — field: email"]
    E -->|"insert ok"| L["201 Created"]`},{id:`race-lost-by-precheck`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Why the eager check loses the race`,caption:`Two concurrent signups both pass the pre-check SELECT; only the unique index decides the winner — and the loser gets a typed exception.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant A as Request A
    participant B as Request B
    participant P as Postgres
    A->>P: SELECT email free? yes
    B->>P: SELECT email free? yes
    A->>P: INSERT customer, commit
    P-->>A: OK
    B->>P: INSERT customer, commit
    P-->>B: ERROR 23505 unique_violation
    Note over B: DbUpdateException translated to UniqueConstraintException
    B->>B: catch → 409 email taken`}],takeaways:[`Drop the eager uniqueness SELECT — the unique index is the race-free guarantee, and the pre-check costs one extra round trip on every insert while still losing races.`,`Never string-match exception messages; wording is provider-, version-, and locale-specific, and 'violates' matches FK failures too.`,`Register UseExceptionProcessor once per DbContext, then catch UniqueConstraintException and read ConstraintName/ConstraintProperties to build a field-level 409.`,`Map only expected input violations to 4xx (unique → 409, required → 400); let every other DbUpdateException bubble to the global handler as an alerted 500.`,`Optimistic uniqueness cuts database round trips per create from 2 to 1 — on a write-heavy endpoint that's a direct, measurable p50 win.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Clean Up Your EF Core Exceptions With This Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHiNiby7lzc`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-CVQz0E33ft4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:42,title:`A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 19:30`,minutes:20,difficulty:3,tags:[`Dapper`,`.NET`,`Cache`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`10:04 AM: The Cache Expired — All Forty Thousand Keys, All at Once`,paragraphs:[`Tuesday, 10:02 AM. Marketing pulls the trigger on the mid-season sale push notification, and the product page — our highest-traffic endpoint — hums along exactly as designed, serving everything from Redis at sub-millisecond latency. Two minutes later the p99 graph goes vertical, and the on-call channel fills with timeout screenshots from checkout.`,`The postmortem took twenty minutes because the root cause was embarrassingly mechanical. Our nightly catalog load had written ~40,000 product keys with a 15-minute absolute TTL — and the whole batch finished inside a 90-second window. Every key in the catalog was now expiring in lockstep, every fifteen minutes. Light morning traffic had absorbed the first two cycles; 8,000 requests per second did not. Each expiry wave became a stampede: thousands of simultaneous cache misses, each one a Dapper query against Postgres, which pegged CPU at 100% and drained the 100-connection pool in under four seconds.`,`We didn't need a bigger database. We needed the miss path to grow up: per-key single-flight locking so a refresh is one query instead of ten thousand, jittered TTLs so keys stop expiring in formation, and tombstones for the 404 traffic that had been quietly bypassing the cache all along.`],problem:`Every hot key expired in the same second, and an unprotected cache-aside miss path turned one routine refresh into thousands of concurrent Postgres queries — connection pool starved, checkout timing out, in under four seconds.`},tech:{stack:[`Redis`,`IDistributedCache`,`.NET 8`,`Dapper`,`Postgres`,`System.Text.Json`,`SemaphoreSlim`,`TTL + Jitter`],note:`A .NET read path with a Redis distributed cache in front of Postgres: JSON serialization at the cache boundary, Dapper on the miss path, and per-key locking plus jittered TTLs for stampede protection.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: cache-aside is just two paths`,body:[`Let's start with the name, because it explains everything. Cache-aside — lazy loading — means the application owns the cache. Redis never talks to Postgres on its own; we decide when to read and when to populate. That gives us exactly two paths: the happy path, where GetStringAsync hands us a JSON string and we deserialize and return in about a millisecond, and the miss path, where we fetch from the database, write the value back into the cache, and return.`,`Now, why not write-through everything and eliminate the miss path entirely? Because write-through pays the cache write on every mutation and happily caches data nobody ever reads. Cache-aside only holds what real traffic has actually asked for, which is exactly what you want on a read-heavy endpoint. In practice the two pair nicely: cache-aside for reads, write-through on the handful of writes where staleness genuinely hurts.`],quote:`Cache-aside is lazy on purpose — the cache only ever holds what real traffic asked for.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Read path`,`Write path`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Cache-aside`,`Hit or miss; app populates on miss`,`Untouched — DB stays source of truth`,`Read-heavy, brief staleness OK`],[`Write-through`,`Almost always a hit`,`Sync write to cache + DB`,`Tight freshness, write cost paid always`],[`Write-behind`,`Almost always a hit`,`Cache first, async flush to DB`,`Bursty writes; risk of data loss`],[`Refresh-ahead`,`Background refresh before expiry`,`Same as cache-aside`,`Predictable, known-hot keys`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the naive miss path`,body:[`Implementation-wise this is almost boring, and boring is the point. Build a key — users:42, products:42 — call GetStringAsync, and if you get a string back, JsonSerializer.Deserialize it and return. On a miss, extract the DB fetch into a local function, pull the row with Dapper, SetStringAsync with DistributedCacheEntryOptions, then return. And because IDistributedCache has no GetOrCreate like IMemoryCache does, wrap the whole thing once in a GetOrSetAsync extension method — you do not want this logic copy-pasted across endpoints.`,`Here's the subtlety the happy-path demo skips: what do we do when the database returns null? Caching null is worthless — you'll deserialize your way straight into a NullReferenceException. But skipping the cache entirely means every request for a nonexistent ID punches through to Postgres forever. The answer is a tombstone: serialize a sentinel value with a short 30-to-60-second TTL and treat it as not-found on read.`],quote:`If you copy the miss path into a second endpoint, you'll fix the race in only one of them.`,table:{headers:[`On miss, store…`,`Behavior`,`Risk`],rows:[[`The value`,`Fast hits for real data`,`None — the default case`],[`A tombstone (30–60 s TTL)`,`Bogus IDs stop hitting Postgres`,`Brief window where a just-created row 404s`],[`Nothing`,`Simplest possible code`,`Every miss is a DB query — scrapers love this`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,at:`3:54`}},{heading:`03 · The part the demo skips: the stampede`,body:[`Everything you just saw demos beautifully — right up until the TTL fires. Here's the mechanic of a stampede: a hot key expires, and every concurrent request for it misses at the same moment. None of them know about each other, so all of them run the miss path. At 8,000 RPS on one product page, that's 8,000 Dapper queries arriving within the same second, against a pool of 100 connections. Postgres doesn't degrade gracefully at that point — it falls over.`,`So why not just make the TTL longer? Because that trades freshness for time and fixes none of the real triggers: a deploy that flushes the cache, a cold start after a Redis restart, an eviction under memory pressure. Prewarming helps known keys but does nothing for the long tail. The actual disease is that our miss path is uncoordinated — every request independently decides to go to the database.`],quote:`A TTL doesn't schedule one refresh — it schedules a race, and Postgres loses it every time.`,table:{headers:[`Stampede trigger`,`What happens`,`Blast radius`],rows:[[`Aligned TTLs`,`Thousands of keys expire in lockstep`,`Whole catalog misses at once`],[`Deploy / cache flush`,`Cold cache under full production traffic`,`Near-100% miss ratio until warm`],[`Redis restart / failover`,`Instant cold start, no warmup`,`Full DB load spike`],[`Eviction under pressure`,`Hot keys vanish mid-traffic`,`Random hot keys — hardest to debug`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,at:`6:49`}},{heading:`04 · Choosing the guard: four ways to single-flight a miss`,body:[`So how do we make the miss path single-flight? Option one: an in-process lock — a ConcurrentDictionary of SemaphoreSlim keyed by cache key. The first request for a key acquires it and loads; everyone else waits, then re-checks the cache and finds the value. Cheap, no new infrastructure — but it only coalesces per process. Ten pods behind the load balancer means ten identical queries. Fine for warm long-tail keys, not fine for one blazing-hot key.`,`Option two: a distributed lock in Redis — SET NX with an expiry — so exactly one pod across the fleet loads while the rest wait or briefly serve stale. That works, but now the lock itself is a failure mode: if the loader is slow and the lock expires, two pods load anyway, so carry a token and only write if you still own the lock. Option three sidesteps locks entirely: serve the stale value while one request refreshes in the background — probabilistic early expiration is the fancy version. And if you're on .NET 9, HybridCache ships with per-process stampede protection built in, which covers most teams' actual needs.`],quote:`Lock the load, not the read — the hit path stays lock-free; only misses pay the coordination tax.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-process SemaphoreSlim`,`Zero new infra, microsecond acquire`,`Per-pod only — N pods, N queries`,`Many warm keys, modest fan-out`],[`Distributed lock (SET NX)`,`One loader fleet-wide`,`Extra round trip; lock-expiry races`,`Single blazing-hot key`],[`Stale-while-revalidate`,`Zero added latency on miss`,`Serves stale data by design`,`Tolerance for seconds-old data`],[`HybridCache (.NET 9)`,`Built-in stampede protection, L1+L2`,`Per-process coalescing only`,`Default choice for new .NET code`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,at:`12:25`}},{heading:`05 · Shipping it: jitter, tombstones, and a fail-open plan`,body:[`When we shipped the fix, it was three small rules bolted onto the GetOrSetAsync extension. One: every TTL gets 5–10% jitter, so forty thousand keys stop expiring in formation. Two: lock waits are capped at roughly twice the DB query time — past that, re-check the cache or serve stale rather than stacking thousands of requests behind one lock. Three: fail open — if Redis is unreachable, go straight to Postgres behind a circuit breaker instead of erroring, because a slow page beats no page.`,`And instrument it. Hit ratio per key family, miss-path latency, lock wait time. The morning of the sale, the tell was sitting in the metrics the whole time: hit ratio oscillating between 99% and near-zero every fifteen minutes, in perfect sync. That sawtooth was the stampede announcing itself — we just weren't looking.`],quote:`A cache without a hit-ratio dashboard is just a second database with extra steps.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,at:`17:18`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cache-aside-single-flight`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cache-Aside Read Path with Stampede Protection`,caption:`The hit path is lock-free; only the miss path acquires the per-key lock, so a refresh becomes one DB query regardless of concurrency.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["GET /products/42"] --> B{"GetStringAsync<br/>products:42 — hit?"}
    B -->|Hit| C["Deserialize JSON<br/>return in ~1 ms"]
    B -->|Miss| D{"Acquire per-key lock<br/>SemaphoreSlim / SET NX"}
    D -->|Acquired| E["Dapper query<br/>against Postgres"]
    E --> F{"Row is null?"}
    F -->|Yes| G["Set tombstone<br/>TTL 30 s"]
    G --> H["Return 404"]
    F -->|No| I["SetStringAsync<br/>TTL 10 min ± jitter"]
    I --> J["Return 200"]
    D -->|Busy| K["Wait ≤ 2x query time<br/>then re-check cache"]
    K --> B`},{id:`stampede-coalescing`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Three Concurrent Misses, One Database Query`,caption:`What the 10:04 AM stampede should have looked like: concurrent misses coalesce behind a per-key lock into a single Postgres fetch.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Request A
    participant B as Request B
    participant C as Request C
    participant R as Redis
    participant P as Postgres
    A->>R: GET products:42 (miss)
    B->>R: GET products:42 (miss)
    C->>R: GET products:42 (miss)
    A->>A: Acquire per-key lock - OK
    B->>B: Lock busy - wait
    C->>C: Lock busy - wait
    A->>P: SELECT (the only query)
    P-->>A: row
    A->>R: SET products:42 TTL 10m + jitter
    A->>A: Release lock
    B->>R: GET products:42 (hit)
    R-->>B: cached JSON
    C->>R: GET products:42 (hit)
    R-->>C: cached JSON
    Note over P: 3 misses coalesced into 1 query + 2 hits`}],takeaways:[`Add 5–10% jitter to every TTL — 15 min ± 45 s turns a synchronized expiry wave into a trickle.`,`Single-flight every miss: a hot key at 5,000 RPS should cause exactly 1 DB query per refresh, never 5,000.`,`Negative-cache not-found lookups for 30–60 s so bogus IDs can't bypass the cache and hammer Postgres.`,`Cap lock waits at ~2× your DB query time; beyond that, re-check the cache or serve stale instead of stacking requests.`,`Alert when a key family's hit ratio drops below 90% — a sawtooth between 99% and 50% is a stampede announcing itself.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — A Step-by-Step Guide for the Cache-Aside Pattern + Stampede Protection`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVQz0E33ft4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-vaVZSh8QqH8`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:43,title:`Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:45`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`],story:{headline:`Release day: six hours of orders our partner never heard about`,paragraphs:[`Release day, 9:40 in the morning. We flipped the flag on the new order pipeline: instead of our fulfillment partner polling us every thirty seconds, we would push them a webhook the instant an order was created. The demo was flawless — create an order, watch the payload land in their sandbox, high-fives in the channel.`,`By noon their endpoint was throwing 503s under their own traffic spike. Our dispatcher fired one POST, caught the exception, logged a warning, and cheerfully moved on to the next order. Our API returned 200 OK all day; their dashboard showed nothing. Six hours of orders sat in 'created' limbo, discovered only when someone reconciled counts during the post-release audit.`,`The first fix made everything worse: retries bolted inline into the order-creation path, so our endpoint's latency now tracked their outage in real time. The real fix was admitting that webhook delivery is a background job with its own lifecycle — subscription, attempt, backoff, dead-letter, compensation — not a line of code after SaveChanges.`],problem:`A webhook that fails once is logged and forgotten — orders silently vanish. A webhook that fails after every retry needs compensation. And naive inline retries weld your API's latency to a stranger's uptime. Delivery has to become its own managed lifecycle.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET 9`,`Minimal APIs`,`HttpClient`,`Positional Records`,`In-Memory Store`],note:`The smallest viable webhook surface: a positional record for subscriptions, an in-memory store, and an HttpClient-based dispatcher hanging off minimal API endpoints — just enough to expose the seams where durability, retries, and signing will bolt on.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just let them poll?`,body:[`Let's start with the obvious counterfactual — why not let the client poll? Picture it: a downstream service hitting our GET endpoint every few seconds asking 'anything new? anything new?' Ninety-nine percent of those requests come back with 'no, nothing changed.' That's real load, real bandwidth, and stale data in between — and it scales linearly with how many clients care about us.`,`Webhooks flip the arrow. This is push-based communication over plain HTTP, and it's essentially event-driven architecture wearing an HTTP costume. The client registers once — 'here's my event type, here's my URL' — and when something significant happens in our system, we POST a predefined payload to that URL. One request, one answer, near-real-time. It's exactly how Stripe tells you a payment succeeded or failed, and it's the standard pattern whenever you're integrating with systems you don't own.`,`But let's be honest about the trade we just made. Push means their availability problem becomes our delivery problem. Polling's inefficiency is bounded and boring; a webhook system's failure modes are unbounded and interesting — retries, compensation, security, scaling. That's the rest of this talk.`],quote:`Polling asks 'are we there yet?' a thousand times; a webhook knocks once, when it matters.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Polling`,`Simple; client owns the schedule`,`Wasteful; stale data; load grows with every client`,`Low-frequency internal checks`],[`Webhooks (push)`,`Efficient; near-real-time; fewer requests served`,`Delivery failures become the provider's problem`,`Event-driven service-to-service updates`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the happy path: registration to 200 OK`,body:[`Two roles on stage: the client application — and mind you, that can be a backend service, not just a browser app — and us, the service provider. Registration is just a POST: give me an event type and a webhook URL, and I'll store a WebHookSubscription with an Id, the EventType, the WebhookUrl, and a CreatedOnUtc timestamp. That record is the entire contract.`,`Then time passes. The event fires in our system — an order is created — and we POST the predefined payload to every subscribed URL. The client returns 200 OK, the flow is complete, and they process the data asynchronously while we carry on serving everyone else. Beautiful. That's the whole happy path.`,`Now the subtle part everyone glosses over: that 200 means 'received,' not 'processed.' Our delivery contract ends at their edge. Everything after the handshake is their problem — and everything before it, including whether that URL deserved our trust in the first place, is ours.`],quote:`A 200 OK means 'I heard you' — never 'I finished.'`,table:{headers:[`Delivery outcome`,`What it means`,`Provider action`],rows:[[`2xx`,`Received — not necessarily processed`,`Mark delivered, move on`],[`4xx (non-410)`,`Likely a permanent client-side problem`,`Log, alert, consider disabling the subscription`],[`5xx / timeout`,`Transient — worth another shot`,`Retry with backoff`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,at:`1:55`}},{heading:`03 · When the push fails: the retry cliff`,body:[`First failure mode: the initial POST fails. Fine — we retry a couple more times, and in most cases we get our 200 and the flow completes. Second, nastier mode: it fails even after every retry. Now we're off the happy path and into compensation territory — dead-letter the delivery, surface it, replay or reconcile it. That's a whole video on its own, but you have to design the seam for it now.`,`Why not just retry inline in the request path? Because then your p99 is their uptime. I've lived this: the order endpoint starts timing out because a partner's server is having a bad afternoon. Delivery belongs in the background, where a slow subscriber costs you queue depth, not customer requests.`,`And why not retry forever? Because you need closure. There's also a trap on the other side: a retry might land after the first attempt actually succeeded — the receiver has to tolerate duplicates, so stamp every payload with a delivery ID and make their handler idempotent.`],quote:`Retries buy you resilience; compensation buys you an exit when resilience runs out.`,table:{headers:[`Retry strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fire once, log`,`Zero complexity`,`Silent data loss on any blip`,`Never, in production`],[`Inline retry in request path`,`Simple and immediate`,`Couples API latency to subscriber health`,`Only for sub-second local calls`],[`Background retry + backoff`,`Isolates failures; tunable; dead-letters cleanly`,`Needs a store and a scheduler`,`The default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,at:`3:16`}},{heading:`04 · Trust but verify: the URL a stranger handed you`,body:[`Here's the uncomfortable one: a stranger hands us a URL and asks us to POST arbitrary payloads to it, forever. How do we know that subscription is even real? The standard answer is a challenge mechanism — at registration, we send a token, they echo it back, and only then does the subscription go live. That's a future video, but the data model needs to leave room for a 'pending verification' state today.`,`Security is the tip of a very large iceberg. Sign every payload so the receiver can prove it came from us and wasn't tampered with in transit. Rate-limit per subscriber so one bad actor can't turn our dispatcher into an amplification cannon. And never let a subscription URL resolve to your own internals — that's how a 'feature' becomes an SSRF-shaped incident.`],quote:`Every subscriber URL is untrusted input with a network effect.`,table:{headers:[`Trust control`,`Protects against`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Challenge handshake`,`Fake or unreachable URLs`,`One extra round-trip at registration`],[`HMAC-signed payloads`,`Spoofed or tampered deliveries`,`Shared secret plus signing code`],[`Per-subscriber rate limits`,`Amplification and abuse`,`Counters and throttling logic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,at:`7:39`}},{heading:`05 · The smallest viable dispatcher in .NET`,body:[`Enough theory — let's build the skeleton. It's a .NET 9 minimal API with an in-memory order repository: a POST endpoint to create an order, a GET endpoint to list them. I add a sealed positional record, WebHookSubscription, with exactly four properties — Id, EventType, WebhookUrl, CreatedOnUtc — plus a small request DTO carrying EventType and WebhookUrl for the registration call.`,`Then the payoff: inject the webhook dispatcher into the order-creation endpoint, and the moment an order is created, we fire a POST at every URL subscribed to that event type. This is deliberately in-memory — it evaporates on restart, it doesn't survive across instances, and that's fine. Its job is to expose the seams where persistence, retries, signing, and fan-out scaling will bolt on in the next parts of the series.`],quote:`Build the dumbest version that shows the seams — then let the failures design the rest.`,table:{headers:[`Store`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory`,`Trivial to build; perfect for learning the shape`,`Lost on restart; single instance only`,`Prototype — this video`],[`Database-backed`,`Durable, queryable, multi-instance safe`,`Schema, migrations, consistency`,`Part 2 and beyond`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,at:`8:11`}}],diagrams:[{id:`webhook-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Webhook registration and delivery lifecycle`,caption:`The full conversation: subscribe once, then every matching event triggers a delivery with a retry branch.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client App
    participant P as Service Provider (.NET 9)
    participant S as Subscription Store

    C->>P: POST /webhooks with eventType and url
    P->>S: Save WebHookSubscription
    P-->>C: 201 Created with subscription Id
    Note over P,S: Later — order created event fires
    P->>S: Find subscriptions matching eventType
    P->>C: POST url with signed payload
    alt 2xx response
        C-->>P: 200 OK
        P->>S: Mark delivery delivered
    else 5xx or timeout
        C-->>P: 503 or no response
        P->>S: Schedule retry with backoff
        Note over P,C: Retries exhausted — dead-letter and compensate
    end`},{id:`delivery-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Delivery decision pipeline`,caption:`What the dispatcher does per event: match subscribers, attempt delivery, back off, and dead-letter when the cliff is reached.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Event occurs in provider"] --> B["Match subscriptions by event type"]
    B --> C{"Subscribers found?"}
    C -- "No" --> Z["Nothing to deliver"]
    C -- "Yes" --> D["POST signed payload to subscriber URL"]
    D --> E{"2xx within timeout?"}
    E -- "Yes" --> F["Mark delivered"]
    E -- "No" --> G{"Retries remaining?"}
    G -- "Yes" --> H["Exponential backoff"]
    H --> D
    G -- "No" --> I["Dead-letter delivery"]
    I --> J["Compensate: alert, replay, or reconcile"]`}],takeaways:[`Deliver off the request path: your order-creation endpoint should still answer in under ~50 ms even when every subscriber is down.`,`Retry transient failures 3–5 times with exponential backoff spanning minutes, then dead-letter — never retry forever.`,`Treat a 2xx as 'received, not processed' and give subscribers a ~10-second response budget before declaring a timeout.`,`Persist every subscription with Id, EventType, WebhookUrl, and CreatedOnUtc before first delivery — an in-memory store is a prototype, not a system.`,`Assume 1–5% of subscriber endpoints will be flaky or hostile: verify URLs with a challenge handshake and sign every payload with HMAC.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building a Webhooks System in .NET From Scratch (Webhooks series part 1)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vaVZSh8QqH8`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-YdEbD53c8Bs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:44,title:`Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:48`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Cache`],story:{headline:`The Product That Refused To Die`,paragraphs:[`It's 2:15 on a Tuesday afternoon — no pager in sight, which is exactly why it stings. The merch team deletes a discontinued sneaker from the admin panel at noon; by half past, support is forwarding screenshots of customers cheerfully ordering it. The storefront still lists it, the cart still accepts it, and the warehouse is about to have a very confusing afternoon.`,`I open the GetProducts query. Textbook cache-aside: check the cache service under the key 'products', and on a miss, hit Postgres and fill the cache. The delete command had committed cleanly — the row is gone from the database, I can see it. The read path just never went back to ask. In Postman I recreate the crime scene: create three products, GET returns three. GET again — cached, still three, and that's fine. Delete product 1. GET returns three.`,`Nothing was wrong with the cache. Something was missing after every write: nobody told it the world had changed. The fix turned out to be a decision, not a library — who announces the change, and how loudly.`],problem:`Every write path — create, update, delete — must invalidate the shared 'products' cache key after the database commit, or the read path keeps serving a world that no longer exists.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`MediatR`,`In-Memory Cache`,`RabbitMQ`,`EF Core`,`CQRS Handlers`],note:`A cache-aside read path guards the product list under a single 'products' key; the write side is CQRS command handlers that either remove that key inline or publish domain events — in-process via MediatR, or over RabbitMQ once the service scales out — that trigger the removal.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the read path is innocent`,body:[`Let's trace GetProducts the way you'd see it live in a demo. One cache key — 'products'. Hit? Return the list, sub-millisecond, done. Miss? Query the database, stuff the result into the cache, return it. On a pure read workload this thing is bulletproof and I would ship it without a second thought.`,`But watch the demo, because this is where the trade-off lives. Create three products, GET returns three. GET again — cached, still three, and that's correct behavior. Now delete product 1. The command commits, the row is gone from Postgres, and GET still returns three. The cache isn't broken; it was simply never told that the source of truth moved.`,`That's the whole disease in one sentence: the database is the source of truth, and the cache is a rumor about the database. Rumors need correcting.`],quote:`A cache never lies — it just stops being asked for the truth.`,table:{headers:[`Request`,`Postgres`,`Cache`,`GET returns`],rows:[[`First GET`,`3 products`,`miss → fill`,`3 (fresh)`],[`Second GET`,`3 products`,`hit`,`3 (cached, fine)`],[`After delete of #1`,`2 products`,`hit`,`3 (stale!)`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where does invalidation live?`,body:[`First instinct: open CreateProductCommandHandler, inject ICacheService, and right after persisting the product — before publishing anything to the bus — await cacheService.RemoveAsync('products'). Repeat in the delete handler, repeat in update. Three handlers, three lines. It's obvious, it's ordered with the commit, and you can read the entire story in one file.`,`The cost shows up later. Every future write path has to remember this ritual, and your write handlers now know about a read-side cache key. Forget it once — in the one handler nobody demos — and you're right back to Tuesday afternoon. And notice the ordering matters: invalidate after the save, so a failed write doesn't pointlessly stampede the cache with misses it didn't earn.`,`So the real question becomes: is invalidation a job of the write handler, or a consequence of the write? Both answers are correct. They just scale very differently.`],quote:`Put invalidation where the transaction commits, or it will be forgotten exactly once — the once that pages you.`,table:{headers:[`Placement`,`Wins`,`Risks`],rows:[[`Inline in each handler`,`Ordered with commit, zero infrastructure`,`Ritual to remember per handler; couples write side to read key`],[`Reaction to a domain event`,`Write handlers stay clean; one policy in one class`,`Delivery must actually be guaranteed`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,at:`1:56`}},{heading:`03 · Choosing the transport: in-memory vs. the bus`,body:[`Solution two: the handlers stop touching the cache entirely. CreateProduct publishes ProductCreatedEvent, delete publishes ProductDeletedEvent with the ID, update publishes ProductUpdatedEvent with the ID, name and price. Then one class — I'll call it CacheInvalidationProductHandler, and yes, naming is the other hard thing, fight me in the comments — implements INotificationHandler for all three events.`,`Inside, all three Handle methods funnel into one private HandleInternal that awaits cacheService.RemoveAsync('products', cancellationToken). Notice the product ID arrives and goes unused — the list cache is one key, so any product change nukes the whole list. All-or-nothing is a feature here: one key, one removal, zero partial-staleness puzzles.`,`Now the crucial fork. This publish is in-memory — MediatR fires the handler inside the same process. One instance? Perfect, zero infrastructure. The moment you run two instances behind a load balancer, instance A's in-memory event does absolutely nothing for instance B's cache, and you need messages over RabbitMQ so every instance hears the news.`],quote:`In-memory events invalidate one process; the bus invalidates a system.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline RemoveAsync`,`Ordered with commit, zero infra`,`Ritual to remember in every handler`,`1 service, few write paths`],[`In-memory events (MediatR)`,`Write handlers decoupled, one policy class`,`Lost on crash, single instance only`,`Modular monolith, 1 instance`],[`Bus events (RabbitMQ)`,`Reaches every instance`,`Duplicates + lag, more infra`,`Scaled-out / distributed`],[`TTL only`,`No code at all`,`Staleness window, wasted misses`,`Staleness tolerable in minutes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,at:`7:42`}},{heading:`04 · The race you can't delete`,body:[`Even with events flowing, there's a window you can't fully close. Picture a GetProducts that misses just before a delete commits: it reads the pre-commit rows, and while it's still round-tripping, the write commits and the invalidation fires. Then the slow read finishes and sets the cache — with the deleted product back in it. Stale until something else removes the key.`,`Same family of problem with fire-and-forget: in the lesson, the individual Handle methods return the task without awaiting it. That's acceptable for in-process notifications, but be honest about what it means — the removal may not have landed before the HTTP response returns, and a fast subsequent read can repopulate the key first. Await the remove in the request path when you can; keep a TTL as the net when you can't.`,`You don't win this race by pretending it doesn't exist. You win by shrinking the window and bounding the damage.`],quote:`Invalidation is a race you win by shrinking the window, not by declaring it closed.`,table:{headers:[`Race`,`What happens`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`Slow read repopulates`,`Miss reads pre-commit data, sets cache after invalidation fired`,`30–60 s TTL safety net`],[`Fire-and-forget remove`,`Remove lands after a newer read has refilled the key`,`Await remove before responding`],[`Multi-instance memory`,`Instance A removes; instance B's local cache never hears`,`Bus broadcast or shared cache`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,at:`9:00`}},{heading:`05 · What I'd ship tomorrow`,body:[`Boring, in order of growth. One instance and a handful of write handlers? Inline RemoveAsync after the commit — done in an afternoon. Handlers multiplying, or you want one invalidation policy in one place? MediatR notifications and a single CacheInvalidationProductHandler. Scaling to multiple instances? Same events, but over RabbitMQ, with idempotent removals — because at-least-once delivery means you will hear them twice.`,`And under every version of this, a TTL of 30 to 60 seconds. Not because the events are unreliable — because everything is, eventually, including you. The TTL is the difference between 'a bug' and 'a bug with a known blast radius'.`,`Cache invalidation doesn't have to be hard. It has to be deliberate: one key per query, one removal per write, one net under it all. That's the whole talk.`],quote:`Ship the boring version — invalidation only gets hard when you make it clever.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,at:`11:11`}}],diagrams:[{id:`read-write-invalidation`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Cache-aside reads + event-driven invalidation`,caption:`The read path only ever fills the cache; the write path is what ever empties it.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph READ["Read path - GetProducts"]
        R1["GET /products"] --> R2{"Cache hit on 'products'?"}
        R2 -- "hit" --> R3["Return cached list"]
        R2 -- "miss" --> R4["Query Postgres"]
        R4 --> R5["Set cache 'products'"]
        R5 --> R3
    end
    subgraph WRITE["Write path - Command handlers"]
        W1["Create / Update / Delete Product"] --> W2["Commit to Postgres"]
        W2 --> W3["Publish ProductCreated / Updated / Deleted"]
        W3 --> H1["CacheInvalidationProductHandler"]
        H1 --> H2["RemoveAsync 'products'"]
    end
    H2 -. "next read misses" .-> R2`},{id:`delete-then-fresh-read`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Sequence: delete product, then a fresh read`,caption:`Commit first, publish second, remove third — the next GET is guaranteed a miss and reads the truth.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as API
    participant D as Postgres
    participant M as MediatR Bus
    participant H as Invalidation Handler
    participant K as Cache
    C->>A: DELETE /products/1
    A->>D: Delete product and commit
    A->>M: Publish ProductDeletedEvent
    M->>H: Handle ProductDeletedEvent
    H->>K: RemoveAsync 'products'
    K-->>H: key removed
    H-->>M: done
    C->>A: GET /products
    A->>K: GetAsync 'products'
    K-->>A: miss
    A->>D: SELECT products
    D-->>A: 2 fresh rows
    A->>K: SetAsync 'products'
    A-->>C: 2 products - fresh`}],takeaways:[`Invalidate after the DB commit and before anything else: one RemoveAsync('products') per write path — 3 handlers (create/update/delete), 3 removals, one shared key.`,`One cache key per query, never per row: N queries → N keys; a key-per-product design turns one removal into thousands.`,`1 instance → in-memory MediatR notifications are enough; 2+ instances → RabbitMQ broadcast or a shared cache, because local memory lies across processes.`,`Back every event-driven invalidation with a 30–60 s TTL — that's your worst-case staleness when an event is lost or a race re-caches stale rows.`,`Await the cache remove in the request path when possible; a fire-and-forget remove can lose to a concurrent read and resurrect deleted data for a full TTL cycle.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Cache Invalidation Doesn't Have To Be Hard`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YdEbD53c8Bs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-wlNHmZHQ5sY`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-data`,order:45,title:`HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:40`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`.NET`,`Rate limiter`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Noon launch, one expired key, 4,000 upstream calls`,paragraphs:[`Release day. The promo email fired at 12:00 sharp and the clickwave hit our movie catalog endpoint at 40x normal traffic. At 12:04, the one cache entry every request leaned on — a 10-minute TTL written at 11:54 — expired. Every single incoming request missed at the same instant.`,`The code was the classic IDistributedCache dance: try Redis, on null call the OMDB API, write the result back. No lock, no coordination. Four thousand concurrent requests produced four thousand upstream calls in about 90 seconds. The free-tier quota evaporated, latency went from 200ms to 9 seconds, the thread pool starved, and timeouts started cascading into checkout.`,`The postmortem fix was embarrassingly small: Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Hybrid just shipped its first stable version, 9.3.0, out of preview. Its headline feature — stampede protection — collapses that miss race into a single factory call. We replayed the same load in staging: upstream calls went from thousands to a dozen, one per pod.`],problem:`How do you serve one hot key to thousands of concurrent callers without stampeding the upstream API — while keeping every pod's local copy fresh and invalidatable?`},tech:{stack:[`HybridCache 9.3.0`,`Redis`,`IDistributedCache`,`In-Memory L1`,`ASP.NET Core`,`OMDB API`],note:`HybridCache sits in front of a per-process in-memory L1 and a Redis-backed L2, coordinating fetches so only one caller pays the factory cost. Per-tier expirations and combinable entry flags let you tune freshness, compression, and which tiers participate.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why check-then-fetch melts your upstream`,body:[`Here's the pattern almost every team ships first: read from IDistributedCache, if it's null call the external API, write the result back. It works beautifully right up until the moment the key expires under load. That miss window is a race, and every concurrent request loses it at exactly the same time.`,`So why not just add a lock? A local SemaphoreSlim dedupes callers within one process, but with twelve pods you still get twelve upstream calls per key — better, not fixed. A Redis distributed lock coordinates across pods, but now you're paying an extra round trip per request and reasoning about lock expiry and crashed holders. You're hand-rolling a concurrency primitive in your hottest request path.`,`That's the exact gap HybridCache was created to close. The readme's number-one highlight is cache stampede protection — something that simply wasn't natively available in .NET before this abstraction, alongside configurable serialization and the performance work that ships with modern .NET.`],quote:`A cache miss under load isn't one problem — it's N problems arriving simultaneously.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Naive check-then-fetch`,`Zero extra code`,`Thundering herd on every expiry`,`Never for hot keys`],[`Per-process SemaphoreSlim`,`Cheap local dedupe`,`No cross-pod coordination`,`Small fleets, cold keys`],[`Redis distributed lock`,`Cross-pod dedupe`,`Extra round trip, lock-expiry edge cases`,`Rare, expensive writes`],[`HybridCache single-flight`,`Built-in, L1+L2, invalidation`,`New dependency to pin`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlNHmZHQ5sY`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the L1/L2 boundary`,body:[`HybridCache is two tiers wearing one interface. L1 is the in-memory cache inside your process — nanosecond hits, no serialization, but private to each pod. L2 is the distributed cache, Redis in our case — shared truth across the fleet, but every hit costs a network round trip plus serialize and deserialize.`,`The subtle part is the per-tier expirations. HybridCacheEntryOptions gives you Expiration for the distributed entry and LocalCacheExpiration for the local copy. Set the local one shorter — say 60 seconds against 5 minutes — so each node's L1 expires first, falls through to a still-warm L2, and refreshes itself. If both tiers expired simultaneously, every pod would hit the factory at once and you'd be back in stampede territory.`,`Why not L1 only? Twelve pods means twelve different answers to the same question. Why not L2 only? You'd pay serialization and a network hop on every single hit — your hottest keys deserve nanoseconds, not milliseconds.`],quote:`L1 is for speed, L2 is for truth — give the local copy a shorter clock so it stays honest.`,table:{headers:[`Tier`,`Latency`,`Scope`,`Best for`],rows:[[`L1 in-memory`,`~100ns, no serialization`,`Per process`,`Hot, read-heavy, shareable data`],[`L2 Redis`,`~1ms + encode/decode`,`Cluster-wide`,`Shared truth, cross-pod consistency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlNHmZHQ5sY`,at:`1:30`}},{heading:`03 · Stampede protection you didn't have to hand-roll`,body:[`The core API is GetOrCreateAsync with a factory. The first caller for a key runs the factory; every concurrent caller for that same key awaits the same in-flight task instead of firing its own fetch. From the upstream's perspective, one call per pod per key — not one call per request.`,`When we replayed the incident load in staging, 4,000 concurrent requests produced 12 factory executions — one per pod — and upstream latency stayed flat instead of climbing into a death spiral. That's the whole war story fixed by one abstraction swap.`,`And it's not just reads. RemoveAsync invalidates both tiers together, which kills the classic distributed-cache bug where you delete from Redis but every pod keeps serving its stale local copy for another four minutes.`],quote:`The best stampede protection is the one you didn't have to write mid-incident.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlNHmZHQ5sY`,at:`2:29`}},{heading:`04 · Flags, expirations, and the API in practice`,body:[`Wiring it up is deliberately boring: builder.Services.AddHybridCache with an options delegate, where you set default HybridCacheEntryOptions — Expiration, LocalCacheExpiration, and Flags. Then a minimal API endpoint, MapGet with an IMDb ID, injecting the HybridCache service and a typed HttpClient for OMDB. The cache call wraps the external fetch, and that's the whole endpoint.`,`The flags enum is where the tuning lives, and the values combine. DisableCompression skips the compression step — pointless overhead for tiny payloads. DisableDistributedCache gives you a local-only, ephemeral cache. DisableLocalCache forces every request through the distributed tier — exactly what you want for per-user data that must never be served to a different request on the same box.`,`Serialization is fully configurable too — System.Text.Json by default, swap in your own serializer factory if you need protobuf or versioned payloads. My advice: start with the defaults, measure, and only reach for the flags when the numbers tell you to.`],quote:`Defaults carry you 90% of the way; the flags exist for the 10% with opinions.`,table:{headers:[`Setting`,`What it controls`,`Rule of thumb`],rows:[[`Expiration`,`Distributed (L2) entry TTL`,`~5 min for stable reference data`],[`LocalCacheExpiration`,`In-memory (L1) TTL`,`~1/5 of L2 so nodes re-validate early`],[`DisableCompression`,`Skip payload compression`,`Off for payloads under ~1KB`],[`DisableLocalCache`,`Bypass in-memory tier`,`Per-user or fast-changing data`],[`DisableDistributedCache`,`Bypass Redis tier`,`Ephemeral, process-local data`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlNHmZHQ5sY`,at:`2:54`}}],diagrams:[{id:`hybrid-cache-request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`HybridCache request path: L1, L2, and the single-flight factory`,caption:`Every request flows through one abstraction — the tiers absorb hits, and only a true miss reaches the upstream API.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["GET /movies/:id"] --> B["HybridCache GetOrCreateAsync"]
    B --> C{"L1 hit?"}
    C -- "yes ~100ns" --> R["Return payload"]
    C -- "no" --> D{"L2 Redis hit?"}
    D -- "yes ~1ms" --> E["Deserialize + fill L1"] --> R
    D -- "no" --> F["Single-flight factory"]
    F --> G["OMDB upstream API"]
    G --> H["Write L2 5min + L1 60s"] --> R`},{id:`stampede-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Stampede protection: N concurrent misses, one factory call`,caption:`Request B awaits Request A's in-flight task instead of stampeding the upstream — this is the race that took down the noon launch.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant A as Request A
    participant B as Request B
    participant HC as HybridCache
    participant L1 as L1 Memory
    participant L2 as Redis L2
    participant UP as OMDB API
    A->>HC: GetOrCreateAsync key
    B->>HC: GetOrCreateAsync key
    HC->>L1: lookup
    L1-->>HC: miss
    HC->>L2: lookup
    L2-->>HC: miss
    HC->>UP: factory — single flight
    Note over B: B awaits A's in-flight task
    UP-->>HC: payload
    HC->>L2: set TTL 5 min
    HC->>L1: set TTL 60s
    HC-->>A: payload
    HC-->>B: same payload`}],takeaways:[`Set L1 TTL to roughly 1/5 of L2 TTL — e.g., 60s local vs 5 min distributed — so every node refreshes from Redis before the shared entry dies.`,`Stampede protection turns N concurrent misses into 1 factory call per pod: our incident replay went from ~4,000 upstream calls to 12.`,`An L1 hit costs ~100ns with zero serialization; an L2 Redis hit costs ~1ms plus encode/decode — design your hottest keys to live in L1.`,`Skip compression for payloads under ~1KB with DisableCompression, and use DisableLocalCache for per-user data that must never be shared across requests.`,`Pin 9.3.0 — the first stable, out-of-preview release — and budget a Redis p99 under ~5ms, or the L2 tier stops paying for itself.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — HybridCache is finally stable!!! FAST L1 + L2 Cache in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlNHmZHQ5sY`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`caching-patterns`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`}];export{e as lessons};